// ============================================================
// Synchronized Synthesis of Peptide-Based Macrocycles by
// Digital Microfluidics.
// Jebrail, Ng, Rai, Hili, Yudin, Wheeler -
// Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 2010, 49, 8625-8629.
// DOI: 10.1002/anie.201001604
//
// PLATFORM: Two-plate DMF device. 10 reagent reservoirs,
// 88 actuation electrodes. Droplet volume: 900 nL each.
// Actuation: electrical potentials applied to electrode array
// coated with a hydrophobic insulator (Teflon-based surface).
// Solvent: trifluoroethanol (TFE) or deionized water.
//
// CHEMISTRY: Peptide-based macrocycles (PMs) are synthesized
// from three components in a one-step Ugi-type reaction:
//   (1) Amino acid (AA) -- 0.1 M in deionized water, 90 nmol
//   (2) Aziridine aldehyde -- 0.05 M in TFE, 45 nmol
//   (3) tert-butyl isocyanide (tBuNC) -- 0.1 M in TFE, 90 nmol
// Reaction: mixed 20 s (circular actuation on 4 electrodes),
// incubated 1 h at RT in parafilm-sealed Petri dish.
// Isolation: top plate removed; TFE evaporated 15 min at RT.
// Five PMs synthesized in parallel (Val, Met, Ile, Thr, Pro).
//
// RING-OPENING: PM aziridines are ring-opened by thiobenzoic
// acid (PhCOSH, 0.1 M in TFE, 90 nmol) to yield macrocyclic
// peptide thioesters (ring-opened products, ROPs).
// Re-dissolution: 4 x TFE droplets (~3.5 uL total) per PM
// spot. PhCOSH merged, incubated 1 h at RT. Isolated by
// TFE evaporation. Total steps: 30 (6 per parallel reaction).
//
// NINE-MEMBERED MACROCYCLE: Pro-Leu dipeptide substrate
// (0.1 M in TFE, 90 nmol) reacted with aziridine aldehyde
// and tBuNC. Incubation: 3 h at RT with mixing every 30 min.
// Produces a [4.2.0] nine-membered ring -- a ring size
// conventionally difficult to access by cyclic peptide synthesis.
//
// VERIFICATION: MS (ESI) and NMR used off-chip. Products
// re-dissolved in 100 uL MeOH + 0.1% formic acid (MS) or
// 250 uL CD3OD (NMR). Reaction kinetics: ~70% Met depletion
// in 1 h by DMF vs ~90% macroscale.
//
// NOTE ON `mix` ABSTRACTION: All `mix` statements model
// DMF electrode-actuated droplet merging and mixing. Active
// mixing is achieved by periodic circular actuation on 4
// electrodes (20 s). Incubation steps are modelled as
// `heat ... at 25c` (room temperature) for the stated
// duration.
//
// NOTE ON PARALLEL SYNTHESIS: BioScript does not support
// explicit parallelism. The five PM syntheses and their
// downstream ring-opening steps are encoded sequentially
// but represent simultaneous on-chip operations. All five
// amino acid droplets, all five aziridine aldehyde droplets,
// and all five tBuNC droplets are dispensed and merged as
// concurrent tracks on the physical DMF device.
//
// NOTE ON SOLVENT EVAPORATION: Isolation by solvent evaporation
// (top plate removal + 15 min RT wait) is modelled as
// `heat ... at 25c for 15m` following the pattern used in
// the reference BioScript files for analogous physical steps.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main               -- entry: parallel PM synthesis
//                              (5 x AA + AzAld + tBuNC),
//                              isolation, ring-opening (5 x),
//                              nine-membered macrocycle synthesis
//   BB2  macrocyclePass     -- macrocycleSignal > 0: PM verified
//                              by MS/NMR QC; dispose
//   BB3  macrocycleFail     -- macrocycleSignal <= 0: QC fail; drain
//   BB4  macrocycleJoin     -- join: ring-opening QC gate
//   BB5  ringOpeningPass    -- ringOpeningSignal > 0: ROP verified;
//                              dispose all ROP droplets
//   BB6  ringOpeningFail    -- ringOpeningSignal <= 0: QC fail;
//                              drain all ROP droplets
//   BB7  finalCleanup       -- terminal: dispose QC signals,
//                              experiment ends
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PHASE 1 -- PEPTIDE MACROCYCLE (PM) SYNTHESIS
// Five parallel tracks: Val, Met, Ile, Thr, Pro amino acids.
// Each track: dispense AA (900 nL) + AzAld (900 nL) + tBuNC
// (900 nL), merge, mix 20 s, incubate 1 h at RT.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// --- Track 1: Val ---

// STEP 1a: Dispense amino acid Val (0.1 M, deionized water).
aaVal        = dispense 1 units of AminoAcid_Val

// STEP 1b: Dispense aziridine aldehyde (0.05 M in TFE).
azAldVal     = dispense 1 units of AziridineAldehyde

// STEP 1c: Merge AA + aziridine aldehyde; mix 20 s (circular
//          actuation, 4 electrodes) to initiate imine formation.
aaMixVal     = mix aaVal with azAldVal for 20s

// STEP 1d: Dispense tBuNC (0.1 M in TFE).
tbuncVal     = dispense 1 units of tBuNC

// STEP 1e: Merge tBuNC into AA+AzAld droplet; mix 20 s.
//          This completes the three-component Ugi-type mix.
rxnMixVal    = mix aaMixVal with tbuncVal for 20s

// STEP 1f: Incubate 1 h at RT (parafilm-sealed Petri dish).
//          Macrocyclization proceeds to completion (~70% yield
//          on DMF vs ~90% macroscale within 1 h).
heat rxnMixVal at 25c for 60m

// STEP 1g: Isolate PM-Val by evaporating TFE (15 min, RT).
//          Top plate removed; solid PM precipitates on electrode.
heat rxnMixVal at 25c for 15m

// --- Track 2: Met ---

aaMet        = dispense 1 units of AminoAcid_Met
azAldMet     = dispense 1 units of AziridineAldehyde
aaMixMet     = mix aaMet with azAldMet for 20s
tbuncMet     = dispense 1 units of tBuNC
rxnMixMet    = mix aaMixMet with tbuncMet for 20s
heat rxnMixMet at 25c for 60m
heat rxnMixMet at 25c for 15m

// --- Track 3: Ile ---

aaIle        = dispense 1 units of AminoAcid_Ile
azAldIle     = dispense 1 units of AziridineAldehyde
aaMixIle     = mix aaIle with azAldIle for 20s
tbuncIle     = dispense 1 units of tBuNC
rxnMixIle    = mix aaMixIle with tbuncIle for 20s
heat rxnMixIle at 25c for 60m
heat rxnMixIle at 25c for 15m

// --- Track 4: Thr ---

aaThr        = dispense 1 units of AminoAcid_Thr
azAldThr     = dispense 1 units of AziridineAldehyde
aaMixThr     = mix aaThr with azAldThr for 20s
tbuncThr     = dispense 1 units of tBuNC
rxnMixThr    = mix aaMixThr with tbuncThr for 20s
heat rxnMixThr at 25c for 60m
heat rxnMixThr at 25c for 15m

// --- Track 5: Pro ---

aaPro        = dispense 1 units of AminoAcid_Pro
azAldPro     = dispense 1 units of AziridineAldehyde
aaMixPro     = mix aaPro with azAldPro for 20s
tbuncPro     = dispense 1 units of tBuNC
rxnMixPro    = mix aaMixPro with tbuncPro for 20s
heat rxnMixPro at 25c for 60m
heat rxnMixPro at 25c for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PHASE 1 QC: MS/NMR off-chip verification of PM products.
// Representative QC performed on PM-Met (Fig. 4b, paper):
//   m/z 342 (PM-Met), confirmed against macroscale reference.
// macrocycleQC module abstracts ESI-MS + 1H-NMR readout.
// QC gate keyed on PM-Met as the representative track.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

macrocycleSignal = detect macrocycleQC on rxnMixMet for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: PM synthesis QC gate
// Pass (BB2): macrocycleSignal > 0 -> PM identity confirmed
//             by MS (m/z match) and NMR (chemical shifts);
//             dispose representative QC droplet.
// Fail (BB3): macrocycleSignal <= 0 -> MS/NMR mismatch;
//             drain representative droplet (synthesis failed).
// Both branches converge at BB4 (macrocycleJoin).
// ============================================================

if (macrocycleSignal > 0) {
    dispose rxnMixMet
} else {
    drain rxnMixMet
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: macrocycleJoin
// Dispose macrocycle QC signal droplet. Control falls into
// the aziridine ring-opening branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose macrocycleSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PHASE 2 -- AZIRIDINE RING-OPENING (ROP) WITH PhCOSH
// Each dried PM spot is re-dissolved in 4 x TFE droplets
// (~3.5 uL total) driven to the dried residue by electrode
// actuation. PhCOSH (0.1 M in TFE, 900 nL) is dispensed,
// merged, mixed 20 s, and incubated 1 h at RT. Products
// are isolated by TFE evaporation (15 min, RT).
//
// NOTE ON RE-DISSOLUTION: Four TFE dispensing events are
// chained via sequential `mix` steps to accumulate ~3.5 uL
// (4 x 900 nL) on each dried PM spot, matching the paper's
// M&M. The compiler does not support multi-unit scalar
// dispense into a solid spot, so each TFE aliquot is
// dispensed individually and merged in sequence.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// --- ROP Track 1: Val ---

// Re-dissolve PM-Val with 4 x TFE aliquots (total ~3.5 uL).
tfe1Val      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe2Val      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe3Val      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe4Val      = dispense 1 units of TFE

tfeMix12Val  = mix tfe1Val with tfe2Val for 5s
tfeMix123Val = mix tfeMix12Val with tfe3Val for 5s
pmSolnVal    = mix tfeMix123Val with tfe4Val for 5s

// Merge re-dissolved PM-Val with PhCOSH; mix 20 s; incubate 1 h.
phcoshVal    = dispense 1 units of PhCOSH
ropMixVal    = mix pmSolnVal with phcoshVal for 20s
heat ropMixVal at 25c for 60m

// Isolate ROP-Val by TFE evaporation (15 min, RT).
heat ropMixVal at 25c for 15m

// --- ROP Track 2: Met ---

tfe1Met      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe2Met      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe3Met      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe4Met      = dispense 1 units of TFE

tfeMix12Met  = mix tfe1Met with tfe2Met for 5s
tfeMix123Met = mix tfeMix12Met with tfe3Met for 5s
pmSolnMet    = mix tfeMix123Met with tfe4Met for 5s

phcoshMet    = dispense 1 units of PhCOSH
ropMixMet    = mix pmSolnMet with phcoshMet for 20s
heat ropMixMet at 25c for 60m
heat ropMixMet at 25c for 15m

// --- ROP Track 3: Ile ---

tfe1Ile      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe2Ile      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe3Ile      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe4Ile      = dispense 1 units of TFE

tfeMix12Ile  = mix tfe1Ile with tfe2Ile for 5s
tfeMix123Ile = mix tfeMix12Ile with tfe3Ile for 5s
pmSolnIle    = mix tfeMix123Ile with tfe4Ile for 5s

phcoshIle    = dispense 1 units of PhCOSH
ropMixIle    = mix pmSolnIle with phcoshIle for 20s
heat ropMixIle at 25c for 60m
heat ropMixIle at 25c for 15m

// --- ROP Track 4: Thr ---

tfe1Thr      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe2Thr      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe3Thr      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe4Thr      = dispense 1 units of TFE

tfeMix12Thr  = mix tfe1Thr with tfe2Thr for 5s
tfeMix123Thr = mix tfeMix12Thr with tfe3Thr for 5s
pmSolnThr    = mix tfeMix123Thr with tfe4Thr for 5s

phcoshThr    = dispense 1 units of PhCOSH
ropMixThr    = mix pmSolnThr with phcoshThr for 20s
heat ropMixThr at 25c for 60m
heat ropMixThr at 25c for 15m

// --- ROP Track 5: Pro ---

tfe1Pro      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe2Pro      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe3Pro      = dispense 1 units of TFE
tfe4Pro      = dispense 1 units of TFE

tfeMix12Pro  = mix tfe1Pro with tfe2Pro for 5s
tfeMix123Pro = mix tfeMix12Pro with tfe3Pro for 5s
pmSolnPro    = mix tfeMix123Pro with tfe4Pro for 5s

phcoshPro    = dispense 1 units of PhCOSH
ropMixPro    = mix pmSolnPro with phcoshPro for 20s
heat ropMixPro at 25c for 60m
heat ropMixPro at 25c for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PHASE 2 QC: MS/NMR off-chip verification of ROP products.
// Representative QC on ROP-Met (Fig. 4c, paper): m/z 480.
// ringOpeningQC module abstracts ESI-MS + 1H-NMR readout.
// QC gate keyed on ROP-Met as the representative track.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ringOpeningSignal = detect ringOpeningQC on ropMixMet for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Ring-opening QC gate
// Pass (BB5): ringOpeningSignal > 0 -> ROP identity confirmed
//             (m/z 480 for ROP-Met); dispose all ROP droplets.
// Fail (BB6): ringOpeningSignal <= 0 -> MS/NMR mismatch;
//             drain all ROP droplets (ring-opening failed).
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (ringOpeningSignal > 0) {
    dispose ropMixVal
    dispose ropMixMet
    dispose ropMixIle
    dispose ropMixThr
    dispose ropMixPro
} else {
    drain ropMixVal
    drain ropMixMet
    drain ropMixIle
    drain ropMixThr
    drain ropMixPro
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose ring-opening QC signal.
//
// NOTE: The nine-membered macrocycle synthesis (Pro-Leu
// dipeptide substrate, Fig. 5, paper) represents an
// orthogonal demonstration on the same device type and is
// encoded below as a self-contained sub-protocol appended
// after the main QC gate. It does not branch into the
// existing CFG; it runs unconditionally after finalCleanup
// to reflect its status as a separate demonstration
// experiment in the paper (not a downstream product of
// the five-PM parallel synthesis above).
// ============================================================

dispose ringOpeningSignal

// ============================================================
// SUPPLEMENTARY PROTOCOL: Nine-Membered Macrocycle Synthesis
// (Pro-Leu dipeptide + AziridineAldehyde + tBuNC)
// Fig. 5a-b, paper. Produces compound 8 ([4.2.0] ring).
//
// Substrate: Pro-Leu dipeptide (0.1 M in TFE, 90 nmol, 900 nL).
// NOTE ON DIPEPTIDE MANIFEST: Pro-Leu dipeptide is modelled
// as AminoAcid_Pro here as BioScript requires all reagents to
// be declared in the manifest block at file header. In a
// full implementation, a separate manifest ProLeu_Dipeptide
// would be declared. The comment documents the physical
// reagent identity for compiler/user traceability.
//
// Reaction conditions differ from the five-PM protocol:
//   - Incubation: 3 h at RT (vs 1 h for single-AA PMs)
//   - Mixing: every 30 min (6 x 20s mixing events over 3 h)
//     modelled as alternating heat + mix steps.
//   - Ring size: 9-membered (vs 7-membered [4.1.0] for AA PMs)
//   - ESI-MS product peak: m/z 421 (Fig. 5b, paper)
// ============================================================

// STEP 9a: Dispense Pro-Leu dipeptide (modelled as AminoAcid_Pro;
//          see note above). 0.1 M in TFE, 900 nL, 90 nmol.
proLeu       = dispense 1 units of AminoAcid_Pro

// STEP 9b: Dispense aziridine aldehyde (0.05 M in TFE, 900 nL).
azAld9mer    = dispense 1 units of AziridineAldehyde

// STEP 9c: Merge dipeptide + aziridine aldehyde; mix 20 s.
dipepMix     = mix proLeu with azAld9mer for 20s

// STEP 9d: Dispense tBuNC (0.1 M in TFE, 900 nL).
tbunc9mer    = dispense 1 units of tBuNC

// STEP 9e: Merge tBuNC; mix 20 s to complete three-component mix.
rxnMix9mer   = mix dipepMix with tbunc9mer for 20s

// STEP 9f: Incubate 3 h at RT with periodic electrode mixing
//   every 30 min (paper M&M). BioScript has no in-place
//   agitation primitive and self-mix (var with same var) is
//   rejected by the symbol visitor as a double-consume.
//   The 3 h incubation is therefore encoded as a single
//   heat statement (180 min). The periodic 20 s electrode
//   actuations are a physical device operation implicit in
//   the DMF controller firmware and do not consume or
//   produce a new droplet variable.
heat rxnMix9mer at 25c for 180m

// STEP 9g: Isolate nine-membered macrocycle by TFE evaporation
//          (15 min at RT). Top plate removed; solid product
//          precipitates. ESI-MS: m/z 421 (compound 8, Fig. 5b).
heat rxnMix9mer at 25c for 15m

// Final disposal of nine-membered macrocycle product droplet.
dispose rxnMix9mer

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module rnaYieldDetection
module rnaPurityDetection

manifest WholeBlood
manifest LysisSolution
manifest RNABindingBeads
manifest Wash1Solution
manifest Wash2Solution
manifest ElutionBuffer
manifest FluorinertFC40
manifest PluronicF127

// ============================================================
// World-to-Digital-Microfluidic Interface Enabling Extraction
// and Purification of RNA from Human Whole Blood.
// Jebrail et al., Anal. Chem. 2014, 86, 3856-3862.
// DOI: 10.1021/ac404085p
//
// System overview:
//   The world-to-DMF system bridges macroscale sample handling
//   (Extraction Module) with microscale DMF purification
//   (Purification Module) via a 10 uL Bridging Droplet formed
//   on the Bridging Electrode at the module interface.
//
//   Extraction Module: two 500 uL microcentrifuge tubes
//     (Reaction Chamber and Waste Chamber) driven by a single
//     peristaltic pump via Connector Tubes and Transfer Tubes.
//   Purification Module: DMF device with 40 chromium electrode
//     pads (6x6 mm, 40 um interelectrode gaps), Parylene-C
//     dielectric (7 um), Teflon-AF hydrophobic coating (50 nm),
//     300 um air gap. Droplets actuated at AC 100 Vrms.
//   Bridging Electrode: exposed ~1 mm x 6 mm edge of the bottom
//     plate, partially uncovered by the top plate, accepting
//     Transfer Tubes at ~70 degrees angle.
//   Bridging Droplet: 10 uL droplet formed on the Bridging
//     Electrode. The large-volume lysate is routed through
//     this droplet three times while a magnet beneath immobilises
//     RNA-bound magnetic beads; supernatant passes to waste.
//
// Blood specimen volumes: 25, 50, or 100 uL (fingerstick range).
//   This file encodes the 50 uL blood specimen arm as the
//   representative protocol (intermediate volume).
//   Lysis Solution: 130 uL; RNA Binding Beads: 20 uL;
//   total mixture volume driven through Bridging Droplet: 200 uL.
//
// Processing performance vs. conventional method:
//   >2-fold faster (20 min vs 45 min).
//   12-fold less reagent consumption (microscale 5-15 uL vs
//   macroscale 150-300 uL wash volumes).
//   RNA yields and quality (A260/A280, RIN) fully comparable.
//   Supports downstream qRT-PCR and RNA-Seq analyses.
//
// NOTE ON BRIDGING DROPLET ABSTRACTION:
//   The core world-to-DMF transition -- driving the macroscale
//   lysate through the 10 uL Bridging Droplet via peristaltic
//   pump while the magnet below captures bead-bound RNA -- is
//   modelled here as repeated `mix` operations. In the physical
//   system this is a pressure-driven convective flow event
//   (pump aspirates into Transfer Tube 2 while dispensing from
//   Transfer Tube 1), not a DMF electrode mixing step. The
//   convective transport is responsible for the slightly higher
//   RNA capture efficiency observed at 100 uL blood volume vs
//   the conventional diffusion-limited method.
//
// NOTE ON MAGNET STEPS:
//   Magnet engagement (bead immobilisation) and disengagement
//   (bead resuspension) are modelled as `heat` statements at
//   25 C (room temperature) for the duration of the operation,
//   as BioScript has no native magnet instruction. This is
//   consistent with the approach used in prior BioScript
//   encodings of magnetic bead protocols.
//
// NOTE ON SURFACTANT ADDITIVES:
//   Pluronic F127 (0.1% w/v) is added to the Elution Buffer
//   per the MagMAX kit adaptation (ref 20 in paper) to maintain
//   droplet actuation fidelity on the Teflon-AF surface.
//   FC-40 (Fluorinert) is the filler fluid in the DMF air gap.
//   Both are modelled as manifest reagents; their dispensing
//   into the device prior to the protocol begins is abstracted
//   in the device initialisation block below.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main            -- device init; lysis; bead binding;
//                          world-to-DMF transition (Bridging
//                          Droplet formation and bead capture);
//                          Wash 1; Wash 2; Elution
//   BB2  yieldPass       -- yieldSignal > 0: acceptable RNA yield;
//                          dispose eluate, proceed to purity QC
//   BB3  yieldFail       -- yieldSignal <= 0: insufficient yield;
//                          drain eluate
//   BB4  yieldJoin       -- join: purity QC branch begins
//   BB5  purityPass      -- puritySignal > 0: A260/A280 >= 1.8;
//                          dispose; experiment complete
//   BB6  purityFail      -- puritySignal <= 0: low purity; drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup    -- terminal: dispose QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DEVICE INITIALISATION:
//   Load filler fluid (FC-40 + Pluronic F127) into the DMF
//   air gap between bottom and top plates to enable droplet
//   actuation. Preload Purification Module reservoirs:
//     Wash 1 Solution:  15 uL (one unit)
//     Wash 2 Solution:  15 uL (one unit)
//     Elution Buffer:   10 uL (one unit, supplemented with
//                              0.1% w/v Pluronic F127)
//   Adjust magnetic stand so Transfer Tubes contact Bridging
//   Electrode at ~70 degrees. DMF actuation: AC 100 Vrms.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fillerFluid      = dispense 1 units of FluorinertFC40
surfactant       = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127
devicePrime      = mix fillerFluid with surfactant for 30s

// Preload wash and elution reagents into on-device reservoirs.
// These droplets sit at rest until actuated by electrowetting.
wash1Reservoir   = dispense 1 units of Wash1Solution
wash2Reservoir   = dispense 1 units of Wash2Solution
elutionReservoir = dispense 1 units of ElutionBuffer

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Introduce blood specimen into Reaction Chamber.
//   50 uL whole blood pipetted directly into Reaction Chamber
//   (500 uL microcentrifuge tube).
//   Blood specimens from healthy donors (ProMedDx, Norton MA).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bloodSpecimen    = dispense 1 units of WholeBlood

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Aspirate Lysis Solution from Transfer Tube 1 into
//   Reaction Chamber and actively mix with blood specimen.
//   Lysis Solution: 130 uL (for 50 uL blood; 2.6:1 v/v ratio).
//   Mixing: shuttle bolus between Transfer Tube 1 and Reaction
//   Chamber for 30 s via peristaltic pump.
//   MagMAX-Blood kit lysis buffer (Life Technologies).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lysisSolution    = dispense 1 units of LysisSolution
bloodLysate      = mix bloodSpecimen with lysisSolution for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Aspirate RNA Binding Beads from Transfer Tube 1
//   into Reaction Chamber. Mix with lysate for 30 s, then
//   allow bead-RNA binding to proceed for 5 min (300 s).
//   Bead volume: 20 uL. MagMAX silica-coated magnetic beads.
//   Total reaction volume: 200 uL (50 + 130 + 20 uL).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rnaBeads         = dispense 1 units of RNABindingBeads
beadLysateMix    = mix bloodLysate with rnaBeads for 30s

// 5 min bead-RNA binding incubation at room temperature (300 s).
heat beadLysateMix at 25c for 300s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Form Bridging Droplet and execute world-to-DMF
//   transition (Pass 1 of 3).
//   The peristaltic pump drives the full 200 uL reaction
//   mixture from Transfer Tube 1 through the 10 uL Bridging
//   Droplet on the Bridging Electrode and into Transfer Tube 2.
//   The Bridging Electrode is activated (AC 100 Vrms) to pin
//   the droplet and prevent spread to adjacent electrodes.
//   A movable external magnet is positioned directly beneath
//   the Bridging Electrode to immobilise bead-bound RNA as
//   the lysate flows through.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts pressure-driven convective transport
//   through the Bridging Droplet, not DMF electrode mixing.
//   Pass 1: establish Bridging Droplet and begin bead capture.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bridgingDroplet1 = mix beadLysateMix with devicePrime for 5s

// Engage magnet beneath Bridging Electrode to capture beads
// (modelled as heat at 25 C for duration of flow-through).
heat bridgingDroplet1 at 25c for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: World-to-DMF transition -- Pass 2 of 3.
//   Pump shuttles lysate back through Bridging Droplet a second
//   time (Transfer Tube 2 -> Bridging Droplet -> Transfer Tube 1)
//   with magnet engaged to maximise bead recovery from the
//   macroscale volume. Pressure-driven convective transport
//   increases capture efficiency over diffusion-limited methods
//   (accounts for slightly higher RNA yield at 100 uL blood
//   volume vs. conventional method; ref Model & Omann 1995).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bridgingDroplet2 = mix bridgingDroplet1 with devicePrime for 5s
heat bridgingDroplet2 at 25c for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: World-to-DMF transition -- Pass 3 of 3.
//   Final pass ensures maximum bead-bound RNA capture from
//   the reaction mixture before supernatant is sent to waste.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bridgingDroplet3 = mix bridgingDroplet2 with devicePrime for 5s
heat bridgingDroplet3 at 25c for 60s

// Send lysate supernatant to Waste Chamber. Beads remain
// immobilised on Bridging Electrode surface via magnet.
// beadCapture represents the bead pellet on the Bridging
// Electrode after all three passes; it is the live handle
// threaded through each subsequent wash/elution step.
// NOTE: bridgingDroplet1, bridgingDroplet2, and bridgingDroplet3
// are all consumed as inputs to their respective successor mix
// calls and must not be read again after those assignments.
// The fresh beadCapture variable avoids the stale-use bug
// that caused wash1Mix and wash2Mix to re-read already-consumed
// bridgingDroplet2[0] and bridgingDroplet1[0] in the original IR.
beadCapture      = mix bridgingDroplet3 with devicePrime for 5s
heat beadCapture at 25c for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Wash 1 -- deliver Wash 1 Solution to Bridging Electrode.
//   Purification Module actuates Wash 1 droplet (15 uL) onto
//   the Bridging Electrode via electrowetting (adjacent electrode
//   pads energised sequentially). Magnet disengaged to release
//   beads into Wash 1 droplet. Bolus shuttled between Transfer
//   Tubes via Bridging Droplet for thorough bead washing.
//   Then magnet re-engaged to immobilise beads; fluid sent to
//   Waste Chamber.
//   Wash 1 composition: per MagMAX-Blood kit instructions.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Disengage magnet (model: heat at 25 C for resuspension time).
heat wash1Reservoir at 25c for 10s

wash1Mix         = mix wash1Reservoir with beadCapture for 30s
heat wash1Mix at 25c for 30s

// Re-engage magnet to recover beads; send wash fluid to waste.
// wash1Beads carries the bead pellet forward to Wash 2.
wash1Beads       = mix wash1Mix with devicePrime for 5s
dispose wash1Mix

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Wash 2 -- deliver Wash 2 Solution to Bridging Electrode.
//   Identical sequence to Wash 1: magnet disengage -> resuspend
//   -> shuttle/mix -> magnet engage -> send supernatant to waste.
//   Wash 2 removes residual chaotropic salts and ethanol
//   carried over from Wash 1, ensuring clean RNA eluate.
//   Wash 2 composition: per MagMAX-Blood kit instructions.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat wash2Reservoir at 25c for 10s

wash2Mix         = mix wash2Reservoir with wash1Beads for 30s
heat wash2Mix at 25c for 30s

// wash2Beads carries the bead pellet forward to Elution.
wash2Beads       = mix wash2Mix with devicePrime for 5s
dispose wash2Mix

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Elution -- deliver Elution Buffer to Bridging Electrode.
//   Elution Buffer (10 uL, +0.1% Pluronic F127) actuated onto
//   Bridging Electrode. Magnet disengaged; beads resuspended.
//   Bead-RNA complex incubated in Elution Buffer at room
//   temperature to release RNA from silica bead surface.
//   Magnet re-engaged to immobilise beads; RNA-containing
//   eluate driven to a recovery site within the Purification
//   Module for collection by pipetting and off-device analyses.
//   Eluate volume: 10 uL (5- to 15-fold concentration vs.
//   conventional 30 uL elution; 12-fold reagent reduction).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat elutionReservoir at 25c for 10s

elutionMix       = mix elutionReservoir with wash2Beads for 30s

// Incubate to maximise RNA elution from bead surface.
heat elutionMix at 25c for 120s

// Re-engage magnet; drive RNA-containing eluate to recovery site.
heat elutionMix at 25c for 30s

// elutionMix is now the RNA eluate at the recovery site.
// Total processing time: ~20 min (vs. 45 min conventional).

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: RNA yield QC.
//   rnaYieldDetection module abstracts NanoDrop 2000 UV-vis
//   spectrophotometry (A260) or Qubit 2.0 fluorometry
//   (off-device). Acceptable yield range: per literature
//   (Robison et al. 2009; Menke et al. 2012) for 50 uL blood.
//   World-to-DMF system gives comparable yield to conventional
//   preparations from 25 and 50 uL blood (p >= 0.16).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

yieldSignal      = detect rnaYieldDetection on elutionMix for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: RNA yield QC gate
// Pass (BB2): yieldSignal > 0 -> acceptable RNA yield detected.
// Fail (BB3): yieldSignal <= 0 -> insufficient yield.
// NOTE: elutionMix must NOT be disposed here. It must remain
// live across the join (BB4) so the purity detect in BB4 can
// read it. Disposing elutionMix inside either branch causes the
// compiler to generate a phantom SSA version (elutionMix2[0])
// at the purity detect site -- an unassigned variable. The
// yield gate therefore only records the signal outcome; actual
// disposal of elutionMix happens in BB5/BB6 after purity QC.
// NOTE: 'drain' is not emitted by this compiler; the IR always
// produces dispose regardless. Both branches use dispose here
// to keep source and IR semantically consistent.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (yieldJoin).
// ============================================================

if (yieldSignal > 0) {
    dispose yieldSignal
} else {
    dispose yieldSignal
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: yieldJoin
// elutionMix is still live here. Control falls into purity QC.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: RNA purity QC.
//   rnaPurityDetection module abstracts A260/A280 ratio
//   measurement (NanoDrop 2000, off-device). A260/A280 >= 1.8
//   indicates acceptable RNA purity free of protein
//   contamination. RNA integrity (RIN) measured on 2100
//   Bioanalyzer with RNA 6000 Pico Chip (off-device).
//   World-to-DMF preparations show RIN and A260/A280 values
//   comparable to conventional preparations (Table S-1, paper).
//   Downstream applications supported: qRT-PCR (B2M, GAPDH,
//   PPIB housekeeping genes) and RNA-Seq (Illumina MiSeq,
//   50 bp single-end, ~99% mapping to hg19, R^2 ~0.85 vs
//   conventional).
//   NOTE: puritySignal is a virtual droplet representing the
//   QC readout, consistent with BioScript conventions for
//   off-device detection operations.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

puritySignal     = detect rnaPurityDetection on elutionMix for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: RNA purity QC gate
// Pass (BB5): puritySignal > 0 -> A260/A280 acceptable (>= 1.8);
//             RNA preparation complete and verified. Dispose eluate.
// Fail (BB6): puritySignal <= 0 -> low purity (salt or protein
//             carryover). Dispose eluate.
// NOTE: 'drain' is not emitted by this compiler; both pass and
// fail branches use dispose so source matches IR output exactly.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (puritySignal > 0) {
    dispose elutionMix
} else {
    dispose elutionMix
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose purity QC signal droplet. Experiment ends.
// RNA eluate has been collected off-device for downstream
// qRT-PCR or RNA-Seq analysis.
// ============================================================

dispose puritySignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module nfkbLocalizationDetection
module tnfSecretionDetection

manifest RAW2647_Macrophages
manifest PluronicF127
manifest PBS
manifest CultureMedium
manifest LPS_500ngmL
manifest TNF_CaptureBeads
manifest TNF_DetectionAntibody
manifest SecondaryFluorescentAntibody
manifest PBSTween

instructions:

// ============================================================
// High-Content Quantification of Single-Cell Immune Dynamics
// Junkin, Kaestli, Cheng, Jordi, Albayrak, Hoffmann, Tay
// Cell Reports 15, 411-422, April 2016
// DOI: 10.1016/j.celrep.2016.03.033
//
// Platform: two-layer PDMS microfluidic chip (Melin & Quake
// design). 40 individually addressable 1.35 nL isolated cell
// chambers. Membrane valves and on-chip peristaltic pumps
// control all fluid routing. Mounted on automated inverted
// epifluorescence microscope (Nikon Eclipse Ti) inside
// atmospheric enclosure (37 C, 5% CO2, saturating humidity).
//
// Cells: RAW 264.7 mouse macrophages. Non-adherent; retained
// by weir-type trap (slits without bypass) inside chambers
// functionalized to be non-adherent (Pluronic F-127 coated).
//
// Stimulation: LPS (Sigma L4524) 500 ng/mL, single 2 hr
// pulse (Fig 4B left; mimics brief pathogen exposure).
// Input switching time < 5 s via 16-inlet binary multiplexer.
//
// Readout 1 -- TNF secretion bead immunoassay (dynamic):
//   Antibody-functionalized beads (Spherotech SVP-100-4,
//   10.3 um streptavidin, biotinylated capture Ab) pre-loaded
//   in 7 bead storage chambers per column. Every 2 hr, the
//   peristaltic pump transfers medium from cell chamber to
//   binding chamber; beads circularly mixed for 90 min.
//   After binding, beads rinsed with PBS-Tween and returned
//   to storage. At run end, detection Ab + secondary Ab
//   complete the sandwich; beads imaged; intensity correlated
//   to on-chip calibration curve (R2 = 0.9984, Fig 2B).
//   LOD: ~60,000 TNF molecules. 7 time windows: 0-2, 2-4,
//   4-6, 6-8, 8-10, 10-12, 12-14 hr post-stimulus.
//
// Readout 2 -- NF-kB (RelA) localization (dynamic):
//   Time-lapse epifluorescence imaging every ~6 min.
//   Cells express RelA-GFP; nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio
//   reports NF-kB activation state continuously.
//
// BioScript abstraction notes:
//   - `mix` is a consuming operation on both operands.
//     The "cell thread" is modelled as a fluid variable
//     that is re-produced at every feeding or sampling step
//     by mixing with the incoming fresh medium or bead-wash
//     product. This reflects how peristaltic pumping cycles
//     fluid through and out of the cell chamber.
//   - `mix cellVar with LPS_500ngmL` abstracts multiplexer
//     routing of the LPS inlet into the chamber for 2 hr.
//   - `heat` at 37c models on-chip incubation (non-consuming).
//   - Bead time-points: each window uses a freshly dispensed
//     bead variable; the washed bead product carries into the
//     next step as the cell-thread carrier, maintaining a
//     single linear ownership chain through the experiment.
//   - `detect nfkbLocalizationDetection` abstracts nuclear
//     RelA ratio measurement; consumes the cell variable.
//   - `detect tnfSecretionDetection` abstracts bead
//     fluorescence + calibration curve lookup (baseline
//     bead used as QC gate key; returns > 0 if any bead
//     window exceeds LOD of ~60,000 TNF molecules).
//   - h token unsupported: 2 hr = 120m throughout.
//   - `dispose` in pass-branches; `drain` in fail-branches.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main           -- chip prep, cell loading, baseline
//                          TNF assay (t0), LPS 2 hr pulse,
//                          6x post-stimulus TNF assays,
//                          sandwich Ab completion, NF-kB detect
//   BB2  nfkbPass       -- nfkbSignal > 0: RelA nuclear peak
//                          detected; dispose cell variable
//   BB3  nfkbFail       -- nfkbSignal <= 0: no activation;
//                          drain cell variable
//   BB4  nfkbJoin       -- join: TNF QC gate follows
//   BB5  tnfPass        -- tnfSignal > 0: TNF detected;
//                          dispose baseline bead variable
//   BB6  tnfFail        -- tnfSignal <= 0: all beads below
//                          LOD; drain baseline bead variable
//   BB7  finalCleanup   -- terminal: dispose QC signals
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Chip surface preparation.
//   All internal surfaces except cell chambers coated with
//   2 mg/mL Pluronic F-127 in DI water overnight via control
//   script to block protein adsorption and bead adhesion.
//   Rinsed extensively with PBS before cell loading.
//   `mix` abstracts the automated overnight rinse sequence.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pluronicSol  = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127
pbsRinse     = dispense 1 units of PBS
devicePrimed = mix pluronicSol with pbsRinse for 720m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Cell loading.
//   RAW 264.7 macrophages (versene-detached, 20 um filtered,
//   1e5-3.5e5 cells/mL) flowed through inlets. Binary
//   multiplexer directs cells to individual chambers. Weir
//   trap captures non-adherent macrophages. < 10 min total.
//   `mix` abstracts the flow-through trapping event.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

macrophages  = dispense 1 units of RAW2647_Macrophages
cellsLoaded  = mix macrophages with devicePrimed for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Post-loading equilibration.
//   Fresh medium pumped into chamber; cells equilibrate
//   inside sealed chambers at 37 C / 5% CO2 for 2 hr.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medEq        = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
cellsEq      = mix cellsLoaded with medEq for 10m
heat cellsEq at 37c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Baseline TNF bead capture (t = 0-2 hr, pre-LPS).
//   Medium surrounding cells sampled for 2 hr to establish
//   pre-stimulus TNF baseline. Bead_1 transferred from
//   storage to binding chamber; medium + bead mixed by
//   circular peristaltic pumping for 90 min (Fig 2A).
//   Bead rinsed with PBS-Tween; returned to storage.
//   `mix cellsEq with medT0` models the medium sampling
//   step (consumes cellsEq); bead1Washed carries the
//   cell-thread forward into the stimulation phase.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medT0        = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
sampleT0     = mix cellsEq with medT0 for 120m

bead1        = dispense 1 units of TNF_CaptureBeads
pbt1         = dispense 1 units of PBSTween
bead1Bound   = mix sampleT0 with bead1 for 90m
bead1Washed  = mix bead1Bound with pbt1 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: LPS stimulation -- 2 hr pulse.
//   LPS at 500 ng/mL injected via multiplexer into cell
//   chamber for exactly 2 hr. Switching time < 5 s.
//   `mix bead1Washed with lpsInput` abstracts the LPS
//   inlet routing; bead1Washed carries the cell state
//   as fluid thread; result is cellsStim.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lpsInput     = dispense 1 units of LPS_500ngmL
cellsStim    = mix bead1Washed with lpsInput for 120m
heat cellsStim at 37c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: LPS washout.
//   Multiplexer switches back to medium inlet; LPS-free
//   medium flows through chamber (< 5 s switchover time).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medWash      = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
cellsWashed  = mix cellsStim with medWash for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: TNF bead capture -- time point 1 (0-2 hr post-LPS).
//   Medium sampled from chamber; bead_2 moved to binding
//   chamber and mixed for 90 min; rinsed with PBS-Tween.
//   bead2Washed carries cell thread into next window.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medTP1       = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
sampleTP1    = mix cellsWashed with medTP1 for 120m

bead2        = dispense 1 units of TNF_CaptureBeads
pbt2         = dispense 1 units of PBSTween
bead2Bound   = mix sampleTP1 with bead2 for 90m
bead2Washed  = mix bead2Bound with pbt2 for 5m

heat bead2Washed at 37c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: TNF bead capture -- time point 2 (2-4 hr post-LPS).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medTP2       = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
sampleTP2    = mix bead2Washed with medTP2 for 120m

bead3        = dispense 1 units of TNF_CaptureBeads
pbt3         = dispense 1 units of PBSTween
bead3Bound   = mix sampleTP2 with bead3 for 90m
bead3Washed  = mix bead3Bound with pbt3 for 5m

heat bead3Washed at 37c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: TNF bead capture -- time point 3 (4-6 hr post-LPS).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medTP3       = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
sampleTP3    = mix bead3Washed with medTP3 for 120m

bead4        = dispense 1 units of TNF_CaptureBeads
pbt4         = dispense 1 units of PBSTween
bead4Bound   = mix sampleTP3 with bead4 for 90m
bead4Washed  = mix bead4Bound with pbt4 for 5m

heat bead4Washed at 37c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: TNF bead capture -- time point 4 (6-8 hr post-LPS).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medTP4       = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
sampleTP4    = mix bead4Washed with medTP4 for 120m

bead5        = dispense 1 units of TNF_CaptureBeads
pbt5         = dispense 1 units of PBSTween
bead5Bound   = mix sampleTP4 with bead5 for 90m
bead5Washed  = mix bead5Bound with pbt5 for 5m

heat bead5Washed at 37c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: TNF bead capture -- time point 5 (8-10 hr post-LPS).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medTP5       = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
sampleTP5    = mix bead5Washed with medTP5 for 120m

bead6        = dispense 1 units of TNF_CaptureBeads
pbt6         = dispense 1 units of PBSTween
bead6Bound   = mix sampleTP5 with bead6 for 90m
bead6Washed  = mix bead6Bound with pbt6 for 5m

heat bead6Washed at 37c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 12: TNF bead capture -- time point 6 (10-12 hr post-LPS).
//   Final medium sampling window. bead7Washed carries the
//   cell-thread terminal state into the sandwich completion.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

medTP6       = dispense 1 units of CultureMedium
sampleTP6    = mix bead6Washed with medTP6 for 120m

bead7        = dispense 1 units of TNF_CaptureBeads
pbt7         = dispense 1 units of PBSTween
bead7Bound   = mix sampleTP6 with bead7 for 90m
bead7Washed  = mix bead7Bound with pbt7 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 13: Sandwich assay completion -- detection antibodies.
//   After all 7 time-point bead captures, detection Ab
//   (genwaybio GWB-489500, 3.19 ug/mL) applied for 30 min
//   with circular mixing, then secondary fluorescent Ab
//   (USB I1903-12H, 3.3 ug/mL) for 30 min. PBS-Tween rinse
//   between steps. bead7Washed carries the accumulated
//   cell-state thread; `mix` with detAb and secAb models
//   the Ab incubation over all stored beads simultaneously.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

detAb        = dispense 1 units of TNF_DetectionAntibody
secAb        = dispense 1 units of SecondaryFluorescentAntibody

detStep      = mix bead7Washed with detAb for 30m
sandwichDone = mix detStep with secAb for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 14: NF-kB (RelA) localization -- aggregate QC readout.
//   Automated stage images every ~6 min throughout the 20 hr
//   run in GFP and brightfield channels. RelA-GFP nuclear/
//   cytoplasmic ratio computed per frame by MATLAB. Here,
//   nfkbLocalizationDetection abstracts the binary
//   determination: was a RelA nuclear peak observed (> 2 SD
//   above pre-stimulus baseline) during or after LPS pulse?
//   ~93.6% of cells (n=46) show NF-kB activation peak
//   following LPS stimulation (Fig 6A).
//   `detect` is applied to sandwichDone which holds the
//   terminal cell state; this consumes sandwichDone.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

nfkbSignal   = detect nfkbLocalizationDetection on sandwichDone for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: NF-kB activation QC gate
// Pass (BB2): nfkbSignal > 0 -> RelA nuclear peak detected;
//             cell categorised NF-kB+; dispose sandwichDone.
// Fail (BB3): nfkbSignal <= 0 -> no activation peak;
//             cell categorised NF-kB-; drain sandwichDone.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (nfkbJoin).
// ============================================================

if (nfkbSignal > 0) {
    dispose sandwichDone
} else {
    drain sandwichDone
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: nfkbJoin
// Dispose NF-kB QC signal. Control falls into TNF
// secretion QC gate (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose nfkbSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 15: TNF secretion fluorescence readout.
//   Beads in storage chambers imaged (brightfield + GFP
//   channel). Fluorescence correlated to on-chip calibration
//   curve (R2 = 0.9984, LOD ~60,000 molecules, Fig 2B).
//   tnfSecretionDetection applied to bead1Washed (baseline
//   bead, still alive from STEP 4) as the QC gate key;
//   signal > 0 if any time-point bead exceeds LOD.
//   ~93.5% of cells produce detectable TNF (Fig 6A).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfSignal    = detect tnfSecretionDetection on bead1Washed for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: TNF secretion QC gate
// Pass (BB5): tnfSignal > 0 -> measurable TNF secretion in
//             at least one 2 hr window (TNF+ cell);
//             dispose bead1Washed.
// Fail (BB6): tnfSignal <= 0 -> all time-point beads below
//             LOD (TNF- cell, ~6.5% per Fig 6A); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (tnfSignal > 0) {
    dispose bead1Washed
} else {
    drain bead1Washed
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose TNF QC signal. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose tnfSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module nfkbActivationDetection
module cytokineSecretionDetection
module migrationMorphologyDetection

manifest RAW264_7_Macrophage
manifest LPS_Stimulus
manifest TNF_Stimulus
manifest PBS_Buffer
manifest CultureMedia
manifest BeadConjugatedAntibody
manifest FluorescentSandwichReagent
manifest NuclearStain
manifest WashBuffer

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Quantitative Multiparameter Measuring of Dynamic
// Single-Cell Immune Responses
// Michael Junkin and Savas Tay
// Department of Biosystems Science and Engineering, ETH Zurich
// DOI: 10.1109/EMBC.2014.6944325 (EMBC 2014)
//
// This protocol encodes the on-chip single-cell microfluidic
// assay for simultaneous measurement of:
//   1. NF-kB nuclear translocation (transcription factor
//      activation, via GFP fusion protein, fluorescence)
//   2. Cytokine/protein secretion (bead-based fluorescent
//      sandwich assay, on-chip, multiplexed)
//   3. Cell migration and morphology (time-lapse imaging)
//
// Cells: RAW264.7 macrophages expressing NF-kB-GFP fusion.
// Stimuli: LPS and/or TNF with arbitrary temporal profiles
//          (brief pulses in seconds to prolonged inputs).
// Platform: Custom PDMS microfluidic device with single-cell
//           trapping chambers. Bead-based assay conducted
//           on-chip. All measurements nondestructive.
//
// NOTE ON TRAPPING ABSTRACTION: `mix` statements in Steps 2
// and 3 abstract hydrodynamic single-cell trapping and
// chamber loading events (channel-flow, not DMF electrode
// mixing). There is no bulk-reagent mixing occurring; `mix`
// is used per BioScript convention for flow-through steps.
//
// NOTE ON STIMULUS PULSES: The system supplies arbitrary
// temporal input profiles. Both LPS (brief pulse) and TNF
// (prolonged) are encoded independently and then presented
// sequentially in the stimulation loop to model dynamic
// multiparameter inputs.
//
// NOTE ON DURATION TOKENS: BioScript (lilott8/BioScript)
// accepts only `s` (seconds) and `m` (minutes) as duration
// units. Hours are converted to minutes (e.g. 4 h = 240m).
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main               -- device prime, cell trap, baseline
//   BB2  stimulusPass       -- nfkbSignal > 0: NF-kB activated;
//                             proceed to secretion assay
//   BB3  stimulusFail       -- nfkbSignal <= 0: no activation;
//                             drain and skip secretion branch
//   BB4  stimulusJoin       -- join: secretion assay branch
//   BB5  secretionPass      -- secretionSignal > 0: cytokines
//                             detected; dispose and record
//   BB6  secretionFail      -- secretionSignal <= 0: no cytokine
//                             signal; drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup       -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Prime microfluidic device with PBS buffer.
//         Removes air bubbles and passivates PDMS channel
//         surfaces to minimise non-specific cell adhesion.
//         Priming run at low flow rate (~1 nL/s) for 5 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primingBuffer = dispense 1 units of PBS_Buffer
chamberWash   = dispense 1 units of PBS_Buffer

primingMix = mix primingBuffer with chamberWash for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Load RAW264.7 macrophages into device.
//         Cells suspended in culture media at ~1e6 cells/mL.
//         Hydrodynamic trapping isolates single cells in
//         distinct chambers (custom trap geometry).
//         NOTE: `mix` abstracts channel-flow loading event;
//         no bulk mixing occurs at this step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

macrophageCells  = dispense 1 units of RAW264_7_Macrophage
cellLoadingMedia = dispense 1 units of CultureMedia

trappedCells = mix macrophageCells with cellLoadingMedia for 2s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Wash untrapped cells from channels.
//         PBS flush removes excess cells; only single cells
//         retained in trapping chambers proceed. Trapping
//         efficiency verified by bright-field imaging.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

washStep = mix trappedCells with primingMix for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Baseline imaging - pre-stimulus.
//         Time-lapse bright-field images and GFP fluorescence
//         images acquired before stimulation.
//         NF-kB-GFP baseline: cytoplasmic (inactive state).
//         Cell morphology and position recorded as t=0.
//         Migration baseline: centroid coordinates logged.
//         Modelled as a `heat` hold at 37 C for 15 min
//         (standard incubation before stimulation).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat washStep at 37c for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Apply dynamic LPS stimulus (brief pulse).
//         LPS (lipopolysaccharide, E. coli O55:B5) delivered
//         as a brief pulse (seconds-range input) via upstream
//         microfluidic control valve actuation.
//         Stimulus mimics pathogen-associated molecular pattern
//         encountered physiologically.
//         Pulse duration: 60 s (representative short input).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lpsStimulus   = dispense 1 units of LPS_Stimulus
lpsPulse      = mix washStep with lpsStimulus for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Apply prolonged TNF stimulus.
//         TNF (tumour necrosis factor) delivered as a
//         sustained input to model cytokine-driven NF-kB
//         reactivation. Prolonged stimulus = 60 min exposure.
//         Sequential delivery allows separate characterisation
//         of LPS-triggered vs TNF-triggered NF-kB dynamics.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfStimulus   = dispense 1 units of TNF_Stimulus
tnfExposure   = mix lpsPulse with tnfStimulus for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Dynamic NF-kB fluorescence imaging (activation).
//         NF-kB-GFP nuclear translocation monitored in real
//         time by fluorescence microscopy. Nuclear/cytoplasmic
//         ratio computed per frame per cell.
//         Images acquired every 3-5 min across the stimulus
//         window (modelled here as a single 4 h observation
//         window = 240m). Cell migration and morphology changes
//         co-analysed from same image series (non-destructive).
//         nfkbActivationDetection module abstracts:
//           - fluorescence image acquisition
//           - nuclear mask segmentation
//           - N/C ratio computation
//           - migration vector extraction
//           - morphology descriptor calculation
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat tnfExposure at 37c for 240m

nfkbSignal = detect nfkbActivationDetection on tnfExposure for 300s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: NF-kB activation QC gate
// Pass (BB2): nfkbSignal > 0 -> NF-kB nuclear translocation
//             detected (activation confirmed); proceed to
//             on-chip cytokine secretion assay branch.
// Fail (BB3): nfkbSignal <= 0 -> no nuclear translocation
//             detected; drain stimulated droplet (no response).
// Both branches converge at BB4 (stimulusJoin).
// ============================================================

if (nfkbSignal > 0) {
    dispose tnfExposure
} else {
    drain tnfExposure
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: stimulusJoin
// Dispose NF-kB QC signal. Control falls into on-chip
// cytokine secretion bead assay branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose nfkbSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Periodically sample conditioned medium.
//         Supernatant from single-cell chamber collected at
//         defined time intervals (every ~30-60 min).
//         Sampling is non-destructive; fresh media returned
//         to maintain cell viability throughout assay.
//         Secreted volume: ~50 nL per sample.
//         This step captures time-resolved cytokine release.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

conditionedMedium = dispense 1 units of CultureMedia
mediumSample      = mix conditionedMedium with washStep for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: On-chip fluorescent bead-based sandwich assay.
//         Antibody-conjugated capture beads mixed with
//         conditioned medium sample in dedicated assay zone.
//         Secreted proteins (cytokines, chemokines, etc.)
//         captured on bead surface.
//         Incubated for 60 min at room temperature (25 C)
//         to allow sandwich complex formation.
//         Multiple cytokines assayed simultaneously (TNF,
//         IL-6, MCP-1, others) via spectrally distinct beads.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

captureBeads      = dispense 1 units of BeadConjugatedAntibody
beadCaptureMix    = mix mediumSample with captureBeads for 5s

heat beadCaptureMix at 25c for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Add fluorescent detection antibody (sandwich).
//          Secondary fluorescent antibody added to complete
//          the sandwich immunoassay. Binds captured secreted
//          proteins on beads. Incubated 30 min at 25 C.
//          Readout: fluorescence intensity per bead cluster
//          proportional to secreted protein concentration.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

detectionAntibody   = dispense 1 units of FluorescentSandwichReagent
sandwichComplex     = mix beadCaptureMix with detectionAntibody for 5s

heat sandwichComplex at 25c for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: Wash unbound detection antibody.
//          Wash buffer removes excess unbound fluorescent
//          antibody to reduce background signal.
//          Critical for quantitative accuracy of secretion
//          measurement at single-cell secretion levels.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

washBuf     = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
washedBeads = mix sandwichComplex with washBuf for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 12: Fluorescence detection of bead assay (secretion).
//          On-chip fluorescence imaging of beads in assay zone.
//          cytokineSecretionDetection module abstracts:
//            - bead cluster fluorescence image acquisition
//            - bead segmentation and intensity quantification
//            - multi-cytokine channel demultiplexing
//            - concentration calibration (standard curve)
//          Result: quantitative secreted protein levels per
//          single cell per time point (pM to nM range).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

secretionSignal = detect cytokineSecretionDetection on washedBeads for 60s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Secretion assay QC gate
// Pass (BB5): secretionSignal > 0 -> cytokine signal above
//             detection threshold; dispose assay droplet and
//             record quantitative secretion profile.
// Fail (BB6): secretionSignal <= 0 -> signal below threshold
//             (below LOD or failed assay); drain all.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (secretionSignal > 0) {
    dispose washedBeads
} else {
    drain washedBeads
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose secretion QC signal. Multiparameter single-cell
// dataset now contains:
//   - NF-kB nuclear translocation time series (per cell)
//   - Cytokine secretion profiles (per cell, per time point)
//   - Migration vectors (from image analysis)
//   - Morphology descriptors (from image analysis)
// Dataset feeds predictive model construction for immune
// input-output characterisation.
// Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose secretionSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module liveCellImagingDetection
module qPCRDetection

manifest PluronicF127
manifest PBS
manifest Fibronectin
manifest NIH3T3_Cells
manifest CellMedium
manifest TNF_Stimulus
manifest Trypsin_EDTA
manifest LysisBuffer
manifest CellsDirect_RTmix
manifest TaqMan_PrimerPool
manifest SuperScriptPlatinumTaq
manifest TEBuffer

instructions:

// ============================================================
// High-Throughput Microfluidic Single-Cell Analysis Pipeline
// for Studies of Signaling Dynamics
// Kellogg, Gomez-Sjoberg, Leyrat & Tay
// Nature Protocols Vol. 9 No. 7 (2014), pp. 1713-1726
// DOI: 10.1038/nprot.2014.120
//
// Platform: Two-layer PDMS cell culture chip (Gomez-Sjoberg
// et al., Anal. Chem. 2007) with 96 independently addressable
// 35-nL chambers (~1 x 1 mm x 35 um). Valve actuation by
// computer-controlled pneumatic solenoids (20-30 psi air).
// On-chip peristaltic pump (3-valve sequence) for metered
// feeding (~0.35 nL per pump cycle). Up to 16 reagent inputs
// via herringbone mixer. Example cell lines: NIH3T3 mouse
// fibroblasts and RAW264.7 macrophages. Stimulus: TNF-alpha.
//
// NOTE ON PLATFORM ABSTRACTION:
// This is a valve-actuated continuous-flow microfluidic chip,
// not a DMF/droplet electrode device. All `mix` steps here
// abstract pressure-driven fluid exchange events through
// microchannels and chamber volumes -- not electrode-driven
// droplet merging. `heat` steps model on-stage incubation
// under environmental control (37 C, 5% CO2, >95% humidity).
//
// NOTE ON FEEDING UNROLL:
// The protocol specifies 2-h feeding intervals with 30%
// chamber-volume exchange. Steps 35-36 call for overnight
// (~12 h) imaging + feeding before stimulation. Six feeding
// rounds (0h, 2h, 4h, 6h, 8h, 10h) are unrolled explicitly
// below (BioScript has no loop construct). Each feeding round
// dispenses fresh CellMedium and mixes for 1m to model one
// pump sequence replacing ~30% of chamber volume (30 cycles
// x ~0.35 nL = ~10.5 nL out of 35 nL).
//
// NOTE ON STIMULATION:
// TNF-alpha concentration range: 0.01-100 ng/mL (Step 38).
// Encoded here as a single representative input. Stimulus
// delivery modelled as pressure-driven flow replacing ~100%
// chamber volume (mode: full flush, not pump cycling).
// 10-min TNF pulse followed by medium washout (Step 40).
//
// NOTE ON qPCR THERMAL CYCLING:
// One-step RT-STA protocol (Step 54):
//   RT:          50 C for 15 min  (= 15m)
//   Taq activation: 95 C for 2 min (= 2m)
//   14 STA cycles: 95 C / 15 s + 60 C / 4 min each.
//     Cycle duration = 15s + 4m = 255s per cycle.
//     14 cycles = 14 x 255s = 3570s. Modelled as a single
//     heat step at 60 C for 3570s (dominant annealing phase).
//   Hold: 4 C (post-STA; modelled as heat at 4c for 5m).
//
// NOTE ON DURATION TOKENS:
// BioScript accepts `s` and `m` only. No `h` token. All
// overnight/multi-hour durations converted to minutes.
//   2 h feeding interval = 120m
//   Fibronectin chamber coating overnight = 720m
//   Imaging + feeding phase (~12 h) = unrolled into 6 x 120m
//   Cell growth and attachment (1 h) = 60m
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main             -- chip priming, surface treatment,
//                           cell loading, feeding, stimulation,
//                           live imaging, cell retrieval, RT-STA
//   BB2  qPCRPass         -- qPCRSignal > 0: valid Ct values;
//                           dispose all lysate/reaction products
//   BB3  qPCRFail         -- qPCRSignal <= 0: no amplification;
//                           drain all
//   BB4  finalCleanup     -- terminal: dispose QC signal
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEPS 24-28: Chip surface treatment -- Pluronic blocking
// Pluronic F-127 (0.2% w/v in PBS) is flowed through all
// non-chamber surfaces for 1 h to block non-specific cell
// attachment to PDMS. Steps 24-29.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primingPBS      = dispense 1 units of PBS
primingPluronic = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127
pluronicMix     = mix primingPBS with primingPluronic for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 30: PBS wash -- remove Pluronic completely.
// Pluronic residue in chambers prevents fibronectin adhesion
// and downstream cell attachment. PBS flow for 30 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pbsWash1        = dispense 1 units of PBS
pluronicWashed  = mix pluronicMix with pbsWash1 for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 31: Fibronectin chamber coating.
// 100 ug/mL fibronectin in PBS flowed into non-chamber areas
// for 10 min to displace PBS, then valves opened to chambers.
// Chambers incubated overnight (720m) to promote ECM coating
// and rapid, reliable cell attachment (tested for 3T3 and
// RAW264.7 cell lines). Step 31.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fibronectinSoln   = dispense 1 units of Fibronectin
fibronectinCoated = mix pluronicWashed with fibronectinSoln for 720m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 32-33: Cell loading.
// NIH3T3 fibroblast single-cell suspension (5e5 to 2e6
// cells/mL) pressurized with 5% CO2 at 3-5 psi and loaded
// through CellsIn port via PEEK tubing. Target: 150 fibroblasts
// per chamber (~80% confluence at experiment start).
// Cells adhere to fibronectin within minutes. Cell loading
// completes in 2-4 h (automated scripted seeding rounds).
// Attachment modelled as 60m incubation at 37 C.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cellSuspension  = dispense 1 units of NIH3T3_Cells
loadedCells     = mix fibronectinCoated with cellSuspension for 60m
heat loadedCells at 37c for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEPS 34-37: Imaging and feeding -- 6 feeding rounds.
// 30% chamber volume replaced per round using on-chip
// peristaltic pump (30 cycles x ~0.35 nL/cycle = ~10.5 nL).
// Interval: every 2 h. Phase-contrast images acquired at each
// round to monitor cell growth (Step 37).
// 6 rounds unrolled covering ~12 h pre-stimulation imaging.
// Each feed: dispense fresh CellMedium, mix for 1m (pump seq).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Feed round 1 (t = 0 h, baseline)
feed1Medium  = dispense 1 units of CellMedium
fedCells1    = mix loadedCells with feed1Medium for 1m
heat fedCells1 at 37c for 120m

// Feed round 2 (t = 2 h)
feed2Medium  = dispense 1 units of CellMedium
fedCells2    = mix fedCells1 with feed2Medium for 1m
heat fedCells2 at 37c for 120m

// Feed round 3 (t = 4 h)
feed3Medium  = dispense 1 units of CellMedium
fedCells3    = mix fedCells2 with feed3Medium for 1m
heat fedCells3 at 37c for 120m

// Feed round 4 (t = 6 h)
feed4Medium  = dispense 1 units of CellMedium
fedCells4    = mix fedCells3 with feed4Medium for 1m
heat fedCells4 at 37c for 120m

// Feed round 5 (t = 8 h)
feed5Medium  = dispense 1 units of CellMedium
fedCells5    = mix fedCells4 with feed5Medium for 1m
heat fedCells5 at 37c for 120m

// Feed round 6 (t = 10 h)
feed6Medium  = dispense 1 units of CellMedium
fedCells6    = mix fedCells5 with feed6Medium for 1m
heat fedCells6 at 37c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEPS 38-41: Cell stimulation experiment.
// TNF-alpha input prepared in CellMedium (0.01-100 ng/mL).
// Stimulus delivered by pressure-driven flow replacing full
// chamber volume (Step 40). 10-min TNF pulse (mode: full flush).
// Log stimulus timing for alignment with image data (Step 40
// critical step note). Delivery: PTFE tubing, 5% CO2 at 7 psi.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfStimulus     = dispense 1 units of TNF_Stimulus
stimulatedCells = mix fedCells6 with tnfStimulus for 10m

// TNF pulse washout: medium restored to chamber (Step 40).
// 60-min window captures NF-kB nuclear translocation
// dynamics (p65-dsRed nuclear intensity tracked over time).
washoutMedium   = dispense 1 units of CellMedium
postStimCells   = mix stimulatedCells with washoutMedium for 60m

// Additional stimulation cycles can be added here for pulse
// trains (e.g. TNF at t=0, 60, 120 min -- Step 40 example).
// Encoded as single representative 10-min TNF pulse.

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEPS 32-43 (parallel): Live-cell fluorescence imaging.
// Automated time-lapse on inverted fluorescence microscope
// (Leica DMI6000B or Nikon Ti-E) with stage incubation
// (37 C, 5% CO2, >95% humidity). GFP and dsRed channels
// at 20x long-distance objective. 5-min frame interval.
// Nuclear p65-dsRed intensity / cytoplasmic p65-dsRed
// intensity quantified per cell per frame (Step 41).
// liveCellImagingDetection abstracts CellProfiler nuclear
// segmentation and NF-kB translocation quantification.
// Imaging runs for 300 min (5 h) post-stimulation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

imagingSignal = detect liveCellImagingDetection on postStimCells for 300m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 43: Fix/stain OR proceed to cell retrieval.
// Protocol Step 43: after stimulation, cells are either fixed
// for antibody endpoint staining OR retrieved live for gene
// expression analysis. Encoded here as live retrieval path.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEPS 44-48: Cell retrieval for gene expression analysis.
// Trypsin-EDTA (0.05% w/v) delivered to each chamber.
// Cells detach within 1-3 min (~90% retrieval efficiency).
// Cells collected through ~10 cm PEEK tubing into 96-well
// plate wells pre-loaded with 5 uL lysis buffer (CellsDirect
// resuspension buffer + lysis enhancer). PBS wash between
// chambers prevents cross-contamination (Step 48).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

trypsinSoln     = dispense 1 units of Trypsin_EDTA
detachedCells   = mix postStimCells with trypsinSoln for 3m

lysisBuffer     = dispense 1 units of LysisBuffer
cellLysate      = mix detachedCells with lysisBuffer for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEPS 49-54: One-step RT-STA (CellsDirect kit).
// Components per 10 uL reaction (Step 51):
//   5 uL 2x CellsDirect reaction mix (contains SuperScript III
//       / Platinum Taq mix)
//   1.5 uL TE buffer
//   2.5 uL 0.2x TaqMan primer pool (48 assays at 45 nM each)
//   1 uL SuperScript III / Platinum Taq mix
//   (+ 1 uL cell lysate added at Step 53)
//
// Thermal protocol (Step 54):
//   RT:           50 C, 15 min
//   Taq activation: 95 C, 2 min
//   14 STA cycles: [95 C / 15 s + 60 C / 4 min] x 14
//     = 3570 s total cycling (dominant 60 C annealing phase)
//   Hold: 4 C, 5 min (post-STA stabilisation)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rtMix           = dispense 1 units of CellsDirect_RTmix
primerPool      = dispense 1 units of TaqMan_PrimerPool
superscriptTaq  = dispense 1 units of SuperScriptPlatinumTaq
teBuffer        = dispense 1 units of TEBuffer

// Assemble RT-STA reaction mix (Steps 50-52)
tmp1            = mix rtMix with primerPool for 5s
tmp2            = mix tmp1 with superscriptTaq for 5s
reactionMix     = mix tmp2 with teBuffer for 5s

// Add 1 uL cell lysate (Step 53)
rtStaReaction   = mix reactionMix with cellLysate for 5s

// RT step: reverse transcription at 50 C for 15 min
heat rtStaReaction at 50c for 15m

// Taq activation: 95 C for 2 min
heat rtStaReaction at 95c for 2m

// 14 STA cycles -- dominant 60 C annealing/extension phase
// 14 x (15s denaturation + 255s extension) = 3570s total
heat rtStaReaction at 60c for 3570s

// Post-STA hold at 4 C for 5 min
heat rtStaReaction at 4c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 55: Dilute RT-STA product 1:3 with TE buffer.
// 22 uL TE added to 11 uL RT-STA product -> ~1:3 dilution
// (Step 55). Required before Fluidigm chip loading (Step 57).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

teDilutionBuffer = dispense 1 units of TEBuffer
dilutedProduct   = mix rtStaReaction with teDilutionBuffer for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEPS 56-58: Fluidigm BioMark qPCR.
// 48.48 Dynamic Array IFC: 48 samples x 48 TaqMan assays
// = up to 9,216 simultaneous qPCR reactions.
// Assay wells (left half): 3.75 uL 2x assay loading reagent
//   + 3.75 uL each TaqMan assay (20x).
// Sample wells (right half): 3.4 uL diluted cDNA +
//   3.75 uL TaqMan PCR master mix + 0.4 uL sample loading
//   reagent. IFC controller MX primes the 48.48 Dynamic Array.
// qPCRDetection abstracts BioMark thermocycling and Ct
// calculation by Fluidigm Real-Time PCR Analysis software.
// Relative expression: 2^(Ct_gapdh - Ct_gene).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

qPCRSignal = detect qPCRDetection on dilutedProduct for 240m

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: qPCR QC gate
// Pass (BB2): qPCRSignal > 0 -> valid Ct values detected;
//             dispose all reaction products.
// Fail (BB3): qPCRSignal <= 0 -> no amplification (failed RT,
//             empty chamber, or IFC loading error); drain all.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (qPCRSignal > 0) {
    dispose dilutedProduct
    dispose cellLysate
    dispose imagingSignal
} else {
    drain dilutedProduct
    drain cellLysate
    drain imagingSignal
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose qPCR QC signal. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose qPCRSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module nfkbTranslocationDetection
module geneExpressionDetection

manifest NIH3T3_NFkB_Cells
manifest LPS_Stock
manifest TNF_Stock
manifest CompleteMedia
manifest FibronectinSolution
manifest LPS_PulseStock
manifest TrypsinEDTA
manifest RNALysisBuffer
manifest cDNA_Primers
manifest GAPDH_Primer

// ============================================================
// Digital Signaling Decouples Activation Probability and
// Population Heterogeneity
// Kellogg RA, Tian C, Lipniacki T, Quake SR, Tay S.
// eLife 2015;4:e08931. DOI: 10.7554/eLife.08931
//
// Cell line: p65-knockout NIH 3T3 fibroblasts expressing
//   p65-DsRed (NF-kB reporter) and H2B-GFP (nuclear reporter).
//   Clonally derived to express p65-DsRed at lowest detectable
//   level to preserve near-endogenous expression.
//
// Two experimental arms are encoded in sequence:
//
// PROTOCOL 1 (BB1-BB6): LPS Dose-Modulation Experiment
//   Constant long-duration (LL) LPS stimulation at varying
//   concentrations (0.25 - 500 ng/ml), delivered via continuous
//   flow through microfluidic chambers. Modulates fraction of
//   responding cells AND NF-kB response timing/heterogeneity.
//   Duplicate chambers per condition on a single chip.
//   Stimulation by step increase in LPS via media exchange.
//   Live-cell imaging at 5-6 min intervals for ~250 min.
//   Outcome: digital (all-or-none) NF-kB activation; Hill
//   coefficient ~2.3; dose-dependent activation delay (35-80 min
//   median peak time); 5% (0.25 ng/ml) to ~100% (500 ng/ml)
//   responding cells. (Figures 2, 3)
//
// PROTOCOL 2 (BB7-BB12): LPS Duration-Modulation Experiment
//   Short-Strong (SS) pulsed LPS at constant 500 ng/ml, with
//   pulse duration varied from 1 to 40 s by washing with media
//   after each pulse duration. Controls fraction of responding
//   cells WITHOUT altering NF-kB response timing or cell-to-cell
//   variability. Outcome: 3.3% (1 s) to 87.5% (40 s) responding;
//   median peak time shifts < 2 min across all durations.
//   (Figures 4, 5, 7)
//
// PROTOCOL 3 (BB13-BB15, off-chip): Gene Expression Assay
//   Bulk 96-well plate LPS stimulation (1-500 ng/ml, 10,000
//   cells/well) for dose-dependent gene expression profiling
//   by Fluidigm qPCR (48.48 dynamic arrays). 8 target genes:
//   nfkbia, a20, nfkbie, tnf, ccl20, icam1, casp4, ccl5.
//   Normalized to GAPDH. (Figure 2E)
//
// TEMPERATURE: All cell culture and stimulation steps run at
//   37 C / 5% CO2 unless otherwise noted.
//   `heat` statements model incubator conditions.
//
// DURATION TOKENS: BioScript accepts s and m only.
//   1 day = 1440 min; used for cell seeding/growth steps.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1   main              -- device coating, cell seeding (Protocol 1)
//   BB2   llDoseHigh        -- LL high-dose LPS arm (500 ng/ml)
//   BB3   llDoseLow         -- LL low-dose LPS arm (0.25 ng/ml)
//   BB4   llDoseJoin        -- join: NF-kB detection (LL arm)
//   BB5   llPassGate        -- llSignal > 0: digital activation detected; dispose
//   BB6   llFailGate        -- llSignal <= 0: no activation; drain
//   BB7   llFinalJoin       -- join: Protocol 2 begins
//   BB8   ssPulseShort      -- SS short-pulse arm (1 s @ 500 ng/ml)
//   BB9   ssPulseLong       -- SS long-pulse arm (40 s @ 500 ng/ml)
//   BB10  ssPulseJoin       -- join: NF-kB detection (SS arm)
//   BB11  ssPassGate        -- ssSignal > 0: digital activation detected; dispose
//   BB12  ssFailGate        -- ssSignal <= 0: no activation; drain
//   BB13  ssFinalJoin       -- join: Protocol 3 (gene expression) begins
//   BB14  gexPassGate       -- gexSignal > 0: expression detected; dispose
//   BB15  gexFailGate       -- gexSignal <= 0: no expression; drain
//   BB16  finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose all QC signals, experiment ends
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// Device priming, fibronectin coating, and cell seeding.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Coat microfluidic chambers with fibronectin.
//   Fibronectin solution flowed through chambers to promote
//   cell adhesion to PDMS walls. Each experimental condition
//   is measured in duplicate chambers on the chip.
//   Models channel coating by mixing priming volume of
//   fibronectin into the microfluidic chamber.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fibronectin     = dispense 1 units of FibronectinSolution
media_prime     = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
coatedChamber   = mix fibronectin with media_prime for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Seed cells into coated chambers.
//   Approx 200 cells/chamber. Cells grown for 1 day (1440 min)
//   with periodic media replenishment until ~80% confluence.
//   `mix` abstracts channel-based cell loading; `heat` models
//   incubator growth at 37 C.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

seedCells       = dispense 1 units of NIH3T3_NFkB_Cells
loadedChamber   = mix seedCells with coatedChamber for 2s

// 24 h growth to ~80% confluence at 37 C (= 1440 min).
heat loadedChamber at 37c for 1440m

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 1: LPS Dose-Modulation (LL Signal)
// Constant, long-duration stimulation at two representative
// doses: 500 ng/ml (high, ~100% cells respond) and 0.25 ng/ml
// (low, ~5% cells respond). Both are parallel branches in the
// CFG that converge at BB4 (llDoseJoin) for NF-kB detection.
//
// NOTE ON ABSTRACTION: The microfluidic chip runs all
// concentrations (0.25 - 500 ng/ml) in parallel duplicate
// chambers. BB2 and BB3 encode two representative concentrations
// as the CFG fork. The full dose-response screen is captured
// in post-processing of the nfkbTranslocationDetection readout.
//
// Stimulation: media equilibrated to 5% CO2 containing LPS
// is delivered as a step increase; ligand loss due to
// cellular internalization is replenished by continuous flow.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Dispense LPS stock solutions for dose-modulation arm.
//   LPS stock concentrations are pre-diluted off-chip in
//   CompleteMedia before loading into chip reservoirs.
//   500 ng/ml: 1.2e7 LPS molecules per cell (see Appendix Table 4).
//   0.25 ng/ml: 6.0e3 LPS molecules per cell.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lpsHighDose     = dispense 1 units of LPS_Stock
lpsLowDose      = dispense 1 units of LPS_Stock

// ============================================================
// BB2: llDoseHigh
// High-dose LL arm: 500 ng/ml continuous LPS stimulation.
// Response: ~100% cells activate; rapid onset (~35 min peak);
// low cell-to-cell timing variability; sustained oscillations
// under continuous ligand replenishment.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4a (High-dose LL): Load 500 ng/ml LPS into the chamber.
//   LPS is delivered to the sealed chamber by continuous media
//   exchange. Duration ~250 min (entire imaging window).
//   `mix` abstracts the flow-through delivery event.
//   `heat` maintains 37 C / 5% CO2 during stimulation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

llHighChamber   = mix loadedChamber with lpsHighDose for 5s
heat llHighChamber at 37c for 250m

llHighSignal    = detect nfkbTranslocationDetection on llHighChamber for 250m

// ============================================================
// BB3: llDoseLow
// Low-dose LL arm: 0.25 ng/ml continuous LPS stimulation.
// Response: ~5% cells activate; delayed onset (>80 min peak);
// high cell-to-cell timing variability (heterogeneous dynamics).
// Same stimulus area achievable via longer duration (Area Rule).
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4b (Low-dose LL): Load 0.25 ng/ml LPS into the chamber.
//   Identical delivery method to high-dose arm; dose pre-diluted
//   in media before chip loading.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

llLowChamber    = mix loadedChamber with lpsLowDose for 5s
heat llLowChamber at 37c for 250m

llLowSignal     = detect nfkbTranslocationDetection on llLowChamber for 250m

// ============================================================
// BB4: llDoseJoin
// Both dose arms converge here. NF-kB translocation signals
// from high- and low-dose chambers are evaluated.
// Gate is keyed on llHighSignal (highest expected signal).
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: LL Dose QC gate
// Pass (BB5): llHighSignal > 0 -> digital NF-kB activation
//             detected in at least one chamber; dispose both
//             stimulated chamber readouts.
// Fail (BB6): llHighSignal <= 0 -> no translocation signal
//             (imaging failure or no activation); drain both.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (llFinalJoin).
// ============================================================

if (llHighSignal > 0) {
    dispose llHighChamber
    dispose llLowChamber
} else {
    drain llHighChamber
    drain llLowChamber
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: llFinalJoin
// Dispose LL dose QC signals. Control falls into
// Protocol 2 (LPS Duration-Modulation / SS arm).
// ============================================================

dispose llHighSignal
dispose llLowSignal

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 2: LPS Duration-Modulation (SS Signal)
// Pulsed LPS at constant 500 ng/ml; pulse duration varied
// from 1 to 40 s. Chambers washed with media after each pulse.
// Controls fraction of responding cells WITHOUT affecting
// NF-kB response timing or cell-to-cell variability.
//
// Two representative pulse durations encoded as CFG branches:
//   BB8:  1 s pulse  -> ~3.3% cells activate (few active)
//   BB9: 40 s pulse  -> ~87.5% cells activate (many active)
// Branches converge at BB10 (ssPulseJoin) for NF-kB detection.
//
// Key result: despite >25x difference in responding fraction,
// median peak time changes by < 2 min between 1 s and 40 s.
// This contrasts with LL dose modulation where peak time
// shifts > 35 min from 0.25 to 500 ng/ml.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Prepare fresh cells in new chambers for SS arm.
//   Same seeding protocol as Protocol 1. Fresh LPS pulse
//   stock (500 ng/ml) is pre-loaded. Media wash solution
//   is in a parallel reservoir for post-pulse washing.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ssCells         = dispense 1 units of NIH3T3_NFkB_Cells
ssMedia         = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
ssChamber       = mix ssCells with ssMedia for 2s
heat ssChamber at 37c for 1440m

// SS pulse stock: 500 ng/ml LPS for both short and long pulse arms.
ssPulseHigh     = dispense 1 units of LPS_PulseStock
ssPulseLong40   = dispense 1 units of LPS_PulseStock

// Media wash: flowed through chamber immediately after pulse
// to remove LPS (no further receptor occupancy after pulse end).
// Abstracts the post-pulse wash by mixing in fresh media.
mediaWashShort  = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
mediaWashLong   = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia

// ============================================================
// BB8: ssPulseShort
// 1 s pulse at 500 ng/ml LPS.
// LPS delivered for exactly 1 s then washed out with media.
// Result: ~3.3% cells activate; rapid, synchronous responses
// in the small activating fraction. Same responding fraction
// as 0.25 ng/ml continuous LL stimulation (Fig 5B vs Fig 2D).
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6a (1 s SS pulse): Deliver 1 s LPS pulse, then wash.
//   `mix for 1s` abstracts the 1 s flow of LPS through the
//   chamber at 500 ng/ml. Media wash immediately follows
//   to terminate receptor-ligand contact.
//   Post-wash: chamber sealed; imaging at 5-6 min intervals.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// 1 s LPS pulse (500 ng/ml): flow-through event.
ss1sPulsed      = mix ssChamber with ssPulseHigh for 1s
// Immediate media wash to terminate stimulus.
ss1sWashed      = mix ss1sPulsed with mediaWashShort for 5s

// Image for 150 min post-pulse (NF-kB peak occurs ~50 min).
heat ss1sWashed at 37c for 150m
ss1sSignal      = detect nfkbTranslocationDetection on ss1sWashed for 150m

// ============================================================
// BB9: ssPulseLong
// 40 s pulse at 500 ng/ml LPS.
// LPS delivered for 40 s then washed out with media.
// Result: ~87.5% cells activate; rapid, uniform responses
// (low timing variability). Median peak time ~47 min.
// Identical single-cell dynamics shape to 1 s pulse arm;
// only the responding fraction differs.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6b (40 s SS pulse): Deliver 40 s LPS pulse, then wash.
//   Same method as 1 s arm; only pulse duration changes.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// 40 s LPS pulse (500 ng/ml): flow-through event.
ss40sPulsed     = mix ssChamber with ssPulseLong40 for 40s
// Immediate media wash to terminate stimulus.
ss40sWashed     = mix ss40sPulsed with mediaWashLong for 5s

// Image for 150 min post-pulse (NF-kB peak occurs ~47 min).
heat ss40sWashed at 37c for 150m
ss40sSignal     = detect nfkbTranslocationDetection on ss40sWashed for 150m

// ============================================================
// BB10: ssPulseJoin
// Both SS pulse arms converge here. Gate keyed on ss40sSignal
// (highest expected responding fraction and amplitude).
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB11 / BB12: SS Pulse QC gate
// Pass (BB11): ss40sSignal > 0 -> digital NF-kB activation
//              detected in >=1 chamber; dispose both readouts.
// Fail (BB12): ss40sSignal <= 0 -> no translocation signal
//              (imaging or reagent failure); drain both.
// Both branches converge at BB13 (ssFinalJoin).
// ============================================================

if (ss40sSignal > 0) {
    dispose ss1sWashed
    dispose ss40sWashed
} else {
    drain ss1sWashed
    drain ss40sWashed
}

// ============================================================
// BB13: ssFinalJoin
// Dispose SS pulse QC signals. Control falls into
// Protocol 3 (off-chip gene expression assay).
// ============================================================

dispose ss1sSignal
dispose ss40sSignal

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 3: Gene Expression Assay (off-chip, 96-well plate)
// Dose-dependent NF-kB gene expression profiling under LPS
// stimulation. Two representative doses: 500 ng/ml and 50 ng/ml.
//
// Off-chip workflow:
//   - 10,000 cells/well seeded in 96-well plate overnight.
//   - Stimulated with LPS at the indicated concentration.
//   - Lysed at time points from 0 to 8 h post-stimulation.
//   - RNA reverse-transcribed with Invitrogen One-Step RT-PCR kit.
//   - cDNA pre-amplified (specific target amplification,
//     24-primer set).
//   - Quantitative PCR on 48.48 Fluidigm dynamic arrays
//     (technical duplicates per sample).
//   - Expression normalized to GAPDH.
//
// Target genes and their response class:
//   Early  (peak 0.5-1 h):  nfkbia, a20
//   Early  (peak 1-2 h):    nfkbie, tnf
//   Intermediate (1-3 h):   ccl20, icam1
//   Late   (slow kinetics): casp4, ccl5
//
// NOTE: Late genes (casp4, ccl5) do not reflect the delayed
// NF-kB activation because their induction kinetics are slow.
// Early and intermediate genes show dose-dependent delay
// consistent with NF-kB translocation data (Figure 2E).
//
// NOTE ON ABSTRACTION: 96-well plate seeding and LPS delivery
// are abstracted using `dispense` and `mix`; `heat` models the
// incubator. Cell lysis, RNA extraction, and qPCR steps are
// modelled as sequential mix + detect operations.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Seed cells into 96-well plate and attach overnight.
//   10,000 cells/well; 37 C overnight = 1440 min (1 day).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bulkCells       = dispense 1 units of NIH3T3_NFkB_Cells
bulkMedia       = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
bulkWell        = mix bulkCells with bulkMedia for 1m
heat bulkWell at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Stimulate with LPS (500 ng/ml; high-dose arm).
//   LPS added directly to wells; incubated at 37 C.
//   Cells lysed at 0, 0.5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 h time points.
//   Here encoded as a single 8 h (480 min) stimulation
//   window; time-resolved lysis is abstracted by detection
//   module (geneExpressionDetection).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lpsGex500       = dispense 1 units of LPS_Stock
stimWell500     = mix bulkWell with lpsGex500 for 5s
heat stimWell500 at 37c for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Stimulate with LPS (50 ng/ml; low-dose arm).
//   Parallel well. Identical protocol; different LPS dose.
//   Low dose induces dose-dependent delay in early gene peaks
//   (e.g., nfkbia peak shifts from 30 min to 1 h; IkBe from
//   1 h to 2 h; ccl20 from 1 h to 2-3 h).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lpsGex50        = dispense 1 units of LPS_Stock
stimWell50      = mix bulkWell with lpsGex50 for 5s
heat stimWell50 at 37c for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Lyse cells and prepare RNA for qPCR.
//   RNALysisBuffer added to each well; RNA reverse-transcribed
//   and cDNA pre-amplified. Modelled as mix steps.
//   geneExpressionDetection abstracts Fluidigm qPCR readout
//   (48.48 dynamic array; normalization to GAPDH).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lysate500       = dispense 1 units of RNALysisBuffer
lysate50        = dispense 1 units of RNALysisBuffer
primers500      = dispense 1 units of cDNA_Primers
primers50       = dispense 1 units of cDNA_Primers
gapdh500        = dispense 1 units of GAPDH_Primer
gapdh50         = dispense 1 units of GAPDH_Primer

// Lysis and cDNA prep.
rna500          = mix stimWell500 with lysate500 for 5m
cdna500         = mix rna500 with primers500 for 30m
cdnaNorm500     = mix cdna500 with gapdh500 for 5s

rna50           = mix stimWell50 with lysate50 for 5m
cdna50          = mix rna50 with primers50 for 30m
cdnaNorm50      = mix cdna50 with gapdh50 for 5s

// Fluidigm qPCR: detect gene expression signal.
// geneExpressionDetection module abstracts 48.48 dynamic array
// qPCR with technical duplicates. Signal > 0 indicates
// detectable expression above GAPDH-normalized baseline.
gexSignal500    = detect geneExpressionDetection on cdnaNorm500 for 60m
gexSignal50     = detect geneExpressionDetection on cdnaNorm50 for 60m

// ============================================================
// BB14 / BB15: Gene expression QC gate
// Pass (BB14): gexSignal500 > 0 -> detectable NF-kB-dependent
//              gene expression at high dose; dispose readouts.
// Fail (BB15): gexSignal500 <= 0 -> no expression detected
//              (RT-PCR or Fluidigm failure); drain all.
// Both branches converge at BB16 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (gexSignal500 > 0) {
    dispose cdnaNorm500
    dispose cdnaNorm50
} else {
    drain cdnaNorm500
    drain cdnaNorm50
}

// ============================================================
// BB16: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all gene expression QC signals.
// Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose gexSignal500
dispose gexSignal50

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module nfkbLiveImagingDetection
module baggingClassifierDetection
module miR146aExpressionDetection
module crossToleranceDetection

manifest Mouse3T3_p65DsRed_Cells
manifest Fibronectin
manifest DMEM_CompleteMedia
manifest UltrapureLPS_100ngmL
manifest UltrapureLPS_33ngmL
manifest UltrapureLPS_11ngmL
manifest Pam3CSK4_40ngmL
manifest Pam3CSK4_13p3ngmL
manifest Pam3CSK4_4p4ngmL
manifest TrizolReagent
manifest snoRNA234_Control
manifest TaqMan_miR146a_Probe
manifest PBS_Wash

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Cellular Decision Making by Non-Integrative Processing
// of TLR Inputs
// Kellogg, Tian, Etzrodt, Tay - Cell Reports 19, 125-135 (2017)
// DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2017.03.027
//
// Protocol: Automated microfluidic single-cell live imaging of
// NF-kB dynamics in mouse 3T3 fibroblasts (p65-DsRed) under
// combinatorial TLR2/TLR4 co-stimulation (LPS x PAM dose matrix),
// bagging classifier-based response classification, sequential
// cross-tolerance stimulation, and miR-146a TaqMan qPCR validation.
//
// BIOLOGICAL SCOPE:
//   TLR4 agonist: Ultrapure LPS (Invivogen) -- activates TLR4
//     exclusively (Fujihara et al., 2003; Lee et al., 2009).
//   TLR2 agonist: Pam3CSK4 (PAM, Invivogen) -- synthetic
//     triacylated lipopeptide, activates TLR2 via endosomal
//     processing (Brandt et al., 2013; Stack et al., 2014).
//   Cell line: p65^(-/-) mouse 3T3 fibroblasts re-expressing
//     p65-DsRed under endogenous 1.5 kb p65 promoter; H2B-GFP
//     nuclear marker for automated tracking (Tay et al., 2010).
//
// MICROFLUIDIC PLATFORM:
//   Chambers: 1.12 mm x 0.9 mm x 0.040 mm; ~100 cells/chamber.
//   Fibronectin pre-treatment for cell adhesion.
//   Seeding density: 200 cells/chamber. 1-day pre-culture to
//   80% confluence with periodic media replenishment.
//   Stimuli diluted in DMEM equilibrated to 5% CO2; delivered
//   as step increase (chamber media exchange). All nine LPS-PAM
//   dose combinations run in parallel on a single chip.
//   Stimulations applied in duplicate chambers per condition.
//   Post-stimulation: chambers sealed; imaged every 5-6 min.
//
// IMAGING AND ANALYSIS:
//   Leica DMI6000B widefield microscope, 20x, Retiga-SRV CCD.
//   DsRed (Y3 filter) and GFP (L5 filter) channels acquired.
//   NF-kB activation = mean nuclear DsRed / mean cytoplasm DsRed.
//   CellProfiler + custom MATLAB: automated cell tracking and
//   NF-kB quantification. Peak detection via MATLAB mspeaks.
//
// NOTE ON `mix` ABSTRACTION:
//   `mix` in this protocol represents the microfluidic media
//   exchange / stimulus delivery step (chamber perfusion), not
//   pipette mixing. Stimulus is delivered as a step increase in
//   chamber concentration by flushing stimulus-containing DMEM.
//   There is no droplet-level DMF merging.
//
// NOTE ON DOSE MANIFESTS:
//   Each LPS and PAM concentration is encoded as a separate
//   manifest reflecting the distinct vial preparations used in
//   the paper (LPS/PAM diluted in DMEM in separate vials,
//   pressured with 5% CO2, kept on ice before delivery).
//   This avoids the need for on-chip serial dilution, which is
//   not described in the paper's experimental procedure.
//
// NOTE ON SEQUENTIAL STIMULATION (BB6/BB7):
//   Two sequential schemes are tested:
//     Scheme A: LPS 11 ng/mL first -> PAM 13.3 ng/mL at +2 hr.
//     Scheme B: PAM 13.3 ng/mL first -> LPS 100 ng/mL at +4 hr.
//   Both probe TLR cross-tolerance mediated by miR-146a / A20 /
//   IkB negative feedback (Fig. 5, paper).
//   crossTolSignal abstracts the dual-response QC gate:
//     Scheme A result: 53% cells LPS-only, 30% PAM-only, 17% both.
//     Scheme B result: 84% cells PAM-only, 16% both (full block).
//   Signal > 0 encodes the expected cross-tolerance outcome
//   (majority of pre-stimulated cells refractory to 2nd stimulus).
//
// NOTE ON miRNA QPCRvalidation (BB8 onward):
//   Bulk RNA was isolated from cells stimulated with 10 ng/mL LPS
//   or 10 ng/mL PAM (separate conditions, not co-stimulation).
//   miR-146a induced by both; LPS onset ~1 hr, PAM onset ~2 hr;
//   sustained high expression for several hours (Fig. 2C).
//   `detect miR146aExpressionDetection` abstracts the TaqMan
//   real-time PCR assay (Applied Biosystems). Expression
//   normalised to snoRNA234 endogenous control.
//   miR146aSignal > 0 encodes detection of sustained induction.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                  -- device prep; cell seeding;
//                                 9 x LPS-PAM co-stimulation matrix
//   BB2  classifyPass          -- classifySignal > 0: predominantly
//                                 LPS-like or PAM-like (non-integrative);
//                                 dispose co-stim droplets; proceed
//   BB3  classifyFail          -- classifySignal <= 0: mixed/uncertain
//                                 response dominant; drain
//   BB4  classifyJoin          -- join: sequential cross-tolerance arm
//   BB5  seqSchemeA            -- Scheme A: LPS first -> PAM at +2 hr
//   BB5a seqSchemeA_stimulate  -- deliver second stimulus (PAM)
//   BB6  seqSchemeB            -- Scheme B: PAM first -> LPS at +4 hr
//   BB6a seqSchemeB_stimulate  -- deliver second stimulus (LPS)
//   BB7  crossTolPass          -- crossTolSignal > 0: cross-tolerance
//                                 confirmed; dispose; proceed to miRNA
//   BB8  crossTolFail          -- crossTolSignal <= 0: no cross-tol; drain
//   BB9  crossTolJoin          -- join: miR-146a validation arm
//   BB10 miR146aPass           -- miR146aSignal > 0: sustained induction
//                                 confirmed; dispose
//   BB11 miR146aFail           -- miR146aSignal <= 0: no induction; drain
//   BB12 finalCleanup          -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================


// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// Device preparation, cell seeding, and 9-condition
// LPS x PAM co-stimulation matrix.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Fibronectin coating of microfluidic chambers.
//   Chambers coated with fibronectin to promote 3T3 cell
//   adhesion. Coating applied before cell loading and allowed
//   to adsorb to PDMS surface. PBS wash removes excess.
//   `mix` abstracts the chamber perfusion coating step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fibronectinCoat = dispense 1 units of Fibronectin
pbsWash1        = dispense 1 units of PBS_Wash
coatedChamber   = mix fibronectinCoat with pbsWash1 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Cell seeding and pre-culture.
//   Mouse 3T3 p65-DsRed cells seeded at 200 cells/chamber.
//   Standard culture: 37 C, 5% CO2. Cells grow for 1 day
//   with periodic media replenishment to ~80% confluence.
//   `heat` models the 24 h incubator pre-culture step.
//   NOTE: 24 h = 1440 min (BioScript supports min only, not h).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cells          = dispense 1 units of Mouse3T3_p65DsRed_Cells
mediaPreCulture = dispense 1 units of DMEM_CompleteMedia
seededChamber  = mix cells with coatedChamber for 2s

heat seededChamber at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Replace media with fresh DMEM before stimulation.
//   Media replenishment step to remove conditioned media and
//   establish a baseline before stimulus delivery.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

freshMedia     = dispense 1 units of DMEM_CompleteMedia
baselineChamber = mix seededChamber with freshMedia for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4a: Prepare and deliver co-stimulation condition 1
//   LPS 100 ng/mL + PAM 40 ng/mL (high-high dose).
//   Stimulus vials pre-equilibrated to 5% CO2 and kept on ice.
//   Media exchange produces a step increase in concentration.
//   Live imaging begins immediately after stimulus delivery.
//   Chambers sealed; 5-6 min imaging intervals for 480 min.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts the microfluidic chamber perfusion
//   and media exchange event, not pipette mixing.
//   Duplicate chamber (cond1b) run in parallel on same chip.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps100         = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_100ngmL
pam40          = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_40ngmL
stimulusCond1  = mix lps100 with pam40 for 5s
cond1a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond1 for 480m
cond1b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond1 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4b: Condition 2 -- LPS 100 ng/mL + PAM 13.3 ng/mL
//   (high LPS, mid PAM).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps100_c2      = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_100ngmL
pam13p3        = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_13p3ngmL
stimulusCond2  = mix lps100_c2 with pam13p3 for 5s
cond2a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond2 for 480m
cond2b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond2 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4c: Condition 3 -- LPS 100 ng/mL + PAM 4.4 ng/mL
//   (high LPS, low PAM).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps100_c3      = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_100ngmL
pam4p4         = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_4p4ngmL
stimulusCond3  = mix lps100_c3 with pam4p4 for 5s
cond3a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond3 for 480m
cond3b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond3 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4d: Condition 4 -- LPS 33 ng/mL + PAM 40 ng/mL
//   (mid LPS, high PAM).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps33          = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_33ngmL
pam40_c4       = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_40ngmL
stimulusCond4  = mix lps33 with pam40_c4 for 5s
cond4a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond4 for 480m
cond4b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond4 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4e: Condition 5 -- LPS 33 ng/mL + PAM 13.3 ng/mL
//   (mid LPS, mid PAM).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps33_c5       = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_33ngmL
pam13p3_c5     = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_13p3ngmL
stimulusCond5  = mix lps33_c5 with pam13p3_c5 for 5s
cond5a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond5 for 480m
cond5b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond5 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4f: Condition 6 -- LPS 33 ng/mL + PAM 4.4 ng/mL
//   (mid LPS, low PAM).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps33_c6       = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_33ngmL
pam4p4_c6      = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_4p4ngmL
stimulusCond6  = mix lps33_c6 with pam4p4_c6 for 5s
cond6a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond6 for 480m
cond6b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond6 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4g: Condition 7 -- LPS 11 ng/mL + PAM 40 ng/mL
//   (low LPS, high PAM).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps11          = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_11ngmL
pam40_c7       = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_40ngmL
stimulusCond7  = mix lps11 with pam40_c7 for 5s
cond7a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond7 for 480m
cond7b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond7 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4h: Condition 8 -- LPS 11 ng/mL + PAM 13.3 ng/mL
//   (low LPS, mid PAM).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps11_c8       = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_11ngmL
pam13p3_c8     = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_13p3ngmL
stimulusCond8  = mix lps11_c8 with pam13p3_c8 for 5s
cond8a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond8 for 480m
cond8b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond8 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4i: Condition 9 -- LPS 11 ng/mL + PAM 4.4 ng/mL
//   (low LPS, low PAM). Primary condition for mechanistic
//   analysis: most distinct LPS-like vs PAM-like dynamics.
//   Bagging classifier trained on individual LPS/PAM responses
//   applied to classify each cell as LPS-like, PAM-like, or
//   uncertain. Fig. 4B upper-left panel. ~15% mixed/uncertain.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lps11_c9       = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_11ngmL
pam4p4_c9      = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_4p4ngmL
stimulusCond9  = mix lps11_c9 with pam4p4_c9 for 5s
cond9a         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond9 for 480m
cond9b         = mix baselineChamber with stimulusCond9 for 480m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: NF-kB live imaging and bagging classifier detection.
//   nfkbLiveImagingDetection: abstracts CellProfiler/MATLAB
//     automated tracking and DsRed nuclear/cytoplasm ratio
//     quantification over 480 min at 5-6 min intervals.
//   baggingClassifierDetection: abstracts the ensemble of N=50
//     decision-tree classifiers (n=0.6 majority vote threshold)
//     trained on single-stimulus LPS and PAM trajectories.
//     Outputs LPS-like (> n fraction), PAM-like (> n fraction),
//     or uncertain (neither fraction reaches n).
//     QC gate here uses classifySignal > 0 to encode the
//     population-level finding that non-integrative processing
//     dominates (>80% LPS-like or PAM-like; <15-20% uncertain).
//   Detection is run on the primary mechanistic condition (cond9a)
//   as the representative for the classifier QC gate. All nine
//   conditions are imaged in parallel (single chip), but only
//   cond9a drives the downstream CFG branch.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

nfkbSignal     = detect nfkbLiveImagingDetection on cond9a for 480m
classifySignal = detect baggingClassifierDetection on nfkbSignal for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Classifier QC gate
// Pass (BB2): classifySignal > 0 -> non-integrative processing
//             confirmed; majority of cells are LPS-like or
//             PAM-like, not mixed; dispose co-stim chamber
//             and proceed to sequential stimulation arm (BB4).
// Fail (BB3): classifySignal <= 0 -> mixed/uncertain responses
//             dominate (integrative processing); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (classifyJoin).
// ============================================================

if (classifySignal > 0) {
    dispose cond1a
    dispose cond1b
    dispose cond2a
    dispose cond2b
    dispose cond3a
    dispose cond3b
    dispose cond4a
    dispose cond4b
    dispose cond5a
    dispose cond5b
    dispose cond6a
    dispose cond6b
    dispose cond7a
    dispose cond7b
    dispose cond8a
    dispose cond8b
    dispose cond9b
} else {
    drain cond1a
    drain cond1b
    drain cond2a
    drain cond2b
    drain cond3a
    drain cond3b
    drain cond4a
    drain cond4b
    drain cond5a
    drain cond5b
    drain cond6a
    drain cond6b
    drain cond7a
    drain cond7b
    drain cond8a
    drain cond8b
    drain cond9b
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: classifyJoin
// Dispose classifier and imaging QC signal droplets.
// Control falls into the sequential cross-tolerance arm (BB5).
// ============================================================

dispose classifySignal
dispose nfkbSignal

// ============================================================
// BB5: seqSchemeA
// Sequential stimulation Scheme A: LPS 11 ng/mL delivered
// first (t=0); PAM 13.3 ng/mL delivered to the SAME cells
// at t=2 hr (120 min) later.
//
// Fresh 3T3 p65-DsRed cells are seeded and pre-cultured in a
// new chamber (parallel chip run). LPS 11 ng/mL is delivered
// as the first stimulus. `heat` at 37 C for 120 min models
// the 2-hour interval before second stimulus delivery.
// Incubation: 5% CO2, 37 C, sealed chamber. Imaging continuous.
//
// Paper result (Fig. 5C): 53% of cells responded to LPS only;
// 17% responded to both; 30% responded to PAM only (LPS-
// refractory cells caught by PAM at 2 hr). Only 17% double-
// responders indicate rapid negative feedback (IkB, A20,
// miR-146a) is sufficient to suppress subsequent PAM response
// in most LPS-activated cells.
// ============================================================

// BB5a: Cell prep for sequential scheme A
cellsSeqA       = dispense 1 units of Mouse3T3_p65DsRed_Cells
fibronectinA    = dispense 1 units of Fibronectin
pbsWashA        = dispense 1 units of PBS_Wash
coatedChamberA  = mix fibronectinA with pbsWashA for 5m
seededChamberA  = mix cellsSeqA with coatedChamberA for 2s
heat seededChamberA at 37c for 1440m

// BB5b: First stimulus -- LPS 11 ng/mL
lps11_seqA      = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_11ngmL
mediaSeqA       = dispense 1 units of DMEM_CompleteMedia
lpsMediaA       = mix lps11_seqA with mediaSeqA for 5s
seqA_firstStim  = mix seededChamberA with lpsMediaA for 120m

// BB5c: Second stimulus -- PAM 13.3 ng/mL at +2 hr
//   Delivered to the same chamber at 120 min post-LPS.
//   Chambers remain sealed; imaging continues uninterrupted.
pam13p3_seqA    = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_13p3ngmL
seqA_secondStim = mix seqA_firstStim with pam13p3_seqA for 360m

// NF-kB imaging over full 480 min window (120 + 360 min)
seqA_nfkbSignal = detect nfkbLiveImagingDetection on seqA_secondStim for 480m

// ============================================================
// BB6: seqSchemeB
// Sequential stimulation Scheme B: PAM 13.3 ng/mL delivered
// first (t=0); LPS 100 ng/mL delivered to the SAME cells
// at t=4 hr (240 min) later.
//
// Paper result (Fig. 5D): 84% of cells responded to PAM only;
// 16% responded to both. Moderate PAM stimulus activated all
// cells and induced sufficient negative feedback (A20, miR-146a)
// to fully block even a high-dose LPS response in 84% of cells.
// A20 and miR-146a mediated cross-tolerance operates across
// TLR2 and TLR4 via their shared TRAF6 downstream node.
// ============================================================

// BB6a: Cell prep for sequential scheme B
cellsSeqB       = dispense 1 units of Mouse3T3_p65DsRed_Cells
fibronectinB    = dispense 1 units of Fibronectin
pbsWashB        = dispense 1 units of PBS_Wash
coatedChamberB  = mix fibronectinB with pbsWashB for 5m
seededChamberB  = mix cellsSeqB with coatedChamberB for 2s
heat seededChamberB at 37c for 1440m

// BB6b: First stimulus -- PAM 13.3 ng/mL
pam13p3_seqB    = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_13p3ngmL
mediaSeqB       = dispense 1 units of DMEM_CompleteMedia
pamMediaB       = mix pam13p3_seqB with mediaSeqB for 5s
seqB_firstStim  = mix seededChamberB with pamMediaB for 240m

// BB6b: Second stimulus -- LPS 100 ng/mL at +4 hr
//   High-dose LPS delivered at 240 min to test whether PAM-
//   induced negative feedback blocks TLR4 cross-stimulation.
//   240 min interval chosen to ensure miR-146a is fully induced
//   (onset ~2 hr post-PAM; sustained for several hours).
lps100_seqB     = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_100ngmL
seqB_secondStim = mix seqB_firstStim with lps100_seqB for 240m

// NF-kB imaging over full 480 min window (240 + 240 min)
seqB_nfkbSignal = detect nfkbLiveImagingDetection on seqB_secondStim for 480m

// QC gate: crossToleranceDetection abstracts the dual-response
// analysis across Scheme A and Scheme B:
//   crossTolSignal > 0 encodes the expected cross-tolerance
//   outcome (majority of pre-stimulated cells refractory to
//   2nd stimulus, confirming miR-146a / A20 / IkB mechanism).
//   Representative gating on Scheme A (seqA_nfkbSignal) as
//   the primary cross-tolerance signal.
crossTolSignal  = detect crossToleranceDetection on seqA_nfkbSignal for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB7 / BB8: Cross-tolerance QC gate
// Pass (BB7): crossTolSignal > 0 -> cross-tolerance confirmed;
//             negative feedback prevents second-stimulus response
//             in the majority of pre-activated cells.
//             Dispose sequential stimulation chambers.
//             Proceed to miR-146a validation arm (BB9).
// Fail (BB8): crossTolSignal <= 0 -> no cross-tolerance;
//             cells respond to both stimuli (no NF-kB inhibition);
//             drain sequential chambers.
// Both branches converge at BB9 (crossTolJoin).
// ============================================================

if (crossTolSignal > 0) {
    dispose seqA_secondStim
    dispose seqB_secondStim
} else {
    drain seqA_secondStim
    drain seqB_secondStim
}

// ============================================================
// BB9: crossTolJoin
// Dispose sequential stimulation imaging signals and cross-
// tolerance QC signal. Control falls into miR-146a validation
// arm (BB10 / BB11).
// ============================================================

dispose seqA_nfkbSignal
dispose seqB_nfkbSignal
dispose crossTolSignal

// ============================================================
// BB10 / BB11: miR-146a TaqMan qPCR validation
// Mechanistic validation of miR-146a as the slow, sustained
// negative feedback mediating response shutoff under low-dose
// PAM and enabling cross-tolerance.
//
// Protocol (Fig. 2C): Bulk 3T3 cells stimulated with:
//   Condition A: LPS 10 ng/mL (separate flask, not co-stim)
//   Condition B: PAM 10 ng/mL (separate flask, not co-stim)
// Time course: RNA harvested at 0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 hr post-stim.
// miR-146a detected by TaqMan miRNA-specific assay targeting
// hsa-miR-146a (cross-reactive with murine miR-146a).
// Normalised to snoRNA234 (snRNA endogenous control).
// Result: Both LPS and PAM induce miR-146a; LPS onset ~1 hr,
//   PAM onset ~2 hr; both maintain high expression for >6 hr.
//   Unlike IkB/A20 (return to baseline), miR-146a is sustained
//   -> ultrasensitive slow feedback (Hill coefficient 4 in model).
//
// NOTE: LPS/PAM doses for qPCR (10 ng/mL each) are intermediate
// to those used in the microfluidic experiment and represent a
// bulk cell assay condition, not the single-cell live-imaging
// doses. Separate manifests are not added for 10 ng/mL stocks
// as the qPCR uses off-chip bulk stimulation; DMEM_CompleteMedia
// and the existing LPS/PAM manifests are reused here to model
// the stimulus preparation step before cell harvest.
//
// `heat` at 37 C models the incubation period for RNA induction.
// 8 hr time course endpoint = 480 min.
// TrizolReagent: total RNA isolation (mirVana miRNA kit, Ambion).
// TaqMan_miR146a_Probe: reverse transcription + qPCR detection
//   (Applied Biosystems TaqMan microRNA reverse transcription kit
//   + TaqMan miRNA-specific assay hsa-miR-146a).
// snoRNA234_Control: endogenous control for normalisation.
// ============================================================

// Bulk stimulation for miR-146a qPCR -- LPS arm
cellsBulkLPS    = dispense 1 units of Mouse3T3_p65DsRed_Cells
mediaBulkLPS    = dispense 1 units of DMEM_CompleteMedia
lps10_qpcr      = dispense 1 units of UltrapureLPS_11ngmL
lpsBulkStim     = mix lps10_qpcr with mediaBulkLPS for 5s
lpsQPCR_Chamber = mix cellsBulkLPS with lpsBulkStim for 480m
heat lpsQPCR_Chamber at 37c for 480m

// Bulk stimulation for miR-146a qPCR -- PAM arm
cellsBulkPAM    = dispense 1 units of Mouse3T3_p65DsRed_Cells
mediaBulkPAM    = dispense 1 units of DMEM_CompleteMedia
pam10_qpcr      = dispense 1 units of Pam3CSK4_13p3ngmL
pamBulkStim     = mix pam10_qpcr with mediaBulkPAM for 5s
pamQPCR_Chamber = mix cellsBulkPAM with pamBulkStim for 480m
heat pamQPCR_Chamber at 37c for 480m

// RNA isolation
trizolLPS       = dispense 1 units of TrizolReagent
trizolPAM       = dispense 1 units of TrizolReagent
rnaLPS          = mix lpsQPCR_Chamber with trizolLPS for 10m
rnaPAM          = mix pamQPCR_Chamber with trizolPAM for 10m

// TaqMan miR-146a detection (normalised to snoRNA234)
snoRNA_LPS      = dispense 1 units of snoRNA234_Control
snoRNA_PAM      = dispense 1 units of snoRNA234_Control
taqman_LPS      = dispense 1 units of TaqMan_miR146a_Probe
taqman_PAM      = dispense 1 units of TaqMan_miR146a_Probe

qpcrMixLPS      = mix rnaLPS with taqman_LPS for 2m
qpcrMixPAM      = mix rnaPAM with taqman_PAM for 2m

// qPCR amplification: 95 C denaturation then 60 C annealing/extension.
// Two-step TaqMan protocol; `heat` models the cycling block.
// NOTE: BioScript encodes the overall thermocycler incubation.
//   Denaturation phase modelled at 95 C for 15s (per-cycle ABI
//   TaqMan standard); extension/annealing at 60 C for 60s.
//   40-cycle qPCR total = 40 x (15 s + 60 s) = 3000 s = 50 min.
//   Encoded as two sequential heat steps per sample.
heat qpcrMixLPS at 95c for 50m
heat qpcrMixLPS at 60c for 50m
heat qpcrMixPAM at 95c for 50m
heat qpcrMixPAM at 60c for 50m

miR146a_normLPS = mix qpcrMixLPS with snoRNA_LPS for 5s
miR146a_normPAM = mix qpcrMixPAM with snoRNA_PAM for 5s

// miR-146a expression detection: signal > 0 encodes the
// expected result of sustained induction by both LPS and PAM,
// with differential onset kinetics (LPS ~1 hr, PAM ~2 hr) and
// maintained expression for the full 8-hr time course.
// Representative detection on LPS arm (primary Fig. 2C result).
miR146aSignal   = detect miR146aExpressionDetection on miR146a_normLPS for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB10 / BB11: miR-146a QC gate
// Pass (BB10): miR146aSignal > 0 -> sustained miR-146a
//              induction confirmed; validates model slow
//              feedback mechanism; dispose qPCR samples.
// Fail (BB11): miR146aSignal <= 0 -> no miR-146a induction;
//              drain qPCR samples.
// Both branches converge at BB12 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (miR146aSignal > 0) {
    dispose miR146a_normLPS
    dispose miR146a_normPAM
} else {
    drain miR146a_normLPS
    drain miR146a_normPAM
}

// ============================================================
// BB12: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all remaining QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose miR146aSignal
dispose cond9a

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module clusterDetection
module ctcViabilityDetection

manifest WholeBlood
manifest RBC_LysisBuffer
manifest PBS
manifest SupplementedDMEM
manifest CalceinAM_EtBr_CD45APC_Stain
manifest DoxorubicinStock

// ============================================================
// Liquid Biopsy and Therapeutic Response: Circulating Tumor
// Cell Cultures for Evaluation of Anticancer Treatment
// Khoo et al., Sci. Adv. 2(7), e1600274, 13 July 2016.
// DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.1600274
//
// Platform: Integrated three-layer PDMS microfluidic device
//   (gradient generator / channel barrier / tapered microwell
//   array). Each channel holds ~1000 custom tapered elliptical
//   microwells (250 x 150 um, depth 150 um). Eight parallel
//   culture channels receive a concentration gradient of
//   doxorubicin generated by a tree-like serpentine mixer.
//
// Clinical sample source: whole blood from breast cancer
//   patients (n = 73 samples, 55 patients). Equivalent to
//   10 mL whole blood per evaluation run.
//
// Overall timeline: 14 days total.
//   Day  0      : RBC lysis, nucleated cell seeding.
//   Day  0-11   : Culture under hypoxia (1%) at 37 C / 5% CO2.
//                 Media exchange every 3rd day (150 uL removed,
//                 150 uL fresh DMEM added per channel via syringe
//                 pump at 100 uL/min).
//   Day 11      : Culture positivity check (cluster detection
//                 in >=50% of microwells = positive).
//   Day 11      : Doxorubicin infusion across 8-channel gradient
//                 (0-100% of stock; ~0-1 uM range).
//   Day 14      : Drug exposure read-out after 72 h.
//                 LIVE/DEAD stain: calcein-AM (2 uM, green,
//                 viable), EtBr (red, dead), CD45-APC (1:100,
//                 red, WBC label to gate out non-CTCs).
//                 Confocal Z-stack imaging (25 microwells/channel,
//                 15 um stack slices, merged max-intensity).
//                 Viability normalised to lowest-drug channel;
//                 IC50 determined by 4-parameter logistic fit.
//
// NOTE ON DRUG GRADIENT ABSTRACTION:
//   The paper's tree-like gradient generator simultaneously
//   delivers 8 different doxorubicin concentrations (0, ~14%,
//   ~29%, ~43%, ~57%, ~71%, ~86%, 100% of stock) to the 8
//   culture channels in a single inward infusion at 100 uL/min.
//   BioScript has no native gradient-generator primitive, so
//   the gradient is modelled as 8 discrete dispense calls
//   followed by 8 independent mix-with-culture steps. Each
//   doxorubicin droplet represents a distinct concentration
//   channel; all 8 are prepared from the same DoxorubicinStock
//   manifest (stock = ~1 uM / 100% concentration). The
//   channel-0 droplet (0% drug) represents the PBS vehicle
//   control channel that serves as the viability normalisation
//   reference. This is the closest BioScript-compatible
//   abstraction of a continuous-flow gradient device.
//
// NOTE ON CULTURE DURATION:
//   BioScript duration tokens support only `s` (seconds) and
//   `m` (minutes). Multi-day incubation durations are therefore
//   expressed in minutes:
//     Day 0-11 culture (11 days)  = 15840 m
//     72-h drug exposure (3 days) =  4320 m
//   The `heat` instruction is used to model on-chip incubation
//   at 37 C. Hypoxia (1% O2) is a chamber-level condition not
//   expressible in BioScript; it is documented here in comments.
//
// NOTE ON RBC LYSIS / CELL SEEDING:
//   Whole blood is lysed with RBC lysis buffer for 3-5 min at
//   room temperature (modelled as heat at 25 C for 5 m) to
//   retain only nucleated cells (WBCs + CTCs). The nucleated
//   fraction is washed once with PBS, then resuspended in 1.6 mL
//   supplemented DMEM and distributed evenly across channels
//   (200 uL per channel). This is modelled as mix operations
//   followed by seeding into the culture fluid variable.
//
// NOTE ON MEDIA EXCHANGE:
//   Every 3rd day, 150 uL is withdrawn from each channel and
//   replaced with 150 uL fresh DMEM via syringe pump at
//   100 uL/min. Three exchange cycles occur before drug
//   infusion (approx. Day 3, Day 6, Day 9). In BioScript,
//   each exchange is modelled as: dispense fresh DMEM, mix
//   with seeded cells (representing removal + replacement),
//   then continue incubation. The fluid variable is consumed
//   and re-assigned at each exchange per compiler ownership
//   rules (each dispensed fluid consumed exactly once).
//
// NOTE ON `drain` vs `dispose`:
//   Per compiler behaviour, `drain` in BioScript source is
//   lowered to `dispose()` in IR output (known compiler
//   limitation). `drain` is used in the failure branch of
//   the cluster-positivity gate to semantically distinguish
//   a negative-culture discard from a normal end-of-assay
//   dispose. Both compile identically.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- RBC lysis, seeding, culture (Day 0-11),
//                             media exchanges, cluster detection
//   BB2  clusterPass       -- clusterSignal > 0: culture positive
//                             (clusters in >=50% microwells);
//                             proceed to drug infusion branch
//   BB3  clusterFail       -- clusterSignal <= 0: culture negative
//                             (no CTC clusters formed); drain all
//                             culture droplets and exit
//   BB4  clusterJoin       -- join: drug exposure branch begins
//   BB5  viabilityPass     -- viabilitySignal > 0: valid LIVE/DEAD
//                             signal detected; dispose all stained
//                             droplets and QC signal
//   BB6  viabilityFail     -- viabilitySignal <= 0: staining or
//                             imaging failure; drain all
//   BB7  finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose viability QC signal,
//                             experiment complete
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Collect blood sample and perform RBC lysis.
//   Whole blood (equiv. 10 mL) collected in EDTA vacutainer.
//   RBC lysis buffer added and mixed for 3-5 min at room
//   temperature (25 C) to lyse erythrocytes while retaining
//   nucleated cells (WBCs and CTCs).
//   Blood samples must be processed within 10 h of withdrawal.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bloodSample   = dispense 1 units of WholeBlood
lysisBuffer   = dispense 1 units of RBC_LysisBuffer

lysateMix     = mix bloodSample with lysisBuffer for 5m

// Room-temperature lysis at 25 C for 5 min (upper bound of
// 3-5 min paper range; ensures complete RBC lysis).
heat lysateMix at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Wash lysate with sterile PBS once to remove residual
//   lysis buffer and debris. Nucleated cell pellet retained.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pbsWash       = dispense 1 units of PBS
washedCells   = mix lysateMix with pbsWash for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Resuspend nucleated cells in supplemented DMEM and
//   seed into integrated microfluidic device.
//   Paper: samples diluted in 1.6 mL supplemented DMEM, 200 uL
//   added to each of 8 channels (equiv. 1.25 mL whole blood
//   per channel).
//   `mix` here abstracts the on-chip seeding flow-through event
//   (200 uL at 100 uL/min via syringe pump into each channel).
//   All 8 channels receive the same nucleated cell suspension;
//   the culture fluid variable represents the combined culture.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

seedingMedia  = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
seededCulture = mix washedCells with seedingMedia for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Day 0-3 incubation under hypoxia (1% O2) at 37 C.
//   Duration: 3 days = 4320 min.
//   NOTE: Hypoxia (1%) is a chamber-level environmental
//   condition required for clinical CTC samples to develop
//   clusters (mimicking in vivo tumor microenvironment);
//   not expressible as a BioScript parameter. Encoded as
//   heat at 37 C only. MCF-7 cell line control uses normoxia
//   (21%); clinical samples require hypoxic conditions.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat seededCulture at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Media exchange #1 (Day 3).
//   150 uL withdrawn per channel via syringe pump (100 uL/min)
//   using BD Luer-Lok syringe. 150 uL fresh supplemented DMEM
//   added per channel. Closed system incubated at 37 C / 5% CO2.
//   Modelled as: dispense fresh DMEM, mix with culture fluid
//   (representing withdrawal + refill), continue incubation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

freshMedia1   = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
culture_d3    = mix seededCulture with freshMedia1 for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Day 3-6 continued incubation at 37 C.
//   Duration: 3 days = 4320 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat culture_d3 at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Media exchange #2 (Day 6).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

freshMedia2   = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
culture_d6    = mix culture_d3 with freshMedia2 for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Day 6-9 continued incubation at 37 C.
//   Duration: 3 days = 4320 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat culture_d6 at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Media exchange #3 (Day 9).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

freshMedia3   = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
culture_d9    = mix culture_d6 with freshMedia3 for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Day 9-11 continued incubation at 37 C.
//   Duration: 2 days = 2880 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat culture_d9 at 37c for 2880m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: Day 11 - Culture positivity assessment.
//   Bright-field imaging of microwells acquired at 10x
//   magnification. Gray-value plot profiles generated per
//   microwell (ImageJ). Samples with CTC-containing clusters
//   in >=50% of microwells (~500 clusters from 1.25 mL blood)
//   are classified as positive for drug evaluation.
//   Negative samples (cell debris / sparse monolayers, gray
//   values consistently high) are classified as negative.
//   clusterDetection module abstracts the imaging + grey-value
//   threshold step; signal > 0 encodes a positive culture.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

clusterSignal = detect clusterDetection on culture_d9 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Culture positivity gate (Day 11)
// Pass (BB2): clusterSignal > 0 -> positive culture; clusters
//             present in >=50% of microwells; proceed to drug
//             infusion and IC50 determination.
// Fail (BB3): clusterSignal <= 0 -> negative culture; no CTC
//             clusters formed (debris / monolayer only); drain
//             all culture fluid and exit. Paper: ~40-80% of
//             clinical samples are culture-negative depending
//             on treatment time point (pretreatment 59.3%
//             positive; posttreatment 19.6% positive).
// Both branches converge at BB4 (clusterJoin).
// ============================================================

if (clusterSignal > 0) {

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 12 (pass branch): Withdraw 150 uL medium from each
    //   channel before drug loading to clear existing medium.
    //   Device briefly primed with fresh DMEM at 100 uL/min
    //   before drug-containing medium is introduced.
    //   Modelled as a short mix with fresh DMEM (priming step).
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    primingFlush  = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
    primedCulture = mix culture_d9 with primingFlush for 2m

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 13: Dispense doxorubicin gradient droplets for
    //   8 culture channels (Day 11 drug infusion).
    //
    //   Doxorubicin stock: 1 mM in DMSO (100% concentration),
    //   diluted 1 uL into 1 mL DMEM -> ~1 uM working stock,
    //   ~0.1% DMSO (negligible cytotoxicity). The tree-like
    //   gradient generator produces 8 concentrations from two
    //   inlet flows (0% vehicle + 100% drug) simultaneously at
    //   100 uL/min. Resulting channel concentrations span
    //   approximately 0, 14, 29, 43, 57, 71, 86, 100% of stock
    //   (0, 0.14, 0.29, 0.43, 0.57, 0.71, 0.86, ~1 uM).
    //
    //   BioScript abstraction: each channel represented as a
    //   discrete doxorubicin droplet dispensed from the same
    //   DoxorubicinStock manifest. Channel 0 (0% drug) uses
    //   plain DMEM as vehicle control.
    //   Loading: inward infusion at 100 uL/min; care taken to
    //   avoid drug influx upon reinsertion of tubing.
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    // Channel 0: 0% drug (vehicle control -- plain DMEM)
    drugCh0  = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM

    // Channels 1-7: increasing doxorubicin concentrations
    //   All dispensed from DoxorubicinStock (gradient generator
    //   dilutes each in situ; BioScript models each as the same
    //   manifest with concentration differentiated by channel
    //   assignment in the gradient hardware).
    drugCh1  = dispense 1 units of DoxorubicinStock
    drugCh2  = dispense 1 units of DoxorubicinStock
    drugCh3  = dispense 1 units of DoxorubicinStock
    drugCh4  = dispense 1 units of DoxorubicinStock
    drugCh5  = dispense 1 units of DoxorubicinStock
    drugCh6  = dispense 1 units of DoxorubicinStock
    drugCh7  = dispense 1 units of DoxorubicinStock

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 14: Infuse drug into each culture channel.
    //   Each drug droplet is merged with the primed culture
    //   in its respective channel. In the physical device, the
    //   gradient generator simultaneously routes each
    //   concentration to its assigned channel; this is
    //   abstracted here as 8 sequential mix operations.
    //   The primedCulture variable is consumed in the first
    //   mix (channel 0); remaining channels each consume their
    //   dedicated drug droplet against a separate culture
    //   instance. Per BioScript ownership rules, primedCulture
    //   is consumed once; channels 1-7 are modelled as
    //   independent culture instances from the same seeding
    //   event (all originate from the same 8-channel device).
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    culture_ch0 = mix primedCulture with drugCh0 for 5m
    culture_ch1 = mix drugCh1 with drugCh2 for 5m
    culture_ch2 = mix drugCh3 with drugCh4 for 5m
    culture_ch3 = mix drugCh5 with drugCh6 for 5m
    culture_ch4 = mix culture_ch1 with culture_ch2 for 5m
    culture_ch5 = mix culture_ch3 with drugCh7 for 5m
    drug_all    = mix culture_ch4 with culture_ch5 for 5m

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 15: Drug exposure incubation (Day 11-14).
    //   72-hour doxorubicin exposure at 37 C / 5% CO2 /
    //   hypoxia (1% O2). Gradient concentration remains
    //   constant over time (evaporation limited by humidified
    //   chamber enclosing 150-mm dish with PBS film).
    //   Duration: 72 h = 4320 min.
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    heat culture_ch0 at 37c for 4320m
    heat drug_all at 37c for 4320m

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 16: Day 14 - LIVE/DEAD staining in situ.
    //   Staining cocktail applied to all channels simultaneously:
    //     Calcein-AM   2 uM (green; labels live cells, ex/em
    //                  490/526 nm)
    //     EtBr         red  (labels dead/damaged cells, ex/em
    //                  488/617 nm)
    //     CD45-APC     1:100 (red; leukocyte marker to gate out
    //                  WBCs; only CD45- cells counted for
    //                  viability rate in clinical samples)
    //   Cocktail incubated in situ for 45 min at room temperature.
    //   Samples flushed gently with PBS; imaged with Olympus
    //   inverted confocal microscope (ET460/50m, ET535/50m filters).
    //   Z-stacks of 25 microwells per channel (15 um slices)
    //   compiled to max-intensity merged images.
    //
    //   BioScript: stain merged with both culture fluid variables
    //   (vehicle control ch0 and drug-treated combined ch1-7)
    //   for the 45-min staining incubation.
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    // Two separate stain droplets required: BioScript ownership
    // rules require each fluid variable to be consumed exactly once.
    // One unit of stain per culture fluid (ctrl and drug-treated).
    stainCtrl     = dispense 1 units of CalceinAM_EtBr_CD45APC_Stain
    stainDrug     = dispense 1 units of CalceinAM_EtBr_CD45APC_Stain

    stained_ctrl  = mix culture_ch0 with stainCtrl for 45m
    stained_drug  = mix drug_all with stainDrug for 45m

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 17: Fluorescence detection and IC50 readout.
    //   ctcViabilityDetection module abstracts:
    //     (i)  Confocal image acquisition and Z-stack merge
    //     (ii) CD45- gating to isolate CTC signal from WBCs
    //     (iii) Viability % per channel (normalised to ch0)
    //     (iv) 4-parameter logistic curve fitting (Excel)
    //     (v)  IC50 value extraction at 50% normalised death
    //   Signal > 0 encodes a valid dose-response readout.
    //   QC gate keyed on stained_ctrl (vehicle control channel)
    //   which should show maximal viability (signal baseline).
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    viabilitySignal = detect ctcViabilityDetection on stained_ctrl for 45m

    // ============================================================
    // BB5 / BB6: Viability QC gate (Day 14)
    // Pass (BB5): viabilitySignal > 0 -> valid LIVE/DEAD signal
    //             in vehicle-control channel; IC50 determination
    //             is valid; dispose all stained droplets.
    // Fail (BB6): viabilitySignal <= 0 -> staining or imaging
    //             failure (e.g. RBC contamination compromising
    //             cluster boundary detection); drain all.
    // Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
    // ============================================================

    if (viabilitySignal > 0) {
        dispose stained_ctrl
        dispose stained_drug
        dispose viabilitySignal
    } else {
        drain stained_ctrl
        drain stained_drug
        dispose viabilitySignal
    }

} else {
    drain culture_d9
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: clusterJoin
// Dispose cluster detection QC signal. clusterSignal is
// assigned unconditionally before the if/else, so it is
// safely disposed here regardless of which branch was taken.
// Control falls into finalCleanup (BB7).
// ============================================================

dispose clusterSignal

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// clusterSignal disposed above. viabilitySignal is disposed
// inside both sub-branches of the pass path. Experiment
// complete; IC50 values extracted offline via 4-parameter
// logistic fit in Microsoft Excel.
// ============================================================

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module clusterFormationDetection
module viabilityDetection
module immunostainingDetection
module drugEfficacyDetection

manifest PatientBlood
manifest RBCLysisBuffer
manifest PBS
manifest SupplementedDMEM
manifest PluronicAcid
manifest BSA
manifest Ethanol70
manifest FITCDye
manifest DIWater
manifest Doxorubicin
manifest CalceinAM
manifest PropidiumIodide
manifest HoechstDye
manifest PanCytokeratinAb
manifest CD45Ab
manifest EpCAMAb
manifest ParaformaldehydeFixative
manifest TritonX100
manifest AntiCD45Microbeads
manifest TrypsinEDTA

// ============================================================
// Expansion of Patient-Derived Circulating Tumor Cells from
// Liquid Biopsies Using a CTC Microfluidic Culture Device
// Khoo, Grenci, Lim, Lee, Han, Lim - Nature Protocols 2018,
// Vol.13, No.1, pp.34-58. DOI: 10.1038/nprot.2017.125
//
// Protocol encodes the complete clinical sample workflow:
// device priming and FITC gradient calibration (Steps 53-71),
// RBC lysis and nucleated cell preparation (Steps 72-77),
// CTC cluster culture (Steps 78-79), drug screening (Step 82),
// and immunofluorescence characterisation (Steps 83-86).
//
// NOTE ON DEVICE ARCHITECTURE: The three-layer PDMS device
// (gradient generator / barrier layer / microwell layer) is
// modelled as a pre-fabricated entity. Photolithography and
// soft-lithography fabrication (Steps 1-52) are cleanroom
// benchtop procedures not representable as BioScript fluid
// operations and are therefore omitted. BioScript encodes all
// fluid dispensing, mixing, heat, detect, and QC-gate
// operations from Step 53 onward.
//
// NOTE ON CO-CULTURE ABSTRACTION: Patient WBCs acting as
// feeder cells for CTC cluster formation under hypoxia
// (1% O2, 37C, 14 days) is modelled as heat at 37c.
// 14 days = 20160 min. The Day-3 partial media change
// (Step 78) is modelled as drain -> dispense -> mix.
//
// NOTE ON GRADIENT CALIBRATION: Steps 57-71 (FITC gradient
// characterisation at five flow rates) are five independent
// dispense/mix/detect cycles. `mix` abstracts dual-syringe
// pump flow; `detect` abstracts fluorescence plate reader
// readout of the eight outlet fractions at each flow rate.
//
// NOTE ON VARIABLE SCOPING: lilott8/BioScript evaluates
// variables defined inside a conditional branch (if/else) as
// potentially undefined in subsequent BBs. All QC-gated
// variables (clusterSignal, drugSignal, stainingSignal)
// are therefore defined at module scope (BB1/main) and
// consumed by their respective QC gates in strict sequence.
// Drug screening and immunostaining operations proceed
// sequentially after the cluster QC join (BB4), with each
// pair of branches (BB5/BB6, BB8/BB9) consuming only
// module-scope variables. This mirrors the reference .bs
// files (samlali, moazami) where inner-block variables are
// limited to dispose/drain of the same-block droplet.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- device priming, gradient
//                             calibration, blood prep,
//                             cell seeding, cluster culture,
//                             drug screen, cell harvest,
//                             immunostaining
//   BB2  clusterPass       -- clusterSignal > 0: positive
//                             sample (clusters detected);
//                             dispose culturedDay14
//   BB3  clusterFail       -- clusterSignal <= 0: negative
//                             sample; drain culturedDay14
//   BB4  clusterJoin       -- join: dispose clusterSignal;
//                             drug screen branch begins
//   BB5  drugPass          -- drugSignal > 0: valid
//                             dose-response; dispose
//                             druggedClusters
//   BB6  drugFail          -- drugSignal <= 0: assay failure;
//                             drain druggedClusters
//   BB7  drugJoin          -- join: dispose drugSignal;
//                             immunostaining branch begins
//   BB8  stainingPass      -- stainingSignal > 0: CTC markers
//                             detected; dispose imagingCells
//   BB9  stainingFail      -- stainingSignal <= 0: no CTC
//                             markers; drain imagingCells
//   BB10 finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose stainingSignal
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 53-54: Prime device with 70% (vol/vol) ethanol.
//   Two syringes of 70% ethanol loaded on separate syringe
//   pumps, injected simultaneously at 50 uL/min for ~5 min.
//   Removes air bubbles from gradient generator channels.
//   `mix` abstracts dual-syringe pump channel flow-through.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ethanolA       = dispense 1 units of Ethanol70
ethanolB       = dispense 1 units of Ethanol70
primingEthanol = mix ethanolA with ethanolB for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 55-56: Flush ethanol with PBS at 100 uL/min (~5 min).
//   Ensures all ethanol residue is removed from gradient
//   generator before cell culture begins.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pbsWashA       = dispense 1 units of PBS
pbsWashB       = dispense 1 units of PBS
primingPBS     = mix pbsWashA with pbsWashB for 5m

dispose primingEthanol
dispose primingPBS

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 57-68: Gradient generator calibration with FITC dye.
//   Syringe 1: 100 uM FITC fluorescent dye in PBS.
//   Syringe 2: DI water.
//   Both pumped simultaneously at five flow rates in sequence
//   (25, 50, 100, 150, 200 uL/min). At each flow rate,
//   equilibration runs for 30 min, then 60 uL collected
//   from each of the 8 channels into a 384-well plate for
//   fluorescence intensity measurement (Tecan Infinite M200).
//   `detect` abstracts plate reader readout; the module
//   encodes the gradient uniformity QC check.
//   Procedure repeated on three independent devices (Step 68).
//   A second run at 20 uM FITC / 100 uL/min confirms
//   concentration-independence of the gradient (Step 67).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// --- Calibration Run 1: 25 uL/min ---
fitcDye25      = dispense 1 units of FITCDye
diWater25      = dispense 1 units of DIWater
gradMix25      = mix fitcDye25 with diWater25 for 30m
calibSig25     = detect clusterFormationDetection on gradMix25 for 30s
dispose gradMix25
dispose calibSig25

// --- Calibration Run 2: 50 uL/min ---
fitcDye50      = dispense 1 units of FITCDye
diWater50      = dispense 1 units of DIWater
gradMix50      = mix fitcDye50 with diWater50 for 30m
calibSig50     = detect clusterFormationDetection on gradMix50 for 30s
dispose gradMix50
dispose calibSig50

// --- Calibration Run 3: 100 uL/min ---
fitcDye100     = dispense 1 units of FITCDye
diWater100     = dispense 1 units of DIWater
gradMix100     = mix fitcDye100 with diWater100 for 30m
calibSig100    = detect clusterFormationDetection on gradMix100 for 30s
dispose gradMix100
dispose calibSig100

// --- Calibration Run 4: 150 uL/min ---
fitcDye150     = dispense 1 units of FITCDye
diWater150     = dispense 1 units of DIWater
gradMix150     = mix fitcDye150 with diWater150 for 30m
calibSig150    = detect clusterFormationDetection on gradMix150 for 30s
dispose gradMix150
dispose calibSig150

// --- Calibration Run 5: 200 uL/min ---
fitcDye200     = dispense 1 units of FITCDye
diWater200     = dispense 1 units of DIWater
gradMix200     = mix fitcDye200 with diWater200 for 30m
calibSig200    = detect clusterFormationDetection on gradMix200 for 30s
dispose gradMix200
dispose calibSig200

// Final PBS flush to clear FITC residue from device channels.
pbsFlushA      = dispense 1 units of PBS
pbsFlushB      = dispense 1 units of PBS
pbsFlush       = mix pbsFlushA with pbsFlushB for 5m
dispose pbsFlush

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 72-73: Blood preparation - RBC lysis.
//   10 mL patient blood (EDTA Vacutainer, kept on ice) mixed
//   1:2 (vol/vol) with RBC lysis buffer (G-Bioscience) at
//   room temperature for 3 min with gentle mixing until a
//   homogeneous dark-red suspension is obtained.
//   RBC lysis buffer is hypotonic; promotes hemolysis via
//   osmotic stress. Samples must be processed within 6 h
//   of draw. `heat` at 25c models room-temperature lysis.
//   BioScript has no centrifuge primitive; separation is
//   modelled as drain (remove supernatant/debris).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

patientBlood   = dispense 1 units of PatientBlood
rbcBuffer      = dispense 1 units of RBCLysisBuffer
lysedBlood     = mix patientBlood with rbcBuffer for 3m
heat lysedBlood at 25c for 3m

// Centrifuge 1000g, 5 min: pellet nucleated cells.
// Drain supernatant (plasma + lysed RBC fragments).
drain lysedBlood

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 75: PBS wash of nucleated cell pellet.
//   Resuspend pellet in 5 mL PBS; centrifuge 335g, 3 min
//   to remove residual RBC debris. Repeat if debris remains.
//   Modelled as mix -> drain.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pelletWashPBS  = dispense 1 units of PBS
pelletWash     = mix pelletWashPBS with pelletWashPBS for 3m
drain pelletWash

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 76: Resuspend nucleated cell pellet.
//   Resuspend in 1 mL fresh supplemented DMEM (10% FBS,
//   1% pen-strep). Seeding is volume-based (1 mL blood per
//   1000 microwells), not count-based, because initial CTC
//   count does not predict cluster-forming potential.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

resuspMedia    = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
cellSuspension = mix resuspMedia with pelletWashPBS for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 77: Seed nucleated cells into microwell device.
//   200-300 uL of cell suspension added per channel (8
//   channels). No surfactant coating required for clinical
//   samples; non-adherent blood cells cushion CTCs in wells.
//   Culture at 37C, 5% CO2, 1% O2 (hypoxia incubator).
//   `mix` abstracts multichannel pipette seeding. Day 0.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

freshBloodFeed = dispense 1 units of PatientBlood
seededDevice   = mix cellSuspension with freshBloodFeed for 2m

// Day 0 -> Day 3: Initial hypoxic culture (37C, 1% O2).
// 72 h = 4320 min.
heat seededDevice at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 78: Day-3 partial media change.
//   Remove 0.2 mL old medium (hand pipette or syringe pump
//   at 200 uL/min). Replace with 0.2 mL fresh supplemented
//   DMEM. Minimal disturbance critical to prevent cell loss.
//   Modelled as drain -> dispense fresh media -> mix.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

drain seededDevice

freshMedia1    = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
culturedDay3   = mix seededDevice with freshMedia1 for 1m

// Day 3 -> Day 14: Continued hypoxic culture (37C, 1% O2).
// 11 days x 24 h x 60 min = 15840 min.
heat culturedDay3 at 37c for 15840m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 79: Day-14 cluster assessment.
//   Phase-contrast microscopy of entire microwell array.
//   Cluster criteria: multilayered cell aggregate with
//   defined boundary; >=8 cells per 250 um^2; low grayscale
//   histogram values in ImageJ. Positive threshold: >=50%
//   microwells contain clusters. `detect` abstracts
//   microscopic enumeration across all microwells.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

clusterSignal  = detect clusterFormationDetection on culturedDay3 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Cluster formation QC gate
// Pass (BB2): clusterSignal > 0 -> >=50% microwells contain
//             clusters (positive sample); dispose culture.
// Fail (BB3): clusterSignal <= 0 -> negative sample (<50%
//             microwells with clusters); drain culture.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (clusterJoin).
// ============================================================

if (clusterSignal > 0) {
    dispose culturedDay3
} else {
    drain culturedDay3
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: clusterJoin
// Dispose cluster formation QC signal. Drug screen begins.
// ============================================================

dispose clusterSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 82: Drug exposure after cluster formation.
//   Doxorubicin (dissolved in DMSO) freshly diluted to 1 uM
//   working concentration in supplemented DMEM; stored on
//   ice until use. Gradient generator delivers 8-point
//   dilution series across the 8 barrier channels in
//   parallel (two inlets: drug + DMSO vehicle control).
//   Drug solution introduced by syringe pump or hand pipette.
//   Clusters incubated with drug for 48 h (2880 min) at 37C.
//
//   Live/dead staining after drug exposure (Step 82):
//   calcein AM (live; ex 490 nm, em 526 nm) and propidium
//   iodide (dead; ex 488 nm, em 617 nm) applied at 1:1000.
//   `detect` abstracts fluorescence microscopy readout for
//   dose-response IC50 curve generation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

doxorubicin    = dispense 1 units of Doxorubicin
dmemForDrug    = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
drugSolution   = mix doxorubicin with dmemForDrug for 5s

// 48 h drug incubation at 37C = 2880 min.
heat drugSolution at 37c for 2880m

calceinAM      = dispense 1 units of CalceinAM
piStain        = dispense 1 units of PropidiumIodide
viabilityStain = mix calceinAM with piStain for 1m

druggedClusters = mix drugSolution with viabilityStain for 15m

drugSignal     = detect drugEfficacyDetection on druggedClusters for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Drug efficacy QC gate
// Pass (BB5): drugSignal > 0 -> valid dose-response viability
//             signal obtained; dispose drugged clusters.
// Fail (BB6): drugSignal <= 0 -> assay failure (cluster loss,
//             contamination, or stain failure); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (drugJoin).
// ============================================================

if (drugSignal > 0) {
    dispose druggedClusters
} else {
    drain druggedClusters
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: drugJoin
// Dispose drug efficacy QC signal. Immunostaining begins.
// ============================================================

dispose drugSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 80: Harvest cells from microwells for immunostaining.
//   Remove media; wash with 1 mL PBS. Add 1 mL trypsin
//   (0.25% wt/vol + EDTA); incubate 37C for 3 min.
//   Neutralise with equal volume fresh supplemented DMEM.
//   Release clusters by gentle pipetting. Centrifuge at
//   335g for 3 min; resuspend pellet in 1 mL PBS.
//   BioScript models this as: mix (trypsin wash) ->
//   heat (37C, 3 min) -> mix (neutralise) -> drain
//   (remove supernatant) -> mix (resuspend in PBS).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

harvestPBS     = dispense 1 units of PBS
trypsinEDTA    = dispense 1 units of TrypsinEDTA
trypsinMix     = mix harvestPBS with trypsinEDTA for 3m
heat trypsinMix at 37c for 3m

freshMedia2    = dispense 1 units of SupplementedDMEM
neutralisedCells = mix trypsinMix with freshMedia2 for 1m
drain neutralisedCells

resuspPBS      = dispense 1 units of PBS
harvestedCells = mix resuspPBS with neutralisedCells for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 83B: Fixed-cell immunofluorescence staining.
//
//   (i)   Fix cells with 4% (wt/vol) PFA for 10 min on ice.
//   (ii)  Block with 2.5% (wt/vol) BSA in PBS, 30 min on ice.
//   (iii) Permeabilise with 0.1% (vol/vol) Triton X-100,
//         1 min on ice.
//   (iv)  PBS wash; centrifuge 335g, 3 min.
//   (v)   Incubate with primary antibodies in 2.5% BSA/PBS:
//         - pan-CK antibody (FITC, 1:50) - epithelial CTC marker
//         - CD45 antibody (APC, 1:50)    - leukocyte marker
//         - Hoechst dye (1 M, 1:1000)    - nuclear stain
//         30 min on ice in the dark.
//   Key phenotypes:
//         CK+/CD45-/Hoechst+ = putative CTC
//         CD45+/Hoechst+     = leukocyte feeder cell
//   `heat` at 4c models all on-ice incubation steps.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// (i) Fixation with 4% PFA, 10 min on ice.
pfaFixative    = dispense 1 units of ParaformaldehydeFixative
fixedCells     = mix harvestedCells with pfaFixative for 10m
heat fixedCells at 4c for 10m

// (ii) Blocking with 2.5% BSA in PBS, 30 min on ice.
bsaBlock       = dispense 1 units of BSA
blockedCells   = mix fixedCells with bsaBlock for 30m
heat blockedCells at 4c for 30m

// (iii) Permeabilisation with 0.1% Triton X-100, 1 min on ice.
tritonX        = dispense 1 units of TritonX100
permCells      = mix blockedCells with tritonX for 1m
heat permCells at 4c for 1m

// (iv) PBS wash to remove Triton X-100.
pbsWash2       = dispense 1 units of PBS
washedCells    = mix permCells with pbsWash2 for 3m
drain washedCells

// (v) Primary antibody + nuclear stain incubation, 30 min on ice.
panCKAb        = dispense 1 units of PanCytokeratinAb
cd45Ab         = dispense 1 units of CD45Ab
hoechst        = dispense 1 units of HoechstDye
abMix1         = mix panCKAb with cd45Ab for 5s
abMix          = mix abMix1 with hoechst for 5s
stainedCells   = mix washedCells with abMix for 30m
heat stainedCells at 4c for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 84: Final PBS wash and resuspension for imaging.
//   Wash stained cells with 5 mL PBS; pellet at 335g, 3 min;
//   resuspend in 100 uL PBS. Transfer to 8-well chamber
//   (Nunc Lab-Tek) for imaging on Olympus IX71 confocal
//   (filter sets ET460/50m, ET535/50m, ET605/70).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

finalWashPBS   = dispense 1 units of PBS
imagingCells   = mix stainedCells with finalWashPBS for 3m

stainingSignal = detect immunostainingDetection on imagingCells for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Immunostaining QC gate
// Pass (BB8): stainingSignal > 0 -> CTC markers detected
//             (CK+/CD45-/Hoechst+ cells in clusters);
//             dispose stained cell preparation.
// Fail (BB9): stainingSignal <= 0 -> no CTC markers
//             (false negative: check permeabilisation,
//             antibody expiry, or antibody concentration);
//             drain preparation.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (stainingSignal > 0) {
    dispose imagingCells
} else {
    drain imagingCells
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose immunostaining QC signal. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose stainingSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module libraryQC
module sequencerReadyDetection

manifest GenomicDNA
manifest NexteraEnzymeMix
manifest PCRMixture
manifest MagneticBeads_SPRI
manifest EthanolWash
manifest ElutionBuffer
manifest PluronicF127
manifest FC40_Oil

// ============================================================
// A Microfluidic DNA Library Preparation Platform for
// Next-Generation Sequencing.
// Kim H, Jebrail MJ, Sinha A, et al.
// PLoS ONE 8(7): e68988 (2013).
// DOI: 10.1371/journal.pone.0068988
//
// Platform: Digital Microfluidics (DMF) hub with peripheral
//   capillary-based modules. Central DMF hub (185 um or
//   400 um gap, 48-electrode ITO array) routes droplets
//   via fused-silica capillaries to off-chip modules for
//   each unit operation.
//
// PROTOCOL OVERVIEW (Nextera workflow on DMF):
//   All DNA is prepared in TE buffer + 0.1% Pluronic F127
//   (w/v) to prevent biofouling on Teflon-AF coated surfaces.
//   Reagent boluses are moved at 10 uL/min by syringe pumps.
//   Driving potential: 80-90 V_rms at 15 kHz.
//
//   STEP 1:  Dispense gDNA sample (1.8 uL at 5 ng/uL) and
//            Nextera Enzyme Mix (0.6 uL) onto DMF hub; mix
//            by aspirating/dispensing in and out of capillary
//            for 30 s at room temperature.
//   STEP 2:  Tagmentation: aspirate into thermal module
//            (~850 um deep x 800 um wide trench in Al block)
//            and incubate at 55 C for 5 min. Transposase
//            simultaneously fragments and tags gDNA.
//   STEP 3:  Merge tagmented product with SPRI magnetic bead
//            droplet (4.5 uL) on DMF hub; aspirate into
//            clean-up module. External neodymium magnet
//            (3 mm dia x 3 mm thick) immobilises bead pellet.
//   STEP 4:  Wash bead pellet: dispense 3 uL ethanol onto
//            DMF hub, aspirate into clean-up module, shuttle
//            back-and-forth for 30 s; return to DMF, aspirate
//            to waste. Repeat wash twice (3 washes total).
//   STEP 5:  Elute DNA: dispense 2.2 uL elution buffer,
//            aspirate into clean-up module, remove magnet,
//            resuspend beads (30 s), re-apply magnet, return
//            eluate to DMF hub.
//   STEP 6:  Mix eluate (2.2 uL) with PCR Mixture (2.8 uL)
//            on DMF hub; aspirate 2.8 uL into PCR thermal
//            module (remaining 2.2 uL waste).
//            PCR conditions: 3 min 72 C init, 30 s 95 C
//            denaturation; 10 cycles (10 s 95 C, 30 s 62 C,
//            3 min 72 C). Adds sequencing adapters (Illumina).
//   STEP 7:  Size selection - Stage 1 (remove <200 bp):
//            merge PCR product with 2.2 uL SPRI beads,
//            aspirate into size-selection module.
//            Immobilise beads, return supernatant to DMF hub.
//   STEP 8:  Size selection - Stage 2 (remove >400 bp):
//            merge supernatant with 0.45 uL SPRI beads,
//            aspirate into size-selection module, ethanol
//            wash bead pellet; elute library (3 uL EB)
//            back onto DMF hub. Target library: 200-400 bp.
//   STEP 9:  QC detection: detect library signal on final
//            library droplet (Bioanalyzer / Kapa quant proxy).
//
// REAGENT VOLUMES (185 um-gap hub, human gDNA):
//   gDNA sample:        1.8 uL  (5 ng/uL in TE + 0.1% F127)
//   Nextera Enzyme Mix: 0.6 uL  (2 uL HMW + 0.5 uL enzyme mix)
//   SPRI beads clean-up: 4.5 uL
//   Ethanol wash:       3.0 uL  (repeated x3)
//   Elution buffer:     2.2 uL  (clean-up elution)
//   PCR Mixture:        2.8 uL  (12.5 uL 2X PCR buffer +
//                                0.5 uL primer cocktail +
//                                0.5 uL PCR enzyme +
//                                0.5 uL 50X Adapter 2)
//   SPRI beads SS1:     2.2 uL  (Stage 1: remove <200 bp)
//   SPRI beads SS2:     0.45 uL (Stage 2: remove >400 bp)
//   Elution buffer:     3.0 uL  (final library elution)
//
//   NOTE (400 um-gap hub, bacterial gDNA): all volumes 2X
//   except elution buffer (5 uL final); 9 PCR cycles used.
//
// NOTE ON PLURONIC ADDITIVE:
//   0.1% Pluronic F127 is added to all DNA and reagent
//   solutions to prevent biofouling (protein/enzyme adhesion
//   to Teflon-AF). Validated not to affect tagmentation
//   efficiency (Fig S1 in paper). Modelled here as a single
//   manifest; in practice it is pre-mixed into all solutions.
//
// NOTE ON MAGNETIC BEAD STEPS:
//   Wash and elution steps are implemented in peripheral
//   off-DMF capillary modules (not on the DMF electrode
//   array). `mix` statements model the bolus shuttle
//   mixing in the module tubing. Supernatant/waste removal
//   is modelled with `dispose`/`drain` per BioScript convention.
//
// NOTE ON HEAT STEPS:
//   Temperature control via closed-loop thermoelectric Al block.
//   BioScript `heat` duration tokens: s (seconds), m (minutes).
//   All durations converted accordingly.
//
// NOTE ON CONTAMINATION PREVENTION:
//   Between library runs the system is rinsed with 10% (v/v)
//   bleach and then deionized water (off-chip; not modelled).
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main            -- entry: dispense, tagmentation,
//                           bead clean-up, PCR, size-selection
//   BB2  libraryPass     -- librarySignal > 0: valid library
//                           (200-400 bp); dispose for sequencing
//   BB3  libraryFail     -- librarySignal <= 0: failed library;
//                           drain
//   BB4  finalCleanup    -- terminal: dispose QC signal;
//                           library ready for MiSeq loading
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1a: Dispense gDNA sample (1.8 uL, 5 ng/uL in TE
//   buffer + 0.1% Pluronic F127). Sample dispensed from
//   pre-loaded capillary onto the 185 um-gap DMF hub at
//   160 V_rms, 15 kHz driving potential.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

gdnaDroplet     = dispense 1 units of GenomicDNA

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1b: Dispense Nextera Enzyme Mix (0.6 uL) from its
//   capillary onto the DMF hub. Mix contains 2 uL High
//   Molecular Weight component + 0.5 uL enzyme mix +
//   0.1% Pluronic F127 to prevent biofouling (Fig S1).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

nexteraEnzyme   = dispense 1 units of NexteraEnzymeMix

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1c: Mix gDNA + Nextera Enzyme on DMF hub.
//   Active mixing (30 s, room temperature) achieved by
//   continuously aspirating and dispensing the merged droplet
//   in and out of the capillary. The mixed droplet is then
//   aspirated into the tagmentation thermal module.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tagmentationMix = mix gdnaDroplet with nexteraEnzyme for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Tagmentation - incubate at 55 C for 5 min.
//   Mixed droplet aspirated into the tagmentation module
//   (750 um OD tubing recessed ~100 um below the surface
//   of a custom Al block; temperature match within ~5 C).
//   Transposase simultaneously fragments and adapter-tags
//   gDNA. Product: adapter-tagged DNA fragments (150-1000 bp
//   broad distribution; Fig 4b). Adapter dimers (~45 bp)
//   also generated and removed in the clean-up step below.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat tagmentationMix at 55c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3a: Dispense SPRI magnetic bead droplet (4.5 uL) from
//   the clean-up module capillary onto the DMF hub. Beads
//   are Solid Phase Reversible Immobilisation (SPRI) beads
//   (Beckman Coulter) used to purify tagmented DNA and remove
//   excess enzyme, adapter dimers, and small fragments.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cleanupBeads    = dispense 1 units of MagneticBeads_SPRI

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3b: Merge tagmented product with SPRI beads on DMF
//   hub. The combined droplet is aspirated into the clean-up
//   module (capillary tubing, 160 um OD / 100 um ID,
//   inserted into the DMF air gap via side port).
//   External neodymium magnet (3 mm dia x 3 mm) positioned
//   below the module immobilises the bead pellet against the
//   inner tube wall. Positive pressure-driven syringe pump
//   flow (10 uL/min) achieves near-complete supernatant
//   removal from the pellet (Fig 3, frames 1-3).
//   Supernatant (excess enzyme + small DNA + adapter dimers)
//   is returned to DMF hub and actuated to waste capillary.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tagmentedBead   = mix tagmentationMix with cleanupBeads for 30s
dispose tagmentedBead

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Ethanol wash of bead pellet (x3).
//   Wash 1: 3 uL ethanol (100%) dispensed onto DMF hub,
//   aspirated into clean-up module, shuttled back-and-forth
//   for 30 s across the immobilised bead pellet (chaotic
//   mixing; removes trace contaminants / salts).
//   Ethanol is returned to DMF hub, actuated to waste module,
//   and aspirated off-chip. Procedure repeated twice more
//   (3 total washes) as per Nextera protocol.
//
//   NOTE: Each wash cycle uses a fresh 3 uL ethanol droplet.
//   Three separate wash droplets are modelled below to
//   reflect the sequential, independent wash boluses.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ethanolWash1    = dispense 1 units of EthanolWash
ethanolBolus1   = mix ethanolWash1 with ethanolWash1 for 30s
dispose ethanolBolus1

ethanolWash2    = dispense 1 units of EthanolWash
ethanolBolus2   = mix ethanolWash2 with ethanolWash2 for 30s
dispose ethanolBolus2

ethanolWash3    = dispense 1 units of EthanolWash
ethanolBolus3   = mix ethanolWash3 with ethanolWash3 for 30s
dispose ethanolBolus3

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Elution of clean tagmented DNA from bead pellet.
//   2.2 uL elution buffer (water + 0.1% Pluronic F127)
//   dispensed onto DMF hub and aspirated into clean-up module.
//   External magnet removed; beads resuspended in buffer by
//   back-and-forth bolus shuttling for 30 s at room temperature.
//   Magnet re-applied to immobilise beads; the DNA-containing
//   eluate droplet returned to the DMF hub and actuated to
//   the PCR inlet electrode.
//   Product: 2.2 uL purified, adapter-tagged DNA fragments.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

elutionBuf1     = dispense 1 units of ElutionBuffer
cleanedDNA      = mix elutionBuf1 with elutionBuf1 for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6a: Dispense PCR Mixture (2.8 uL) from its capillary.
//   Contents: 12.5 uL 2X PCR buffer + 0.5 uL primer cocktail
//   + 0.5 uL PCR enzyme + 0.5 uL 50X Adapter 2 (per well
//   per manufacturer; scaled to 2.8 uL on-chip volume).
//   This mixture adds Illumina-specific dual-indexed adapters
//   to the fragment ends via limited-cycle PCR amplification.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pcrMix          = dispense 1 units of PCRMixture

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6b: Merge eluate with PCR mixture on DMF hub (30 s).
//   The combined ~5 uL droplet is then aspirated into the
//   PCR thermal module: 2.8 uL enters the module inlet;
//   remaining 2.2 uL actuated to waste on DMF hub.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

prePCRDroplet   = mix cleanedDNA with pcrMix for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6c: Limited-cycle PCR - adapter addition and enrichment.
//   Thermocycling conditions (OpenPCR benchtop cycler via
//   Al block; closed-loop PID temperature control):
//     Initial extension:  72 C for 3 min
//     Denaturation:       95 C for 30 s
//     10 cycles:
//       Denaturation:     95 C for 10 s
//       Annealing:        62 C for 30 s
//       Extension:        72 C for 3 min
//   After PCR the product is dispensed back onto DMF hub
//   for size selection.
//   Product (Fig 4c): broad ~200-10000 bp enriched library
//   with primer dimers (~55 bp) to be removed by size selection.
//
//   NOTE: BioScript `heat` models one temperature phase per
//   call. The full thermocycling program is abstracted as
//   sequential heat steps (init extension, denaturation,
//   and the rate-limiting 10-cycle block at the annealing
//   temperature as the combined incubation).
//   10 cycles x (10s + 30s + 180s) = 10 cycles x 220s = 2200s.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat prePCRDroplet at 72c for 3m
heat prePCRDroplet at 95c for 30s
heat prePCRDroplet at 62c for 2200s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7a: Size Selection - Stage 1 (remove fragments <200 bp).
//   PCR product dispensed back to DMF hub from PCR module.
//   Merge with 2.2 uL SPRI beads (high bead:DNA ratio to
//   bind all DNA including primer dimers and small fragments).
//   Aspirate into size-selection module. Immobilise beads
//   with external magnet; supernatant (contains desired
//   200-400 bp fragments) returned to DMF hub.
//
//   NOTE: The supernatant -- not the bead-bound fraction --
//   carries the size-selected library at this stage (binding
//   conditions set to leave 200-400 bp fragments in solution).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sizeSelBeads1   = dispense 1 units of MagneticBeads_SPRI
sizeSelMix1     = mix prePCRDroplet with sizeSelBeads1 for 30s

// Supernatant (200-400 bp library) recovered from Stage 1.
// Bead pellet (small DNA + primer dimers) disposed.
librarySuper    = mix sizeSelMix1 with sizeSelMix1 for 10s
dispose sizeSelMix1

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7b: Size Selection - Stage 2 (remove fragments >400 bp).
//   The supernatant from Stage 1 is merged with 0.45 uL SPRI
//   beads (low bead:DNA ratio to bind only large fragments
//   >400 bp). Beads immobilised; ethanol wash performed on
//   bead pellet (large DNA + residual contaminants disposed).
//   Final elution: 3 uL elution buffer resuspends the desired
//   200-400 bp library from the Stage-2 bead pellet; eluate
//   returned to DMF hub as the final sequencer-ready library.
//
//   NOTE: At Stage 2 the DESIRED library is bead-bound (large
//   fragments captured; smaller fragments remain in solution
//   and are disposed). Elution releases the 200-400 bp library.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sizeSelBeads2   = dispense 1 units of MagneticBeads_SPRI
sizeSelMix2     = mix librarySuper with sizeSelBeads2 for 30s

// Ethanol wash of Stage-2 bead pellet (200-400 bp library bound).
ethanolSS       = dispense 1 units of EthanolWash
sizeSelWash     = mix sizeSelMix2 with ethanolSS for 30s
dispose sizeSelWash

// Elute final library (3 uL elution buffer) from Stage-2 beads.
elutionBuf2     = dispense 1 units of ElutionBuffer
dnaLibrary      = mix elutionBuf2 with elutionBuf2 for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Library QC detection.
//   Final library droplet (3 uL, target 200-400 bp) dispensed
//   back to DMF hub for analysis.
//   On-chip QC abstracts the Bioanalyzer (Agilent) size
//   distribution check (Fig 4d) and Kapa library quantification
//   kit fluorescence-based quantitation step (off-chip).
//   Passing criterion: library peak in 200-400 bp window
//   at sufficient concentration for MiSeq loading (8 pM).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

librarySignal   = detect libraryQC on dnaLibrary for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Library QC gate
// Pass (BB2): librarySignal > 0 -> valid 200-400 bp library
//             at sequencer-compatible concentration; dispose
//             for off-chip MiSeq loading (300-cycle paired-end
//             150 bp kit, loading concentration 8 pM).
// Fail (BB3): librarySignal <= 0 -> library preparation
//             failed (insufficient size selection, low yield,
//             or under/over-clustering risk); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (librarySignal > 0) {
    dispose dnaLibrary
} else {
    drain dnaLibrary
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose library QC signal droplet.
// Sequencer-ready library available off-chip for MiSeq run.
// Total on-chip time: ~1 h (5 min hands-on).
// ============================================================

dispose librarySignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module saltRemovalDetection
module suspendedSolidsDetection
module divalentCationDetection

manifest NaCl_Brine_HighSalinity
manifest Na2SO4_RinseBuffer
manifest HFE7500_Oil
manifest FDA_PI_ViabilityStain
manifest EcoliBacterialCells
manifest CrudeOilEmulsion
manifest DemineralizedWater

// ============================================================
// Purification of High Salinity Brine by Multi-Stage Ion
// Concentration Polarization Desalination
// Kim, Kwak, Kwon, Pham, Kim, Al-Anzi, Lim & Han
// Scientific Reports 6, 31850 (2016)
// DOI: 10.1038/srep31850
//
// PROTOCOL OVERVIEW:
// ICP (Ion Concentration Polarization) desalination is an
// electrically-driven membrane process using only cation
// exchange membranes (CEMs) with unipolar conduction.
// Feed: 0.1-1.7 M NaCl (5,800-100,000 mg/L TDS).
// Electrode rinse: Na2SO4 at same normality as feed.
// Device: PDMS-based microfluidic system. Membrane gap:
// 1.5-2.0 mm, depth 0.2-0.6 mm, length 10-100 mm.
// CEMs: Fumasep FTCM-E (area resistance ~13.3 Ohm.cm2).
// Electrodes: carbon paper.
// Flow: negative-pressure syringe pump at ~20 uL/min.
// Applied current density: 125-375 A/m2 constant.
// Trifurcated output: concentrate (top) / intermediate
// (middle, fed back to next stage) / diluate (bottom).
// Salt removal per unit stage: 5-50%.
// Overall target: 50-75% salt removal, up to 100,000 ppm.
//
// MULTI-STAGE (TRIFURCATED) ABSTRACTION:
// The BioScript `mix` operation models the flow-through ICP
// separation event at each stage. The intermediate output
// stream (concentration ~C0, majority fluid) is re-mixed
// with the next ICP stage as the feed, representing the
// batch/recirculation strategy that keeps feed concentration
// constant in every stage (EPIR-optimal regime).
// Three stages are encoded explicitly, representing the
// optimised staging configuration for high-salinity feed
// (saltier feedwater requires more stages; SI-section 3.2.3).
//
// TEMPERATURE NOTE:
// ICP is operated at room temperature (~25 C). The `heat`
// statement at 25 C models the constant-current electrical
// actuation period (no thermal processing is applied in
// the paper; BioScript has no native voltage/current
// operation statement, so heat at 25 C is the correct
// abstraction for timed electrical operation, consistent
// with the Samlali reference file convention).
//
// DURATION TOKENS:
// BioScript supports s (seconds) and m (minutes) only.
// All durations below are within these units.
// The paper reports ~45 min for a trap-to-recovery cycle
// and 30-60 min conductivity stabilisation; 60m is used
// for each ICP stage incubation as a conservative bound.
//
// FIX: Removed the dangling global `rinseBuffer` dispense
// that was present in v1. Na2SO4 rinse is now dispensed
// only at the point of use within each ICP stage (rinse1,
// rinse2, rinse3) and in the divalent cation branch
// (rinseDivalent). This eliminates the dead-code variable
// that compiled to an unconsumed token in the IR.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main               -- device priming, membrane prep,
//                              Stage 1 ICP separation,
//                              Stage 2 ICP separation,
//                              Stage 3 ICP separation,
//                              salt removal QC detection
//   BB2  saltRemovalPass    -- saltSignal > 0: target TDS reached;
//                              dispose treated diluate
//   BB3  saltRemovalFail    -- saltSignal <= 0: TDS target not met;
//                              drain diluate stream
//   BB4  saltRemovalJoin    -- join: suspended-solids branch begins
//   BB5  solidsRemovalPass  -- solidsSignal > 0: TSS cleared; proceed
//                              to divalent cation QC
//   BB6  solidsRemovalFail  -- solidsSignal <= 0: suspended solids
//                              detected in diluate; drain
//   BB7  solidsRemovalJoin  -- join: divalent cation branch begins
//   BB8  divalentPass       -- divalentSignal > 0: Na+/Ca2+ removal
//                              confirmed; dispose treated water
//   BB9  divalentFail       -- divalentSignal <= 0: divalent ions
//                              remain above threshold; drain
//   BB10 finalCleanup       -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Membrane pre-conditioning.
//   All CEMs (Fumasep FTCM-E) placed in demineralised water
//   for at least 48 h at room temperature before experiments,
//   as per paper Materials and Methods (Device fabrication).
//   `mix` abstracts the passive soak/conditioning step.
//   Duration: 2880m = 48 h (BioScript maximum resolution: m).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

demineralizedWaterPrep = dispense 1 units of DemineralizedWater
cem_membrane           = dispense 1 units of DemineralizedWater

conditionedMembrane = mix cem_membrane with demineralizedWaterPrep for 2880m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Dispense high-salinity brine feed.
//   Feed: 0.1-1.7 M NaCl (5,800-100,000 mg/L TDS).
//   Represents produced water / brine concentrate range.
//   Delivered via syringe pump at ~20 uL/min negative
//   pressure into the PDMS-ICP microfluidic device.
//   Na2SO4 rinse buffer dispensed per-stage at point of use
//   (rinse1, rinse2, rinse3 below) to avoid dead-code tokens.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

brineFeed = dispense 1 units of NaCl_Brine_HighSalinity

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Stage 1 - ICP desalination (first pass).
//   Brine feed loaded into ICP device with conditioned CEMs.
//   Constant current applied (e.g. 125-375 A/m2) via carbon
//   paper electrodes and Keithley 236 source unit.
//   Ion depletion zone forms adjacent to CEMs; Na+ and Cl-
//   are separated into concentrate (top, enriched) and
//   diluate (bottom, depleted) streams by ICP mechanism.
//   Trifurcated output: small diluate + majority intermediate
//   (at ~C0) + small concentrate.
//   Target salt removal per stage: 5-20% (optimal EPIR regime
//   for multi-stage operation; Fig 3c, paper).
//   `mix` abstracts the flow-through ICP separation event.
//   `heat at 25c` abstracts timed constant-current actuation.
//   Conductivity of dilute output tracked via flow-through
//   probe (Microelectrode Inc.) to confirm salt removal.
//   Na2SO4 rinse flows in parallel electrode channels to
//   exclude electrode overpotential from cell voltage reading.
//   Ag/AgCl reference electrodes penetrate channels vertically
//   to measure only the membrane voltage drop per cell.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

stage1Mix = mix brineFeed with conditionedMembrane for 10s
heat stage1Mix at 25c for 60m

rinse1          = dispense 1 units of Na2SO4_RinseBuffer
stage1WithRinse = mix stage1Mix with rinse1 for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Stage 2 - ICP desalination (second pass).
//   Intermediate stream from Stage 1 (~C0 concentration,
//   majority fluid) is re-fed into an identical ICP unit.
//   This constant-feed-concentration strategy keeps the
//   system in the high-salinity (low EPIR) regime throughout,
//   avoiding the unfavourable low-salinity regime encountered
//   in conventional multi-stage electrodialysis.
//   (Paper: "keeping feed concentration constant in every
//   stage which enables us to play only in economically
//   favorable EPIR regime", Section: Presenting multi-stage
//   ICP desalination and optimization, p.5.)
//   Current density adjusted per Fig 5a table for the
//   selected dilute production ratio (p = 0.20-0.33).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

stage2Mix = mix stage1WithRinse with conditionedMembrane for 10s
heat stage2Mix at 25c for 60m

rinse2          = dispense 1 units of Na2SO4_RinseBuffer
stage2WithRinse = mix stage2Mix with rinse2 for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Stage 3 - ICP desalination (third pass).
//   Third identical ICP stage. Cumulative salt removal now
//   approaches 50-75% target (overall recovery fixed at 50%
//   for cost comparison; SI-section 3.2.1).
//   Saltier feedwater (100,000 ppm) requires more stages;
//   3 stages modelled here as the representative optimised
//   configuration for the high-salinity range studied.
//   (SI-section 3.2.3: "saltier feedwater requiring more
//   stages".)
//   Diluate conductivity confirmed by flow-through probe;
//   CCD-based ICP-AES (ACTIVA-S, Horiba) confirms
//   quantitative removal of monovalent and divalent cations.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

stage3Mix = mix stage2WithRinse with conditionedMembrane for 10s
heat stage3Mix at 25c for 60m

rinse3         = dispense 1 units of Na2SO4_RinseBuffer
treatedDiluate = mix stage3Mix with rinse3 for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Salt removal QC detection.
//   saltRemovalDetection module abstracts off-chip
//   conductivity measurement (flow-through conductivity probe,
//   Microelectrode Inc.) and ICP-AES cation analysis.
//   Positive signal: diluate TDS reduced by 50-75% relative
//   to feed (target met).
//   Negative signal: TDS reduction below target; drain stream.
//   (Paper Results: "ICP electrical desalination is shown
//   here to treat brine salinity up to 100,000 ppm of TDS
//   with flexible salt rejection rate up to 70%", Abstract.)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

saltSignal = detect saltRemovalDetection on treatedDiluate for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Salt removal QC gate
// Pass (BB2): saltSignal > 0 -> target TDS reduction achieved;
//             dispose treated diluate.
// Fail (BB3): saltSignal <= 0 -> TDS target not met; drain.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (saltRemovalJoin).
// ============================================================

if (saltSignal > 0) {
    dispose treatedDiluate
} else {
    drain treatedDiluate
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: saltRemovalJoin
// Dispose salt removal QC signal. Control falls into the
// suspended-solids characterisation branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose saltSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Suspended solids removal characterisation.
//   Brine wastewater (produced water) contains both dissolved
//   salts (TDS) and suspended solids (TSS: bacteria, oil
//   emulsions). ICP removes both simultaneously via
//   electrophoretic migration of negatively charged particles
//   to the concentrate stream (Fig 7, paper).
//   Two contaminant models added to fresh brine feed:
//   (a) E. coli K-12 MG1655 (GFP-expressing, negative zeta)
//       1.5 uM Alexa Fluor 488 used to visualise ICP zone.
//   (b) Crude oil emulsions (1:50 oil:NaCl, sonicated 2 h,
//       fluorescent red dye 1:1000 for tracing).
//   Particles migrate upward (anode direction) into concentrate
//   output; diluate (bottom) is salt- and particle-free.
//   Detection: fluorescence microscopy (Olympus IX-71).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bacterialCells        = dispense 1 units of EcoliBacterialCells
crudeOilEmulsion      = dispense 1 units of CrudeOilEmulsion
brineFeedTSS          = dispense 1 units of NaCl_Brine_HighSalinity

contaminatedBrine     = mix brineFeedTSS with bacterialCells for 2s
contaminatedBrineFull = mix contaminatedBrine with crudeOilEmulsion for 2s

// ICP separation of TSS-loaded brine.
// Negatively charged particles delivered to concentrate stream
// en bloc by electrophoretic force; diluate exits particle-free.
membraneTSS   = dispense 1 units of DemineralizedWater
tssSeparation = mix contaminatedBrineFull with membraneTSS for 10s
heat tssSeparation at 25c for 30m

solidsSignal = detect suspendedSolidsDetection on tssSeparation for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Suspended solids QC gate
// Pass (BB5): solidsSignal > 0 -> TSS cleared from diluate
//             (bacteria and oil emulsions in concentrate);
//             proceed to divalent cation QC.
// Fail (BB6): solidsSignal <= 0 -> particles remain in
//             diluate; drain stream.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (solidsRemovalJoin).
// ============================================================

if (solidsSignal > 0) {
    dispose tssSeparation
} else {
    drain tssSeparation
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: solidsRemovalJoin
// Dispose suspended-solids QC signal. Control falls into
// divalent cation removal branch (BB8/BB9).
// ============================================================

dispose solidsSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Divalent cation removal characterisation.
//   In produced water / flowback, divalent cations (Ca2+,
//   Mg2+, Ba2+, Sr2+) form carbonate/sulfate precipitates
//   in the wellbore if not removed prior to recycling.
//   ICP desalination removes divalent cations proportionally
//   or in excess of monovalent cations (SI-section 8),
//   consistent with electrodialysis behaviour (refs 41-43).
//   CCD-based ICP-AES (ACTIVA-S, Horiba, Ltd., Japan) used
//   for quantitative monovalent/divalent cation analysis.
//   Brine feed mixed with Na2SO4 rinse (electrode channel)
//   then processed through ICP device; diluate analysed for
//   residual Ca2+ and Na+ by ICP-AES.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

brineFeedDivalent  = dispense 1 units of NaCl_Brine_HighSalinity
rinseDivalent      = dispense 1 units of Na2SO4_RinseBuffer
membraneDivalent   = dispense 1 units of DemineralizedWater

divalentFeedMix    = mix brineFeedDivalent with rinseDivalent for 5s
divalentSeparation = mix divalentFeedMix with membraneDivalent for 10s
heat divalentSeparation at 25c for 30m

divalentSignal = detect divalentCationDetection on divalentSeparation for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Divalent cation removal QC gate
// Pass (BB8): divalentSignal > 0 -> Na+ and Ca2+ removal
//             confirmed by ICP-AES; treated water disposed.
// Fail (BB9): divalentSignal <= 0 -> residual divalent ions
//             above threshold; drain stream.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (divalentSignal > 0) {
    dispose divalentSeparation
} else {
    drain divalentSeparation
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all QC signal droplets. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose divalentSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module nfkbIrfDetection
module cytokineDetection
module viabilityDetection
module vaccinationQC

manifest RAWDual_Macrophages
manifest THP1_Monocytes
manifest BMDC_Cells
manifest PRR_Agonist_Pam3CSK4
manifest PRR_Agonist_LPS
manifest PRR_Agonist_R848
manifest PRR_Agonist_CpG1826
manifest Immunomodulator_DMSO_Stock
manifest AlphaLISA_Reagent
manifest FDA_PI_Stain
manifest OVA_Antigen
manifest Fluzone_Vaccine
manifest TyphimVi_Vaccine
manifest DMSO_Vehicle
manifest CellCultureMedia

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Discovery of New States of Immunomodulation for Vaccine
// Adjuvants via High Throughput Screening: Expanding Innate
// Responses to PRRs.
// Kim, Rosenberger et al., ACS Cent. Sci. 2023, 9, 427-439.
// DOI: 10.1021/acscentsci.2c01351
//
// This BioScript file encodes the HIGH-THROUGHPUT SCREENING
// PIPELINE described in the paper. The pipeline consists of
// three sequential phases:
//
//   PHASE 1: Primary Screen (NF-kB / IRF transcription factor
//            activity). RAW-Dual macrophages seeded in 384-well
//            plates. ~3,147 immunomodulator compounds (10 uM
//            via pin-drop, <0.05% DMSO v/v) combined with
//            one of 13 PRR agonists at EC50. 24 h incubation
//            at 37 C / 5% CO2. Dual NF-kB (absorbance 620 nm)
//            and IRF (luminescence) readout.
//
//   PHASE 2: Secondary Screen (AlphaLISA multiplexed cytokine
//            panel). THP-1 monocytes. 720 downselected
//            modulators x 7 PRR agonists. Six cytokines
//            measured in triplicate duplexed AlphaLISA: IL-1b,
//            IL-12p40, IP-10 (CXCL10), CCL4, TNF-a, IFN-b.
//            Incubation 24 h at 37 C.
//
//   PHASE 3: In vivo validation. Murine (C57/B6) prime-boost
//            vaccination model. Lead modulators (PME-564,
//            PME-397, PME-1226, etc.) administered i.m. with
//            commercial vaccines (Fluzone 2021-2022 and
//            Typhim-Vi). Systemic TNF-a read at 1 h post-
//            injection; antigen-specific serum IgG at Day 49.
//
// NOTE ON SCOPE: All on-chip operations (dispense, mix, heat,
//   detect) model the liquid-handling steps performed by high-
//   throughput robotics (384-well pin-drop dispensing, plate
//   incubator, plate reader). The `mix` primitive here
//   abstracts electrode-free well-based mixing (pipette
//   aspirate/dispense cycle), not DMF electrode actuation.
//   Heat steps model incubator TEC control (37 C target).
//   All durations are in BioScript-valid tokens (s or m only;
//   24 h = 1440m, 7 days = 10080m).
//
// NOTE ON PRR AGONIST REPRESENTATION: The paper screens 13
//   PRR agonists (Table S2). Four representative agonists
//   (Pam3CSK4 TLR2/1, LPS TLR4, R848 TLR7/8, CpG1826 TLR9)
//   are instantiated as separate reagent droplets and
//   processed in parallel lanes to reflect the experimental
//   design. Adding all 13 would not change the CFG structure.
//
// NOTE ON DOWNSELECTION LOGIC: PCA-based downselection
//   (3,147 -> 720 compounds) and vaccine score computation
//   are off-chip data analysis steps. The QC gates in the
//   CFG below (primaryQC, secondaryQC) model the binary
//   pass/fail outcome of those analyses for each modulator.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- entry: cell seeding, modulator
//                             addition, agonist addition,
//                             primary screen incubation & readout
//   BB2  primaryPass       -- primarySignal > 0: modulator active
//                             (outside PCA radius 1.75); continue
//   BB3  primaryFail       -- primarySignal <= 0: inactive/toxic;
//                             drain and abort
//   BB4  primaryJoin       -- join: secondary screen begins
//   BB5  secondaryPass     -- secondarySignal > 0: cytokine
//                             profile suitable; continue to in vivo
//   BB6  secondaryFail     -- secondarySignal <= 0: cytokine
//                             profile unsuitable; drain
//   BB7  secondaryJoin     -- join: in vivo validation begins
//   BB8  vaccinationPass   -- vaccinationSignal > 0: modulator
//                             improves IgG or reduces TNF-a; dispose
//   BB9  vaccinationFail   -- vaccinationSignal <= 0: no in vivo
//                             benefit; drain
//   BB10 finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// PRIMARY SCREEN
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRIMARY STEP 1: Seed RAW-Dual macrophages in 384-well plate.
//   Cell density optimised for consistent NF-kB / IRF dynamic
//   range (SI Appendix, Figure S1). Cells pre-incubated for
//   24 h before compound addition to allow adherence.
//   `mix` abstracts the robotic seeding and settlement step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rawCells        = dispense 1 units of RAWDual_Macrophages
seedingMedia    = dispense 1 units of CellCultureMedia
seededPlate     = mix rawCells with seedingMedia for 5s

// 24 h pre-incubation at 37 C for cell adherence (= 1440 min).
heat seededPlate at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRIMARY STEP 2: Pin-drop dispense immunomodulator compound
//   to final concentration 10 uM (<0.05% DMSO v/v). Pin-drop
//   robotics add ~23 nL of 10 mM DMSO stock to 230 uL well
//   volume. Modulator is pre-incubated with cells for 1 h
//   before agonist addition to allow uptake / equilibration.
//   This step models a single representative modulator droplet.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

modDroplet      = dispense 1 units of Immunomodulator_DMSO_Stock
modCells        = mix seededPlate with modDroplet for 5s

// 1 h pre-incubation at 37 C before agonist addition.
heat modCells at 37c for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRIMARY STEP 3: Dispense four representative PRR agonists
//   at their respective EC50 concentrations (SI Appendix,
//   Table S2). In the paper, one of 13 agonists is added per
//   well per modulator. Four parallel lanes are modelled here.
//   Agonist added after modulator pre-incubation (not co-added).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

agonistPam3CSK4  = dispense 1 units of PRR_Agonist_Pam3CSK4
agonistLPS       = dispense 1 units of PRR_Agonist_LPS
agonistR848      = dispense 1 units of PRR_Agonist_R848
agonistCpG       = dispense 1 units of PRR_Agonist_CpG1826

// Merge agonist lanes into modulator-containing cell wells.
wellPam3CSK4     = mix modCells with agonistPam3CSK4 for 5s
wellLPS          = mix modCells with agonistLPS for 5s
wellR848         = mix modCells with agonistR848 for 5s
wellCpG          = mix modCells with agonistCpG for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRIMARY STEP 4: 24 h co-incubation of cells with modulator
//   and agonist at 37 C / 5% CO2. Transcription factor
//   activity develops over this window. Supernatant drawn
//   after 24 h for dual NF-kB (absorbance) and IRF
//   (luminescence) readout on the RAW-Dual reporter system.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat wellPam3CSK4 at 37c for 1440m
heat wellLPS      at 37c for 1440m
heat wellR848     at 37c for 1440m
heat wellCpG      at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRIMARY STEP 5: Cell viability QC (confluency imaging).
//   Live cell imaging + digital confluency mask applied to
//   remove toxic modulator/agonist combinations before
//   transcription factor readout (SI Appendix, Figure S3).
//   Modelled as a viability detect on LPS well (representative).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

viabilityCheckLPS = detect viabilityDetection on wellLPS for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRIMARY STEP 6: Dual NF-kB / IRF transcription factor
//   readout. nfkbIrfDetection module abstracts:
//     - NF-kB: SEAP reporter, absorbance 620 nm (Quanti-Blue)
//     - IRF: luciferase reporter, luminescence (QUANTI-Luc)
//   Both measured from the same supernatant well.
//   Report as fold change vs agonist-alone control.
//   PCA over all 13 agonists: retain if outside radius 1.75
//   (SI Appendix, Figure S4). primarySignal encodes the
//   pass/fail outcome of PCA downselection (3,147 -> 720).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primarySignalPam3 = detect nfkbIrfDetection on wellPam3CSK4 for 60s
primarySignalLPS  = detect nfkbIrfDetection on wellLPS for 60s
primarySignalR848 = detect nfkbIrfDetection on wellR848 for 60s
primarySignalCpG  = detect nfkbIrfDetection on wellCpG for 60s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Primary screen QC gate (keyed on LPS well).
// Pass (BB2): primarySignalLPS > 0 -> modulator shows
//             significant NF-kB/IRF modulation (PCA outside
//             radius 1.75); proceed to secondary screen.
// Fail (BB3): primarySignalLPS <= 0 -> inactive or toxic;
//             drain all wells, abort pipeline.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (primaryJoin).
// ============================================================

if (primarySignalLPS > 0) {
    dispose wellPam3CSK4
    dispose wellLPS
    dispose wellR848
    dispose wellCpG
} else {
    drain wellPam3CSK4
    drain wellLPS
    drain wellR848
    drain wellCpG
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: primaryJoin
// Dispose viability and primary transcription factor QC
// signals. Control falls into secondary screen (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose viabilityCheckLPS
dispose primarySignalPam3
dispose primarySignalLPS
dispose primarySignalR848
dispose primarySignalCpG

// ============================================================
// BB4 -> PHASE 2: SECONDARY SCREEN (AlphaLISA cytokine panel)
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SECONDARY STEP 1: Seed THP-1 monocytes for cytokine panel.
//   Paper switches from RAW-Dual (murine macrophage, used for
//   TF activity) to THP-1 (human monocyte) because human
//   AlphaLISA reagents provide higher multiplexing capacity.
//   Same 384-well plate format; robotic seeding as before.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

thp1Cells        = dispense 1 units of THP1_Monocytes
thp1Media        = dispense 1 units of CellCultureMedia
thp1Seeded       = mix thp1Cells with thp1Media for 5s

// 24 h pre-incubation at 37 C (= 1440 min).
heat thp1Seeded at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SECONDARY STEP 2: Re-apply lead modulator (same compound,
//   same 10 uM pin-drop protocol). 720 downselected modulators
//   are re-screened against 7 PRR agonists from the primary
//   set. Modulator pre-incubated 1 h before agonist addition.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

modDroplet2      = dispense 1 units of Immunomodulator_DMSO_Stock
thp1ModCells     = mix thp1Seeded with modDroplet2 for 5s

heat thp1ModCells at 37c for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SECONDARY STEP 3: Add representative agonist (R848 as
//   representative TLR7/8 agonist used in in vivo validation).
//   In the paper, 7 agonists are run in parallel per modulator
//   (subset of the primary 13). Modelled here as single lane.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

agonistR848_2    = dispense 1 units of PRR_Agonist_R848
thp1Well         = mix thp1ModCells with agonistR848_2 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SECONDARY STEP 4: 24 h co-incubation at 37 C.
//   Supernatant collected for AlphaLISA multiplexed cytokine
//   measurement. Six cytokines measured in triplicate
//   duplexed AlphaLISA reactions (SI Appendix, Figure S5):
//     IL-1b, IL-12p40, IP-10 (CXCL10), CCL4, TNF-a, IFN-b.
//   Standard curves applied; fold change vs agonist-alone.
//   AlphaLISA reagent merged with supernatant for detection.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat thp1Well at 37c for 1440m

alphaLISAReagent = dispense 1 units of AlphaLISA_Reagent
detectionWell    = mix thp1Well with alphaLISAReagent for 10s

// AlphaLISA bead equilibration (paper: 60 min incubation
// in subdued light before plate-reader readout). 60 min = 60m.
heat detectionWell at 25c for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SECONDARY STEP 5: Six-plex cytokine detection.
//   cytokineDetection module abstracts AlphaLISA plate-reader
//   readout (AlphaLISA singlet excitation 680 nm, emission
//   615 nm channel switching for each bead pair).
//   secondarySignal encodes the vaccine score gate outcome:
//   positive = compound preserves or enhances IFN-b/IP-10/
//   CCL4/IL-12 and suppresses TNF-a/IL-1b (high vaccine
//   score >= 6); negative = low or negative vaccine score.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

secondarySignal  = detect cytokineDetection on detectionWell for 60s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Secondary screen QC gate (vaccine score).
// Pass (BB5): secondarySignal > 0 -> modulator has favourable
//             cytokine profile (vaccine score >= 6); proceed
//             to BMDC validation and in vivo testing.
// Fail (BB6): secondarySignal <= 0 -> unfavourable cytokine
//             profile; drain, do not advance to in vivo.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (secondaryJoin).
// ============================================================

if (secondarySignal > 0) {
    // BMDC validation (SI Appendix, Figure S10): lead modulators
    // confirmed in murine bone-marrow-derived dendritic cells
    // (BMDC) for cytokine production and cell surface markers
    // before in vivo dosing. Modelled as a dispose of the
    // validated detection well (off-chip transfer to BMDC plate).
    dispose detectionWell
} else {
    drain detectionWell
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: secondaryJoin
// Dispose secondary screen cytokine QC signal. Control falls
// into in vivo validation branch (BB8/BB9).
// ============================================================

dispose secondarySignal

// ============================================================
// BB7 -> PHASE 3: IN VIVO VACCINATION VALIDATION
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// IN VIVO STEP 1: Prime injection (Day 0).
//   C57/B6 mice injected i.m. with commercial vaccine +
//   lead modulator. DMSO:Addavax (1:1 v/v) vehicle used for
//   hydrophobic modulator formulation (1.5 umol/mouse).
//   Two commercial vaccine arms run in parallel:
//     (A) Fluzone 2021-2022 (inactivated quadrivalent flu)
//         + modulator -> IgG antibody titer readout at Day 49.
//     (B) Typhim-Vi (carbohydrate typhoid vaccine)
//         + modulator -> systemic TNF-a at 1 h post-injection.
//   Modelled as two parallel dispense-mix-heat sequences.
//   NOTE: in vivo `mix` abstracts i.m. injection co-formulation
//   (syringe admixture), not DMF electrode mixing.
//   NOTE: `heat` abstracts mouse body temperature (37 C) over
//   the prime-to-boost interval and antibody development window.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

vehicle          = dispense 1 units of DMSO_Vehicle
modFormulation   = dispense 1 units of Immunomodulator_DMSO_Stock
formulated       = mix modFormulation with vehicle for 5s

// Flu vaccine arm (Arm A): Fluzone + modulator formulation.
fluzoneVaccine   = dispense 1 units of Fluzone_Vaccine
fluzoneInjection = mix fluzoneVaccine with formulated for 5s

// Typhoid vaccine arm (Arm B): Typhim-Vi + modulator formulation.
typhimVaccine    = dispense 1 units of TyphimVi_Vaccine
typhimInjection  = mix typhimVaccine with formulated for 5s

// OVA subunit model arm: used for PME-564 initial in vivo
// characterisation (Fig. 5). OVA + R848/CpG/Flagellin +
// modulator. Provides mechanistic data before commercial
// vaccine experiments.
ovaAntigen       = dispense 1 units of OVA_Antigen
ovaAdjuvant      = dispense 1 units of PRR_Agonist_R848
ovaModMix        = mix ovaAntigen with ovaAdjuvant for 5s
ovaInjection     = mix ovaModMix with formulated for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// IN VIVO STEP 2: Early cytokine window (1 h post-prime).
//   Serum collected at 1 h post-injection for systemic
//   pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-a as primary readout;
//   correlates with reactogenicity and clinical tolerability).
//   Modelled as a detect on Typhim arm (primary readout arm
//   for cytokine reduction, paper Fig. 6C).
//   TNF-a measured by AlphaLISA or ELISA on serum samples.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// 1 h post-injection blood collection (= 60 min at 37 C body
// temperature modelling murine in vivo distribution).
heat typhimInjection at 37c for 60m

earlyTNF         = detect cytokineDetection on typhimInjection for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// IN VIVO STEP 3: Boost injection (Day 21).
//   Identical formulation to prime. Paper: traditional
//   prime-boost schedule (Day 0 prime, Day 21 boost).
//   Boost is modelled as re-heat at 37 C for the Day 0 to
//   Day 21 interval (= 21 days = 30240 min) followed by
//   re-mix to represent the boost injection formulation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Day 0 to Day 21 interval (= 30240 min).
heat fluzoneInjection at 37c for 30240m
heat ovaInjection     at 37c for 30240m

// Boost injection formulation (re-apply same modulator dose).
boostFormulation = dispense 1 units of Immunomodulator_DMSO_Stock
boostVehicle     = dispense 1 units of DMSO_Vehicle
boostFormMix     = mix boostFormulation with boostVehicle for 5s

fluzoneVaccine2  = dispense 1 units of Fluzone_Vaccine
fluzoneBoost     = mix fluzoneInjection with boostFormMix for 5s

ovaAdjuvant2     = dispense 1 units of PRR_Agonist_R848
ovaBoost         = mix ovaInjection with ovaAdjuvant2 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// IN VIVO STEP 4: Antibody titer window (Day 35 and Day 49).
//   Serum collected for antigen-specific IgG measurement.
//   Paper: anti-OVA IgG (Fig 5C) and anti-HA IgG against
//   A/Victoria/2570/2019 and B/Phuket/3073/2013 strains
//   (Fig 6D) measured by ELISA, log AUC reported.
//   Day 35 = 14 days post-boost (= 20160 min from boost).
//   Day 49 = 28 days post-boost (= 40320 min from boost).
//   Modelled as 28-day post-boost incubation then final
//   antibody detection at Day 49 (primary endpoint, paper).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Day 21 to Day 49 interval (= 28 days = 40320 min).
heat fluzoneBoost at 37c for 40320m
heat ovaBoost     at 37c for 40320m

// Antibody titer detection (anti-HA IgG for Fluzone arm;
// anti-OVA IgG for OVA arm). vaccinationQC abstracts
// ELISA AUC quantification. Positive = significant titer
// increase vs Fluzone/OVA+adjuvant without modulator.
// Paper: PME-564 + Fluzone -> ~4-fold log AUC increase
// vs Fluzone alone (Fig 6D, ****P<0.0001).
iggFluzone       = detect vaccinationQC on fluzoneBoost for 60s
iggOVA           = detect vaccinationQC on ovaBoost for 60s

// Combine vaccinationSignal: positive if either antibody arm
// shows improvement (modeller's union; keyed on Fluzone arm
// as primary commercial-vaccine endpoint).
vaccinationSignal = detect vaccinationQC on iggFluzone for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: In vivo vaccination QC gate.
// Pass (BB8): vaccinationSignal > 0 -> modulator improves
//             antigen-specific IgG titer AND/OR reduces
//             systemic TNF-a; compound validated as lead.
//             Dispose all vaccination samples.
// Fail (BB9): vaccinationSignal <= 0 -> no in vivo benefit;
//             drain all vaccination samples.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (vaccinationSignal > 0) {
    dispose fluzoneBoost
    dispose ovaBoost
    dispose typhimInjection
    dispose earlyTNF
    dispose iggFluzone
    dispose iggOVA
} else {
    drain fluzoneBoost
    drain ovaBoost
    drain typhimInjection
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all remaining QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends. Lead modulator identified and validated.
// ============================================================

dispose vaccinationSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module crpBindingDetection

manifest StreptavidinBeads
manifest BiotinAntiCRP
manifest AlexaCRP
manifest BSA_Block
manifest Buffer
manifest PBS

// ============================================================
// Massively Parallel Concentration Device for Multiplexed
// Immunoassays. Ko, Kim, Cheow, Li, Kang, Han.
// Lab Chip, 2011, 11, 1351-1358. DOI: 10.1039/c0lc00349b
//
// Platform: PDMS-glass nanofluidic concentration device with
// Nafion nanoporous junction. Microchannels: 50 um wide x
// 15 um deep. Device parallelizes up to 128 concentration
// channels from a single inlet/outlet reservoir pair.
// Voltage applied via Ag/AgCl electrodes (Keithley 6487).
//
// PROTOCOL OVERVIEW:
//   (1) Bead functionalization (off-chip, modelled on-chip):
//       streptavidin beads conjugated to biotin-anti-CRP
//       antibody. 18 h stirring, then 3x PBS wash.
//   (2) Device blocking with 1% BSA for 10 min.
//   (3) 3x buffer wash of channel before bead loading.
//   (4) Load functionalized beads into pillar-array traps.
//       Post-loading wash (3x) removes clogged non-trapped
//       beads from cathodic side.
//   (5) Fill channels with Alexa-488-labeled CRP in 10 mM
//       sodium phosphate dibasic buffer (pH 8.4).
//   (6) Electrokinetic concentration: 70 V for 30 min via
//       Nafion nanojunction. Ion concentration polarization
//       accumulates CRP plug at each channel. Serial
//       concentration achieved by varying inlet microchannel
//       length (ET = deltaV / length), giving 100-4000x
//       factors across ch#1 to ch#16.
//   (7) 3x buffer wash to remove unbound CRP.
//   (8) Fluorescence detection of Alexa-488 signal on beads.
//       LOD: 2.52 ng/mL (ch#16) vs 162.2 ng/mL (reference).
//       Dynamic range: 2874.60 vs 44.66 (ULD/LOD, Table 1).
//
// BIOSCRIPT MODELLING NOTES:
//
//   CONCENTRATION STEP: The electrokinetic concentration
//   event (70 V, 30 min, Nafion nanojunction) is modelled
//   as `heat ... at 25c for 1800s`. 25c = ambient room
//   temperature; no thermal component in the real step.
//   h duration token is unsupported; all durations use s/m.
//
//   SERIAL CONCENTRATION (16 CHANNELS): BioScript has no
//   native loop or array construct. Two representative
//   channels are encoded explicitly: ch16 (shortest inlet,
//   ~4000x concentration factor) and ch1 (longest inlet,
//   ~100x factor). These bracket the dynamic range endpoints
//   reported in Fig. 5b and Table 1. The QC gate keys on
//   ch16 (highest signal, lowest LOD = 2.52 ng/mL).
//
//   BEAD LOADING: `mix` abstracts pressure-driven bead
//   infusion into pillar-array traps. No electrode mixing
//   occurs at this step.
//
//   FLUID OWNERSHIP: Each variable is consumed exactly once.
//   Wash chains (pre-bead, post-loading, post-concentration)
//   are modelled as sequential mix+dispose pairs where each
//   wash step produces a new intermediate variable so that
//   the compiler can track distinct droplet instances. Fresh
//   buffer dispenses drive each wash cycle.
//
//   BEAD FUNCTIONALIZATION: 18 h stirring = 1080m.
//   3x PBS wash (centrifugation) modelled as sequential
//   mix+dispose pairs on the bead pellet re-dispensed after
//   each centrifuge step (no native magnetic-separation /
//   pellet primitive available; pellet re-modelled as fresh
//   dispense per established compiler convention).
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main             -- functionalization; blocking;
//                           bead loading; CRP concentration;
//                           post-wash; detection
//   BB2  bindingPass      -- bindingSignal > 0: ch16 CRP
//                           binding confirmed; dispose
//   BB3  bindingFail      -- bindingSignal <= 0: no ch16
//                           signal; drain
//   BB4  bindingJoin      -- join: ch1 QC branch begins
//   BB5  ch1Pass          -- ch1Signal > 0: low-conc end of
//                           dynamic range confirmed; dispose
//   BB6  ch1Fail          -- ch1Signal <= 0: no ch1 signal;
//                           drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup     -- terminal: dispose ch1 QC signal
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Bead functionalization (off-chip, modelled on-chip).
//   Streptavidin beads (Applied Biosystems, 6-8 um, 35e6
//   beads/mL) mixed with biotin-anti-CRP (Abcam ab86246)
//   and stirred 18 h at room temperature to allow
//   biotin-streptavidin conjugation.
//   18 h = 1080 min (h token unsupported in compiler).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beads              = dispense 1 units of StreptavidinBeads
antiCRP            = dispense 1 units of BiotinAntiCRP
functionalizedBeads = mix beads with antiCRP for 10s

// 18 h conjugation at room temperature (25 C).
heat functionalizedBeads at 25c for 1080m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Wash functionalized beads 3x with PBS.
//   Centrifugation removes excess unbound antibody.
//   After each centrifuge step the bead pellet is
//   re-suspended in fresh PBS. Pellet re-modelled as a
//   fresh StreptavidinBeads dispense after each wash
//   (no native pellet primitive in compiler).
//   Paper: "washed three times with 1x PBS."
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Wash 1: pellet from functionalization + PBS.
pbs_w1             = dispense 1 units of PBS
wash1Out           = mix functionalizedBeads with pbs_w1 for 5s
dispose wash1Out

// Wash 2: re-suspend pellet + fresh PBS.
pellet2            = dispense 1 units of StreptavidinBeads
pbs_w2             = dispense 1 units of PBS
wash2Out           = mix pellet2 with pbs_w2 for 5s
dispose wash2Out

// Wash 3: re-suspend pellet + fresh PBS.
pellet3            = dispense 1 units of StreptavidinBeads
pbs_w3             = dispense 1 units of PBS
wash3Out           = mix pellet3 with pbs_w3 for 5s
dispose wash3Out

// Tenfold dilution of washed bead stock before loading.
// Paper: "Finally the sample was diluted tenfold."
pellet4            = dispense 1 units of StreptavidinBeads
antiCRPFinal       = dispense 1 units of BiotinAntiCRP
reboundBeads       = mix pellet4 with antiCRPFinal for 5s
diluentPBS         = dispense 1 units of PBS
readyBeads         = mix reboundBeads with diluentPBS for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Device blocking with 1% BSA (10 min flush).
//   Flushed through channel before bead loading to passivate
//   PDMS walls and prevent nonspecific CRP adsorption.
//   Paper: "1% BSA was flushed through the channel for
//   10 min to prevent nonspecific binding."
//   Modelled as mix of BSA_Block with ready beads stock to
//   represent co-occupancy of the channel during blocking.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bsaBlock           = dispense 1 units of BSA_Block
blockingStep       = mix bsaBlock with readyBeads for 10s

// 10 min BSA blocking at room temperature.
heat blockingStep at 25c for 600s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Wash channel 3x with 10 mM buffer before loading.
//   Paper: "microchannels were then washed with buffer
//   solution (10 mM sodium phosphate dibasic) 3 times."
//   Sequential chain: each wash consumes prior output.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

buf_pre1           = dispense 1 units of Buffer
prewash1           = mix blockingStep with buf_pre1 for 5s

buf_pre2           = dispense 1 units of Buffer
prewash2           = mix prewash1 with buf_pre2 for 5s

buf_pre3           = dispense 1 units of Buffer
prewash3           = mix prewash2 with buf_pre3 for 5s
dispose prewash3

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Load functionalized beads into pillar-array traps.
//   Positive pressure at anodic reservoir pushes beads into
//   DRIE-fabricated pillar trap zone (100 um x 15 um).
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts pressure-driven flow-through bead
//   trapping; no DMF electrode mixing occurs here.
//   Paper: "Functionalized-beads were loaded into the
//   microchannels by applying positive pressure on the
//   anodic reservoir."
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beadsLoad          = dispense 1 units of StreptavidinBeads
antiCRPLoad        = dispense 1 units of BiotinAntiCRP
beadMix            = mix beadsLoad with antiCRPLoad for 5s

buf_loadCath       = dispense 1 units of Buffer
trappedBeads       = mix beadMix with buf_loadCath for 5s

// Post-loading cathodic wash (3x) removes clogged beads.
// Paper: "positive pressure should be subsequently applied
// at the cathodic reservoir to wash out the excess beads."
// Sequential chain: each wash step produces distinct var.
buf_cl1            = dispense 1 units of Buffer
clwash1            = mix trappedBeads with buf_cl1 for 5s

buf_cl2            = dispense 1 units of Buffer
clwash2            = mix clwash1 with buf_cl2 for 5s

buf_cl3            = dispense 1 units of Buffer
clwash3            = mix clwash2 with buf_cl3 for 5s
dispose clwash3

// Final wash before CRP loading (3x buffer flush).
// Paper: "microchannels were washed with sodium phosphate
// dibasic buffer three times again."
// Re-dispense bead pellet to model pellet re-suspension
// after cathodic wash (prior beadMix already consumed
// via trappedBeads chain above).
pellet5            = dispense 1 units of StreptavidinBeads
buf_final1         = dispense 1 units of Buffer
finalwash1         = mix pellet5 with buf_final1 for 5s

buf_final2         = dispense 1 units of Buffer
finalwash2         = mix finalwash1 with buf_final2 for 5s

buf_final3         = dispense 1 units of Buffer
finalwash3         = mix finalwash2 with buf_final3 for 5s
dispose finalwash3

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Fill channels with Alexa-488-labeled CRP solution.
//   10 mM sodium phosphate dibasic buffer (pH 8.4) spiked
//   with Alexa-488-labeled CRP (Invitrogen A10235 kit).
//   Representative concentration: 10 ng/mL (run3, mid-range
//   across run1-run5 in paper; 1 ug/mL, 100 ng/mL, 10 ng/mL,
//   1 ng/mL, 100 pg/mL). CRP solution loaded 30 min before
//   voltage is applied.
//   Paper: "10 mM sodium phosphate dibasic buffer spiked
//   with Alexa-488 labeled CRP ... filled into the
//   microchannels."
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Ch16 arm: independent CRP+buffer fill for high-ET channel.
crpCh16            = dispense 1 units of AlexaCRP
buf_ch16           = dispense 1 units of Buffer
fillCh16           = mix crpCh16 with buf_ch16 for 5s

// Ch1 arm: independent CRP+buffer fill for low-ET channel.
crpCh1             = dispense 1 units of AlexaCRP
buf_ch1            = dispense 1 units of Buffer
fillCh1            = mix crpCh1 with buf_ch1 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Electrokinetic concentration - Channel 16 (ch16).
//   70 V dc applied across Ag/AgCl electrodes for 30 min.
//   Nafion nanojunction generates ion depletion zone; CRP
//   plug accumulates on anodic side of each microchannel.
//   Ch16 has the shortest inlet microchannel -> highest ET
//   -> ~4000x concentration factor (Fig. 3c, paper).
//   Modelled as heat at 25c for 1800s (ambient operation;
//   no thermal component; `h` token unsupported).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat fillCh16 at 25c for 1800s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Electrokinetic concentration - Channel 1 (ch1).
//   Same voltage and duration (70 V, 30 min). Ch1 has the
//   longest inlet microchannel -> lowest ET -> ~100x factor
//   (dynamic range low end; Fig. 3c, paper).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat fillCh1 at 25c for 1800s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Wash out unbound CRP (3x buffer flush per channel).
//   Applied after concentration to remove bulk CRP before
//   fluorescence readout on beads.
//   Paper: "the sample containing Alexa-488 labeled CRP
//   was removed by flushing sodium phosphate dibasic
//   buffer solution through the device."
//   Sequential chain per channel arm (ch16 then ch1).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Ch16 post-concentration wash.
buf_pw16a          = dispense 1 units of Buffer
pwash16a           = mix fillCh16 with buf_pw16a for 5s

buf_pw16b          = dispense 1 units of Buffer
pwash16b           = mix pwash16a with buf_pw16b for 5s

buf_pw16c          = dispense 1 units of Buffer
pwash16c           = mix pwash16b with buf_pw16c for 5s
dispose pwash16c

// Ch1 post-concentration wash.
buf_pw1a           = dispense 1 units of Buffer
pwash1a            = mix fillCh1 with buf_pw1a for 5s

buf_pw1b           = dispense 1 units of Buffer
pwash1b            = mix pwash1a with buf_pw1b for 5s

buf_pw1c           = dispense 1 units of Buffer
pwash1c            = mix pwash1b with buf_pw1c for 5s
dispose pwash1c

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Fluorescence detection of bead-bound CRP.
//   crpBindingDetection module abstracts the inverted
//   fluorescence microscope readout (Olympus IX-51 +
//   SensiCam CCD; IPLab 3.6 analysis). Alexa-488 emission
//   intensity on trapped beads is proportional to bound CRP.
//   Detection applied to the final washed-channel droplet
//   for each arm (post-wash outputs re-dispensed as fresh
//   channel fill droplets; the detect op reads bead signal
//   in situ rather than consuming a mobile droplet, modelled
//   here by detecting on the independently filled channel
//   arm droplets after wash chain completion).
//   QC gate uses ch16 signal (LOD 2.52 ng/mL; highest sens).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// NOTE: detect operates on the post-concentration, post-wash
// channel fill. Because fillCh16 / fillCh1 were consumed
// by the wash chains above, fresh detect targets are the
// independently filled channel volumes re-introduced here.
// This reflects the physical reality that the bead signal is
// read from the bead-laden channel area, not from a mobile
// droplet removed to a detection zone.

detectCh16         = dispense 1 units of AlexaCRP
detectCh1          = dispense 1 units of AlexaCRP

bindingSignal      = detect crpBindingDetection on detectCh16 for 30s
ch1Signal          = detect crpBindingDetection on detectCh1 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: CRP binding QC gate (ch16 - ~4000x factor)
// Pass (BB2): bindingSignal > 0 -> Alexa-488 fluorescence
//             above background (40-50 AU) detected on beads;
//             CRP binding confirmed at high-conc end.
//             Dispose ch16 detect volume.
// Fail (BB3): bindingSignal <= 0 -> no fluorescence above
//             background; concentration or binding failed.
//             Drain ch16 detect volume.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (bindingJoin).
// ============================================================

if (bindingSignal > 0) {
    dispose detectCh16
} else {
    drain detectCh16
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: bindingJoin
// Dispose ch16 QC signal. Control falls into ch1 QC branch
// (BB5/BB6) to confirm low-concentration end of dynamic range.
// ============================================================

dispose bindingSignal

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: CRP binding QC gate (ch1 - ~100x factor)
// Pass (BB5): ch1Signal > 0 -> weak but detectable Alexa-488
//             signal above background (signals 56-66 AU;
//             background 40-50 AU; Fig. 5a, paper).
//             Dynamic range low end confirmed. Dispose ch1.
// Fail (BB6): ch1Signal <= 0 -> no detectable signal at ch1;
//             drain ch1 detect volume.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (ch1Signal > 0) {
    dispose detectCh1
} else {
    drain detectCh1
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose ch1 QC signal droplet. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose ch1Signal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module icpBoundaryDetection
module concentrationDetection
module cellCountDetection

manifest FluoresceinSodiumSalt
manifest rGFP
manifest FITC_Lectin_pH87
manifest FITC_Lectin_pH74
manifest RedBloodCells
manifest EColi_ER2738
manifest Phosphate_Buffer_1mM_pH87
manifest Phosphate_Buffer_1mM_pH74
manifest PBS_150mM
manifest PluronicF108
manifest BSA_Solution
manifest LB_Tet_Medium
manifest NafionMembrane

// ============================================================
// Continuous-Flow Biomolecule and Cell Concentrator by Ion
// Concentration Polarization
// Kwak, Kim, Han -- Anal. Chem. 2011, 83, 7348-7355
// DOI: 10.1021/ac2012619
//
// Device: PDMS microfluidic chip with Nafion nanojunction
//   (cation-selective membrane) patterned at 30 deg angle
//   along the concentrated channel. Main channel bifurcates
//   into a narrow concentrated channel and a wider filtered
//   channel.
//
// Principle: ICP (ion concentration polarization) generates a
//   depletion zone near the Nafion junction. Charged biomolecules
//   and cells are blocked by the depletion force and guided
//   hydrodynamically into the concentrated channel, achieving
//   continuous-flow concentration without trapping (unlike
//   batch-type preconcentrators).
//
// Concentration factor r = 1 + (R1 / R2), where R1 and R2 are
//   hydrodynamic resistances of the concentrated and filtered
//   channels respectively. Controlled by width ratio
//   w' = (w1+w2)/w1 (tested: 10, 20, 50, 100) and length ratio
//   l' = L1/L2 (tested: 0.3, 0.7, 2). Factors from 1 to ~200.
//
// Operating conditions: 0-150 V applied voltage, 0-5 uL/min
//   flow rate (syringe pump). Max voltage capped at 150 V/cm
//   (~1000x below PDMS breakdown voltage of ~25 V/um).
//
// NOTE ON BioScript ABSTRACTION:
//   ICP is an electrokinetic phenomenon in a continuous-flow
//   microchannel. `mix` steps below abstract the hydrodynamic
//   merging of the sample stream with the ICP depletion zone
//   boundary at the Nafion junction, driving concentration.
//   `heat` steps model TEC/oven incubations where applicable.
//   `detect` abstracts the epifluorescence CCD readout
//   (Olympus IX-71, Hamamatsu CCD, ImageJ or hemocytometer
//   for cells).
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                 -- device priming, fluorescein/rGFP
//                                preconcentration demonstration,
//                                ionic strength characterisation
//   BB2  concentrationPass    -- concSignal > 0: valid concentration
//                                enhancement detected; dispose
//   BB3  concentrationFail    -- concSignal <= 0: ICP boundary
//                                unstable or no signal; drain
//   BB4  concentrationJoin    -- join: polarity assay branch begins
//   BB5  polarityPass         -- polaritySignal > 0: lectin
//                                concentrated successfully; dispose
//   BB6  polarityFail         -- polaritySignal <= 0: nonspecific
//                                binding loss or instability; drain
//   BB7  polarityJoin         -- join: cell concentration branch begins
//   BB8  cellPass             -- cellSignal > 0: RBC/E.coli density
//                                increase confirmed; dispose
//   BB9  cellFail             -- cellSignal <= 0: concentration
//                                not achieved; drain
//   BB10 finalCleanup         -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Prepare sample solution.
//   3 pM fluorescein sodium salt (Sigma-Aldrich) in 1 mM
//   dibasic sodium phosphate buffer at pH 8.7.
//   3 pM rGFP (BD Bioscience) prepared in the same buffer.
//   Both are negatively charged at pH 8.7 and trackable by
//   epifluorescence (ex/em: 490/514 nm for fluorescein;
//   488/507 nm for GFP).
//   NOTE: fluorescein and rGFP are run in separate experiments;
//   this file encodes the fluorescein arm as the primary
//   demonstration. rGFP shares identical protocol parameters.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sampleFluorescein = dispense 1 units of FluoresceinSodiumSalt
bufferMain        = dispense 1 units of Phosphate_Buffer_1mM_pH87

sampleMix = mix sampleFluorescein with bufferMain for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Prime device with buffer.
//   Flow 1 mM phosphate buffer (pH 8.7) through the main
//   channel and buffer channel to equilibrate the Nafion
//   nanojunction and eliminate air bubbles.
//   Flow rate: ~2 uL/min (syringe pump, Harvard PHD 2200).
//   Duration: 2 min (sufficient to fill and equilibrate a
//   1 cm inlet-to-bifurcation channel at 2 uL/min).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primingBuffer = dispense 1 units of Phosphate_Buffer_1mM_pH87

// `mix` abstracts buffer flushing through the microchannel
// to equilibrate the Nafion nanojunction before voltage is
// applied. No chemical reaction occurs.
primedDevice = mix primingBuffer with sampleMix for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Apply electric field and hydrodynamic pressure to
//   initiate ICP-based continuous-flow concentration.
//   Applied voltage: 80 V (for w'=50, l'=0.7 demonstration).
//   Flow rate: 5 uL/min (syringe pump).
//   The ICP depletion zone forms along the Nafion junction
//   at 30 deg, blocking charged fluorescein from entering
//   the filtered channel and directing it into the 20 um-wide
//   concentrated channel continuously.
//   Steady state achieved within the first 30 min of operation;
//   device operated stably for >30 min without ICP boundary
//   compromise.
//   `mix` abstracts the continuous-flow concentration event
//   (electrokinetic + hydrodynamic force balance at junction).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// `heat` at 25c models room-temperature steady-state operation
// during voltage application (no thermal step in paper;
// used here to anchor the 30 min concentration window).
heat primedDevice at 25c for 30m

concentratedFlow = mix primedDevice with bufferMain for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Detect concentration enhancement.
//   Fluorescence intensity measured by inverted epifluorescence
//   microscope (Olympus IX-71) with thermoelectrically cooled
//   CCD (Hamamatsu). Internal reference: known concentrations
//   (0.03, 0.3, 3, 30 pM; 0.3, 3, 30 nM) for calibration.
//   Concentration factor measured by comparing fluorescence
//   intensity of concentrated channel to sample flow.
//   Expected result: ~50-fold enhancement at w'=50, l'=0.7
//   (rtheoretical=62; experimental ~50-fold for fluorescein).
//   QC gate: concSignal > 0 if fluorescence intensity in
//   concentrated channel exceeds sample flow baseline.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

concSignal = detect concentrationDetection on concentratedFlow for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Concentration QC gate
// Pass (BB2): concSignal > 0 -> valid concentration enhancement
//             detected (concentrated channel fluorescence above
//             sample baseline); dispose and proceed.
// Fail (BB3): concSignal <= 0 -> ICP boundary unstable or
//             no enhancement; drain (high ionic strength or
//             voltage instability regime).
// Both branches converge at BB4 (concentrationJoin).
// ============================================================

if (concSignal > 0) {
    dispose concentratedFlow
} else {
    drain concentratedFlow
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: concentrationJoin
// Dispose concentration QC signal. Control falls into the
// polarity assay branch (BB5/BB6) for FITC-lectin tests.
// ============================================================

dispose concSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Prepare FITC-lectin samples for polarity assay.
//   FITC-conjugated lectin from Lens culinaris (lentil),
//   MW ~49 kDa, pI ~8.0-8.8 (Sigma-Aldrich, 5 ug/mL).
//
//   Condition A (negatively charged): 1 mM phosphate pH 8.7.
//     Zeta potential: -18.8 mV (-1.464e-8 m2/V.s).
//     Lectin is above pI -> net negative charge.
//   Condition B (positively charged): 1 mM phosphate pH 7.4.
//     Zeta potential: +3.35 mV (+0.263e-8 m2/V.s).
//     Lectin is below pI -> net positive charge.
//   0.1% BSA added to pH 7.4 buffer to prevent nonspecific
//   electrostatic binding to negatively charged PDMS/Nafion.
//
//   Three concentration factors tested: 12-fold (w'=10,
//   l'=0.7, w1=20 um), 46-fold (w'=20, l'=0.7, w1=10 um),
//   130-fold (w'=50, l'=0.7, w1=10 um).
//   Flow rate: 1 uL/min; height: 15 um; voltage: 0-150 V.
//   This step encodes the 46-fold (w'=20) condition as
//   representative for both pH arms.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lectinNeg  = dispense 1 units of FITC_Lectin_pH87
lectinPos  = dispense 1 units of FITC_Lectin_pH74
bsaBuffer  = dispense 1 units of BSA_Solution
bufferPH74 = dispense 1 units of Phosphate_Buffer_1mM_pH74

// Positive lectin sample prepared with BSA to prevent
// nonspecific binding (paper Section 2.2, Fig. 4).
lectinPosMix = mix lectinPos with bsaBuffer for 5s

// Negative lectin run directly in pH 8.7 buffer.
lectinNegMix = mix lectinNeg with bufferMain for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: ICP concentration of FITC-lectin (both polarities).
//   Voltage adjusted 0-150 V to generate stable ICP boundary
//   at 1 uL/min flow rate and 15 um channel height.
//   Concentrated flow velocity vc = (Q1+Q2)/(r_theoretical *
//   w1 * h1) used to quantify nonspecific binding risk
//   (lower vc -> higher nonspecific binding for positive lectin).
//
//   Key finding: positive lectin (pH 7.4) shows larger drop
//   from theoretical concentration factor than negative lectin
//   (pH 8.7) due to electrostatic nonspecific binding to
//   negatively charged PDMS/Nafion walls, especially at
//   slow concentrated flow velocities (130-fold condition).
//   `mix` abstracts continuous-flow concentration event.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

concentratedLectinNeg = mix lectinNegMix with bufferMain for 30m
concentratedLectinPos = mix lectinPosMix with bufferPH74 for 30m

// Fluorescence internal references: 0.5, 5, 50, 100, 500 ug/mL
// FITC-lectin used for calibration (paper M&M Section).
polaritySignal = detect concentrationDetection on concentratedLectinNeg for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Polarity QC gate
// Pass (BB5): polaritySignal > 0 -> FITC-lectin concentration
//             enhancement confirmed for at least the negative
//             (pH 8.7) condition; dispose both concentrated flows.
// Fail (BB6): polaritySignal <= 0 -> no concentration signal
//             (BSA suppression failure or ICP instability);
//             drain both.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (polarityJoin).
// ============================================================

if (polaritySignal > 0) {
    dispose concentratedLectinNeg
    dispose concentratedLectinPos
} else {
    drain concentratedLectinNeg
    drain concentratedLectinPos
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: polarityJoin
// Dispose polarity QC signal. Control falls into the cell
// concentration branch (BB8/BB9).
// ============================================================

dispose polaritySignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Prepare cell samples for cell preconcentration demo.
//   Red Blood Cells (RBCs): human whole blood, 2000x dilution
//   (Hoechst, Innovative Research) in 1x PBS (~150 mM).
//   0.2% Pluronic F108 added to prevent osmosis hemolysis,
//   nonspecific binding, and aggregation.
//   Cell Tracker Orange CMRA (3 uM, Molecular Probes) used
//   for fluorescence tracking in supporting information video.
//   Zeta potential of RBCs: -13.3 mV (Zetasizer nano ZS).
//
//   E. coli ER2738 (New England BioLabs): cultured in LB-Tet
//   medium (50 mL LB + 50 uL Tet, 37 C, 24 h). Mixed 1:10
//   with RBCs in 1x PBS + LB-Tet (final ~168 mM ionic strength).
//   Zeta potential of E. coli: -15.5 mV.
//
//   Device: w'=10, l'=1.2; 20-fold concentrator.
//   RBC+E.coli co-concentration experiment (Fig. 6).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rbcSample    = dispense 1 units of RedBloodCells
ecoliSample  = dispense 1 units of EColi_ER2738
pbsBuffer    = dispense 1 units of PBS_150mM
pluronicF108 = dispense 1 units of PluronicF108
lbTetMedium  = dispense 1 units of LB_Tet_Medium

// Prepare RBC suspension with Pluronic F108.
rbcBuffer   = mix pbsBuffer with pluronicF108 for 5s
rbcMix      = mix rbcSample with rbcBuffer for 5s

// Prepare E. coli in LB-Tet mixed buffer (1x PBS + LB-Tet).
ecoliBuffer = mix pbsBuffer with lbTetMedium for 5s
ecoliMix    = mix ecoliSample with ecoliBuffer for 5s

// Co-mix RBCs and E. coli at 1:10 volume ratio
// (paper: mixed buffer ~168 mM ionic strength).
cellMix = mix rbcMix with ecoliMix for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: ICP-based continuous-flow cell concentration.
//   Applied voltage: 14 V; flow rate: 0.1 uL/min (Fig. 6).
//   (Separate RBC-only run: 90 V, 5 uL/min, Fig. 5.)
//   Both RBCs and E. coli are negatively charged -> blocked
//   by ICP depletion zone -> guided into concentrated channel.
//   Leakage rate to filtered flow: 0% during 120 s observation
//   (live imaging, paper Section Results & Discussion).
//
//   Cell density quantification:
//     RBCs: hemocytometer (SKC Inc.) for sample and filtered
//           channels; ImageJ phase contrast cell counting for
//           concentrated channel (due to low output volume
//           ~0.05 uL/min).
//     E. coli: ImageJ phase contrast counting.
//   Expected result: RBC density 7.3e5 -> 1.4e7 cells/mL (19x);
//     E. coli density 3.2e7 -> 6.5e8 cells/mL (20x). Both
//     match theoretical 20-fold concentration factor. (Fig. 6)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// `heat` at 25c models room-temperature continuous-flow
// operation during ICP voltage application for cell concentration.
heat cellMix at 25c for 2m

concentratedCells = mix cellMix with pbsBuffer for 30m

cellSignal = detect cellCountDetection on concentratedCells for 120s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Cell concentration QC gate
// Pass (BB8): cellSignal > 0 -> cell density increase confirmed
//             in concentrated outlet (RBC: ~19-fold;
//             E. coli: ~20-fold by ImageJ counting); dispose.
// Fail (BB9): cellSignal <= 0 -> no density difference detected
//             (ICP unstable at 168 mM ionic strength or
//             voltage too low); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (cellSignal > 0) {
    dispose concentratedCells
} else {
    drain concentratedCells
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose cell count QC signal. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose cellSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module cellRetentionDetection
module igG1Quantification
module cellViabilityDetection

manifest CHO_DG44_Cells
manifest CD_OptiCHO_Medium
manifest FreshMedium
manifest PBS
manifest PluronicF127
manifest TrypanBlueExclusion
manifest IgG1_Reference_Standard

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Microfluidic Cell Retention Device for Perfusion of
// Mammalian Suspension Culture
// Kwon, Prentice, De Oliveira, Madziva, Warkiani, Hamel,
// Han -- Scientific Reports 7:6703, 2017.
// DOI: 10.1038/s41598-017-06949-8
//
// Protocol: Inertial microfluidic spiral cell retention
// device for continuous perfusion culture of CHO-DG44
// cells producing human IgG1 against CD40 ligand.
// Device: PDMS spiral microchannel (600 um width,
// 80 um inner depth, 130 um outer depth, 8 loops,
// 28 uL volume). Four spiral channels combined in parallel.
// Cell retention principle: inertial focusing (F_L and F_D)
// focuses CHO cells (17.7 +/- 2.5 um diameter) near the
// inner wall -> retained via inner outlet (retentate).
// Cell-limited harvest exits via outer outlet (permeate).
// Perfusion bioreactor: 350 mL spinner flask, 2 VVD.
// Run duration: 18-25 days per bioreactor culture.
//
// NOTE ON FLOW ABSTRACTION: `mix` steps involving
// CHO_DG44_Cells model a continuous-flow peristaltic pump
// recirculation event through the spiral microchannel at
// 1 mL/min (single chip, <1.5 mL/min optimal). This is a
// BioScript abstraction for a channel-based inertial sorting
// event, not a bulk mixing step.
// Cell retention efficiency R(%) = (X_I - X_OO)/X_I x 100.
// Target: >99% at <15e6 cells/mL; ~82% at 20-23e6 cells/mL.
//
// NOTE ON IgG1 RECOVERY: Since the spiral device has no
// micro- or nanoporous membrane, IgG1 passes freely into the
// harvest stream (outer outlet). Recovery efficiency
// AR(%) = X_OO/X_I x 100 -> ~100% after Day 2 adsorption
// equilibration to tubing and fluidic adaptors.
//
// NOTE ON TEMPERATURE: All cell culture incubation steps
// run at 37 C / 5% CO2. BioScript `heat` statements model
// CO2 incubator dwell and peristaltic pump operation.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                 -- entry: device priming,
//                               batch growth phase (4 days),
//                               perfusion initiation at 2 VVD
//   BB2  retentionPass        -- retentionSignal > 0: cell
//                               retention confirmed (R > 80%);
//                               continue perfusion sampling
//   BB3  retentionFail        -- retentionSignal <= 0: retention
//                               failure (clog or flow loss); drain
//   BB4  retentionJoin        -- join: IgG1 QC branch begins
//   BB5  igG1Pass             -- igG1Signal > 0: IgG1 detected
//                               in harvest; dispose and continue
//   BB6  igG1Fail             -- igG1Signal <= 0: product loss;
//                               drain harvest sample
//   BB7  igG1Join             -- join: viability branch begins
//   BB8  viabilityPass        -- viabilitySignal > 0: viability
//                               >97%; dispose and end
//   BB9  viabilityFail        -- viabilitySignal <= 0: viability
//                               drop (<97%); drain and end
//   BB10 finalCleanup         -- terminal: dispose QC signals
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Prime the spiral microchannel device with 2%
//         Pluronic F-127 in PBS to reduce cell adhesion to
//         PDMS surfaces and equilibrate the fluidic adaptors.
//         Channel sterilised prior with 10% bleach (10 min)
//         then 70% ethanol (10 min) via peristaltic pump.
//         Device connected to sidearm ports of spinner flask
//         and harvest bottle inside a biosafety cabinet.
//         NOTE: Pluronic priming is modelled as a 5-minute
//         mix (channel equilibration dwell time).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primingPBS      = dispense 1 units of PBS
primingPluronic = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127
primedChannel   = mix primingPBS with primingPluronic for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Batch growth phase (Days 0-4, no perfusion).
//         CHO-DG44 cells inoculated at 0.3-0.4e6 cells/mL
//         into 350 mL spinner flask containing pre-warmed
//         CD OptiCHO medium. Stirring at 45 RPM.
//         Cells grown in batch mode for 4 days to establish
//         a viable exponential culture before initiating
//         perfusion. pH (7.0) and DO (40% air saturation)
//         controlled via BioFlo/CelliGen 115 or BIOSTAT A
//         bioreactor controllers.
//         400 mL pre-warmed medium transferred to flask.
//         Incubation at 37 C / 5% CO2 for 4 days (5760 min).
//
//         NOTE ON DURATION TOKENS: BioScript supports s and
//         m only. 4 days = 5760 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

choCells     = dispense 1 units of CHO_DG44_Cells
batchMedium  = dispense 1 units of CD_OptiCHO_Medium
batchCulture = mix choCells with batchMedium for 5760m

heat batchCulture at 37c for 5760m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Initiate perfusion at 2 VVD (vessel volumes per
//         day) on Day 4. Fresh CD OptiCHO medium supplied
//         continuously to bioreactor via peristaltic pump.
//         Cell culture continuously recycled through the
//         4-spiral microfluidic device at 1 mL/min (single
//         chip) via a second peristaltic pump.
//         98.4% +/- 1.1% of total cells recycled back to
//         bioreactor via inner outlet (retentate).
//         Harvest solution (IgG1-containing) collected from
//         outer outlet (permeate). Harvest bottle replaced
//         daily. Working volume maintained at ~350 mL by
//         customised software (bioreactor weight control).
//         NOTE: `mix` here abstracts the spiral channel
//         inertial sorting recirculation event, not a DMF
//         electrode step. Flow rate: 1 mL/min input;
//         fluidic resistance ratio R_OO/R_IO > 2.7 set by
//         tubing diameter (510 um ID) and length.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

freshMedium      = dispense 1 units of FreshMedium
perfusionCulture = mix batchCulture with freshMedium for 10m

// Recirculate culture through spiral channel at 37 C.
// Models 18-25 day perfusion dwell at 2 VVD.
// 18 days = 25920 min (minimum run duration, Run 1).
heat perfusionCulture at 37c for 25920m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Cell retention efficiency measurement (daily QC).
//         Samples collected from bioreactor (inner outlet)
//         and harvest stream (outer outlet).
//         Cell concentration measured via BioProfile CDV
//         Analyzer (Nova Biomedical). Three aliquots per
//         sample. Max deviation 3.8% +/- 2.9% from mean.
//         R(%) = (X_I - X_OO) / X_I x 100.
//         Expected: >99% at <15e6 cells/mL;
//                   ~82% at 20-23e6 cells/mL (peak density).
//         cellRetentionDetection module abstracts the
//         automated BioProfile CDV cell concentration readout
//         comparing inner outlet vs outer outlet samples.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bioreactorSample = mix perfusionCulture with primedChannel for 2s
retentionSignal  = detect cellRetentionDetection on bioreactorSample for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Cell retention QC gate
// Pass (BB2): retentionSignal > 0 -> R > 80% confirmed,
//             device operating within spec; dispose sample
//             and continue to IgG1 branch.
// Fail (BB3): retentionSignal <= 0 -> retention failure
//             (device clogged or flow disrupted); drain
//             bioreactor sample.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (retentionJoin).
// ============================================================

if (retentionSignal > 0) {
    dispose bioreactorSample
} else {
    drain bioreactorSample
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: retentionJoin
// Dispose retention QC signal. Control falls into
// IgG1 product recovery branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose retentionSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: IgG1 product recovery measurement (daily QC).
//         IgG1 concentrations measured from bioreactor
//         (feed, X_I) and harvest stream outer outlet
//         (permeate, X_OO) by HPLC affinity chromatography
//         (Applied Biosystems 2-1001-00 column, Agilent 1100
//         Series system). Equilibration buffer: 50 mM
//         phosphate + 150 mM NaCl, pH 7.0. Elution buffer:
//         100 mM phosphate + 0.4% (v/v) phosphoric acid,
//         pH 2.5. Reference: purified IgG1-Kappa from human
//         myeloma plasma (Sigma I5154).
//         AR(%) = X_OO / X_I x 100.
//         Expected: ~80% on Days 1-2 (adsorption to tubing);
//                   ~100% from Day 3 onward.
//         Peak IgG1 in bioreactor: ~40 ug/mL after Day 9.
//         Total IgG1 yield over 18-day run: 263 mg.
//         Production rate from Day 9: 26.8 mg/day.
//         igG1Quantification module abstracts the HPLC
//         affinity chromatography antibody concentration
//         measurement on both bioreactor and harvest samples.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

harvestSample = dispense 1 units of IgG1_Reference_Standard
igG1Signal    = detect igG1Quantification on harvestSample for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: IgG1 product recovery QC gate
// Pass (BB5): igG1Signal > 0 -> IgG1 detected in harvest
//             stream at expected concentration (~100% AR);
//             dispose harvest sample and continue.
// Fail (BB6): igG1Signal <= 0 -> product loss or no signal
//             (possible retention in tubing); drain sample.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (igG1Join).
// ============================================================

if (igG1Signal > 0) {
    dispose harvestSample
} else {
    drain harvestSample
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: igG1Join
// Dispose IgG1 QC signal. Control falls into cell
// viability branch (BB8/BB9).
// ============================================================

dispose igG1Signal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Cell viability assessment (daily QC).
//         CHO cells from bioreactor sample spread in single
//         layer on chamber slide; live cells marked by trypan
//         blue dye exclusion. 40 images from 40 different
//         fields of view per measurement. Cell diameter
//         determined by pixel distance; concentration by
//         counting (6,000 cells -> 5e6 cells/mL;
//         12,000 cells -> 10e6 cells/mL as calibrants).
//         Measurement by BioProfile FLEX and CDV Analyzers
//         (Nova Biomedical).
//         Long-term biocompatibility confirmed: CHO cells
//         processed continuously for >145 h at 1 mL/min
//         with average retention efficiency 98% (1.6% range)
//         and NO significant decrease in viability (n=3).
//         Target viability: >97% throughout perfusion culture
//         (99% +/- 1%, mean +/- s.d., n=9 after Day 10).
//         cellViabilityDetection module abstracts the trypan
//         blue exclusion viability readout.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

viabilitySample  = dispense 1 units of CHO_DG44_Cells
trypanStain      = dispense 1 units of TrypanBlueExclusion
stainedViability = mix viabilitySample with trypanStain for 2m

viabilitySignal  = detect cellViabilityDetection on stainedViability for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Cell viability QC gate
// Pass (BB8): viabilitySignal > 0 -> cell viability >97%,
//             biocompatibility confirmed; dispose stained
//             sample and end experiment successfully.
// Fail (BB9): viabilitySignal <= 0 -> viability drop below
//             threshold (possible shear damage or device
//             fouling); drain stained sample and end.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (viabilitySignal > 0) {
    dispose stainedViability
} else {
    drain stainedViability
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose viability QC signal; experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose viabilitySignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module massSpecDetection
module fluorescenceDetection
module doeElutionModel

manifest C18_MagneticBeads
manifest Acetonitrile
manifest Methanol
manifest Water
manifest TFA_Solution
manifest Fluorescein_Sample
manifest Sitamaquine_Sample
manifest AngiotensinI_Sample
manifest Sitamaquine_AngiotensinI_Mix
manifest DBS_Extract
manifest InternalStandard

// ============================================================
// Attractive Design: An Elution Solvent Optimization Platform
// for Magnetic-Bead-based Fractionation Using Digital
// Microfluidics and Design of Experiments.
// Lafreniere et al., Anal. Chem. 2015, 87, 3902-3910.
// DOI: 10.1021/ac504697r
//
// This BioScript file encodes the full protocol including:
//   (1) DMF magnetic bead SPE (six-step method)
//   (2) Loading capacity characterisation with fluorescein
//   (3) DOE-guided elution solvent optimisation for
//       sitamaquine (small molecule) and angiotensin I (peptide)
//   (4) Two-fraction sample fractionation
//   (5) Dried blood spot (DBS) extraction and cleanup
//
// DEVICE: Bottom plate: 15x4 array of 2x2 mm driving electrodes
//   + 8 reservoir electrodes (16x6 mm). Top plate: ITO/Teflon AF.
//   Gap: 270 um (3 layers double-sided tape). Actuation: 100-120
//   V_rms, 10 kHz. Magnetic lens: motorised vertical step-motor
//   for reversible bead immobilisation.
//
// SIX-STEP SPE METHOD (each step = three sub-operations):
//   (i)  Engulf: dispense droplet, drive to bead bed
//   (ii) Mix:    suspend beads, mix under DMF actuation
//  (iii) Remove: engage magnet, drive supernatant away
//   Steps:
//     1 - Dispense bead bed (10 uL, ~9e7 beads, 5 mg/mL)
//     2 - Activate C18 with acetonitrile (10 uL)
//     3 - Wash with water (10 uL)
//     4 - Load sample and incubate (10 uL, 5 min)
//     5 - Wash twice with water (2 x 10 uL)
//     6 - Elute with optimised solvent (10 uL)
//   Total run time: ~12 min. Throughput: 15 samples/h (3 parallel).
//
// NOTE ON `mix` ABSTRACTION: All `mix` calls model the full
//   engage-magnet / engulf / mix / disengage / remove-supernatant
//   sequence for a given SPE step. The magnetic lens toggling is
//   intrinsic to the DMF instrument and is not separately encoded.
//
// NOTE ON PLURONICS: Pluronic F-127 (common DMF antifouling
//   additive) was found to be INCOMPATIBLE with this protocol
//   (micellar encapsulation of hydrophobic analytes and/or
//   coating of C18 bead surfaces). Pluronics are NOT used
//   anywhere in this file. See paper Section "DMF-Magnetic
//   Bead-SPE", first paragraph.
//
// NOTE ON SOLVENT MIXING: On-chip solvent composition uses
//   unit droplets (2.5 uL, one electrode). Mixtures are formed
//   by dispensing and combining unit droplets from reservoir
//   electrodes containing neat Water, Acetonitrile, Methanol.
//   `mix` steps below represent this automated on-chip blending.
//
// DOE DESIGN: Simplex-centroid design; 7 solvent combinations:
//   neat ACN, neat MeOH, neat H2O, 1:1 ACN:MeOH,
//   1:1 ACN:H2O, 1:1 MeOH:H2O, 1:1:1 ACN:MeOH:H2O.
//   Data fit to three-component special cubic model (eq 2).
//   Optimal solvent: 100% MeOH for sitamaquine;
//                    1:1 ACN:H2O for angiotensin I.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                  -- bead dispensing, C18 activation,
//                                 loading capacity QC
//   BB2  loadingPass           -- loadingSignal > 0: acceptable
//                                 loading (<10% unbound); proceed
//   BB3  loadingFail           -- loadingSignal <= 0: beads saturated
//                                 (>80% unbound at >=10 mg/mL); drain
//   BB4  loadingJoin           -- join: DOE solvent optimisation begins
//   BB5  doeOptPass            -- doeSignal > 0: model converged,
//                                 optimal solvent identified; proceed
//   BB6  doeOptFail            -- doeSignal <= 0: DOE inconclusive; drain
//   BB7  doeJoin               -- join: sample fractionation begins
//   BB8  fractionationPass     -- fractionSignal > 0: fractionation
//                                 successful (<10% coelution); proceed
//   BB9  fractionationFail     -- fractionSignal <= 0: excessive
//                                 coelution (>10%); drain
//   BB10 fractionationJoin     -- join: DBS extraction and cleanup
//   BB11 dbsCleanupPass        -- dbsSignal > 0: cleanup improved
//                                 sitamaquine signal; dispose
//   BB12 dbsCleanupFail        -- dbsSignal <= 0: no signal improvement;
//                                 drain
//   BB13 finalCleanup          -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Dispense C18 magnetic bead bed.
//   10 uL of bead suspension (5 mg/mL, ~9e7 beads in ~9e-5 cm3).
//   Beads pipetted onto device; magnetic lens engaged to form
//   the bead bed on the DMF device surface.
//   Sorbent bed size is tunable on-the-fly (paper Fig. 2B):
//   6 uL -> ~5.4e7 beads; 12 uL -> ~1.08e8 beads.
//   This protocol uses the standard 10 uL (9e7 beads) bed.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beadBed = dispense 1 units of C18_MagneticBeads

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Activate C18 stationary phase with acetonitrile.
//   10 uL acetonitrile droplet dispensed, driven to bead bed.
//   Beads mixed (magnet off, DMF actuation) then supernatant
//   removed (magnet on). Activation time: ~30 s.
//   Activates collapsed C18 chains into extended conformation
//   for reversed-phase interaction (paper Fig. 1B).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

activationSolvent  = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
activatedBeads     = mix beadBed with activationSolvent for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Wash beads with water to equilibrate to aqueous phase.
//   10 uL water droplet. Beads mixed then supernatant removed.
//   Wash time: ~30 s.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

washSolvent1   = dispense 1 units of Water
washedBeads    = mix activatedBeads with washSolvent1 for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// LOADING CAPACITY CHARACTERISATION:
//   Model analyte: fluorescein in 0.1% TFA (aqueous).
//   10 uL droplet loaded onto C18 bead bed, incubated 5 min
//   with DMF actuation mixing. Supernatant recovered off-chip
//   and fluorescence measured (PheraStar, ex 480 nm, em 520 nm).
//   Result (paper Fig. 2A): ~10% unbound for 1-1000 ug/mL;
//   saturation onset at 10 mg/mL (>80% unbound).
//   This characterisation uses the washed bead bed from STEP 3.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// STEP 4a (loading capacity): Load fluorescein sample.
fluoresceingSample   = dispense 1 units of Fluorescein_Sample
loadedBeads_QC       = mix washedBeads with fluoresceingSample for 5m

// STEP 5a (loading capacity): Wash unbound analyte.
washSolvent_QC1      = dispense 1 units of Water
washSolvent_QC2      = dispense 1 units of Water
washed_QC1           = mix loadedBeads_QC with washSolvent_QC1 for 30s
washed_QC2           = mix washed_QC1 with washSolvent_QC2 for 30s

// STEP 6a (loading capacity QC detection):
//   fluorescenceDetection module abstracts off-chip plate reader
//   measurement of unbound fluorescein in supernatant.
//   loadingSignal > 0: <80% unbound (beads not saturated).
//   loadingSignal <= 0: >=80% unbound (saturation detected).
loadingSignal = detect fluorescenceDetection on washed_QC2 for 10s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Loading capacity QC gate
// Pass (BB2): loadingSignal > 0 -> acceptable loading (<80%
//             unbound); proceed to DOE optimisation.
// Fail (BB3): loadingSignal <= 0 -> beads saturated; dispense
//             a larger bead bed off-chip and drain.
//             (Resolution: increase bead suspension volume,
//              e.g., from 10 uL to 12 uL, per paper Fig. 2B)
// Both branches converge at BB4 (loadingJoin).
// ============================================================

if (loadingSignal > 0) {
    dispose washed_QC2
} else {
    drain washed_QC2
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: loadingJoin
// Dispose loading QC signal. Begin DOE solvent optimisation.
// ============================================================

dispose loadingSignal

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 2: DOE Elution Solvent Optimisation
// Simplex-centroid design. Seven solvent combinations screened
// for sitamaquine (1 ug/mL) and angiotensin I (50 ug/mL).
// On-chip solvent blending uses unit droplets (2.5 uL each).
// Solvents dispensed from reservoir electrodes (neat stocks).
//
// NOTE ON COMPILER CONSTRAINT: Seven full independent SPE
// runs (one per DOE point) would require re-dispensing fresh
// bead beds for each condition. To remain compiler-compatible
// with lilott8/BioScript (no loop/array-index semantics for
// multi-run DOE), the three most analytically distinct solvent
// conditions are encoded explicitly as representative DOE
// points covering the extremes and optimum of the ternary
// mixture space:
//   DOE Point 1: 100% MeOH       (optimal for sitamaquine)
//   DOE Point 2: 1:1 ACN:H2O     (optimal for angiotensin I)
//   DOE Point 3: 1:1:1 ACN:MeOH:H2O (ternary centroid point)
// The doeElutionModel detection module abstracts the special
// cubic model fitting and response surface generation over all
// seven points (paper eq 2, Fig. 4A/B).
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DOE POINT 1: 100% Methanol
//   Optimal elution solvent for sitamaquine.
//   1 unit = 1 x 2.5 uL unit droplet of neat methanol.
//   Sitamaquine log K ~6 (high affinity for C18); methanol
//   best solubiliser per Abraham solvation model
//   (MeOH > ACN > H2O for sitamaquine).
//   Expected recovery: ~45-55% (Fig. 4A, darkest region).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Fresh bead bed for DOE Point 1.
beadBed_DOE1       = dispense 1 units of C18_MagneticBeads
activSolvent_DOE1  = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
activBeads_DOE1    = mix beadBed_DOE1 with activSolvent_DOE1 for 30s
wash1_DOE1         = dispense 1 units of Water
washed_DOE1        = mix activBeads_DOE1 with wash1_DOE1 for 30s

// Load sitamaquine sample (1 ug/mL, aqueous).
sitamaquine_DOE1   = dispense 1 units of Sitamaquine_Sample
loaded_DOE1        = mix washed_DOE1 with sitamaquine_DOE1 for 5m

// Wash (2x water).
wash2_DOE1         = dispense 1 units of Water
wash3_DOE1         = dispense 1 units of Water
washed2_DOE1       = mix loaded_DOE1 with wash2_DOE1 for 30s
washed3_DOE1       = mix washed2_DOE1 with wash3_DOE1 for 30s

// Elute: 100% MeOH (1 unit = 2.5 uL, 4 units = 10 uL total).
// Four unit droplets combined on-chip to reach 10 uL elution volume.
meoH_u1      = dispense 1 units of Methanol
meoH_u2      = dispense 1 units of Methanol
meoH_u3      = dispense 1 units of Methanol
meoH_u4      = dispense 1 units of Methanol
meoh_mix_a   = mix meoH_u1 with meoH_u2 for 5s
meoh_mix_b   = mix meoH_u3 with meoH_u4 for 5s
eluent_DOE1  = mix meoh_mix_a with meoh_mix_b for 5s

eluate_DOE1  = mix washed3_DOE1 with eluent_DOE1 for 5m

// Quantify by ESI-MS/MS (SRM 344->271, sitamaquine; 354->271, IS).
IS_DOE1          = dispense 1 units of InternalStandard
sita_signal_DOE1 = detect massSpecDetection on eluate_DOE1 for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DOE POINT 2: 1:1 Acetonitrile:Water
//   Optimal elution solvent for angiotensin I (peptide).
//   On-chip mixing: 2 unit droplets ACN + 2 unit droplets H2O
//   -> 5 uL ACN + 5 uL H2O = 10 uL total.
//   Expected recovery: ~45-50% (Fig. 4B, darkest region).
//   Consistent with common reversed-phase HPLC peptide practice.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beadBed_DOE2       = dispense 1 units of C18_MagneticBeads
activSolvent_DOE2  = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
activBeads_DOE2    = mix beadBed_DOE2 with activSolvent_DOE2 for 30s
wash1_DOE2         = dispense 1 units of Water
washed_DOE2        = mix activBeads_DOE2 with wash1_DOE2 for 30s

// Load angiotensin I sample (50 ug/mL, aqueous).
angiotensin_DOE2   = dispense 1 units of AngiotensinI_Sample
loaded_DOE2        = mix washed_DOE2 with angiotensin_DOE2 for 5m

wash2_DOE2         = dispense 1 units of Water
wash3_DOE2         = dispense 1 units of Water
washed2_DOE2       = mix loaded_DOE2 with wash2_DOE2 for 30s
washed3_DOE2       = mix washed2_DOE2 with wash3_DOE2 for 30s

// Elute: 1:1 ACN:H2O. 2 units ACN + 2 units H2O = 10 uL total.
acn_u1       = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
acn_u2       = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
h2o_u1       = dispense 1 units of Water
h2o_u2       = dispense 1 units of Water
acn_mix      = mix acn_u1 with acn_u2 for 5s
h2o_mix      = mix h2o_u1 with h2o_u2 for 5s
eluent_DOE2  = mix acn_mix with h2o_mix for 5s

eluate_DOE2  = mix washed3_DOE2 with eluent_DOE2 for 5m

// Quantify by ESI-MS ([M+3H]3+ at 433 m/z for angiotensin I;
// bradykinin IS at 354 m/z).
IS_DOE2          = dispense 1 units of InternalStandard
angio_signal_DOE2 = detect massSpecDetection on eluate_DOE2 for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DOE POINT 3: 1:1:1 ACN:MeOH:H2O (ternary centroid)
//   Simplex-centroid ternary mixture point.
//   On-chip: 2 unit droplets each of ACN, MeOH, H2O ->
//   ~3.33 uL ACN + 3.33 uL MeOH + 3.33 uL H2O = 10 uL total.
//   NOTE: Compiler-compatible approximation: dispense 1 unit
//   each (2.5 uL each = 7.5 uL), then mix. The doeElutionModel
//   module accounts for this approximation in the cubic fit.
//   Used for both sitamaquine and angiotensin I QC.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beadBed_DOE3       = dispense 1 units of C18_MagneticBeads
activSolvent_DOE3  = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
activBeads_DOE3    = mix beadBed_DOE3 with activSolvent_DOE3 for 30s
wash1_DOE3         = dispense 1 units of Water
washed_DOE3        = mix activBeads_DOE3 with wash1_DOE3 for 30s

// Load sitamaquine/angiotensin I mixture for ternary point.
// Paper uses separate SPE runs per analyte; this point encodes
// a combined load as a compiler-efficient approximation for the
// ternary centroid (both analytes screened under same solvent).
mixture_DOE3   = dispense 1 units of Sitamaquine_AngiotensinI_Mix
loaded_DOE3    = mix washed_DOE3 with mixture_DOE3 for 5m

wash2_DOE3     = dispense 1 units of Water
wash3_DOE3     = dispense 1 units of Water
washed2_DOE3   = mix loaded_DOE3 with wash2_DOE3 for 30s
washed3_DOE3   = mix washed2_DOE3 with wash3_DOE3 for 30s

// Elute: 1:1:1 ACN:MeOH:H2O. 1 unit each solvent.
acn_ternary    = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
meoh_ternary   = dispense 1 units of Methanol
h2o_ternary    = dispense 1 units of Water
ternary_ab     = mix acn_ternary with meoh_ternary for 5s
eluent_DOE3    = mix ternary_ab with h2o_ternary for 5s

eluate_DOE3    = mix washed3_DOE3 with eluent_DOE3 for 5m

// DOE model detection: abstracts special cubic model fitting
// (eq 2) over all 7 simplex-centroid points and generates
// response surface plots (Fig. 4A/B). Signal > 0 indicates
// the model converged and optimal conditions were identified.
doeSignal = detect doeElutionModel on eluate_DOE3 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: DOE optimisation QC gate
// Pass (BB5): doeSignal > 0 -> response surface converged;
//             optimal solvents identified; dispose eluates.
// Fail (BB6): doeSignal <= 0 -> model inconclusive (e.g.,
//             flat response surface or instrument error); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (doeJoin).
// ============================================================

if (doeSignal > 0) {
    dispose eluate_DOE1
    dispose eluate_DOE2
    dispose eluate_DOE3
    dispose sita_signal_DOE1
    dispose angio_signal_DOE2
    dispose IS_DOE1
    dispose IS_DOE2
} else {
    drain eluate_DOE1
    drain eluate_DOE2
    drain eluate_DOE3
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: doeJoin
// Dispose DOE model signal. Begin sample fractionation.
// ============================================================

dispose doeSignal

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 3: Two-Fraction Sample Fractionation
// Analyte mixture: sitamaquine (1 ug/mL) + angiotensin I (50 ug/mL).
// Elution 1: 100% MeOH -> fraction enriched in sitamaquine.
//   (MeOH elutes ~90-95% sitamaquine, only 5-10% angiotensin I)
// Elution 2: 1:1 ACN:H2O -> fraction enriched in angiotensin I.
//   (ACN:H2O elutes ~85-90% angiotensin I, only 10-15% sitamaquine)
// Coelution: <10% for both fractions (paper Fig. 6B).
// ============================================================

// Fresh bead bed for fractionation.
beadBed_FRAC       = dispense 1 units of C18_MagneticBeads
activSolvent_FRAC  = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
activBeads_FRAC    = mix beadBed_FRAC with activSolvent_FRAC for 30s
wash1_FRAC         = dispense 1 units of Water
washed_FRAC        = mix activBeads_FRAC with wash1_FRAC for 30s

// Load mixture (sitamaquine 1 ug/mL + angiotensin I 50 ug/mL).
mixtureFRAC    = dispense 1 units of Sitamaquine_AngiotensinI_Mix
loaded_FRAC    = mix washed_FRAC with mixtureFRAC for 5m

// Wash 2x with water (steps 5 of SPE method).
wash2_FRAC     = dispense 1 units of Water
wash3_FRAC     = dispense 1 units of Water
washed2_FRAC   = mix loaded_FRAC with wash2_FRAC for 30s
washed3_FRAC   = mix washed2_FRAC with wash3_FRAC for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ELUTION 1: 100% MeOH (sitamaquine-enriched fraction).
//   4 unit droplets MeOH = 10 uL. Mixed with bead bed for 5 min.
//   Supernatant = Fraction 1 (sitamaquine-enriched).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

mf1        = dispense 1 units of Methanol
mf2        = dispense 1 units of Methanol
mf3        = dispense 1 units of Methanol
mf4        = dispense 1 units of Methanol
meoh_frac_ab  = mix mf1 with mf2 for 5s
meoh_frac_cd  = mix mf3 with mf4 for 5s
eluentF1   = mix meoh_frac_ab with meoh_frac_cd for 5s

fraction1  = mix washed3_FRAC with eluentF1 for 5m

// Detect sitamaquine in Fraction 1 by ESI-MS/MS (344->271 SRM).
// Spiked with IS (2H10-sitamaquine 500 ng/mL + bradykinin 10 ug/mL)
// and diluted off-chip to 100 uL in 1:1 ACN:H2O, 0.1% formic acid.
IS_FRAC1       = dispense 1 units of InternalStandard
fractionSignal = detect massSpecDetection on fraction1 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Fractionation QC gate (based on Fraction 1 purity)
// Pass (BB8): fractionSignal > 0 -> sitamaquine signal detected
//             with <10% angiotensin I coelution; proceed.
// Fail (BB9): fractionSignal <= 0 -> excessive coelution or
//             no signal; drain.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (fractionationJoin).
// ============================================================

if (fractionSignal > 0) {
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // ELUTION 2: 1:1 ACN:H2O (angiotensin I-enriched fraction).
    //   2 units ACN + 2 units H2O = 10 uL. Performed on the same
    //   bead bed after Fraction 1 supernatant removed.
    //   Supernatant = Fraction 2 (angiotensin I-enriched).
    // ------------------------------------------------------------

    af1        = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
    af2        = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
    wf1        = dispense 1 units of Water
    wf2        = dispense 1 units of Water
    acn_f2     = mix af1 with af2 for 5s
    h2o_f2     = mix wf1 with wf2 for 5s
    eluentF2   = mix acn_f2 with h2o_f2 for 5s

    fraction2  = mix fraction1 with eluentF2 for 5m

    // Detect angiotensin I in Fraction 2 by ESI-MS (433 m/z,
    // [M+3H]3+). IS: bradykinin doubly protonated peak (531 m/z).
    IS_FRAC2   = dispense 1 units of InternalStandard
    angio_FRAC = detect massSpecDetection on fraction2 for 30s

    dispose fraction1
    dispose fraction2
    dispose IS_FRAC1
    dispose IS_FRAC2
    dispose angio_FRAC
} else {
    drain fraction1
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: fractionationJoin
// Dispose fractionation QC signal. Begin DBS extraction.
// ============================================================

dispose fractionSignal

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 4: Dried Blood Spot (DBS) Extraction and Cleanup
// DBS: 15 uL human whole blood spiked with sitamaquine (5 ug/mL)
//   spotted on PerkinElmer 226 cards. 2 mm biopsy punch cut.
// Off-chip extraction: punch extracted into methanol (30 uL, 5 min).
// On-chip: 2.5 uL unit droplet of extract split off, combined with
//   22.5 uL water (diluted on-chip to aqueous matrix compatible
//   with C18 SPE). Total load: 25 uL at ~0.5 ug/mL sitamaquine.
// C18 cleanup removes matrix interferents (lipids, haem, proteins)
//   that suppress sitamaquine ESI-MS/MS ionisation.
// Elution: neat methanol (10 uL, as per DOE optimum).
// Detection: ESI-MS/MS, API 4000 Triple Quad, 344->271 m/z,
//   direct infusion 2 uL/min, positive ion mode, spray voltage 5 kV.
// Result: sitamaquine signal at 271 m/z strongly enhanced
//   post-cleanup vs. raw extract (paper Fig. 7).
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DBS STEP 1: Dispense and dilute DBS extract.
//   2.5 uL DBS methanol extract (1 unit) + 22.5 uL water (9 units).
//   Nine water unit droplets combined to accumulate 22.5 uL.
//   NOTE: compiler-compatible accumulation via sequential mixing.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dbsExtract     = dispense 1 units of DBS_Extract

// Nine water unit droplets (9 x 2.5 uL = 22.5 uL).
dw1   = dispense 1 units of Water
dw2   = dispense 1 units of Water
dw3   = dispense 1 units of Water
dw4   = dispense 1 units of Water
dw5   = dispense 1 units of Water
dw6   = dispense 1 units of Water
dw7   = dispense 1 units of Water
dw8   = dispense 1 units of Water
dw9   = dispense 1 units of Water

dw_12    = mix dw1 with dw2 for 5s
dw_34    = mix dw3 with dw4 for 5s
dw_56    = mix dw5 with dw6 for 5s
dw_78    = mix dw7 with dw8 for 5s
dw_1234  = mix dw_12 with dw_34 for 5s
dw_5678  = mix dw_56 with dw_78 for 5s
dw_12345678 = mix dw_1234 with dw_5678 for 5s
diluentWater    = mix dw_12345678 with dw9 for 5s

dilutedExtract = mix dbsExtract with diluentWater for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DBS STEP 2: C18 bead SPE cleanup of diluted DBS extract.
//   Fresh bead bed. Activate (ACN), wash (H2O), load extract
//   (5 min incubation), wash 2x (H2O), elute (neat MeOH).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beadBed_DBS       = dispense 1 units of C18_MagneticBeads
activSolvent_DBS  = dispense 1 units of Acetonitrile
activBeads_DBS    = mix beadBed_DBS with activSolvent_DBS for 30s
wash1_DBS         = dispense 1 units of Water
washed_DBS        = mix activBeads_DBS with wash1_DBS for 30s

loaded_DBS        = mix washed_DBS with dilutedExtract for 5m

wash2_DBS         = dispense 1 units of Water
wash3_DBS         = dispense 1 units of Water
washed2_DBS       = mix loaded_DBS with wash2_DBS for 30s
washed3_DBS       = mix washed2_DBS with wash3_DBS for 30s

// Elute with neat methanol (4 units = 10 uL).
dm1        = dispense 1 units of Methanol
dm2        = dispense 1 units of Methanol
dm3        = dispense 1 units of Methanol
dm4        = dispense 1 units of Methanol
meoh_dbs_ab   = mix dm1 with dm2 for 5s
meoh_dbs_cd   = mix dm3 with dm4 for 5s
eluent_DBS    = mix meoh_dbs_ab with meoh_dbs_cd for 5s

dbsEluate  = mix washed3_DBS with eluent_DBS for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DBS STEP 3: Detect sitamaquine in cleaned-up DBS eluate.
//   Off-chip: 10 uL eluate diluted to 100 uL in 1:1 ACN:H2O
//   + 0.5% formic acid. Direct infusion ESI-MS/MS, 2 uL/min.
//   SRM transition: 344->271 (sitamaquine), averages of 12 scans.
//   dbsSignal > 0: cleanup enhanced 271 m/z sitamaquine signal
//   relative to raw DBS extract (matrix suppression reduced).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dbsSignal = detect massSpecDetection on dbsEluate for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB11 / BB12: DBS cleanup QC gate
// Pass (BB11): dbsSignal > 0 -> sitamaquine signal enhanced
//              post-cleanup; dispose.
// Fail (BB12): dbsSignal <= 0 -> no signal improvement
//              (insufficient cleanup or instrument error); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB13 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (dbsSignal > 0) {
    dispose dbsEluate
} else {
    drain dbsEluate
}

// ============================================================
// BB13: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all remaining QC signal droplets. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose dbsSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module preconcentrationDetection

manifest NafionResin
manifest PhosphateBuffer
manifest BetaPhycoerythrin_Sample

// ============================================================
// Multiplexed Proteomic Sample Preconcentration Device Using
// Surface-Patterned Ion-Selective Membrane.
// Lee, Song & Han
// Lab Chip, 2008, 8(4): 596-601
// DOI: 10.1039/b717220f
//
// Platform: PDMS microfluidic chip (20 mm x 25 mm) with
// surface-patterned planar Nafion ion-selective membrane
// embedded at the PDMS/glass interface by plasma bonding.
// Microchannels: 50 um deep x 50 um wide (all channels).
// Channel layout (dual gate design): one central sample
// channel flanked symmetrically by two buffer channels;
// Nafion membrane stripe printed on glass substrate connects
// all three channels at the channel bottom as a permselective
// junction (analogous to nanochannels in prior silicon device).
//
// Separation physics: electrokinetic trapping.
// Phase 1 - Depletion mode: voltage difference (Vdiff = V1-V2)
//   applied across sample and buffer channels through Nafion
//   membrane (V1=50V, V2=50V, buffer GND). Normal field (En)
//   generates a depletion region in the sample channel; charged
//   molecules are pushed away from the membrane toward V1.
// Phase 2 - Concentration mode: V2 reduced to 25V while V1
//   held at 50V. Tangential field (Et) drives electroosmotic
//   flow (EOF) along sample channel toward the depletion zone.
//   Molecules are stacked in front of the depletion boundary.
//   Balance of En and Et produces a stable concentrated plug.
// Optimum Vdiff = 25V (V1=50V, V2=25V); above 25V depletion
// zone destabilized by secondary nonlinear electrokinetics.
//
// Model protein: beta-Phycoerythrin (beta-PE; MW 240 kDa,
//   pI 4.3; Sigma-Aldrich). Dissolved in 10^-2 M phosphate
//   buffer pH 7. Concentrations tested: 40 pM, 400 pM, 4 nM,
//   40 nM. Internal reference standards: 2, 4, 8 uM.
// Performance: concentration factor ~10^4 in 5 min (400 pM
//   starting -> 8 uM reference in 5 min); ~5x10^4 in 10 min
//   (40 pM starting); concentrated plug volume ~200 pL;
//   coefficient of variance 11.5% across 3 devices.
//
// Nafion membrane:
//   Material  : Nafion perfluorinated ion-exchange resin
//               (Sigma-Aldrich, 5 wt% in aliphatic alcohols/H2O,
//               15-20% water content)
//   Patterning: Micro-flow patterning (preferred; more
//               reproducible thickness than micro-stamping):
//               (1) 1 uL Nafion resin filled by capillary force
//               into reversibly bonded PDMS channel (120 um
//               deep x 200 um wide); (2) liquid resin flushed
//               out by negative pressure through syringe tubing,
//               leaving thin residual layer on hydrophilic glass;
//               (3) cure at 95 C for 10 min; (4) plasma bond
//               PDMS chip to patterned glass substrate.
//   Thickness : Target 191-400 nm; >400 nm causes PDMS sealing
//               failure and leakage at membrane edges.
//               Micro-stamping alternative: plasma-treat PDMS
//               stamp, ink with Nafion, transfer to clean glass,
//               cure 95 C 10 min; thickness 0.5-1 um (less
//               reproducible; risk of leakage).
//   Bonding   : O2 plasma bonding, PDMS chip on top of Nafion-
//               patterned glass; post-bond bake 70 C for 12 h
//               to enhance bonding strength.
//
// Array format: 10 single preconcentrators arranged radially
//   with a common collection reservoir at center. Samples S1-S10
//   each at V1=50V; common reservoir at V2=25V; individual
//   ground reservoirs per buffer channel. Nafion patterned in
//   half-circle shape per unit. Concentrated volume scales
//   linearly with number of units: single ~200 pL; 10-unit
//   array ~2 nL (3500x increase vs. prior silicon device).
//   NOTE ON ARRAY ENCODING: BioScript has no loop construct;
//   explicit unrolling of 10 identical channels would require
//   10 identical dispense/mix/heat/detect blocks. This file
//   encodes the single-preconcentrator canonical unit, which
//   is the repeating element of the array. Array behavior is
//   documented in the JSON/TXT companion files.
//
// NOTE ON VOLTAGE ABSTRACTION: BioScript has no electrical
//   construct (no `voltage`, `electrode`, or `field` keyword).
//   Electrokinetic voltage application steps (depletion and
//   concentration modes) are abstracted as `heat` statements:
//   - Depletion mode (V1=50V, V2=50V, GND): heat at 50c
//     models the first voltage phase; 50c is a placeholder
//     encoding the V1 voltage level (50V) as a temperature
//     token, consistent with conventions in this library for
//     representing electrode-driven operations without a
//     native BioScript electrical primitive.
//   - Concentration mode (V1=50V, V2=25V, GND): heat at 25c
//     models the second voltage phase with the reduced V2=25V
//     as the temperature token, reflecting the voltage
//     differential that drives the electroosmotic flow.
//   Both phases operate on the same physical droplet/channel
//   contents; unique intermediate variable names are used for
//   each phase to satisfy the no-reassignment constraint.
//
// NOTE ON DURATION TOKENS: BioScript supports s and m only.
//   All durations in m or s; no h tokens used.
//   Nafion cure    : 10 min   = 10m
//   Post-bond bake : 12 h     = 720m
//   Depletion mode : 1 min    = 1m  (per paper Fig. 5 V sweep)
//   Concentration  : 5 min    = 5m  (optimum; 10^4 factor)
//   Detection      : 5 min    = 5m  (fluorescence acquisition)
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- Nafion membrane patterning (cure),
//                            post-bond bake, buffer channel
//                            loading, sample loading, depletion
//                            mode, concentration mode, detection
//   BB2  concenPass        -- concenSignal > 0: concentrated plug
//                            detected above reference threshold
//                            (>=8 uM equivalent fluorescence);
//                            dispose all channels
//   BB3  concenFail        -- concenSignal <= 0: no plug detected
//                            (membrane leak, voltage failure, or
//                            insufficient preconcentration time);
//                            drain all channels
//   BB4  finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose QC signal, end
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Nafion membrane patterning - micro-flow patterning.
//   1 uL Nafion resin placed at reservoir of reversibly bonded
//   PDMS channel (120 um deep x 200 um wide); capillary force
//   fills channel immediately. Liquid resin flushed out by
//   negative pressure via syringe tubing on opposite end,
//   leaving a thin Nafion layer on the hydrophilic glass.
//   Cure patterned stripe at 95 C for 10 min on hotplate.
//   Target thickness: 191-400 nm.
//   NOTE: The patterning PDMS channel is temporary and
//   reversibly bonded (removed before device assembly).
//   This step is encoded as a heat operation on NafionResin
//   abstracting the flow-coat + thermal cure sequence.
//   Off-chip physical fabrication; no liquid handling on the
//   final device at this stage.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

nafionResin    = dispense 1 units of NafionResin
nafionCured    = mix nafionResin with nafionResin for 1m
heat nafionCured at 95c for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: PDMS chip plasma bonding and post-bond bake.
//   PDMS chip (50 um deep x 50 um wide channels, dual gate
//   layout: 1 sample channel center + 2 buffer channels
//   flanking) plasma bonded on top of Nafion-patterned glass.
//   No microscope alignment required (non-critical alignment).
//   Post-bond bake: 70 C for 12 h (= 720 min) to enhance
//   bonding strength between PDMS and glass/Nafion.
//   Encoded as heat on cured membrane; abstracts bonding bake.
//   NOTE: Self-mix pattern used (nafionCured with itself) to
//   represent a single-material bake step with no second
//   reagent. Same pattern as warkiani_spiral_ctc_isolation.bs
//   ethanol priming step; documented compiler behavior.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat nafionCured at 70c for 720m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Load buffer solution into buffer channels (flanking).
//   10^-2 M phosphate buffer pH 7 loaded into both outer
//   buffer channels (left and right) of the dual gate design.
//   Buffer channels are grounded (GND) during both depletion
//   and concentration modes. Dual buffer channels improve
//   stability of the depletion region by generating depletion
//   zones from both sides of the sample channel simultaneously.
//   Dispense two independent buffer droplets representing the
//   two flanking buffer channels.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bufferLeft  = dispense 1 units of PhosphateBuffer
bufferRight = dispense 1 units of PhosphateBuffer
bufferDual  = mix bufferLeft with bufferRight for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Load protein sample into sample channel (center).
//   beta-Phycoerythrin (beta-PE; MW 240 kDa, pI 4.3) at
//   working concentration (e.g. 4 nM as canonical condition
//   from Fig. 6) dissolved in 10^-2 M phosphate buffer pH 7.
//   Sample loaded into central microchannel; both ends of
//   sample channel accessible for electrode connections
//   (V1 at one end, V2 at other end during concentration mode).
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts sample loading into the microchannel
//   co-resident with the buffer; this is not a DMF droplet
//   merge but represents channel filling with protein solution.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

proteinSample   = dispense 1 units of BetaPhycoerythrin_Sample
loadedSample    = mix proteinSample with bufferDual for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Depletion mode (Phase 1).
//   Voltage configuration: V1=50V (sample channel inlet),
//   V2=50V (sample channel outlet), buffer channels at GND.
//   A potential difference is applied across sample and buffer
//   channels through the Nafion membrane. The normal field
//   component (En) across the membrane depletes ions in the
//   sample channel and pushes charged beta-PE molecules away
//   from the membrane toward the V1 reservoir.
//   Duration: 1 min to establish and stabilize the depletion
//   region before switching to concentration mode.
//   ABSTRACTION: heat at 50c encodes V1=50V operating voltage.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

depletedSample  = mix loadedSample with bufferDual for 1m
heat depletedSample at 50c for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Concentration mode (Phase 2).
//   Voltage configuration: V1=50V, V2=25V, buffer GND.
//   Vdiff = V1-V2 = 25V (optimum; above 25V secondary
//   nonlinear electrokinetics destabilize concentrated zone).
//   Tangential field (Et) generates electroosmotic flow (EOF)
//   along sample channel that injects molecules toward the
//   stable depletion zone boundary. Molecules stack in front
//   of the depletion region and form a concentrated plug.
//   Duration: 5 min (canonical; achieves ~10^4 concentration
//   factor from 400 pM starting to 8 uM reference intensity;
//   from 40 pM, ~5x10^4 factor reached in 10 min).
//   Concentrated plug volume: ~200 pL; coefficient of
//   variance 11.5% across 3 devices.
//   ABSTRACTION: heat at 25c encodes V2=25V reduced voltage;
//   unique variable concentratedPlug used (no reassignment).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

concentratedPlug = mix depletedSample with bufferDual for 5m
heat concentratedPlug at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Fluorescence detection of concentrated plug.
//   Platform: Olympus IX71 inverted epifluorescence microscope
//   with thermoelectrically cooled CCD camera (Hamamatsu).
//   Fluorescence images taken every 30 s; analyzed with IPLab.
//   Mechanical shutter blocks UV between acquisitions to
//   minimize photobleaching. Neutral density filter prevents
//   CCD saturation (increased dynamic range).
//   Internal reference standards: 2, 4, 8 uM beta-PE.
//   Pass criterion: fluorescence intensity of concentrated plug
//   >= 8 uM reference (corresponding to ~10^4 fold increase
//   from 400 pM starting concentration at 5 min).
//   preconcentrationDetection module abstracts the fluorescence
//   intensity readout and comparison to reference standards.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

concenSignal = detect preconcentrationDetection on concentratedPlug for 5m

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Preconcentration detection gate
// Pass (BB2): concenSignal > 0 -> concentrated plug detected
//             at or above 8 uM reference intensity threshold;
//             preconcentration successful. Dispose all.
// Fail (BB3): concenSignal <= 0 -> no detectable plug or
//             below threshold (membrane leakage, voltage
//             failure, or Nafion film >400 nm causing
//             imperfect sealing). Drain all channels.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (concenSignal > 0) {
    dispose concentratedPlug
} else {
    drain concentratedPlug
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose preconcentration QC signal. Experiment ends.
// For array format (10-unit radial array): repeat BB1 main
// block for each of S1-S10 sample channels (explicit unroll);
// all share common reservoir at V2=25V and individual ground
// reservoirs per buffer channel. Total concentrated volume
// scales to ~2 nL (10 x ~200 pL) for mass spectrometer
// interfacing. Array encoding is a candidate for a separate
// .bs file with explicit 10-unit unrolled structure.
// ============================================================

dispose concenSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module ddPCRProteinDetection
module rtDdPCRmRNADetection
module liveCellImagingDetection
module viralProteinDetection

manifest PBS
manifest LysisBuffer
manifest PLAProbes
manifest LigationMix
manifest MilliQWater
manifest BSA_BlockingBuffer
manifest SingleCell
manifest PurifiedProteinStandard
manifest RNaseOUT
manifest RTddPCR_Supermix
manifest ReversTranscriptase
manifest DTT
manifest UniversalPCRAssay
manifest TaqMan_TNFR1_Probe
manifest ddPCR_Supermix
manifest DropletGenerationOil
manifest HFE7500_Surfactant
manifest HSV1_InfectedCell
manifest ControlCell

// ============================================================
// Ultra-sensitive Digital Quantification of Proteins and mRNA
// in Single Cells (u-dPLA)
// Lin et al., Nature Communications (2019) 10:3544
// DOI: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-019-11531-z
//
// OVERVIEW:
// Automated microfluidic digital proximity ligation assay
// (u-dPLA) for ultrasensitive, single-cell quantification of
// proteins (via ddPCR readout of PLA products) and mRNA (via
// one-step RT-ddPCR), simultaneously from the same single cell.
//
// KEY PERFORMANCE METRICS:
//   LOD (TNFR1):  2,277 protein molecules per cell
//   LOD (CD147):  2,795 protein molecules per cell
//   LOD (CSTB):  18,824 protein molecules per cell
//   Detection efficiency (CD147): as low as 29 protein molecules
//   Improvement over regular dPLA: up to 55x better LOD
//   Linear dynamic range: 3-4 orders of magnitude
//   Chip capacity: 144 parallel single-cell dPLA assays
//   Chamber (trap) volume: 7 nL
//   Sample used in ddPCR: 100% (vs 9% in regular dPLA)
//   Pipetting error: <0.1% (vs ~10% manual)
//
// CHIP ARCHITECTURE (one assay unit, Chambers I-V):
//   Chamber I:   Reagent input / wash channel
//   Chamber II:  Single-cell trap (hydrodynamic, 50 um constriction)
//   Chamber III: 7-nL lysis chamber; cell lysed here; probe incubation
//   Chamber IV:  Probe-lysate diffusion mixing zone
//   Chamber V:   Ligation reaction chamber
//   Output port: PLA products retrieved for ddPCR/RT-ddPCR
//
// FABRICATION NOTE: Two-layer push-up PDMS device.
//   Control layer (thin): microfluidic valves (SU-8 22 um),
//     operated by Matlab-controlled pneumatic actuators.
//   Fluidic layer (thick): reagent channels (SU-8 70 um + AZ 18 um).
//   Chambers sealed by dead-end filling for precise nanoliter volumes.
//   Wash channels prevent cross-contamination between steps.
//
// BIOSCRIPT ABSTRACTIONS:
//   - `mix X with Y for T` abstracts valve-gated reagent introduction
//     and diffusion-based mixing inside closed nanoliter chambers.
//     No DMF electrode actuation is involved; fluid movement is driven
//     by pneumatic pressure and Matlab-controlled valve sequencing.
//   - `heat` abstracts on-chip or bench incubation steps with
//     temperature control (enzyme steps, ligation, etc.).
//   - Duration tokens follow BioScript compiler constraints:
//     s (seconds) and m (minutes) only; hours converted to minutes.
//   - Cell occupancy: ~90% average per assay run; a single cell is
//     modelled as one `SingleCell` manifest unit per assay unit.
//   - ddPCR/RT-ddPCR readout is modelled via detect modules;
//     actual droplet generation and thermal cycling occur off-chip
//     after product retrieval.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                -- chip prep, calibration curve,
//                               single-cell trap, lysis, PLA, ligation,
//                               product retrieval, ddPCR QC gate
//   BB2  proteinPass         -- proteinSignal > 0: valid protein count;
//                               dispose lysate, continue to mRNA branch
//   BB3  proteinFail         -- proteinSignal <= 0: below LOD; drain
//   BB4  proteinJoin         -- join: duplex mRNA/protein branch begins
//   BB5  mrnaPass            -- mrnaSignal > 0: valid mRNA count; dispose
//   BB6  mrnaFail            -- mrnaSignal <= 0: no mRNA detected; drain
//   BB7  mrnaJoin            -- join: HSV-1 viral infection branch begins
//   BB8  infectionPass       -- infectionSignal > 0: ICP4-YFP+ infected
//                               cell; dispose viral protein readout
//   BB9  infectionFail       -- infectionSignal <= 0: uninfected or
//                               abortive; drain
//   BB10 finalCleanup        -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Block non-specific binding.
//   Chip surface coated with 10% BSA in PBS for 1 h at RT
//   to minimise non-specific adsorption of PLA probes and
//   cell-lysate proteins to PDMS channel walls.
//   After incubation, chip is washed with MilliQ water and
//   ventilated with compressed air before cell loading.
//   1 h = 60 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bsaBlock     = dispense 1 units of BSA_BlockingBuffer
pbsWash0     = dispense 1 units of PBS

chipSurface  = mix bsaBlock with pbsWash0 for 60m

// Wash off excess BSA with MilliQ water.
waterWash0   = dispense 1 units of MilliQWater
blockedChip  = mix chipSurface with waterWash0 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Calibration curve (on-chip, performed in parallel
//   with single-cell experiments on same chip run).
//   Serial dilution of purified protein standard (e.g., TNFR1,
//   CD147, or CSTB) loaded from lowest to highest concentration.
//   8 min washing between standards through Channel I.
//   Calibration units are loaded into chambers I-V by the
//   same dead-end filling protocol as single-cell samples.
//   PLA probes: 500 pM stock, diluted to 2 nM working solution
//   in probe binding buffer (0.1% BSA, 0.05% Tween-20,
//   100 nM goat IgG, 0.1 g/L sheared salmon sperm DNA in PBS).
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts sequential dead-end filling of each
//   calibration concentration into the assay chambers.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

proteinStd   = dispense 1 units of PurifiedProteinStandard
probeWash1   = dispense 1 units of PBS

// Load calibration standard into chamber III (7 nL).
stdInChamber = mix proteinStd with probeWash1 for 2m

// Flush Channel I and II with PLA probes (500 pM -> 2 nM).
plaProbeStd  = dispense 1 units of PLAProbes
stdProbeIncub = mix stdInChamber with plaProbeStd for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Load ligation mix into calibration chambers.
//   Ligation mix (per 100 uL): 89.1 uL MilliQ water,
//   10 uL 10x ligase buffer, 0.4 uL 1/500-diluted T4 ligase,
//   0.5 uL 1 uM connector oligonucleotide.
//   Pushed from chambers III/IV to chamber V.
//   30 min on ice for diffusion mixing; 10 min ligation at 37 C;
//   5 min heat inactivation at 65 C.
//   Modelled as three sequential heat steps on the same droplet.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ligMixStd    = dispense 1 units of LigationMix
stdLigMix    = mix stdProbeIncub with ligMixStd for 30m

// Ligation: 37 C for 10 min.
heat stdLigMix at 37c for 10m

// Heat inactivation of T4 ligase: 65 C for 5 min.
heat stdLigMix at 65c for 5m

// Retrieve calibration PLA products (9 uL) for ddPCR.
waterFlush1  = dispense 1 units of MilliQWater
stdProducts  = mix stdLigMix with waterFlush1 for 2m

// ============================================================
// SINGLE-CELL u-dPLA ASSAY
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4 (Chamber II): Single-cell loading and trapping.
//   Cells resuspended in sterile PBS, filtered through 40 um
//   strainer, diluted to 2x10^5 cells/mL.
//   Loaded at 5 psi input pressure into Channel I.
//   Single cells are hydrodynamically trapped in Chamber II
//   (50 um constriction) with ~90% average occupancy.
//   Cell loading verified visually under microscope before
//   proceeding. Chip immediately moved to ice after loading.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts pressure-driven cell loading into
//   the hydrodynamic trap; no reagent mixing in the DMF sense.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

singleCell   = dispense 1 units of SingleCell
pbsTrap      = dispense 1 units of PBS

trappedCell  = mix singleCell with pbsTrap for 2s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5 (Chamber III): Single-cell lysis.
//   Lysis buffer: 0.3x TM buffer + protease inhibitor in cell
//   resuspension buffer. Introduced from Chamber I to push and
//   lyse the trapped cell from Chamber II into Chamber III (7 nL).
//   Lysis complete within 10 min on ice. Visually confirmed
//   under microscope. Entire 7-nL lysate remains in Chamber III;
//   zero sample loss (cf. regular dPLA: 91% sample loss).
//   Reducing dilution factor is the primary source of 55x
//   sensitivity improvement over regular well-plate dPLA.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lysisBuffer  = dispense 1 units of LysisBuffer
cellLysate   = mix trappedCell with lysisBuffer for 10m

// Chip maintained on ice during lysis (modelled as 0 C, 10 min).
heat cellLysate at 0c for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6 (Chambers III-IV): PLA probe incubation with lysate.
//   PLA probes (oligonucleotide-tagged antibody pairs) flushed
//   through Channels I and II into Chamber III. Probe-lysate
//   mix occupies Chambers III and IV with valve between open.
//   Incubated at 37 C for 1 h. Diffusion-based mixing.
//   Antibody pairs recognise two distinct epitopes on target
//   protein. Proximity ligation requires both probes bound
//   to the same protein molecule within ~3-5 nm.
//   PLA probes: TNFR1: BAF225; CD147: BAF972 (R&D Systems).
//   HSV-1: anti-HSV-1 polyclonal (ab9533, Abcam) conjugated
//   in-house via DBCO-NHS/azide click chemistry.
//   1 h = 60 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

plaProbeCell = dispense 1 units of PLAProbes
probeIncub   = mix cellLysate with plaProbeCell for 60m

// Incubate at 37 C for 1 h.
heat probeIncub at 37c for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7 (Chamber V): Ligation reaction.
//   Ligation mix introduced from Channels I/II to push the
//   probe-lysate mix (Chambers III/IV) into Chamber V.
//   Connector oligonucleotide (TTTCGACACGACACGATTTAGGTC)
//   bridges the two probe oligonucleotides when both probes
//   are bound to the same target protein.
//   T4 DNA ligase seals the nick to form a circular DNA product.
//   30 min on ice for diffusion mixing (valve between III and V
//   open); then 37 C for 10 min ligation; 65 C for 5 min
//   heat inactivation of T4 ligase.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ligMixCell   = dispense 1 units of LigationMix
ligOnIce     = mix probeIncub with ligMixCell for 30m

heat ligOnIce at 0c for 30m
heat ligOnIce at 37c for 10m
heat ligOnIce at 65c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Retrieve PLA products for ddPCR.
//   PLA products flushed out of Chamber V by MilliQ water
//   through the output retrieval port (1930 um punch) into
//   a collection tube. 9 uL retrieved per unit.
//   ddPCR Supermix for Probes (Bio-Rad QX200) + Universal
//   PCR Assay added; 20 uL total loaded into DG8 cartridge
//   with 65 uL Droplet Generation Oil for droplet generation.
//   PCR program: 95 C 10 min; 40x (94 C 30s / 60 C 1 min);
//   98 C 10 min; ramp 1.5 C/s.
//   FAM channel readout: counts positive droplets containing
//   ligated PLA DNA product. Each positive droplet = 1 protein.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

waterFlush2  = dispense 1 units of MilliQWater
plaProducts  = mix ligOnIce with waterFlush2 for 2m

ddpcrMix     = dispense 1 units of ddPCR_Supermix
universalPCR = dispense 1 units of UniversalPCRAssay
dropletOil   = dispense 1 units of DropletGenerationOil

// Assemble ddPCR reaction mix.
ddpcrRxn1    = mix plaProducts with ddpcrMix for 5s
ddpcrRxn2    = mix ddpcrRxn1 with universalPCR for 5s
ddpcrLoaded  = mix ddpcrRxn2 with dropletOil for 5s

// ddPCR thermal cycling: 95 C 10 min activation.
heat ddpcrLoaded at 95c for 10m

// 40 amplification cycles: 94 C 30s + 60 C 1 min = 90s each.
// 40 cycles x 90s = 3600s = 60m.
heat ddpcrLoaded at 94c for 60m

// Final extension / signal stabilisation: 98 C 10 min.
heat ddpcrLoaded at 98c for 10m

// Detect protein copy number (FAM channel; positive droplet count).
proteinSignal = detect ddPCRProteinDetection on ddpcrLoaded for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Protein detection QC gate
// Pass (BB2): proteinSignal > 0 -> protein count above LOD
//             (LOD: TNFR1=2277; CD147=2795; CSTB=18824 molecules);
//             dispose ddPCR droplets, continue to mRNA branch.
// Fail (BB3): proteinSignal <= 0 -> below LOD (e.g. HEK293T
//             TNFR1 cells all below LOD, consistent with paper
//             finding that TNFR1 is not expressed in HEK293T);
//             drain all droplets.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (proteinJoin).
// ============================================================

if (proteinSignal > 0) {
    dispose ddpcrLoaded
} else {
    drain ddpcrLoaded
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: proteinJoin
// Dispose protein QC signal. Control falls into the duplex
// mRNA/protein quantification branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose proteinSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Duplex protein/mRNA quantification (same single cell).
//   Instead of splitting the lysate, one-step RT-ddPCR is
//   integrated with u-dPLA in the same ddPCR droplets.
//   RNaseOUT (20x dilution in all reagent mixes) protects mRNA
//   throughout PLA steps. After product retrieval, 9 uL PLA
//   products + mRNA are mixed with:
//     5 uL one-step RT-ddPCR Supermix
//     2 uL reverse transcriptase
//     1 uL 300 mM DTT
//     1 uL 20x Universal PCR Assay (FAM; PLA/protein readout)
//     1 uL 20x TaqMan TNFR1 probe (VIC; mRNA readout)
//     1 uL nuclease-free water
//   Total: 20 uL. Same droplet generation as Step 8.
//   FAM channel -> protein (PLA ligation products).
//   VIC channel -> mRNA (RT-ddPCR amplicons from TNFR1 mRNA).
//   Cross-talk validation: VIC/FAM crosstalk negligible
//   (Supplementary Fig. 5a/b). N=38 cells; Spearman rho=0.45.
//   RT program: 42 C 1h; 95 C 10 min; 40x (95 C 30s/60 C 1 min);
//   98 C 10 min; ramp 2 C/s.
//   1 h RT step = 60 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Retrieve combined PLA+mRNA products (same protocol as Step 8).
rnaseOut1    = dispense 1 units of RNaseOUT

// RNaseOUT added to all reagent mixes (20x dilution modelled
// as direct mix with products before RT-ddPCR assembly).
protectedRNA = mix plaProducts with rnaseOut1 for 1s

// Assemble duplex RT-ddPCR reaction mix.
rtSupermix   = dispense 1 units of RTddPCR_Supermix
revTranscrip = dispense 1 units of ReversTranscriptase
dttSolution  = dispense 1 units of DTT
univPCR2     = dispense 1 units of UniversalPCRAssay
tnfr1Probe   = dispense 1 units of TaqMan_TNFR1_Probe
dropletOil2  = dispense 1 units of DropletGenerationOil

duplexRxn1   = mix protectedRNA with rtSupermix for 5s
duplexRxn2   = mix duplexRxn1 with revTranscrip for 5s
duplexRxn3   = mix duplexRxn2 with dttSolution for 5s
duplexRxn4   = mix duplexRxn3 with univPCR2 for 5s
duplexRxn5   = mix duplexRxn4 with tnfr1Probe for 5s
duplexLoaded = mix duplexRxn5 with dropletOil2 for 5s

// RT step: 42 C for 1 h (= 60 min) to reverse transcribe mRNA.
heat duplexLoaded at 42c for 60m

// Activation: 95 C for 10 min.
heat duplexLoaded at 95c for 10m

// 40 amplification cycles: 95 C 30s + 60 C 1 min = 90s each.
// 40 cycles x 90s = 3600s = 60m.
heat duplexLoaded at 95c for 60m

// Final extension / signal stabilisation: 98 C 10 min.
heat duplexLoaded at 98c for 10m

// Detect mRNA (VIC channel; RT-ddPCR amplicons from TNFR1 mRNA).
mrnaSignal   = detect rtDdPCRmRNADetection on duplexLoaded for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: mRNA detection QC gate
// Pass (BB5): mrnaSignal > 0 -> TNFR1 mRNA detected above LOD;
//             dispose duplex ddPCR droplets.
//             Paper result: Spearman rho=0.45 (N=38 H1299 cells),
//             significantly higher than previous ~0.1 estimates,
//             demonstrating that including lowly-expressing cells
//             (below previous dPLA LOD) raises mRNA-protein
//             correlation. (Fig. 3d/e)
// Fail (BB6): mrnaSignal <= 0 -> mRNA below detection; drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (mrnaJoin).
// ============================================================

if (mrnaSignal > 0) {
    dispose duplexLoaded
} else {
    drain duplexLoaded
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: mrnaJoin
// Dispose mRNA QC signal. Control falls into the HSV-1 viral
// infection profiling branch (BB8/BB9).
// ============================================================

dispose mrnaSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: HSV-1 viral infection heterogeneity assay.
//   A549 human lung epithelial cells infected with HSV-1
//   expressing ICP4-YFP reporter (MOI 2; overnight).
//   Cells harvested, loaded on chip alongside control cells
//   (no virus). Loaded at 5 psi; chip moved to ice post-loading.
//
//   Live-cell time-lapse imaging (fluorescence) performed
//   on-chip before lysis to identify infected (ICP4-YFP+,
//   nuclear YFP signal) vs uninfected/control (YFP-) cells.
//   YFP images acquired; total fluorescence per cell calculated
//   by Matlab (imfindcircles + Rolling Ball Background
//   Subtraction in ImageJ).
//
//   After imaging, all cells lysed simultaneously with 0.3x
//   TM buffer (same lysis protocol as Steps 5-7).
//
//   HSV-1 PLA probes: polyclonal anti-HSV-1 antibody (ab9533,
//   Abcam) conjugated in-house via DBCO-NHS click chemistry.
//   Detects ~80 HSV-1 viral protein species.
//
//   Key finding (Fig. 4d): Spearman rho=0.48 between ICP4-YFP
//   fluorescence and total viral protein count, indicating
//   that not all HSV-1-infected cells (YFP+) progress past
//   the immediate-early protein synthesis stage; some undergo
//   abortive infection. Wide single-cell distribution:
//   highest expressors show ~5x more viral protein than lowest.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Dispense HSV-1 infected A549 cell and control (no virus) cell.
infectedCell = dispense 1 units of HSV1_InfectedCell
ctrlCell     = dispense 1 units of ControlCell
pbsTrap2     = dispense 1 units of PBS
pbsTrap3     = dispense 1 units of PBS

// Hydrodynamic trapping (Chamber II) of infected and control cells.
trappedInfected = mix infectedCell with pbsTrap2 for 2s
trappedControl  = mix ctrlCell with pbsTrap3 for 2s

// Live-cell fluorescence imaging before lysis.
// liveCellImagingDetection: abstracts on-chip time-lapse YFP
// imaging. ICP4-YFP signal (nuclear) classifies infected cells.
// Uninfected and control cells show no YFP signal.
infectionSignal = detect liveCellImagingDetection on trappedInfected for 10m

// Lysis of all cells simultaneously (0.3x TM buffer, on ice).
lysisBuffer2     = dispense 1 units of LysisBuffer
viralLysate      = mix trappedInfected with lysisBuffer2 for 10m
heat viralLysate at 0c for 10m

// HSV-1 PLA probes incubation (Chamber III/IV, 37 C, 1 h = 60 min).
plaProbeHSV  = dispense 1 units of PLAProbes
viralProbeIncub = mix viralLysate with plaProbeHSV for 60m
heat viralProbeIncub at 37c for 60m

// Ligation (Chamber V): ice 30 min, 37 C 10 min, 65 C 5 min.
ligMixViral  = dispense 1 units of LigationMix
viralLigMix  = mix viralProbeIncub with ligMixViral for 30m
heat viralLigMix at 0c for 30m
heat viralLigMix at 37c for 10m
heat viralLigMix at 65c for 5m

// Retrieve viral PLA products for ddPCR.
waterFlush4   = dispense 1 units of MilliQWater
viralProducts = mix viralLigMix with waterFlush4 for 2m

ddpcrMix2    = dispense 1 units of ddPCR_Supermix
universalPCR2 = dispense 1 units of UniversalPCRAssay
dropletOil3  = dispense 1 units of DropletGenerationOil

viralRxn1    = mix viralProducts with ddpcrMix2 for 5s
viralRxn2    = mix viralRxn1 with universalPCR2 for 5s
viralLoaded  = mix viralRxn2 with dropletOil3 for 5s

// ddPCR thermal cycling (same program as Step 8).
heat viralLoaded at 95c for 10m
heat viralLoaded at 94c for 60m
heat viralLoaded at 98c for 10m

// Detect viral protein count (FAM channel; positive droplet count).
// viralProteinDetection: abstracts ddPCR readout of HSV-1 PLA
// products, correlated with ICP4-YFP (infectionSignal) per cell.
viralProteinReadout = detect viralProteinDetection on viralLoaded for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Viral infection profiling QC gate
// Pass (BB8): infectionSignal > 0 -> ICP4-YFP+ cell confirmed
//             infected (nuclear entry occurred); dispose viral
//             protein readout droplets. Partial correlation
//             (rho=0.48) between YFP level and total viral protein
//             count suggests a sub-population undergoes abortive
//             infection after immediate-early gene expression.
//             (Fig. 4c: p=5.356e-11, two-sided Welch's t test,
//             N=27 infected vs N=19 control cells)
// Fail (BB9): infectionSignal <= 0 -> cell is uninfected (YFP-)
//             or control (no virus); drain viral protein droplets.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (infectionSignal > 0) {
    dispose viralLoaded
} else {
    drain viralLoaded
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all remaining QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose infectionSignal
dispose viralProteinReadout
dispose stdProducts

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module organoidDetection
module propidiumIodideDetection
module mtsViabilityDetection
module morphologyAnalysis

manifest PDAC_Organoids
manifest Gemcitabine_3nM
manifest Gemcitabine_10nM
manifest Gemcitabine_30nM
manifest Gemcitabine_100nM
manifest Gemcitabine_300nM
manifest Gemcitabine_1000nM
manifest PropidiumIodide
manifest MatrigelMatrix
manifest CompleteMedia
manifest DMSO_Vehicle

// ============================================================
// OrganoID: A Versatile Deep Learning Platform for Tracking
// and Analysis of Single-Organoid Dynamics.
// Matthews JM, Schuster B, Kashaf SS, et al.
// PLoS Comput Biol 18(11): e1010584 (2022).
// DOI: 10.1371/journal.pcbi.1010584
//
// Protocol: Gemcitabine dose-response experiment on PDAC
// (pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma) organoids. Organoids
// are cultured in Matrigel, treated with a serial dilution
// of gemcitabine (3 nM - 1000 nM), and imaged every 4 hours
// over 72 hours in brightfield + PI fluorescence channels.
// OrganoID platform segments and tracks individual organoids
// pixel-by-pixel across timelapse frames, normalises PI
// fluorescence by organoid area, and computes morphological
// features (circularity, solidity, eccentricity). A gold-
// standard MTS viability assay is run at endpoint.
//
// NOTE ON BIOLOGICAL SCOPE:
// Six gemcitabine concentrations (3, 10, 30, 100, 300,
// 1000 nM) plus a 0 nM vehicle control (DMSO) are tested
// (n=6 replicates per condition; paper Fig. 3, S6-S7 Fig).
// This BioScript file encodes the full 7-condition experiment
// in sequence. Each condition block: (1) embed organoids in
// Matrigel, (2) add drug / vehicle, (3) add PI stain,
// (4) image for 72 h (modelled as `heat` at 37 C for 4320m),
// (5) detect PI signal, (6) branch on viability signal.
// The MTS assay and final morphology analysis are encoded
// after all treatment arms converge at the endpoint join.
//
// NOTE ON TIMELAPSE ABSTRACTION:
// The paper images every 4 h over 72 h (18 frames per well).
// BioScript does not natively support loop constructs. The
// 72 h incubation period with continuous imaging is therefore
// abstracted as a single `heat` step (37 C, 4320m = 72 h).
// The `organoidDetection` module abstracts the OrganoID
// U-Net inference (pixel-by-pixel segmentation, watershed
// identification, Munkres tracking) run on all acquired frames.
//
// NOTE ON DURATION TOKENS:
// lilott8/BioScript accepts `s` (seconds) and `m` (minutes)
// only. All hour-level durations are converted to minutes:
//   72 h  = 4320 m  (imaging + incubation)
//   4 h   = 240 m   (MTS endpoint assay)
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- embed organoids; 7 treatment arms
//                             (0 nM control + 6 drug conditions)
//   BB2  controlPass       -- controlSignal > 0: viable control;
//                             dispose and proceed to drug arms
//   BB3  controlFail       -- controlSignal <= 0: experiment fail;
//                             drain control; flag abort
//   BB4  controlJoin       -- join: 3 nM treatment arm begins
//   BB5  drug3Pass         -- 3 nM PI signal > 0: dispose
//   BB6  drug3Fail         -- 3 nM PI signal <= 0: drain
//   BB7  drug3Join         -- join: 10 nM treatment arm begins
//   BB8  drug10Pass        -- 10 nM PI signal > 0: dispose
//   BB9  drug10Fail        -- 10 nM PI signal <= 0: drain
//   BB10 drug10Join        -- join: 30 nM treatment arm begins
//   BB11 drug30Pass        -- 30 nM PI signal > 0: dispose
//   BB12 drug30Fail        -- 30 nM PI signal <= 0: drain
//   BB13 drug30Join        -- join: 100 nM treatment arm begins
//   BB14 drug100Pass       -- 100 nM PI signal > 0: dispose
//   BB15 drug100Fail       -- 100 nM PI signal <= 0: drain
//   BB16 drug100Join       -- join: 300 nM treatment arm begins
//   BB17 drug300Pass       -- 300 nM PI signal > 0: dispose
//   BB18 drug300Fail       -- 300 nM PI signal <= 0: drain
//   BB19 drug300Join       -- join: 1000 nM treatment arm begins
//   BB20 drug1000Pass      -- 1000 nM PI signal > 0: dispose
//   BB21 drug1000Fail      -- 1000 nM PI signal <= 0: drain
//   BB22 drug1000Join      -- join: endpoint MTS + morphology
//   BB23 mtsPass           -- mtsSignal > 0: valid endpoint;
//                             dispose; run morphology QC
//   BB24 mtsFail           -- mtsSignal <= 0: invalid endpoint;
//                             drain all endpoint samples
//   BB25 morphologyPass    -- morphologySignal > 0: valid shape
//                             metrics; dispose
//   BB26 morphologyFail    -- morphologySignal <= 0: invalid;
//                             drain morphology sample
//   BB27 finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Embed PDAC organoids in Matrigel matrix.
//   Organoids (passage 3-8) resuspended in ice-cold Matrigel
//   at ~500 organoids/mL. 40 uL domes seeded per well in a
//   24-well plate and polymerised at 37 C for 20 min before
//   media overlay. BioScript `mix` abstracts the embedding
//   and dome-formation step (no DMF electrode mixing).
//   Matrigel dome solidification modelled as `heat` at 37 C
//   for 20 min.
//
//   NOTE: All 7 treatment conditions use the same organoid
//   batch. Embedding step is shared; media + drug additions
//   are condition-specific from STEP 3 onwards.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

organoidBatch   = dispense 1 units of PDAC_Organoids
matrigelBase    = dispense 1 units of MatrigelMatrix

embeddedOrganoids = mix organoidBatch with matrigelBase for 5s

// Matrigel polymerisation: 37 C for 20 min.
heat embeddedOrganoids at 37c for 20m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Overlay with complete growth media and equilibrate.
//   500 uL complete media (Advanced DMEM/F12 + 10% FBS +
//   EGF + Noggin + R-spondin1 + Wnt3a) added after gelation.
//   Organoids equilibrated at 37 C / 5% CO2 for 24 h before
//   drug addition. 24 h = 1440 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

baseMedia       = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
cultureDomes    = mix embeddedOrganoids with baseMedia for 2s

// 24 h pre-treatment equilibration at 37 C.
heat cultureDomes at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3 (0 nM CONTROL): Vehicle-treated control condition.
//   DMSO vehicle added at equivalent volume to highest drug
//   condition (0.1% v/v final DMSO). Propidium iodide (PI)
//   added at 1 ug/mL final concentration to all wells for
//   continuous dead-cell labelling throughout imaging.
//   PI: excitation 488 nm / emission 617 nm.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

controlMedia    = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
dmsoVehicle     = dispense 1 units of DMSO_Vehicle
piStainCtrl     = dispense 1 units of PropidiumIodide

controlDrug     = mix controlMedia with dmsoVehicle for 5s
controlWell     = mix cultureDomes with controlDrug for 5s
controlWellPI   = mix controlWell with piStainCtrl for 2s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4 (0 nM CONTROL): 72 h timelapse imaging at 37 C.
//   Brightfield images acquired every 4 h (18 frames total).
//   PI fluorescence channel co-acquired at each timepoint.
//   `heat` at 37 C for 4320 m abstracts the full 72 h
//   incubation + concurrent timelapse imaging run.
//   organoidDetection module abstracts:
//     (i)  OrganoID U-Net inference on each brightfield frame
//     (ii) Canny edge + watershed single-organoid identification
//     (iii) Munkres Hungarian tracking across frames
//   Outputs: per-organoid area, count, PI fluorescence,
//   circularity, solidity, eccentricity trajectories.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat controlWellPI at 37c for 4320m

controlSignal   = detect organoidDetection on controlWellPI for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Control viability QC gate
// Pass (BB2): controlSignal > 0 -> organoids detected and
//             growing normally in vehicle control; proceed.
// Fail (BB3): controlSignal <= 0 -> no organoids detected
//             in control (experiment setup failure); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (controlJoin).
// ============================================================

if (controlSignal > 0) {
    dispose controlWellPI
} else {
    drain controlWellPI
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: controlJoin
// Dispose control QC signal. Proceed to 3 nM drug arm.
// ============================================================

dispose controlSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5 (3 nM GEMCITABINE): Lowest dose treatment arm.
//   Gemcitabine: FDA-approved nucleoside analogue (dFdC),
//   inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and DNA synthesis.
//   Serial dilutions prepared in complete media from 10 mM
//   DMSO stock. Final DMSO: 0.1% v/v in all conditions.
//   PI added at same concentration as control (1 ug/mL).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

media3nM        = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
drug3nM         = dispense 1 units of Gemcitabine_3nM
pi3nM           = dispense 1 units of PropidiumIodide

treatMedia3     = mix media3nM with drug3nM for 5s
well3nM         = mix cultureDomes with treatMedia3 for 5s
well3nMpi       = mix well3nM with pi3nM for 2s

heat well3nMpi at 37c for 4320m

signal3nM       = detect organoidDetection on well3nMpi for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: 3 nM arm QC gate
// ============================================================

if (signal3nM > 0) {
    dispose well3nMpi
} else {
    drain well3nMpi
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: drug3Join
// ============================================================

dispose signal3nM

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6 (10 nM GEMCITABINE)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

media10nM       = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
drug10nM        = dispense 1 units of Gemcitabine_10nM
pi10nM          = dispense 1 units of PropidiumIodide

treatMedia10    = mix media10nM with drug10nM for 5s
well10nM        = mix cultureDomes with treatMedia10 for 5s
well10nMpi      = mix well10nM with pi10nM for 2s

heat well10nMpi at 37c for 4320m

signal10nM      = detect organoidDetection on well10nMpi for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: 10 nM arm QC gate
// ============================================================

if (signal10nM > 0) {
    dispose well10nMpi
} else {
    drain well10nMpi
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: drug10Join
// ============================================================

dispose signal10nM

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7 (30 nM GEMCITABINE)
//   Paper reports the clearest dose-response phenotype at
//   30 nM (Fig 3A): organoid area increases initially then
//   decreases; organoid count drops sharply above 10 nM.
//   PI fluorescence rises proportionally to concentration.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

media30nM       = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
drug30nM        = dispense 1 units of Gemcitabine_30nM
pi30nM          = dispense 1 units of PropidiumIodide

treatMedia30    = mix media30nM with drug30nM for 5s
well30nM        = mix cultureDomes with treatMedia30 for 5s
well30nMpi      = mix well30nM with pi30nM for 2s

heat well30nMpi at 37c for 4320m

signal30nM      = detect organoidDetection on well30nMpi for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB11 / BB12: 30 nM arm QC gate
// ============================================================

if (signal30nM > 0) {
    dispose well30nMpi
} else {
    drain well30nMpi
}

// ============================================================
// BB13: drug30Join
// ============================================================

dispose signal30nM

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8 (100 nM GEMCITABINE)
//   At 100 nM the PI total fluorescence signal is paradoxically
//   stronger than at 1000 nM (Fig 3C). OrganoID's per-organoid
//   area normalisation corrects this discrepancy (Fig 3C
//   bottom), aligning 100 nM and 1000 nM responses correctly.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

media100nM      = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
drug100nM       = dispense 1 units of Gemcitabine_100nM
pi100nM         = dispense 1 units of PropidiumIodide

treatMedia100   = mix media100nM with drug100nM for 5s
well100nM       = mix cultureDomes with treatMedia100 for 5s
well100nMpi     = mix well100nM with pi100nM for 2s

heat well100nMpi at 37c for 4320m

signal100nM     = detect organoidDetection on well100nMpi for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB14 / BB15: 100 nM arm QC gate
// ============================================================

if (signal100nM > 0) {
    dispose well100nMpi
} else {
    drain well100nMpi
}

// ============================================================
// BB16: drug100Join
// ============================================================

dispose signal100nM

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9 (300 nM GEMCITABINE)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

media300nM      = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
drug300nM       = dispense 1 units of Gemcitabine_300nM
pi300nM         = dispense 1 units of PropidiumIodide

treatMedia300   = mix media300nM with drug300nM for 5s
well300nM       = mix cultureDomes with treatMedia300 for 5s
well300nMpi     = mix well300nM with pi300nM for 2s

heat well300nMpi at 37c for 4320m

signal300nM     = detect organoidDetection on well300nMpi for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB17 / BB18: 300 nM arm QC gate
// ============================================================

if (signal300nM > 0) {
    dispose well300nMpi
} else {
    drain well300nMpi
}

// ============================================================
// BB19: drug300Join
// ============================================================

dispose signal300nM

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10 (1000 nM GEMCITABINE): Highest dose arm.
//   At 1000 nM essentially all organoids are killed; area
//   and count drop to near-zero by 48 h. Circularity and
//   solidity decrease markedly; eccentricity increases,
//   reflecting disrupted, irregular organoid architecture
//   (Cohen's d effect sizes shown in paper Fig. 3E).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

media1000nM     = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
drug1000nM      = dispense 1 units of Gemcitabine_1000nM
pi1000nM        = dispense 1 units of PropidiumIodide

treatMedia1000  = mix media1000nM with drug1000nM for 5s
well1000nM      = mix cultureDomes with treatMedia1000 for 5s
well1000nMpi    = mix well1000nM with pi1000nM for 2s

heat well1000nMpi at 37c for 4320m

signal1000nM    = detect organoidDetection on well1000nMpi for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB20 / BB21: 1000 nM arm QC gate
// ============================================================

if (signal1000nM > 0) {
    dispose well1000nMpi
} else {
    drain well1000nMpi
}

// ============================================================
// BB22: drug1000Join
// All six drug treatment arms + control have completed.
// Endpoint assays begin: MTS viability + morphology analysis.
// ============================================================

dispose signal1000nM

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: MTS endpoint viability assay (gold standard).
//   MTS reagent: (3-(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-5-(3-carboxy-
//   methoxyphenyl)-2-(4-sulfophenyl)-2H-tetrazolium).
//   Metabolically active cells reduce MTS to formazan
//   (absorbance 490 nm). Used as reference EC50 benchmark.
//   Paper reports MTS EC50 = 26.13 nM (Fig. 3D).
//   Assay incubation: 4 h at 37 C = 240 min.
//
//   NOTE: MTS is run on a fresh organoid aliquot from the
//   same batch (endpoint only), not on the timelapse wells.
//   `mtsViabilityDetection` abstracts the 490 nm absorbance
//   readout across all drug concentrations at endpoint.
//   Only one representative endpoint droplet (vehicle control
//   matched fresh organoids) is encoded here for the QC gate;
//   the dose-response curve fitting is an off-device analysis.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

mtsOrganoids    = dispense 1 units of PDAC_Organoids
mtsMatrix       = dispense 1 units of MatrigelMatrix
mtsMedia        = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia

mtsEmbedded     = mix mtsOrganoids with mtsMatrix for 5s
heat mtsEmbedded at 37c for 20m

mtsWell         = mix mtsEmbedded with mtsMedia for 2s

// MTS incubation: 4 h at 37 C (= 240 min).
heat mtsWell at 37c for 240m

mtsSignal       = detect mtsViabilityDetection on mtsWell for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB23 / BB24: MTS endpoint QC gate
// Pass (BB23): mtsSignal > 0 -> valid formazan absorbance
//              detected; dispose MTS well; run morphology.
// Fail (BB24): mtsSignal <= 0 -> MTS assay failure (reagent
//              or instrument issue); drain MTS well.
// Both branches converge at BB25/BB26 (morphology gate).
// ============================================================

if (mtsSignal > 0) {
    dispose mtsWell
} else {
    drain mtsWell
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 12: Single-organoid morphology analysis.
//   OrganoID computes per-organoid shape metrics from the
//   timelapse segmentation masks for each treatment condition:
//     - Circularity: inverse ratio of organoid perimeter to
//       perimeter of a perfect circle with equal area.
//       (Higher = rounder; decreases with gemcitabine dose.)
//     - Solidity: ratio of organoid area to its convex hull.
//       (Higher = more compact; decreases with drug dose.)
//     - Eccentricity: elliptical deviation from a circle.
//       (Increases with drug dose; reflects disorganised shape.)
//   Sigmoidal dose-response EC50 (paper Fig. S7):
//     Circularity EC50  = 28.47 nM
//     Solidity EC50     = 27.05 nM
//     Eccentricity EC50 = 18.08 nM
//   Cohen's d effect sizes computed across concentration pairs
//   (paper Fig. 3E). Moderate-to-large effects at EC50 crossing.
//
//   `morphologyAnalysis` module abstracts the scikit-image
//   regionprops computation on OrganoID-labeled organoid masks,
//   plus Cohen's d statistic calculation across conditions.
//
//   NOTE: Morphology QC uses a fresh brightfield image of the
//   vehicle-control well at endpoint as a reference sample
//   to verify the segmentation pipeline is still operational.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

morphMedia      = dispense 1 units of CompleteMedia
morphOrganoids  = dispense 1 units of PDAC_Organoids
morphMatrix     = dispense 1 units of MatrigelMatrix

morphEmbedded   = mix morphOrganoids with morphMatrix for 5s
heat morphEmbedded at 37c for 20m

morphWell       = mix morphEmbedded with morphMedia for 2s

morphologySignal = detect morphologyAnalysis on morphWell for 60s

// ============================================================
// BB25 / BB26: Morphology QC gate
// Pass (BB25): morphologySignal > 0 -> circularity, solidity,
//              and eccentricity metrics successfully computed
//              by OrganoID; dispose morphology reference well.
// Fail (BB26): morphologySignal <= 0 -> morphology pipeline
//              failure (segmentation returned no organoids in
//              reference image); drain morphology well.
// Both branches converge at BB27 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (morphologySignal > 0) {
    dispose morphWell
} else {
    drain morphWell
}

// ============================================================
// BB27: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all remaining QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends. Off-device analysis:
//   - EC50 curve fitting (organoid area, PI fluorescence,
//     area-normalised PI, MTS) using 4-parameter logistic.
//   - Cohen's d effect size heatmaps (Fig. 3E).
//   - Tracking accuracy assessment vs manual ground truth.
// ============================================================

dispose mtsSignal
dispose morphologySignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module depletionQC
module fluorescenceDetection

manifest ProteinAG_Beads
manifest AntiHSA_Beads
manifest PlasmaProteinSample
manifest FITC_IgG
manifest FITC_HSA
manifest Hemopexin_Sample
manifest PBS_Buffer
manifest PluronicF68

// ============================================================
// A Digital Microfluidic Platform for Human Plasma Protein
// Depletion
// Mei, N. - M.Sc. Thesis, Queen's University, 2014
// Supervisors: Dr. Richard D. Oleschuk & Dr. Aaron A. Wheeler
//
// Protocol: Automated on-chip depletion of high-abundance
// plasma proteins (HSA and IgG) using paramagnetic bead-based
// immunocapture on a two-plate DMF device. Eight-step procedure
// performed on four samples simultaneously. Depleted samples
// collected for downstream MALDI-MS or LC-MS/MS analysis.
//
// KEY REAGENTS:
//   PureProteome(TM) Protein A/G Mix Magnetic Beads (10 um,
//   LSKMAGAG02, Millipore) -- captures IgG via Protein A/G
//   PureProteome(TM) Albumin Magnetic Beads (10 um,
//   LSKMAGL02, Millipore) -- captures HSA via anti-HSA coating
//   Pluronic F-68 (0.05%) in PBS -- selected surfactant;
//   F-127 and L64 interfered with bead binding (Chapter 3).
//
// DEVICE OPERATION:
//   Two-plate glass DMF chip fabricated at TNFC, U of Toronto.
//   Driving voltage: 100-120 V_rms, 10 kHz sine wave.
//   Unit droplet volume: 4 uL. Four 4 uL droplets merged per run.
//   Automated controller: custom Microdrop software + Pogo-pin
//   interface. Motor-controlled magnetic lens assembly (~600 uN
//   force; threshold 470 uN). Incubation via figure-8 pattern
//   across 4 electrodes for continuous mixing.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- entry: bead washing, IgG+HSA
//                             depletion, sample mixing, separation
//   BB2  depletionPass     -- depletionSignal > 0: depletion
//                             successful; dispose depleted sample
//   BB3  depletionFail     -- depletionSignal <= 0: no depletion
//                             signal; drain
//   BB4  depletionJoin     -- join: fluorescence kinetics branch
//   BB5  fluorescencePass  -- fluorescenceSignal > 0: valid
//                             FITC-IgG depletion kinetics signal;
//                             dispose kinetics droplet
//   BB6  fluorescenceFail  -- fluorescenceSignal <= 0: no signal;
//                             drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Load reagents onto device.
//   One droplet each of mixed beads (Protein A/G + anti-HSA)
//   and protein sample loaded into reservoirs (4 uL each).
//   Protein sample: 2 mg/mL IgG + 0.5 mg/mL HSA +
//   0.1 mg/mL hemopexin (low-abundance marker) in PBS + F-68.
//   Beads pre-diluted off-chip: Protein A/G at 1:4 from stock;
//   anti-HSA at 1:2 from stock, in PBS.
//   NOTE: `mix` here abstracts reservoir loading (pipette
//   addition), not DMF electrode-based mixing.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

agBeads       = dispense 1 units of ProteinAG_Beads
antiHsaBeads  = dispense 1 units of AntiHSA_Beads

// Combine bead types into single mixed-bead suspension.
// Both bead types used simultaneously for dual depletion
// of IgG (via Protein A/G) and HSA (via anti-HSA coating).
mixedBeads    = mix agBeads with antiHsaBeads for 5s

proteinSample = dispense 1 units of PlasmaProteinSample
pbsBuffer     = dispense 1 units of PBS_Buffer
pluronic      = dispense 1 units of PluronicF68

// Prepare protein sample with Pluronic F-68 additive for
// on-chip droplet stability and prevention of non-specific
// protein adsorption (Chapter 3: F-68 selected as optimal
// Pluronic -- does not interfere with bead binding).
samplePlusMix = mix proteinSample with pluronic for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Actuate magnetic beads onto device array.
//   Mixed bead droplet is moved from reservoir into the
//   active electrode array under DMF actuation (100-120 V_rms,
//   10 kHz). Beads remain suspended in droplet during transfer.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beadDroplet   = mix mixedBeads with pbsBuffer for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Engage magnet -- immobilize beads.
//   Motor-controlled magnet positioned below chip (~600 uN
//   force) to pellet paramagnetic beads onto device surface.
//   `heat` at room temperature (25 C) for 30s models the
//   immobilization dwell time while magnet is engaged.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat beadDroplet at 25c for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Remove supernatant from beads.
//   Supernatant (bead storage buffer) is actuated away from
//   immobilized bead pellet by DMF electrode switching.
//   Supernatant is moved to waste reservoir and wicked off
//   with KimWipes. Beads remain immobilized on device.
//   `mix` with buffer models the supernatant-removal wash step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

washBuffer1   = dispense 1 units of PBS_Buffer
beadWash1     = mix beadDroplet with washBuffer1 for 5s

// Immobilize beads again after wash addition.
heat beadWash1 at 25c for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Merge protein sample droplet with bead pellet.
//   Sample droplet actuated over immobilized beads; magnet
//   disengaged to resuspend beads into protein sample.
//   Mixed bead-sample droplet now contains IgG, HSA,
//   hemopexin, and both bead types.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beadProteinMix = mix beadWash1 with samplePlusMix for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Active incubation -- figure-8 mixing for 10 min.
//   Droplet moved continuously in figure-8 pattern across
//   four electrodes (100-120 V_rms, 10 kHz) for 10 min to
//   ensure particle dispersal and maximize antibody:antigen
//   contact. Final depletion efficiency: up to 98% for both
//   IgG and HSA (Chapter 4, Fig 4.5).
//   10 min = 600s.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat beadProteinMix at 37c for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Re-engage magnet -- immobilize bead-protein complex.
//   Magnet re-engaged beneath chip. Beads (now carrying
//   captured IgG and HSA) are re-pelletted. Depleted protein
//   supernatant is actuated away from bead pellet by DMF.
//   `heat` models the bead re-immobilization dwell.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat beadProteinMix at 25c for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Separate depleted sample from beads.
//   Supernatant droplet (depleted of IgG and HSA; hemopexin
//   retained) is actuated to collection reservoir via DMF.
//   Top plate removed off-chip; droplet transferred by pipette
//   for downstream MALDI-MS or LC-MS/MS analysis.
//   QC: detect fluorescence signal on collected depleted sample.
//   (Fluorescence characterisation uses FITC-IgG as proxy;
//   see Section 4.1.6.)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

washBuffer2   = dispense 1 units of PBS_Buffer
depletedSample = mix beadProteinMix with washBuffer2 for 5s

depletionSignal = detect depletionQC on depletedSample for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Depletion QC gate
// Pass (BB2): depletionSignal > 0 -> depletion successful;
//             dispose depleted sample (collected for analysis).
// Fail (BB3): depletionSignal <= 0 -> depletion failed;
//             drain.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (depletionJoin).
// ============================================================

if (depletionSignal > 0) {
    dispose depletedSample
} else {
    drain depletedSample
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: depletionJoin
// Dispose depletion QC signal. Control falls into fluorescence
// kinetics characterisation branch (BB5/BB6).
// (Section 4.1.6: depletion kinetics probed with FITC-IgG
// using 30s incubation intervals up to 10 min total.)
// ============================================================

dispose depletionSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// Fluorescence kinetics branch:
//   FITC-IgG (0.5 mg/mL) is mixed with Protein A/G beads
//   and incubated for 30 s (one kinetic interval). Supernatant
//   extracted and measured on PHERAstar plate reader
//   (485 nm excitation / 520 nm emission, gain 100).
//   Repeated up to 10 min total (20 x 30s intervals, 3 reps).
//   Here, one representative 30s incubation is encoded;
//   BioScript has no loop construct so iterative intervals
//   are abstracted as a single representative kinetics step.
//
//   For HSA kinetics: identical process with FITC-HSA
//   (0.5 mg/mL, gain 125) and anti-HSA beads.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fitcIgG        = dispense 1 units of FITC_IgG
kineticsBeads  = dispense 1 units of ProteinAG_Beads

kineticsMix    = mix fitcIgG with kineticsBeads for 5s

// 30s kinetic incubation interval (figure-8 mixing).
heat kineticsMix at 25c for 30s

// Immobilize beads; extract supernatant for fluorescence read.
heat kineticsMix at 25c for 30s

pbsKinetics    = dispense 1 units of PBS_Buffer
kineticsSupern = mix kineticsMix with pbsKinetics for 5s

fluorescenceSignal = detect fluorescenceDetection on kineticsSupern for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Fluorescence kinetics QC gate
// Pass (BB5): fluorescenceSignal > 0 -> valid FITC-IgG
//             depletion signal detected; dispose kinetics droplet.
// Fail (BB6): fluorescenceSignal <= 0 -> no signal (reagent
//             failure or bead aggregation); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (fluorescenceSignal > 0) {
    dispose kineticsSupern
} else {
    drain kineticsSupern
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose fluorescence kinetics QC signal.
// Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose fluorescenceSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module nfkbTranslocationDetection

manifest BoneMarrowCells
manifest mCSF_DifferentiationMedia
manifest PBS
manifest FibronectinSolution
manifest CompleteRPMI
manifest Hoechst33342
manifest TNFalpha
manifest LipidA
manifest Pam2CSK4
manifest R848_Resiquimod
manifest CpG_ODN1668

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Accurate Processing of Ultra-Short Immune Signals by Single
// Macrophages
// Mercado-Vasquez, Vizzard, Celiker, Xia, Wang, Padhi, Tay
// bioRxiv 2025.10.26.684377
// DOI: https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.10.26.684377
//
// Protocol: Primary bone-marrow-derived macrophage (BMDM)
// isolation and differentiation from mVenus-RelA (RelA^V/V)
// C57BL/6J transgenic mice, seeding into multilayer PDMS
// microfluidic culture chambers, nuclear staining, and
// short-pulse stimulation (1-second) with five NF-kB ligands
// (TNF, Lipid A, Pam2CSK4, R848, CpG) delivered by pneumatic
// valve control at 4 psi. NF-kB nuclear translocation of the
// mVenus-RelA p65 reporter is tracked by time-lapse
// epifluorescence microscopy (6-min intervals, 6 h total) and
// quantified as nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio.
//
// LIGANDS AND DOSES (from paper Methods/Figure 1A):
//   TNF alpha     10  ng/mL   -- cytokine (TNFR pathway)
//   Lipid A       10  ng/mL   -- bacterial TLR4 ligand
//   Pam2CSK4       3  ng/mL   -- bacterial TLR2/6 ligand
//   R848 Resiquimod 300 ng/mL -- viral TLR7 ligand (endosomal)
//   CpG ODN 1668  300 nM      -- DNA TLR9 ligand
//
// SCOPE NOTE: Each ligand stimulation arm is encoded as a
// sequential independent sub-experiment (STEP 4a-e) within
// a single main BB. In practice the five conditions are run
// in parallel across separate microfluidic chambers on the
// same chip; BioScript sequential encoding abstracts this.
// Each 1-second pulse is modelled as a 1s `mix` of the
// pre-loaded cell droplet with the ligand stimulus, which
// represents pneumatic valve opening (4 psi, PEEK tubing)
// for exactly 1 second to deliver the chemical pulse.
// The paper validates 1-second pulse delivery with 6%
// timing variability (CV = 0.06) across all analyzed cells.
//
// DURATION NOTES:
//   7-9 day BMDM differentiation -> 9 days = 12960m
//   4-6 h microfluidic attachment -> 6 h = 360m
//   Imaging window = 6 h = 360m (6-min frames over 60 cycles)
//   Overnight = 720m (not used here; explicit durations given)
//   BioScript duration tokens: s and m only (no h token)
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main             -- BMDM prep, device priming, cell
//                           seeding, nuclear staining, five
//                           1-second ligand pulse deliveries
//   BB2  responsePass     -- nfkbSignal > 0: NF-kB nuclear
//                           translocation detected; dispose
//                           stimulated cell droplets
//   BB3  responseFail     -- nfkbSignal <= 0: no detectable
//                           NF-kB response; drain droplets
//   BB4  finalCleanup     -- terminal: dispose QC signal,
//                           experiment ends
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: BMDM isolation and differentiation.
//   Bone marrow cells flushed from tibias and femurs of 6-12
//   week-old male and female RelA^V/V C57BL/6J mice and
//   resuspended in complete RPMI supplemented with 20 ng/mL
//   m-CSF. Cells differentiated for 7-9 days (encoded as
//   9 days = 12960 min) at 37 C / 5% CO2 before harvesting.
//   m-CSF: Peprotech murine m-CSF, reconstituted in ultrapure
//   water, stored at -80 C.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rawBoneMarrow          = dispense 1 units of BoneMarrowCells
mCSF_Media             = dispense 1 units of mCSF_DifferentiationMedia
differentiatingBMDMs   = mix rawBoneMarrow with mCSF_Media for 5s

// 9-day differentiation at 37 C / 5% CO2 (= 12960 min)
heat differentiatingBMDMs at 37c for 12960m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Microfluidic device priming with fibronectin.
//   Multilayer PDMS device fabricated from silicon wafer
//   molds (10:1 PDMS monomer:catalyst; oxygen plasma bonding,
//   overnight bake). Chambers pre-incubated with 0.2 mg/mL
//   fibronectin in PBS to promote cell adhesion.
//   `mix` abstracts the channel-flow fibronectin coating step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fibronectin            = dispense 1 units of FibronectinSolution
pbsPrime               = dispense 1 units of PBS
coatedChamber          = mix fibronectin with pbsPrime for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: BMDM seeding and equilibration.
//   Harvested BMDMs on day 7-9, resuspended in complete RPMI
//   (phenol red-free), and seeded at 10^6 cells/mL into
//   fibronectin-coated microfluidic chambers. Cells left
//   unperturbed for 4-6 h (encoded as 6 h = 360 min) at
//   37 C / 5% CO2 until attached and equilibrated.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

harvestedBMDMs         = mix differentiatingBMDMs with coatedChamber for 1m

// 6-hour attachment and equilibration at 37 C (= 360 min)
heat harvestedBMDMs at 37c for 360m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Nuclear tracking stain.
//   Cells stained with 1 uM Hoechst 33342 (Invitrogen) in
//   phenol red-free RPMI per producer instructions to enable
//   nuclear segmentation and cell tracking throughout the
//   time-lapse imaging run.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

hoechstStain           = dispense 1 units of Hoechst33342
completeRPMI_Stain     = dispense 1 units of CompleteRPMI
stainedBMDMs           = mix harvestedBMDMs with hoechstStain for 1m
equilibratedBMDMs      = mix stainedBMDMs with completeRPMI_Stain for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5a: TNF alpha 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   Proinflammatory cytokine TNF alpha (R&D Systems murine
//   TNFalpha), 10 ng/mL in complete phenol red-free RPMI.
//   Stored on ice during experiment; delivered at 4 psi via
//   PEEK tubing (VICI). Pneumatic valve open for 1 second.
//   Models intracellular (cell-cell) cytokine signaling
//   via TNFR1 (CD120a) pathway.
//   `mix for 1s` abstracts 1-second pneumatic valve pulse.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfStimulus            = dispense 1 units of TNFalpha
tnfStimulatedCells     = mix equilibratedBMDMs with tnfStimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5b: Lipid A 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   Ultrapure Lipid A from E. coli O111:B4 (Invivogen),
//   10 ng/mL. Bacterial TLR4 ligand. Analogous to LPS
//   component targeting the extracellular receptor TLR4.
//   Delivered at 4 psi for 1 second.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lipidAStimulus         = dispense 1 units of LipidA
lipidAStimulatedCells  = mix equilibratedBMDMs with lipidAStimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5c: Pam2CSK4 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   PAM2CSK4 (Invivogen), 3 ng/mL. Diacylated lipopeptide
//   analog of bacterial outer membrane components. Induces
//   TLR2/TLR6 stable heterodimer formation (m-shape complex)
//   activating MyD88/TIRAP downstream NF-kB via IKK module.
//   Delivered at 4 psi for 1 second.
//   NOTE: Pam2CSK4 shows highest NF-kB response amplitude
//   and maximal channel capacity (CC_max ~0.82) under 1-second
//   pulse, surpassing Lipid A which is most potent under
//   persistent stimulation (key finding of the paper).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pam2csk4Stimulus       = dispense 1 units of Pam2CSK4
pamStimulatedCells     = mix equilibratedBMDMs with pam2csk4Stimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5d: R848 (Resiquimod) 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   R848 Resiquimod (Invivogen), 300 ng/mL. Small molecule
//   viral RNA mimic that binds TLR7 located in the endosome
//   (intracellular receptor pathway). Induces TLR7 homodimer
//   formation activating MyD88 downstream. Modelled here as
//   endosomal receptor-mediated pathogen detection.
//   Delivered at 4 psi for 1 second.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

r848Stimulus           = dispense 1 units of R848_Resiquimod
r848StimulatedCells    = mix equilibratedBMDMs with r848Stimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5e: CpG ODN 1668 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   ODN 1668 CpG (Invivogen), 300 nM. Single-stranded CpG
//   DNA dinucleotide targeting TLR9. TLR9 is an endosomal
//   receptor for foreign/pathogenic DNA. Lowest pairwise
//   mutual information (MI = 0) vs CpG, indicating weakest
//   distinguishability from TNF under 1-second pulse.
//   Delivered at 4 psi for 1 second.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cpgStimulus            = dispense 1 units of CpG_ODN1668
cpgStimulatedCells     = mix equilibratedBMDMs with cpgStimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Time-lapse NF-kB live-cell imaging.
//   Nikon Ti2 epifluorescence microscope; 20x magnification;
//   Hamamatsu ORCA-Flash4.0 V2 camera. mVenus-RelA channel
//   (508 nm excitation) and Hoechst channel (395 nm) acquired
//   every 6 minutes for 6 hours = 60 imaging cycles = 360 min
//   total. No photobleaching or phototoxicity observed.
//   Custom MATLAB image processing software segments nuclei
//   via Hoechst, extracts nuclear/cytoplasmic mVenus-RelA
//   ratio per cell per frame. > 200 individual cells analyzed
//   per stimulation condition.
//
//   QC gate: nfkbTranslocationDetection module abstracts the
//   MATLAB image analysis pipeline and reports a positive
//   signal when NF-kB nuclear translocation peak amplitude
//   exceeds the activation threshold (2 s.d. above unstimulated
//   mean nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio). The Pam2CSK4 condition
//   is used as the QC reference (strongest responder under
//   1-second pulse). All five stimulated cell populations
//   (STEP 5a-e) are pooled into a single merged imaging
//   droplet for the QC detect call.
//
//   NOTE ON COMPILER CONSTRAINT: The five stimulated cell
//   populations (tnfStimulatedCells, lipidAStimulatedCells,
//   pamStimulatedCells, r848StimulatedCells, cpgStimulatedCells)
//   are merged sequentially via distinct intermediate variables
//   (no variable reassignment) before the detect call.
//   This abstracts the single Nikon Ti2 field-of-view that
//   images all five stimulation chambers during the 6-hour
//   time-lapse run. Detection duration = 360 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Sequential merging of all five stimulated cell populations
// for the unified imaging and QC detect call.
// Distinct intermediate variable names required (no reassignment).

imagingPool_ab     = mix tnfStimulatedCells with lipidAStimulatedCells for 1m
imagingPool_abc    = mix imagingPool_ab with pamStimulatedCells for 1m
imagingPool_abcd   = mix imagingPool_abc with r848StimulatedCells for 1m
imagingPool_all    = mix imagingPool_abcd with cpgStimulatedCells for 1m

// 6-hour live imaging incubation at 37 C / 5% CO2 (= 360 min)
// Microfluidic in-situ microbiome box maintains temperature,
// humidity and CO2 conditions throughout the imaging run.
heat imagingPool_all at 37c for 360m

// NF-kB nuclear translocation QC detect (6-hour imaging window)
// nfkbTranslocationDetection: custom MATLAB-based image analysis
// module quantifying mVenus-RelA nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio.
// Positive signal: activation threshold exceeded (peak > 2 s.d.
// above unstimulated mean) in >= 1 detected cell.
nfkbSignal = detect nfkbTranslocationDetection on imagingPool_all for 360m

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: NF-kB translocation QC gate
// Pass (BB2): nfkbSignal > 0 -> nuclear NF-kB translocation
//             detected above activation threshold; ligand
//             delivery and cell response confirmed; dispose
//             stimulated cell imaging pool.
// Fail (BB3): nfkbSignal <= 0 -> no detectable NF-kB response
//             (cells non-viable, ligand delivery failure, or
//             imaging artifact); drain imaging pool.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (nfkbSignal > 0) {
    dispose imagingPool_all
} else {
    drain imagingPool_all
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose NF-kB QC signal. Experiment ends.
// Downstream quantitative analysis (8 dynamic NF-kB features:
// early AUC, late AUC, amplitude, speed, duration, oscillations,
// activation time, time to peak; plus k-means subpopulation
// clustering into early responders, late responders, and
// non-responders; plus channel capacity and mutual information
// calculations) performed off-chip in MATLAB 2024a using
// custom software (github.com/tay-lab/Accurate-processing-
// of-ultra-short-immune-signals-by-single-macrophages).
// ============================================================

dispose nfkbSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module immunoassayDetection

manifest AntiHumanIgG_Capture
manifest HumanIgG_Sample
manifest FITC_AntiHumanIgG_Detection
manifest PluronicF127_RinseBuffer
manifest BovineSerum_Spiked

// ============================================================
// A Digital Microfluidic Approach to Heterogeneous Immunoassays
// Miller, Ng, Uddayasankar, Wheeler - Anal. Bioanal. Chem. (2011)
// DOI: 10.1007/s00216-010-4368-2
//
// Protocol: Heterogeneous sandwich immunoassay for Human IgG
// on a DMF device. Capture antibody (Fc-specific anti-human IgG)
// is pre-spotted (physisorbed) on the Cytop/ITO top plate.
// All washing and incubation steps are performed via DMF
// actuation at 75 V_rms, 18 kHz.
//
// DEVICE CONFIGURATION:
//   Bottom plate: patterned Cr electrodes, 1x1 mm actuation
//     electrodes + 2.5x2.5 mm reservoir electrodes.
//     Coated with 2 um parylene C + 130 nm Cytop.
//   Top plate: unpatterned ITO/glass, 130 nm Cytop.
//     Pre-spotted with capture antibody (0.22-2.2 mg/mL,
//     250-500 nL per spot, air-dried, rinsed with 0.1% w/v
//     Pluronic F-127 in DI water, dried before use).
//   Gap spacer: ~150 um (two pieces double-sided tape).
//   Droplet volume: 1 uL unit droplets.
//   Driving potential: 75 V_rms, 18 kHz function generator.
//   Surroundings: air (no carrier oil; Pluronic prevents NSB).
//   Detection: PheraStar plate reader, lambda_ex=485 nm,
//     lambda_em=520 nm, focal height=12.1 mm.
//
// REAGENT NOTES:
//   All solutions contain 0.00625% (w/v) Pluronic F-127
//   (except diluted serum: 0.0113% Pluronic F-127) to
//   prevent non-specific protein adsorption to device surfaces
//   and to facilitate DMF droplet movement. This eliminates
//   the need for BSA blocking steps used in well-plate methods.
//   All solutions buffered in 10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7.4.
//   Pluronic F-127 is included in the rinse buffer manifest.
//
// ASSAY SUMMARY (sandwich format):
//   1. Sample droplet (antigen) cycled 10x over capture spot
//   2. Rinse buffer droplet (2 passes) removes non-specific IgG
//   3. Detection antibody droplet cycled 10x over spot
//   4. Final rinse buffer droplet (2 passes)
//   5. Fluorescence readout on plate reader
//
// TIMING:
//   Conventional 96-well plate: up to 2 days (12 h antibody
//     immobilization + 2-12 h BSA blocking + 1-2 h per
//     binding step).
//   DMF assay: 2.5 h total from spot printing to completion.
//   Diffusion distances ~100x smaller + active droplet transport
//   eliminates diffusion-limited kinetics.
//
// THROUGHPUT:
//   Multiplexed devices: 6-10 platforms wired in series,
//   electrodes bussed together for simultaneous parallel
//   control of replicate assays. This .bs file encodes a
//   single-platform assay; replicate platforms share identical
//   protocol steps driven simultaneously.
//
// WASTE REDUCTION:
//   Well plate: ~3.5 mL per well.
//   DMF: ~4 uL per assay spot (reduction of ~3 orders of
//   magnitude).
//
// VALIDATION:
//   Analyte: Human IgG (0-5 ng total mass on device).
//   Matrix tested: bovine serum diluted 100x, spiked with
//     human IgG (0-10 ug/mL). Bovine IgG at ~100 ug/mL
//     (100-fold excess over highest human IgG doped). No
//     significant cross-reactivity observed.
//   Dynamic range: ~1 order of magnitude (R^2=0.9964 on DMF).
//   Reproducibility: %RSD < 25% per data point (3-5 replicates).
//
// FIX (v2): captureSpot is never disposed -- it models the
//   physical antibody spot immobilized on the ITO top plate,
//   which persists throughout all assay steps. In v1,
//   antigenBound was disposed after STEP 3 but then reused
//   in STEP 4 (rinse) and STEP 6 (detection antibody), causing
//   a use-after-dispose in BioScript's data-flow model.
//   Fix: all downstream mix steps reference captureSpot
//   directly. Only liquid droplets (antigenBound,
//   rinseStep1, sandwichComplex, rinseStep2) are disposed.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- sample incubation, rinse 1,
//                             detection antibody incubation,
//                             rinse 2, fluorescence detection
//   BB2  assayPass         -- assaySignal > 0: valid FITC signal
//                             detected; dispose final droplet
//   BB3  assayFail         -- assaySignal <= 0: no signal;
//                             drain final droplet (failed assay)
//   BB4  finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose QC signal;
//                             experiment ends
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRE-CONDITION: Capture antibody spot already immobilized on
//   Cytop/ITO top plate before device assembly.
//   Fc-specific anti-human IgG spotted at 0.22-2.2 mg/mL,
//   250-500 nL. Air-dried, rinsed with 0.1% Pluronic F-127,
//   dried. Top plate assembled onto bottom plate with spacer.
//   Reservoirs loaded with 1-uL aliquots of antigen sample
//   and rinse buffer before assembly.
//
// NOTE ON CYTOP vs TEFLON-AF:
//   Teflon-AF (50 nm, standard DMF coating) was found to be
//   too fragile for surface-based assays -- antibody printing
//   caused tears that prevented actuation. Cytop (130 nm)
//   was used instead for its greater mechanical ruggedness,
//   allowing spot drying and rinsing without delamination.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Dispense antigen sample droplet.
//   Human IgG in 10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7.4 with 0.00625%
//   Pluronic F-127. Concentrations tested: 0-1 ug/mL (on-chip
//   volume 1 uL = 0-1 ng per assay; standard curve range
//   0-5 ng total IgG per spot as reported in Fig. 4).
//   For serum experiments: bovine serum diluted 100x, spiked
//   with human IgG 0-10 ug/mL (serum uses 0.0113% Pluronic).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sampleDroplet = dispense 1 units of HumanIgG_Sample

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Dispense capture spot (surface-bound antibody).
//   captureSpot models the pre-immobilized Fc-specific
//   anti-human IgG spot on the Cytop/ITO top plate.
//   This variable is NEVER disposed -- it represents a
//   physical surface feature that persists for all assay
//   steps, not a liquid droplet consumed by actuation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

captureSpot = dispense 1 units of AntiHumanIgG_Capture

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Sample incubation - antigen binding to capture spot.
//   DMF actuation (75 V_rms, 18 kHz) translates the 1-uL
//   sample droplet across the capture antibody spot and cycles
//   it back and forth 10 times (Fig. 2a/b).
//   Active transport eliminates diffusion-limited kinetics:
//   diffusion distances are ~100x smaller than in well plates,
//   and droplet motion continually replenishes antigen at the
//   surface. This reduces binding time dramatically relative
//   to static well-plate incubation (1-2 h per step).
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts 10-pass cyclic actuation across
//   the capture antibody spot on the ITO top plate.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

antigenBound = mix sampleDroplet with captureSpot for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Sample removal.
//   Sample droplet withdrawn to reservoir via DMF actuation.
//   Waste droplet (containing unbound IgG and matrix proteins)
//   manually removed from chip with a laboratory tissue (Fig. 2b).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dispose antigenBound

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: First rinse - remove non-specifically bound IgG.
//   1-uL rinse buffer droplet (10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7.4,
//   0.00625% Pluronic F-127) is actuated across the spot in
//   2 passes to wash away non-specifically adsorbed proteins.
//   Two passes shown in Fig. 3 (inset) to be sufficient for
//   removing NSB while retaining specific binding.
//   Rinse droplet removed to waste reservoir after completion.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts 2-pass rinse actuation.
//   References captureSpot (persistent surface), not the
//   disposed antigenBound droplet variable.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rinseBuffer1 = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127_RinseBuffer
rinseStep1   = mix rinseBuffer1 with captureSpot for 2m

dispose rinseStep1

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Device reload for detection antibody.
//   After removing sample waste and rinse waste, the device
//   top plate (Cytop/ITO with capture spot) is replaced and
//   the bottom plate reservoirs are reloaded with 1-uL
//   aliquots of FITC-anti-human IgG (Fab-specific, raised
//   in goat) and rinse buffer (Fig. 2c).
//   The detection antibody solution contains 0.00625% Pluronic.
//
// NOTE ON DETECTION ANTIBODY BINDING (from Fig. 3):
//   Binding curve shows saturation after only 2 passes of the
//   detection antibody droplet across the antigen spot.
//   10 passes are used in the optimized protocol to eliminate
//   variance from fluid movement timing. Pluronic in the droplet
//   does not strip previously bound antibody during subsequent
//   passes, confirming binding stability.
//   Reynolds number ~210 (laminar flow); spot captures antibody
//   from the layer in direct contact with the surface per pass.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

detectionAntibody = dispense 1 units of FITC_AntiHumanIgG_Detection

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Detection antibody incubation.
//   DMF delivers the FITC-labeled detection antibody (Fab-
//   specific anti-human IgG) to the assay spot and cycles it
//   back and forth 10 times (Fig. 2c/d).
//   The detection antibody binds the antigen (human IgG)
//   already captured on the spot, completing the sandwich.
//   FITC label: excitation 485 nm, emission 520 nm.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts 10-pass cyclic actuation across
//   the assay spot for detection antibody binding.
//   References captureSpot (persistent surface).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sandwichComplex = mix detectionAntibody with captureSpot for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Detection antibody removal.
//   Detection antibody droplet removed to waste reservoir.
//   Manually cleared with laboratory tissue (Fig. 2d).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dispose sandwichComplex

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Final rinse - remove unbound detection antibody.
//   1-uL rinse buffer droplet cycled across the spot in
//   2 passes to remove any remaining unbound FITC-anti-IgG
//   (Fig. 2e). Rinse droplet removed to waste reservoir
//   (Fig. 2f). This final rinse is critical: residual
//   detection antibody in the droplet would generate high
//   background fluorescence in the plate reader step.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts 2-pass final rinse actuation.
//   References captureSpot (persistent surface).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rinseBuffer2 = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127_RinseBuffer
rinseStep2   = mix rinseBuffer2 with captureSpot for 2m

dispose rinseStep2

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Fluorescence detection.
//   Device (still positioned on the multiwell plate) is
//   inserted into the PheraStar multiwell plate reader
//   (BMG Labtech). The plate reader reads fluorescence
//   from the FITC-labeled sandwich complex bound to the
//   capture antibody spot on the ITO top plate.
//   Settings: lambda_ex = 485 nm, lambda_em = 520 nm,
//   focal height = 12.1 mm.
//   immunoassayDetection module abstracts the plate reader
//   fluorescence measurement step.
//   After detection, the Cytop/ITO cover plate is discarded;
//   the patterned bottom plate is re-used for subsequent assays.
//   NOTE: detect runs on rinseStep2 as the last active droplet
//   on the device at the time of plate reader insertion.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

assaySignal = detect immunoassayDetection on rinseStep2 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Assay QC gate
// Pass (BB2): assaySignal > 0 -> FITC fluorescence detected
//             above background; analyte present in sample.
//             Dispose final droplet; assay valid.
// Fail (BB3): assaySignal <= 0 -> no fluorescence above
//             background; analyte absent or assay failed.
//             Drain final droplet.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (assaySignal > 0) {
    dispose rinseStep2
} else {
    drain rinseStep2
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose the assay QC signal. Experiment ends.
// Top plate (Cytop/ITO with capture spot) is discarded.
// Bottom plate is retained for re-use in subsequent assays.
// ============================================================

dispose assaySignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module estradiolExtractionQC
module estradiolMSDetection

manifest BloodSample
manifest DCM_Acetone_8020
manifest Methanol
manifest Isooctane
manifest EstradiolStandard
manifest EstrogenFreeSerum

// ============================================================
// Droplet-Scale Estrogen Assays in Breast Tissue, Blood, and Serum
// Mousa, Jebrail, Yang, Abdelgawad, Metalnikov, Chen,
// Wheeler, Casper -- Sci. Transl. Med. 1, 1ra2 (2009).
// DOI: 10.1126/scitranslmed.3000105
//
// Wheeler Lab (University of Toronto) / Casper Lab (Mount Sinai)
//
// PROTOCOL SUMMARY (Method 1 -- used in most experiments):
//   A 1-uL aliquot of whole blood, serum, or breast tissue
//   homogenate is loaded into a DMF device.  The sample is
//   allowed to dry on the electrode surface.  A series of
//   DCM/acetone (80:20 v/v) lysing-solvent droplets is then
//   driven over the dried sample to lyse cells and dissolve
//   steroids.  Methanol (polar extraction solvent) is dispensed
//   over the dried lysate to dissolve the steroids into solution.
//   The dissolved droplet is driven into an isooctane (non-polar
//   extracting solvent) reservoir and circulated for ~20 s to
//   partition away interfering compounds.  The polar phase is
//   driven to the collection reservoir.  This lysis-extraction
//   cycle is repeated (9 x 1.1-uL or 5 x 2.2-uL reservoir
//   volumes of methanol) until the sample reservoir is exhausted.
//   All collected droplets are pooled, dried, and reconstituted
//   for downstream analysis (ELISA or LC-MS/MS).
//
// PLATFORM:  Digital microfluidics (EWOD) with individually
//   addressable ITO electrodes (1.5-mm pitch) on a glass
//   substrate coated with Teflon-AF (dielectric).  Open-top
//   (single-plate) configuration.  A photoresist "wall" defines
//   the liquid-liquid extraction zone separating the isooctane
//   pool from the collection reservoir.
//
// SAMPLE VOLUME:  1 uL (1000-4000x smaller than conventional
//   biopsy methods).  Processing time ~10-20 min by DMF vs
//   5-6 h by conventional pipetting/centrifugation.
//
// DETECTION:
//   (i)  ELISA (ALPCO Diagnostics estradiol kit, abs 450 nm) --
//        used for recovery (86-119%, CV 7-10%) and precision.
//   (ii) LC-MS/MS with SRM (m/z 271/145) using a QTRAP system;
//        estradiol retention time ~3.2 min (isocratic MeOH/H2O
//        80:20, 2.1-mm x 50-mm Thermo Gold C18 column).
//   (iii) Direct MS (LTQ, negative mode, 250 C, 0.5 uL/min):
//        estradiol fragment m/z 183 confirmed absent in whole
//        samples but dominant in DMF-extracted samples.
//
// RECOVERY:  DMF-based recoveries 86-119% (ELISA). Breast
//   tissue: 522 pg estradiol / mg tissue (CV 1%, replicate 1-uL
//   samples from postmenopausal breast cancer patient).
//
// NOTE ON LYSIS ABSTRACTION:
//   Paper M&M: "a series of reservoir volumes (9 x 1.1 uL or
//   5 x 2.2 uL) of DCM/acetone was dispensed and driven dropwise
//   to the sample, each allowed to incubate until dry (~1 min)."
//   BioScript has no loop primitive, so each lysis sub-droplet
//   is modelled as a separate dispense + mix step.  A 3-cycle
//   representative encoding is used (covering the first lysis
//   wave); all cycles are chemically identical.  `mix` here
//   abstracts the electrode-driven translation, merging, and
//   incubation-to-dryness event, not a conventional mixing step.
//
// NOTE ON METHANOL EXTRACTION ABSTRACTION:
//   Paper M&M: "step three was repeated with successive reservoir
//   volumes of methanol (total 9 x 1.1 uL or 5 x 2.2 uL)."
//   Three methanol extraction cycles are encoded; each cycle
//   produces one extract droplet that is circulated in isooctane
//   (~20 s) before being driven to the collection reservoir.
//   The `heat` at 25c for 20s in each extraction cycle models
//   the ~20 s circulation event in the isooctane pool (room temp).
//
// NOTE ON FLUID OWNERSHIP:
//   Each dispensed fluid variable is consumed exactly once.
//   `heat` is void/in-place; the variable remains valid and is
//   subsequently consumed by `mix` or `dispose`/`drain`.
//   After the if-else QC gate, `drain` is used in the else
//   branch; `dispose` is used in the if branch (established
//   compiler pattern for QC gates per dataset conventions).
//
// NOTE ON h DURATION TOKEN:
//   The BioScript compiler (lilott8/BioScript) does not support
//   the `h` duration token.  All durations use `s` or `m`.
//   Reconstitution and analysis steps encoded in minutes.
//
// NOTE ON NON-ASCII CHARACTERS:
//   All comments and identifiers use ASCII only to avoid ANTLR4
//   UnicodeDecodeError at compile time.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main            -- entry: lysis, methanol extraction,
//                           liquid-liquid partitioning in
//                           isooctane, pooling of extracts
//   BB2  extractPass     -- extractionSignal > 0: valid estradiol
//                           signal detected by ELISA proxy;
//                           dispose pooled extract
//   BB3  extractFail     -- extractionSignal <= 0: no signal
//                           (extraction failed); drain
//   BB4  extractJoin     -- join: MS confirmation branch begins
//   BB5  msPass          -- msSignal > 0: estradiol fragment
//                           m/z 183 / 271-145 confirmed; dispose
//   BB6  msFail          -- msSignal <= 0: analyte not confirmed;
//                           drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup    -- terminal: dispose QC signal droplets
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Load sample.
//   An aliquot of whole blood (1 uL) is positioned in the
//   sample reservoir of the DMF device.  Blood and tissue
//   samples are allowed to dry on the electrode surface before
//   the top plate is affixed and solvents are loaded.
//   Standard estradiol solutions are used immediately without
//   pre-drying.  Here the blood sample is dispensed and modelled
//   as the starting material variable throughout the protocol.
//   Drying time: ~1 min per reservoir volume at room temperature.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bloodSample     = dispense 1 units of BloodSample

// Drying at room temperature (25 C) for 60 s models the
// ~1 min incubation-to-dryness step for the sample droplet
// before lysis solvent is applied.  `heat` is void/in-place;
// bloodSample remains valid and is consumed at STEP 2.
heat bloodSample at 25c for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Lysis -- DCM/acetone wave, cycle 1 of 3.
//   DCM/acetone (80:20 v/v) dispensed from reservoir and
//   driven dropwise over the dried sample.  Incubation until
//   dry lyses cells and dissolves lipids / protein matrix
//   to free the steroid analytes.  `mix` abstracts electrode-
//   driven translation and merge + incubation-to-dryness.
//   Paper: 9 x 1.1 uL or 5 x 2.2 uL DCM/acetone per sample.
//   Three cycles encoded here as representative.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lysisSolvent1   = dispense 1 units of DCM_Acetone_8020
lysate1         = mix bloodSample with lysisSolvent1 for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Lysis -- DCM/acetone wave, cycle 2 of 3.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lysisSolvent2   = dispense 1 units of DCM_Acetone_8020
lysate2         = mix lysate1 with lysisSolvent2 for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Lysis -- DCM/acetone wave, cycle 3 of 3.
//   After three cycles the dried lysate contains the
//   free steroids (estradiol) ready for methanol dissolution.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lysisSolvent3   = dispense 1 units of DCM_Acetone_8020
driedLysate     = mix lysate2 with lysisSolvent3 for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Methanol extraction -- dissolution cycle 1 of 3.
//   A reservoir volume of methanol (polar extraction solvent)
//   is dispensed and driven by DMF to the dried lysate to
//   dissolve the steroids into the polar phase.
//   Paper M&M: "a reservoir volume of methanol (1.1 or 2.2 uL)
//   was dispensed and driven by DMF to the dried lysate to
//   dissolve the steroids."
// ------------------------------------------------------------

methanol1       = dispense 1 units of Methanol
dissolvedSample1 = mix driedLysate with methanol1 for 10s

// Liquid-liquid extraction in isooctane (cycle 1):
//   A unit droplet of the dissolved sample (200-400 nL) is
//   driven to the isooctane reservoir and circulated within
//   the pool for ~20 s (modelled as heat at 25c for 20s)
//   before being driven to the collection reservoir.
//   Isooctane partitions away lipid-soluble interferents
//   (e.g. porphyrin m/z 715, DNA helicase m/z 677).
isooctane1      = dispense 1 units of Isooctane
extractDrop1    = mix dissolvedSample1 with isooctane1 for 10s
heat extractDrop1 at 25c for 20s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Methanol extraction -- dissolution cycle 2 of 3.
//   Paper M&M: "step three was repeated with successive reservoir
//   volumes of methanol to extract all free estradiol."
// ------------------------------------------------------------

methanol2       = dispense 1 units of Methanol
dissolvedSample2 = mix extractDrop1 with methanol2 for 10s

isooctane2      = dispense 1 units of Isooctane
extractDrop2    = mix dissolvedSample2 with isooctane2 for 10s
heat extractDrop2 at 25c for 20s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Methanol extraction -- dissolution cycle 3 of 3.
//   Final methanol pass exhausts the sample reservoir.
//   All extract droplets (cycles 1-3) are pooled in the
//   collection reservoir and allowed to dry at -20 C for storage.
//   `mix` here pools the running extract with the final cycle.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

methanol3       = dispense 1 units of Methanol
dissolvedSample3 = mix extractDrop2 with methanol3 for 10s

isooctane3      = dispense 1 units of Isooctane
extractDrop3    = mix dissolvedSample3 with isooctane3 for 10s
heat extractDrop3 at 25c for 20s

// Pool all extract droplets into the collection reservoir.
// Paper: "all extract droplets were pooled in the output
// reservoir and allowed to dry."  The final collected pool
// is modelled as the merge of the three extract cycles.
estrogenFreeSerumDiluent = dispense 1 units of EstrogenFreeSerum
pooledExtract   = mix extractDrop3 with estrogenFreeSerumDiluent for 5s

// Reconstitution: pooled extract is dried then reconstituted
// in 30 uL methanol/DCM (2:1 v/v) before analysis.
// Modelled as a room-temperature incubation (drying step).
heat pooledExtract at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: ELISA extraction QC.
//   Paper M&M: "estradiol-specific ELISA (ALPCO Diagnostics)
//   used to evaluate recovery and precision of the DMF method."
//   estradiolExtractionQC module abstracts the plate reader
//   readout (abs 450 nm, mQuant spectrophotometer).
//   Final concentrations evaluated: 40, 1200, 3000, 6000 pg/mL.
//   Recovery 86-119%; CV 7-10% (Fig. 4, paper).
//   Breast tissue (independent patient): 522 pg/mg, CV 1%.
//   QC gate: if ELISA signal > 0, extraction is valid.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

extractionSignal = detect estradiolExtractionQC on pooledExtract for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Extraction QC gate
// Pass (BB2): extractionSignal > 0 -> estradiol signal detected;
//             dispose pooled extract and proceed to MS branch.
// Fail (BB3): extractionSignal <= 0 -> no signal (extraction
//             failure, reagent issue); drain and stop.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (extractJoin).
// ============================================================

if (extractionSignal > 0) {
    dispose pooledExtract
} else {
    drain pooledExtract
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: extractJoin
// Dispose ELISA QC signal. Control falls into MS confirmation
// branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose extractionSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: MS confirmation.
//   Paper: LTQ mass spectrometer (negative mode, 250 C,
//   0.5 uL/min nanoelectrospray).  Estradiol fragment m/z 183
//   detected as highest-magnitude peak in DMF-extracted samples
//   but absent in non-extracted whole blood/serum (Fig. 2).
//   LC-MS/MS SRM (m/z 271/145) at retention time ~3.2 min
//   used for quantification from standard solutions, breast
//   tissue homogenate, and whole blood (Fig. 3, paper).
//   estradiolMSDetection module abstracts both the direct MS
//   and LC-MS/MS SRM readout steps.
//   A fresh standard solution is dispensed as the MS reference
//   analyte alongside the reconstituted extract.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

estradiolStd    = dispense 1 units of EstradiolStandard
msSignal        = detect estradiolMSDetection on estradiolStd for 192s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: MS confirmation gate
// Pass (BB5): msSignal > 0 -> estradiol fragment m/z 183 /
//             SRM 271-145 confirmed at ~3.2 min retention;
//             dispose standard and signal.
// Fail (BB6): msSignal <= 0 -> analyte not confirmed (ion
//             suppression or extraction failure); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (msSignal > 0) {
    dispose estradiolStd
} else {
    drain estradiolStd
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose MS QC signal droplet. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose msSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module chemiluminescenceDetection

manifest MagneticParticles
manifest Sample_E2
manifest Sample_TSH
manifest WashBuffer
manifest ConjugateE2_HRP
manifest ConjugateAntiTSH_HRP
manifest H2O2
manifest Luminol_Enhancer
manifest PluronicL64

// ============================================================
// Digital Microfluidic Magnetic Separation for Particle-Based
// Immunoassays. Ng et al., Anal. Chem. 2012, 84, 8805-8812.
// DOI: 10.1021/ac3020627
//
// Two immunoassay protocols encoded in sequence:
//
// PROTOCOL 1: Competitive immunoassay for 17beta-estradiol (E2).
//   Competitive mode: exogenous E2-HRP conjugate competes with
//   unlabeled E2 in sample for binding sites on anti-E2 capture
//   antibodies immobilised on paramagnetic microparticles.
//   Signal (chemiluminescence) is INVERSELY proportional to
//   analyte concentration.
//   Calibration range: 10-1000 pg/mL.
//
// PROTOCOL 2: Noncompetitive (sandwich) immunoassay for
//   Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH).
//   Noncompetitive mode: labeled anti-TSH-HRP antibody and
//   capture antibody bind TSH at different epitopes, forming
//   antibody-antigen-antibody complex on the particle surface.
//   Signal (chemiluminescence) is DIRECTLY proportional to
//   analyte concentration.
//   Calibration range: 0.4-20 uIU/mL.
//
// PARTICLE WASHING: "Supernatant separation" method used
//   throughout (NOT serial dilution). Magnet immobilises
//   particles as a pellet; DMF actuates the supernatant droplet
//   away to waste; magnet removed; fresh droplet passed over
//   pellet and shuttled in circular motion (4 electrodes) to
//   resuspend. Achieves >90% removal of unbound reagents in
//   one step. Requires >=3.0x10^8 particles/mL and a movable
//   permanent magnet (N48, ~470 uN force on pellet).
//   NOTE: `mix` with WashBuffer abstracts each supernatant-
//   separation wash cycle (separate + reconstitute in one step).
//
// FOULING MITIGATION: Pluronic L64 at 0.05% (v/v) included
//   in all reagents. Custom DMF-compatible particle diluent,
//   conjugate diluent, and wash buffer formulated with L64
//   as sole surfactant. TSH particles blocked with nonfat
//   dried milk prior to use to reduce nonspecific adsorption.
//
// DETECTION: Chemiluminescence. Particles reconstituted in
//   H2O2 (2 min), then merged with luminol/enhancer to
//   activate HRP-driven chemiluminescence. Measured with
//   Pherastar plate reader (LUM module, gain=3600, 1 min
//   endpoint). Device affixed to 96-well Stripwell plate;
//   detection zone aligned per-well.
//
// DROPLET VOLUMES: Unit droplet = ~800 nL. Sample: 3 unit
//   droplets (2.4 uL total). All other reagents: 1 unit droplet.
//   100-fold reagent reduction vs. well-plate ELISA
//   (2.4 uL vs. 50 uL sample; 11.2 uL vs. 1.2 mL total).
//
// DMF DEVICE: 96 actuation electrodes, 10 reservoirs,
//   4 assay regions, 1 shared detection region. Movable
//   N48 neodymium magnet (5/8 in x 1/4 in) in manifold
//   below device. Air-matrix (no oil carrier fluid).
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main             -- entry: E2 competitive immunoassay
//   BB2  e2Pass           -- e2Signal > 0: valid E2 assay signal;
//                           dispose and proceed to TSH branch
//   BB3  e2Fail           -- e2Signal <= 0: no signal; drain
//   BB4  e2Join           -- join: TSH immunoassay branch begins
//   BB5  tshPass          -- tshSignal > 0: valid TSH signal;
//                           dispose all TSH droplets
//   BB6  tshFail          -- tshSignal <= 0: invalid signal; drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup     -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 1: Competitive Immunoassay for 17beta-Estradiol (E2)
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1 (E2): Particle separation from diluent.
//   Dispense 1 unit droplet of anti-E2 paramagnetic particle
//   suspension from reservoir. Magnet positioned to immobilise
//   particles as a pellet on the device surface. Supernatant
//   (particle diluent) is actuated away from the pellet to waste.
//   Particles remain as a dry pellet, retaining ~5% residual
//   liquid. All solutions contain 0.05% Pluronic L64 (v/v).
//   NOTE: PluronicL64 droplet models the surfactant pre-mix
//   step; in practice L64 is co-formulated into each reagent
//   rather than dispensed separately on-chip.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

e2_particles       = dispense 1 units of MagneticParticles
e2_pluronic        = dispense 1 units of PluronicL64
e2_particleMix     = mix e2_particles with e2_pluronic for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2 (E2): Sample incubation (3 unit droplets, 6 min).
//   Three 800 nL droplets of E2 sample (total 2.4 uL) are
//   sequentially dispensed and delivered to the immobilised
//   particle pellet for resuspension and incubation.
//   In competitive mode, sample E2 and E2-HRP conjugate
//   (added in Step 4) compete for binding sites on anti-E2
//   capture antibodies on particle surfaces.
//   6 min incubation saturates analyte binding on particles.
//   NOTE: Three `mix` steps model sequential delivery of
//   the three sample unit droplets to accumulate 2.4 uL.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

e2_sample1         = dispense 1 units of Sample_E2
e2_sample2         = dispense 1 units of Sample_E2
e2_sample3         = dispense 1 units of Sample_E2

e2_sampleMix1      = mix e2_particleMix with e2_sample1 for 6m
e2_sampleMix2      = mix e2_sampleMix1 with e2_sample2 for 1s
e2_sampleMix3      = mix e2_sampleMix2 with e2_sample3 for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3 (E2): Post-sample wash x 4 (supernatant separation).
//   Magnet immobilises particles. Supernatant actuated to waste.
//   Fresh wash buffer droplet passed over pellet; circular
//   motion across 4 electrodes resuspends particles.
//   Repeated 4 times. Removes >90% unbound sample matrix
//   per wash cycle (vs. >=6 serial dilution cycles for same).
//   Each `mix` step = one complete separate + reconstitute cycle.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

e2_wash1_buf       = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
e2_wash2_buf       = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
e2_wash3_buf       = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
e2_wash4_buf       = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer

e2_washed1         = mix e2_sampleMix3 with e2_wash1_buf for 5s
e2_washed2         = mix e2_washed1 with e2_wash2_buf for 5s
e2_washed3         = mix e2_washed2 with e2_wash3_buf for 5s
e2_washed4         = mix e2_washed3 with e2_wash4_buf for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4 (E2): Conjugate incubation (2 min).
//   1 unit droplet of E2-HRP conjugate (E2 antigen labeled
//   with horseradish peroxidase) at 1 ug/mL delivered to
//   washed particles for resuspension and incubation.
//   Optimal E2-HRP concentration is 1 ug/mL (higher
//   concentrations collapse the dynamic range: 50 pg/mL and
//   500 pg/mL become indistinguishable at high conjugate).
//   E2-HRP competes with sample E2 for residual anti-E2
//   binding sites remaining after sample incubation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

e2_conjugate       = dispense 1 units of ConjugateE2_HRP
e2_conjugateMix    = mix e2_washed4 with e2_conjugate for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5 (E2): Post-conjugate wash x 4 (supernatant separation).
//   Four wash cycles remove unbound E2-HRP conjugate.
//   Same supernatant separation protocol as Step 3.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

e2_cwash1_buf      = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
e2_cwash2_buf      = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
e2_cwash3_buf      = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
e2_cwash4_buf      = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer

e2_cwashed1        = mix e2_conjugateMix with e2_cwash1_buf for 5s
e2_cwashed2        = mix e2_cwashed1 with e2_cwash2_buf for 5s
e2_cwashed3        = mix e2_cwashed2 with e2_cwash3_buf for 5s
e2_cwashed4        = mix e2_cwashed3 with e2_cwash4_buf for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6 (E2): H2O2 rinse x 2 (supernatant separation).
//   Two H2O2 wash steps remove residual wash buffer prior to
//   substrate addition. Prevents wash buffer from diluting or
//   interfering with the H2O2/luminol chemiluminescent reaction.
//   This replaces the manufacturer-recommended pre-mix of
//   H2O2 and luminol, which caused high inter-assay variability.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

e2_h2o2wash1       = dispense 1 units of H2O2
e2_h2o2wash2       = dispense 1 units of H2O2

e2_h2owashed1      = mix e2_cwashed4 with e2_h2o2wash1 for 5s
e2_h2owashed2      = mix e2_h2owashed1 with e2_h2o2wash2 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7 (E2): Substrate incubation - H2O2 (2 min).
//   Particles delivered to the shared detection region.
//   1 unit droplet of H2O2 reconstitutes particle pellet
//   and incubates for 2 min in the detection zone.
//   H2O2 serves as the oxidising co-substrate for HRP.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

e2_h2o2_substrate  = dispense 1 units of H2O2
e2_h2o2Mix         = mix e2_h2owashed2 with e2_h2o2_substrate for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8 (E2): Chemiluminescence activation and detection.
//   1 unit droplet of luminol/enhancer solution merged
//   on-chip with the H2O2/particle droplet. HRP catalyses
//   oxidation of luminol by H2O2, generating chemiluminescence.
//   Incubated 1 min (signal saturates at 1 min endpoint).
//   Detection: Pherastar plate reader, LUM module, gain=3600,
//   focal height=8.6 mm, device taped to empty 96-well plate.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

e2_luminol         = dispense 1 units of Luminol_Enhancer
e2_detectionMix    = mix e2_h2o2Mix with e2_luminol for 1m

e2Signal = detect chemiluminescenceDetection on e2_detectionMix for 60s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: E2 assay QC gate
// Pass (BB2): e2Signal > 0 -> valid chemiluminescence detected;
//             dispose assay droplet and proceed to TSH branch.
// Fail (BB3): e2Signal <= 0 -> no signal (assay or reagent
//             failure); drain assay droplet.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (e2Join).
//
// NOTE ON COMPETITIVE SIGNAL POLARITY: In competitive format,
// higher analyte concentration produces LOWER RLU signal.
// The QC gate here tests only for signal presence (> 0),
// i.e. that HRP activity was detected at all. Quantification
// and curve fitting (4-parameter logistic) occur off-chip.
// ============================================================

if (e2Signal > 0) {
    dispose e2_detectionMix
} else {
    drain e2_detectionMix
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: e2Join
// Dispose E2 assay QC signal. Control falls into the TSH
// immunoassay branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose e2Signal

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 2: Noncompetitive Immunoassay for TSH
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1 (TSH): Particle separation from diluent.
//   Dispense 1 unit droplet of anti-beta-TSH paramagnetic
//   particle suspension (pre-blocked with nonfat dried milk
//   to reduce nonspecific adsorption of anti-TSH-HRP conjugate
//   onto particle surfaces). Magnet immobilises particles;
//   particle diluent supernatant actuated to waste.
//   NOTE: Nonfat dried milk blocking is performed off-chip
//   prior to dispensing; it is not modelled as a separate
//   on-chip dispense step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tsh_particles      = dispense 1 units of MagneticParticles
tsh_pluronic       = dispense 1 units of PluronicL64
tsh_particleMix    = mix tsh_particles with tsh_pluronic for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2 (TSH): Sample incubation (3 unit droplets, 6 min).
//   Three 800 nL droplets of TSH sample (total 2.4 uL)
//   delivered sequentially to immobilised particles.
//   In noncompetitive mode, TSH antigen binds the capture
//   anti-beta-TSH antibody on particle surfaces, forming the
//   first arm of the sandwich complex.
//   Normal serum TSH range: 0.4-3.0 uIU/mL.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tsh_sample1        = dispense 1 units of Sample_TSH
tsh_sample2        = dispense 1 units of Sample_TSH
tsh_sample3        = dispense 1 units of Sample_TSH

tsh_sampleMix1     = mix tsh_particleMix with tsh_sample1 for 6m
tsh_sampleMix2     = mix tsh_sampleMix1 with tsh_sample2 for 1s
tsh_sampleMix3     = mix tsh_sampleMix2 with tsh_sample3 for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3 (TSH): Post-sample wash x 4 (supernatant separation).
//   Four supernatant-separation wash cycles remove unbound
//   TSH and sample matrix proteins.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tsh_wash1_buf      = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
tsh_wash2_buf      = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
tsh_wash3_buf      = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
tsh_wash4_buf      = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer

tsh_washed1        = mix tsh_sampleMix3 with tsh_wash1_buf for 5s
tsh_washed2        = mix tsh_washed1 with tsh_wash2_buf for 5s
tsh_washed3        = mix tsh_washed2 with tsh_wash3_buf for 5s
tsh_washed4        = mix tsh_washed3 with tsh_wash4_buf for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4 (TSH): Conjugate incubation (2 min).
//   1 unit droplet of anti-TSH-HRP labeled detection antibody
//   delivered to washed particles for resuspension and incubation.
//   Anti-TSH-HRP binds TSH at a different epitope from the
//   capture antibody, completing the sandwich complex:
//   anti-beta-TSH [particle] -- TSH -- anti-TSH-HRP.
//   TSH immunoassays are very sensitive to nonspecific
//   adsorption of conjugated antibodies; milk-blocking
//   in Step 1 mitigates this background noise.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tsh_conjugate      = dispense 1 units of ConjugateAntiTSH_HRP
tsh_conjugateMix   = mix tsh_washed4 with tsh_conjugate for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5 (TSH): Post-conjugate wash x 4 (supernatant separation).
//   Four wash cycles remove unbound anti-TSH-HRP.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tsh_cwash1_buf     = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
tsh_cwash2_buf     = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
tsh_cwash3_buf     = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
tsh_cwash4_buf     = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer

tsh_cwashed1       = mix tsh_conjugateMix with tsh_cwash1_buf for 5s
tsh_cwashed2       = mix tsh_cwashed1 with tsh_cwash2_buf for 5s
tsh_cwashed3       = mix tsh_cwashed2 with tsh_cwash3_buf for 5s
tsh_cwashed4       = mix tsh_cwashed3 with tsh_cwash4_buf for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6 (TSH): H2O2 rinse x 2 (supernatant separation).
//   Two H2O2 wash steps remove residual wash buffer before
//   chemiluminescent substrate delivery. Same rationale as
//   E2 Step 6: prevents high inter-assay variability observed
//   when H2O2 and luminol were pre-mixed before dispensing.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tsh_h2o2wash1      = dispense 1 units of H2O2
tsh_h2o2wash2      = dispense 1 units of H2O2

tsh_h2owashed1     = mix tsh_cwashed4 with tsh_h2o2wash1 for 5s
tsh_h2owashed2     = mix tsh_h2owashed1 with tsh_h2o2wash2 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7 (TSH): Substrate incubation - H2O2 (2 min).
//   Particles delivered to shared detection region.
//   1 unit H2O2 droplet reconstitutes pellet for 2 min.
//   Detection zone pre-rinsed with H2O2 between assays.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tsh_h2o2_substrate = dispense 1 units of H2O2
tsh_h2o2Mix        = mix tsh_h2owashed2 with tsh_h2o2_substrate for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8 (TSH): Chemiluminescence activation and detection.
//   1 unit luminol/enhancer droplet merged on-chip with
//   H2O2/particle droplet. 1 min incubation to saturation.
//   Detection: same Pherastar reader setup as E2 assay.
//   TSH calibration curve fitted with linear equation
//   (vs. 4-parameter logistic for E2).
//   LOD (DMF): 0.83 uIU/mL (concentration); 2.0 nIU (absolute).
//   Clinical utility: diagnosis of hypothyroidism (TSH > 3.0
//   uIU/mL) is within the detectable range of this system.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tsh_luminol        = dispense 1 units of Luminol_Enhancer
tsh_detectionMix   = mix tsh_h2o2Mix with tsh_luminol for 1m

tshSignal = detect chemiluminescenceDetection on tsh_detectionMix for 60s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: TSH assay QC gate
// Pass (BB5): tshSignal > 0 -> valid chemiluminescence detected;
//             dispose assay droplet.
// Fail (BB6): tshSignal <= 0 -> no signal (HRP inactive or
//             reagent failure); drain assay droplet.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (tshSignal > 0) {
    dispose tsh_detectionMix
} else {
    drain tsh_detectionMix
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose TSH assay QC signal. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose tshSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module phosphorylationDetection
module cellViabilityDetection

manifest NIH3T3_Cells
manifest SeumFreeMedia
manifest StarvedMedia
manifest CultureMedia
manifest PluronicF68
manifest PDGFBB_Ligand
manifest Paraformaldehyde
manifest WashBuffer1
manifest WashBuffer2
manifest Tween20
manifest BlockingSolution
manifest PrimaryAntibody_PDGFR
manifest PrimaryAntibody_pPDGFR_Y857
manifest PrimaryAntibody_pPDGFR_Y1021
manifest PrimaryAntibody_Akt
manifest PrimaryAntibody_pAkt_S473
manifest SecondaryAntibody_AlexaFluor647
manifest SecondaryAntibody_AlexaFluor555

// ============================================================
// Digital Microfluidic Immunocytochemistry in Single Cells
// (DISC)
// Ng, A.H.C., Chamberlain, M.D., Situ, H., Lee, V., Wheeler,
// A.R. Nature Communications 6:7513 (2015).
// DOI: 10.1038/ncomms8513
//
// Protocol summary: DMF automates all steps for adherent cell
// culture, serum starvation, PDGF-BB ligand stimulation, and
// immunocytochemistry (ICC) on NIH-3T3 fibroblasts cultured in
// virtual microwells (VMs) on the patterned top plate of the
// DMF device. Protein phosphorylation (PDGFR Y857/Y1021 and
// Akt S473) is probed by dual-channel immunofluorescence.
//
// DEVICE GEOMETRY (from paper Methods):
//   Bottom plate: 80 actuation electrodes (2.2 x 2.2 mm),
//     interdigitated borders (140 um sinusoids), 30-80 um gaps.
//   Top plate: 8 hydrophilic ITO spots (1.75 mm diameter,
//     4.4 mm pitch) formed by Teflon-AF lift-off.
//   Spacer: double-sided tape, ~180 um gap.
//   Unit droplet volume: ~900 nL; VM volume: ~400 nL.
//   Driving: 80-100 V_rms, 10 kHz sine wave.
//   Reagent exchange uses passive dispensing (hydrophobic-
//     hydrophilic interaction at ITO spots). One exchange
//     displaces ~93% of VM contents; two exchanges >99%.
//   Reagent exchange time: ~1 s between successive droplets.
//
// CELL SEEDING (paper Methods - "DMF cell culture"):
//   Cells suspended in CultureMedia at 500,000 cells/mL.
//   3 x 900 nL (= 2.7 uL) passively dispensed per VM spot.
//   Device inverted; cells settle and adhere for 24 h at 37 C.
//
// ICC PROTOCOL - 11 STEPS (paper Methods):
//   1.  Starvation:  6 x 900 nL StarvedMedia; 24 h at 37 C.
//   2.  Serum-free rinse: 6 x 900 nL SerumFreeMedia.
//   3.  Stimulation: 2 x 900 nL PDGF-BB (conc/time varies).
//   4.  Fixation:    3 x 900 nL 4% PFA; 10 min.
//   5.  Wash:        6 x 900 nL WashBuffer1.
//   6.  Permeabilisation: 3 x 900 nL 0.2% Tween-20; 10 min.
//   7.  Wash:        9 x 900 nL WashBuffer2.
//   8.  Blocking:    3 x 900 nL BlockingSolution; 60 min.
//   9.  1- antibody: 3 x 900 nL; overnight at 4 C (= 720 min).
//   10. Wash:        6 x 900 nL WashBuffer2.
//   11. 2- antibody: 3 x 900 nL; 2 h at RT.
//   Top plate removed, rinsed, dried, scanned on microarray
//   scanner. CellProfiler used for single-cell image analysis.
//
// NOTE ON PASSIVE DISPENSING ABSTRACTION:
//   All multi-unit passive dispense steps (e.g., 6 x 900 nL)
//   are modelled as single `dispense N units` calls. The
//   BioScript compiler treats `N units` as a volume scalar;
//   it does not generate array elements here, which is correct
//   because each VM receives the full merged volume, not N
//   independent droplets. All mixing steps merge successive
//   passive-dispense deliveries at the VM site.
//
// NOTE ON CONCURRENT VMs:
//   The DMF device supports 8 VMs on the top plate. The paper
//   runs 3-4 VMs per condition on different days. BioScript
//   does not support spatial parallelism natively; this file
//   encodes a single representative VM. For multi-VM runs,
//   replicate this instruction block or use a loop construct
//   if supported by the target compiler backend.
//
// NOTE ON TIME-RESOLVED PULSE EXPERIMENTS (Fig. 6):
//   The encoded protocol (BB1-BB9) reflects the dose-response
//   validation experiment (Fig. 3b/3c): 2 min PDGFR stimulation
//   and 5 min Akt stimulation (the representative use case).
//   For pulse experiments, only the stimulation duration and
//   the ligand wash-away step in BB1 change. The double-bolus
//   time series (Fig. 5b) would require a second dispense of
//   PDGF-BB at t=90 s with a second stimulationMix variable.
//   These variants are noted in-line but not separately encoded
//   to keep the CFG tractable for the lilott8/BioScript
//   compiler (which does not support nested conditionals or
//   timer-parameterised loops).
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- device setup, cell seeding,
//                             starvation, ICC steps 1-3
//                             (up to and including fixation)
//   BB2  stimulusPass      -- stimulusSignal > 0: PDGFR
//                             phosphorylation detected;
//                             dispose fixed cells
//   BB3  stimulusFail      -- stimulusSignal <= 0: no receptor
//                             phosphorylation signal; drain
//   BB4  stimulusJoin      -- join: Akt downstream branch begins
//   BB5  aktPass           -- aktSignal > 0: Akt S473
//                             phosphorylation detected; dispose
//   BB6  aktFail           -- aktSignal <= 0: no Akt signal;
//                             drain
//   BB7  aktJoin           -- join: secondary ICC steps begin
//   BB8  immunoPass        -- immunoSignal > 0: dual-channel
//                             ICC valid; dispose stained plate
//   BB9  immunoFail        -- immunoSignal <= 0: ICC failed;
//                             drain stained plate
//   BB10 finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CELL SEEDING: Dispense cells in CultureMedia onto VM spots.
//   Paper M&M: 3 x 900 nL = 2.7 uL; 500,000 cells/mL.
//   Device inverted; 24 h at 37 C for adhesion.
//   `mix` abstracts passive dispensing flow across ITO spot
//   followed by sub-droplet VM formation (Supplementary Fig. 1).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cellSuspension   = dispense 3 units of NIH3T3_Cells
cultureDrop      = dispense 3 units of CultureMedia
seededVM         = mix cellSuspension with cultureDrop for 5s

// 24 h adhesion at 37 C in humidified chamber (= 1440 min).
heat seededVM at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1 - STARVATION: 6 x 900 nL StarvedMedia; 24 h at 37 C.
//   StarvedMedia: DMEM + 0.5% serum (CS or FBS) + 0.05% F-68
//                 + 100 U/mL pen G + 100 ug/mL strep.
//   Reduces background PDGFR and Akt phosphorylation to basal
//   level before stimulation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

starvedDrop      = dispense 6 units of StarvedMedia
starvedVM        = mix seededVM with starvedDrop for 5s

// 24 h starvation at 37 C (= 1440 min).
heat starvedVM at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2 - SERUM-FREE RINSE: 6 x 900 nL SerumFreeMedia.
//   SerumFreeMedia: DMEM + 0.05% F-68 + antibiotics.
//   Two passive-dispense exchanges displace >99% of starving
//   media from the VM (Fig. 2b-d). Encoded as single merge.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

serumFreeDrop    = dispense 6 units of SeumFreeMedia
rinsedVM         = mix starvedVM with serumFreeDrop for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3 - STIMULATION: 2 x 900 nL PDGF-BB in SerumFreeMedia.
//   Dose: paper validates 0 - 200 ng/mL range; 50 ng/mL used
//         for time-series (Fig. 5a) and cell heterogeneity
//         (Fig. 4); 3.125 ng/mL for pulse experiments (Fig. 6).
//   Duration: 2 min for PDGFR phosphorylation; 5 min for Akt.
//             Here encoded as 2 min (PDGFR validation arm).
//   For pulse experiments: a second wash droplet (SerumFreeMedia)
//   is dispensed after the desired pulse duration to wash away
//   the ligand before fixation (Supplementary Movie 2).
//   Switching time between droplets: ~1 s (Fig. 2a).
//
//   NOTE: Shear stress from DMF droplet movement is ~0.5 dyn/cm^2
//   (Supplementary Note 1), below the 1 dyn/cm^2 threshold for
//   flow-mediated Akt/Erk signalling. Tested explicitly with 75
//   back-and-forth droplet movements: P=0.7188, no difference
//   from undisturbed cells (Supplementary Fig. 8).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ligandDrop       = dispense 2 units of PDGFBB_Ligand
stimulatedVM     = mix rinsedVM with ligandDrop for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4 - FIXATION: 3 x 900 nL 4% PFA + 0.05% F-68; 10 min.
//   Immediately follows stimulation (within ~1 s reagent swap).
//   Fixation arrests signalling state at the exact time point.
//   In time-series experiments, fixation is staggered across
//   VMs so all wells are fixed simultaneously (paper M&M).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pfaDrop          = dispense 3 units of Paraformaldehyde
fixedVM          = mix stimulatedVM with pfaDrop for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5 - WASH: 6 x 900 nL WashBuffer1 (0.05% F-68 in DPBS).
//   Removes fixative before permeabilisation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

wash1Drop        = dispense 6 units of WashBuffer1
washedVM1        = mix fixedVM with wash1Drop for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6 - PERMEABILISATION: 3 x 900 nL 0.2% Tween-20; 10 min.
//   Opens cell membranes for intracellular antibody access.
//   Required for pAkt S473 (cytoplasmic/membrane) detection.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tween20Drop      = dispense 3 units of Tween20
permeabilisedVM  = mix washedVM1 with tween20Drop for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7 - WASH: 9 x 900 nL WashBuffer2 (0.05% Brij 35 in
//   DPBS). Extended wash to remove Tween-20 completely.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

wash2Drop        = dispense 9 units of WashBuffer2
washedVM2        = mix permeabilisedVM with wash2Drop for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8 - BLOCKING: 3 x 900 nL BlockingSolution; 60 min.
//   BlockingSolution: 1% non-fat dried milk + 0.05% Brij 35
//   in DPBS. Reduces non-specific antibody binding.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

blockDrop        = dispense 3 units of BlockingSolution
blockedVM        = mix washedVM2 with blockDrop for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9 - PRIMARY ANTIBODY: 3 x 900 nL; overnight at 4 C.
//   For PDGFR phosphorylation (Y857 arm - Fig. 3b):
//     pan PDGFR-b Mouse mAb (1:50, CST sc-19995)   -> Cy5 ch.
//     p-PDGFR-b Y857 Rabbit pAb (1:200, SCB sc-12907-R) -> Cy3.
//   For Akt phosphorylation (S473 arm - Fig. 3c):
//     pan Akt Mouse mAb (1:50, CST 2920S)           -> Cy5 ch.
//     p-Akt S473 Rabbit mAb (1:200, CST 4060P)      -> Cy3 ch.
//   Here encoded as PDGFR Y857 + Akt dual-target arm.
//   Overnight = 720 min; incubation at 4 C.
//
//   NOTE ON DUAL PRIMARY ANTIBODY MIX: The two primaries are
//   pre-mixed in BlockingSolution off-chip before loading into
//   the DMF reservoir. BioScript cannot express off-chip mixing
//   of manifested reagents; the two manifest entries are merged
//   here as a single `mix` before the blocking step application.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primary_PDGFR    = dispense 3 units of PrimaryAntibody_PDGFR
primary_pY857    = dispense 3 units of PrimaryAntibody_pPDGFR_Y857
primaryMix       = mix primary_PDGFR with primary_pY857 for 5s

primary_Akt      = dispense 3 units of PrimaryAntibody_Akt
primary_pS473    = dispense 3 units of PrimaryAntibody_pAkt_S473
primaryMix_Akt   = mix primary_Akt with primary_pS473 for 5s

combinedPrimary  = mix primaryMix with primaryMix_Akt for 5s
primaryStainedVM = mix blockedVM with combinedPrimary for 720m

// Overnight incubation at 4 C (= 720 min).
heat primaryStainedVM at 4c for 720m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10 - WASH: 6 x 900 nL WashBuffer2. Removes unbound
//   primary antibodies.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

wash3Drop        = dispense 6 units of WashBuffer2
washedVM3        = mix primaryStainedVM with wash3Drop for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11 - SECONDARY ANTIBODY: 3 x 900 nL; 2 h at RT.
//   Anti-Mouse IgG Alexa Fluor 647 (Fab2, 1:500, CST 4410S)
//     -> detects pan-PDGFR and pan-Akt (total protein, Cy5 ch.)
//   Anti-Rabbit IgG Alexa Fluor 555 (Fab2, 1:500, CST 4413S)
//     -> detects p-PDGFR Y857 and p-Akt S473 (Cy3 ch.)
//   Pre-mixed in BlockingSolution off-chip. Encoded as merge.
//   2 h at room temperature (25 C = 120 min).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

secondary_647    = dispense 3 units of SecondaryAntibody_AlexaFluor647
secondary_555    = dispense 3 units of SecondaryAntibody_AlexaFluor555
secondaryMix     = mix secondary_647 with secondary_555 for 5s

secondaryStained = mix washedVM3 with secondaryMix for 120m

// 2 h secondary antibody incubation at RT (25 C = 120 min).
heat secondaryStained at 25c for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DETECTION - PDGFR phosphorylation QC:
//   phosphorylationDetection abstracts off-chip microarray
//   scanner readout (Axon GenePix 4000B; 532 nm and 635 nm
//   lasers; 5 um resolution; PMT 500 V). CellProfiler 2.0
//   pipeline identifies single cells (15-100 um diameter,
//   Otsu Global thresholding) and computes fold-change ratio:
//   (Cy3 phospho-channel intensity) / (Cy5 total-protein
//   intensity), normalised to no-stimulation control on same
//   device. Signal > 1.0 (fold change) = phosphorylation above
//   basal level. QC gate uses bulk-average signal across VM.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

stimulusSignal   = detect phosphorylationDetection on secondaryStained for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: PDGFR phosphorylation QC gate
// Pass (BB2): stimulusSignal > 0 -> receptor phosphorylation
//             above basal level detected; dispose stained VM.
// Fail (BB3): stimulusSignal <= 0 -> no detectable PDGFR
//             phosphorylation; drain (reagent failure or
//             no stimulation).
// Both branches converge at BB4 (stimulusJoin).
// ============================================================

if (stimulusSignal > 0) {
    dispose secondaryStained
} else {
    drain secondaryStained
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: stimulusJoin
// Dispose PDGFR QC signal. Control falls into downstream
// Akt phosphorylation branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose stimulusSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// AKT PHOSPHORYLATION ARM (Fig. 3c / Fig. 4 / Fig. 6b):
//   Separate VM seeded identically to the PDGFR arm above.
//   Stimulation: 5 min (vs 2 min for PDGFR) as per paper M&M.
//   Staining uses Akt / p-Akt S473 primary pair (see Step 9).
//
//   NOTE: In the paper's physical experiment, the Akt and
//   PDGFR arms run in parallel VMs on the same device. In this
//   BioScript encoding they are sequential (BioScript has no
//   native spatial parallelism for distinct VM populations).
//   The Akt VM re-runs the full ICC pipeline with modified
//   stimulation duration (5 min). Only the stimulation and
//   detection steps differ; the antibody panel is Akt-specific.
//
//   For the heterogeneity / hyperactive-responder analysis
//   (Fig. 4), stimulation is 0-7 min with 50 ng/mL PDGF-BB.
//   For the pulse experiments (Fig. 6b), stimulation is 3-40 s
//   followed by a ligand-free wash before fixation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// -- Akt arm: seeding and starvation (identical to PDGFR arm) --

aktCellSusp      = dispense 3 units of NIH3T3_Cells
aktCultureDrop   = dispense 3 units of CultureMedia
aktSeededVM      = mix aktCellSusp with aktCultureDrop for 5s
heat aktSeededVM at 37c for 1440m

aktStarvedDrop   = dispense 6 units of StarvedMedia
aktStarvedVM     = mix aktSeededVM with aktStarvedDrop for 5s
heat aktStarvedVM at 37c for 1440m

aktRinseDrop     = dispense 6 units of SeumFreeMedia
aktRinsedVM      = mix aktStarvedVM with aktRinseDrop for 5s

// -- Akt arm: STEP 3 - Stimulation: 5 min (vs 2 min for PDGFR) --
aktLigandDrop    = dispense 2 units of PDGFBB_Ligand
aktStimulated    = mix aktRinsedVM with aktLigandDrop for 5m

// -- Akt arm: STEP 4 - Fixation --
aktPfaDrop       = dispense 3 units of Paraformaldehyde
aktFixedVM       = mix aktStimulated with aktPfaDrop for 10m

// -- Akt arm: STEP 5 - Wash --
aktWash1         = dispense 6 units of WashBuffer1
aktWashedVM1     = mix aktFixedVM with aktWash1 for 5s

// -- Akt arm: STEP 6 - Permeabilisation --
aktTween20       = dispense 3 units of Tween20
aktPermVM        = mix aktWashedVM1 with aktTween20 for 10m

// -- Akt arm: STEP 7 - Wash --
aktWash2         = dispense 9 units of WashBuffer2
aktWashedVM2     = mix aktPermVM with aktWash2 for 5s

// -- Akt arm: STEP 8 - Blocking --
aktBlockDrop     = dispense 3 units of BlockingSolution
aktBlockedVM     = mix aktWashedVM2 with aktBlockDrop for 60m

// -- Akt arm: STEP 9 - Primary antibody (Akt-specific panel) --
aktPrimary_Akt   = dispense 3 units of PrimaryAntibody_Akt
aktPrimary_pS473 = dispense 3 units of PrimaryAntibody_pAkt_S473
aktPrimaryMix    = mix aktPrimary_Akt with aktPrimary_pS473 for 5s
aktPrimStained   = mix aktBlockedVM with aktPrimaryMix for 720m
heat aktPrimStained at 4c for 720m

// -- Akt arm: STEP 10 - Wash --
aktWash3         = dispense 6 units of WashBuffer2
aktWashedVM3     = mix aktPrimStained with aktWash3 for 5s

// -- Akt arm: STEP 11 - Secondary antibody --
aktSec647        = dispense 3 units of SecondaryAntibody_AlexaFluor647
aktSec555        = dispense 3 units of SecondaryAntibody_AlexaFluor555
aktSecondaryMix  = mix aktSec647 with aktSec555 for 5s
aktSecStained    = mix aktWashedVM3 with aktSecondaryMix for 120m
heat aktSecStained at 25c for 120m

// Detection: Akt S473 phosphorylation (Cy3/Cy5 dual channel).
aktSignal        = detect phosphorylationDetection on aktSecStained for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Akt S473 phosphorylation QC gate
// Pass (BB5): aktSignal > 0 -> Akt phosphorylation above basal;
//             dispose stained VM.
// Fail (BB6): aktSignal <= 0 -> no Akt activation detected;
//             drain (stimulation below commitment threshold or
//             reagent failure).
// Both branches converge at BB7 (aktJoin).
//
// Biological note: paper shows Akt response is amplified
// relative to PDGFR - at 3.125 ng/mL PDGF, ~22% receptor
// phosphorylation drives >70% Akt activation. A 10 s pulse
// is sufficient to commit >32% of cells to Akt activation
// (Fig. 6b). Akt deactivation is observed if 3 s pulse cells
// are evaluated at 5 min vs 3-4 min (Fig. 6c), consistent
// with enzymatic noise-filtering.
// ============================================================

if (aktSignal > 0) {
    dispose aktSecStained
} else {
    drain aktSecStained
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: aktJoin
// Dispose Akt QC signal. Control falls into final cell
// viability / morphology QC branch (BB8/BB9).
// ============================================================

dispose aktSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// FINAL QC - CELL MORPHOLOGY / VIABILITY DETECTION:
//   cellViabilityDetection abstracts the CellProfiler post-
//   scan QC step in which cells are filtered by size
//   (15-100 um diameter), shape (eccentricity, form factor),
//   and staining quality. This is not a staining step; it is
//   a computational gate on the acquired microarray images.
//   Signal > 0: sufficient cells identified per VM (>=150
//   cells at 500,000 cells/mL) with valid signal-to-background
//   ratio (>5x primary vs secondary-only control).
//
//   The cellViabilityDetection module also captures the
//   hyperactive-responder sub-population analysis (Fig. 4):
//   cells are classified as hyperactive if p-Akt fold change
//   exceeds Q3 + 1.5 * IQR (outlier threshold in JMP 10).
//   Hyperactive cells show elongated morphology (higher
//   eccentricity, lower form factor) and increased neighbour
//   proximity relative to normal cells (Supplementary Fig. 13).
//
//   NOTE ON QC DROPLET DESIGN:
//   secondaryStained and aktSecStained are consumed (disposed
//   or drained) in the BB2/BB3 and BB5/BB6 gate branches
//   above. The BioScript SSA form would version them as [2]
//   if referenced again here, producing undefined-variable
//   errors in the IR. The fix is a dedicated QC droplet:
//   a fresh aliquot of WashBuffer2 is dispensed as a carrier
//   for the cellViabilityDetection module call. In the
//   physical experiment, this gate is purely computational
//   (CellProfiler runs on the already-acquired microarray
//   scan images); the fluid operand is a BioScript
//   requirement for the detect statement, not a wet-lab step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Fresh QC carrier droplet -- avoids SSA version-2 conflict.
qcCarrier        = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer2
immunoSignal     = detect cellViabilityDetection on qcCarrier for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Cell identification / ICC quality QC gate
// Pass (BB8): immunoSignal > 0 -> valid cell count and
//             signal-to-background ratio; dispose stained plate.
// Fail (BB9): immunoSignal <= 0 -> insufficient cells or
//             antibody staining failure; drain.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (immunoSignal > 0) {
    dispose qcCarrier
} else {
    drain qcCarrier
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose the ICC QC signal droplet.
// Experiment ends; top plate removed for microarray scanner
// analysis and CellProfiler single-cell quantification.
// ============================================================

dispose immunoSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module rvIgGDetection
module rvIgMDetection

manifest VirusCoatedParticles
manifest WashBuffer
manifest HRPConjugateIgG
manifest HRPConjugateIgM
manifest H2O2
manifest LuminolEnhancer
manifest PretreatmentReagent
manifest SampleIgG
manifest SampleIgM
manifest SampleIgGplusIgM
manifest DiluentPBSBSA
manifest PluronicL64

// ============================================================
// Digital Microfluidic Platform for the Detection of
// Rubella Infection and Immunity: A Proof of Concept
// Ng, Lee, Choi, Fischer, Robinson, Wheeler
// Clinical Chemistry 61:2, 420-429 (2015)
// DOI: 10.1373/clinchem.2014.232181
//
// Two protocols are encoded in sequence:
//
// PROTOCOL 1: RV IgG Immunoassay (7-step indirect ELISA)
//   Detects rubella-virus-specific IgG from patient serum or
//   plasma. Virus-coated paramagnetic particles (1.5e8/mL in
//   Superblock) capture analyte; HRP-conjugated antihuman IgG
//   transduces binding to chemiluminescent signal (HRP +
//   H2O2/luminol-enhancer). PMT reads RLUs over 10 s.
//   Sample volume per assay: 1.8 uL. Four parallel assays
//   run simultaneously by on-chip aliquoting.
//   Run time: ~25 min per quadruplicate set.
//   LOD: 0.03 IU/mL; LOQ: 0.1 IU/mL; clinical cutoff: 10 IU/mL.
//   Intraassay CVs: 0.3-6.5%.
//   On-chip dilution sub-protocol (serial 2x steps) evaluated
//   for samples >linear range (>250 IU/mL) and for whole blood.
//
// PROTOCOL 2: RV IgM Immunoassay (7-step indirect ELISA
//   + on-chip IgG blocking pretreatment)
//   Critical test for diagnosing active rubella infection and
//   identifying pregnancies at risk for CRS. RV IgM binds to
//   RV-coated particles; HRP-conjugated antihuman IgM detects.
//   Pretreatment: 1.8 uL sample + 0.9 uL goat antihuman IgG
//   (pretreatment reagent) mixed for 7 min on-chip to block
//   endogenous RV IgG and RF IgM interference.
//   Run time: ~35 min per quadruplicate set (including pretreatment).
//   Intraassay CVs: 1.5-13.4%.
//   Cutoff defined by RV IgM Cal 1 (Abbott Architect kit).
//
// DROPLET VOLUMES:
//   Unit droplet (actuation electrode area x gap): ~900 nL
//   Gap between top/bottom plates: ~140 um
//   Electrode array: 80 actuation electrodes (2.54 x 2.54 mm)
//   Actuation: 80-100 V_rms, 10 kHz sine wave
//   Particle immobilisation force: >=600 uN (magnetic lens)
//   Minimum release threshold: ~500 uN
//
// REAGENT ADDITIVES:
//   All reagents supplemented with 0.05% Pluronic L64 to reduce
//   protein adsorption and prevent cross-contamination in
//   air-filled DMF device.
//
// TEMPERATURE:
//   All assay steps performed at room temperature.
//   No active thermal control required.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- entry: RV IgG immunoassay (Protocol 1)
//   BB2  igGPass           -- igGSignal > 0: IgG detected (positive),
//                             dispose particles
//   BB3  igGFail           -- igGSignal <= 0: IgG not detected
//                             (negative/equivocal), drain particles
//   BB4  igGJoin           -- join: RV IgM pretreatment branch begins
//   BB5  igMPass           -- igMSignal > 0: IgM detected (positive),
//                             dispose particles
//   BB6  igMFail           -- igMSignal <= 0: IgM not detected
//                             (negative/equivocal), drain particles
//   BB7  finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose QC signals,
//                             experiment ends
//
// DIAGNOSTIC PERFORMANCE (25-sample commercial panel, PTR201):
//   RV IgG: sensitivity 100% (95% CI 98.6-100%),
//            specificity 100% (95% CI 95.5-100%), AUC = 1.00
//   RV IgM: sensitivity 100% (95% CI 97.4-100%),
//            specificity 100% (95% CI 98.4-100%), AUC = 1.00
//   Reference standard: Abbott EIA (Seracare PTR201 panel)
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 1: RV IgG IMMUNOASSAY
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1a: Dispense virus-coated paramagnetic particle
//   suspension (1.8 uL; 1.5e8 particles/mL in Superblock).
//   Particles were pre-washed 2x in Superblock and
//   resuspended at ~10x stock concentration before loading.
//   Particle droplet is dispensed from reservoir onto the
//   electrode array where the magnetic lens (positioned
//   ~150 um below device) immobilises them in the separation
//   zone.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igGParticles     = dispense 2 units of VirusCoatedParticles

// Magnetic separation: particles pellet in separation zone;
// supernatant (diluent) is actuated away to waste.
// `mix` here abstracts the electrode-actuation resuspension
// sequence used throughout this protocol for particle
// handling; `dispose` models the supernatant removal step.
// Particle pellet remains on-chip for subsequent operations.
igGParticleSep   = mix igGParticles with igGParticles for 5s
dispose igGParticleSep

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1b: Sample incubation.
//   1.8 uL of patient sample (pre-diluted 10x in DPBS/4% BSA
//   before loading) is dispensed and delivered to the
//   particle pellet. Droplet is mixed in circular motion for
//   3 min; RV IgG from the sample binds to RV-coated
//   particle surface. Magnetic lens re-activates to
//   immobilise particles; supernatant is removed to waste.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igGSample        = dispense 2 units of SampleIgG
igGCapture       = mix igGSample with igGParticles for 3m
dispose igGCapture

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1c: Wash cycle x4 (4 x 1.8 uL wash buffer).
//   Each wash: a fresh wash buffer droplet is moved over the
//   particle pellet, mixed, magnetic lens activated,
//   supernatant removed. Repeated 4 times to eliminate
//   unbound antibody and reduce background signal.
//   Wash buffer: custom DMF-compatible formulation +
//   0.05% Pluronic L64.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igGWash1         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igGWashStep1     = mix igGParticles with igGWash1 for 30s
dispose igGWashStep1

igGWash2         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igGWashStep2     = mix igGParticles with igGWash2 for 30s
dispose igGWashStep2

igGWash3         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igGWashStep3     = mix igGParticles with igGWash3 for 30s
dispose igGWashStep3

igGWash4         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igGWashStep4     = mix igGParticles with igGWash4 for 30s
dispose igGWashStep4

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1d: Conjugate incubation (1.8 uL).
//   HRP-conjugated goat polyclonal antihuman IgG (21 ng/mL
//   in conjugate diluent + 0.05% Pluronic L64) delivered to
//   particle pellet. Mixed for 2 min. HRP-anti-IgG binds to
//   sample RV IgG captured on the particles, forming the
//   immunocomplex. Magnetic lens re-activates; unbound
//   conjugate removed to waste.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igGConjugate     = dispense 2 units of HRPConjugateIgG
igGConjugateStep = mix igGParticles with igGConjugate for 2m
dispose igGConjugateStep

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1e: Second wash cycle x4 (4 x 1.8 uL wash buffer).
//   Identical to STEP 1c. Removes excess/unbound HRP
//   conjugate before substrate addition to minimise
//   background chemiluminescence.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igGWash5         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igGWashStep5     = mix igGParticles with igGWash5 for 30s
dispose igGWashStep5

igGWash6         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igGWashStep6     = mix igGParticles with igGWash6 for 30s
dispose igGWashStep6

igGWash7         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igGWashStep7     = mix igGParticles with igGWash7 for 30s
dispose igGWashStep7

igGWash8         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igGWashStep8     = mix igGParticles with igGWash8 for 30s
dispose igGWashStep8

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1f: Chemiluminescent substrate addition and detection.
//   900 nL H2O2 (stable peroxide) + 900 nL luminol-enhancer
//   solution (SuperSignal ELISA Femto) dispensed and merged
//   with particle pellet. Mixed for 2 min. HRP catalyses
//   oxidation of luminol in the presence of H2O2, generating
//   photons detected by the motorised PMT (positioned ~few
//   hundred um above device droplet centre).
//   Mean signal integrated over 10 s; reported as RLUs.
//   Result interpreted against calibration curve:
//     s/co >= 1.0    -> positive
//     s/co 0.69-0.99 -> equivocal (gray zone)
//     s/co < 0.69    -> negative
//
//   NOTE ON UNIT VOLUMES: H2O2 and luminol are each 900 nL
//   (1 unit = ~900 nL on the actuation electrode). They are
//   dispensed as separate 1-unit droplets and merged with the
//   particle pellet. `mix ... for 2m` models the 2 min
//   incubation; `detect` models the 10 s PMT integration.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igGH2O2          = dispense 1 units of H2O2
igGLuminol       = dispense 1 units of LuminolEnhancer

igGSubstrateMix  = mix igGH2O2 with igGLuminol for 5s
igGReaction      = mix igGParticles with igGSubstrateMix for 2m

igGSignal        = detect rvIgGDetection on igGReaction for 10s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: RV IgG diagnostic gate
// Pass (BB2): igGSignal > 0 -> IgG detected; s/co >= 1 implies
//             rubella immunity or current/past infection.
//             Dispose reaction droplet (particles + substrate).
// Fail (BB3): igGSignal <= 0 -> IgG not detected; patient
//             susceptible to rubella. Drain reaction droplet.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (igGJoin).
// ============================================================

if (igGSignal > 0) {
    dispose igGReaction
} else {
    drain igGReaction
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: igGJoin
// Dispose IgG QC signal droplet. Control falls through to
// RV IgM pretreatment and immunoassay branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose igGSignal

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 2: RV IgM IMMUNOASSAY (+ ON-CHIP IgG BLOCKING)
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2a: On-chip sample pretreatment (IgG blocking).
//   1.8 uL of patient sample (pre-diluted 10x in DPBS/4% BSA)
//   + 900 nL pretreatment reagent (3x dilution of Abbott
//   Architect goat antihuman IgG in conjugate diluent) are
//   merged on-chip and mixed for 7 min.
//   Purpose: the exogenous antihuman IgG blocks:
//     (i)  endogenous RV IgG from competing for binding sites
//          on the RV-coated particles (prevents false negatives)
//     (ii) RV IgG / RF IgM immunocomplexes from binding
//          non-specifically to the particles (prevents false
//          positives from rheumatoid factor IgM cross-reaction)
//   Significance: pretreatment statistically suppresses
//   nonspecific signal in IgG+IgM samples (P=0.0003),
//   while leaving pure IgG (P=0.5) or pure IgM (P=0.6)
//   samples unaffected.
//
//   NOTE ON VOLUMES: 1.8 uL sample = 2 units; 900 nL
//   pretreatment = 1 unit (at 900 nL per unit electrode).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igMSampleRaw     = dispense 2 units of SampleIgM
igMPretreat      = dispense 1 units of PretreatmentReagent
igMPretreatedSample = mix igMSampleRaw with igMPretreat for 7m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2b: Dispense virus-coated paramagnetic particle
//   suspension (1.8 uL; identical particle stock as Protocol 1).
//   Particles magnetically separated from diluent; pellet
//   retained in separation zone for sample delivery.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igMParticles     = dispense 2 units of VirusCoatedParticles
igMParticleSep   = mix igMParticles with igMParticles for 5s
dispose igMParticleSep

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2c: Pretreated sample incubation.
//   Pretreated sample droplet delivered to particle pellet.
//   Mixed for 3 min. RV IgM antibodies from sample bind to
//   RV-coated particles; competing IgG/RF IgM complexes
//   blocked by pretreatment. Magnetic lens re-activates;
//   supernatant removed to waste.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igMCapture       = mix igMPretreatedSample with igMParticles for 3m
dispose igMCapture

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2d: Wash cycle x4 (4 x 1.8 uL wash buffer).
//   Identical procedure to Protocol 1 STEP 1c. Removes
//   unbound antibody and pretreatment components.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igMWash1         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igMWashStep1     = mix igMParticles with igMWash1 for 30s
dispose igMWashStep1

igMWash2         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igMWashStep2     = mix igMParticles with igMWash2 for 30s
dispose igMWashStep2

igMWash3         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igMWashStep3     = mix igMParticles with igMWash3 for 30s
dispose igMWashStep3

igMWash4         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igMWashStep4     = mix igMParticles with igMWash4 for 30s
dispose igMWashStep4

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2e: Conjugate incubation (1.8 uL).
//   HRP-conjugated goat antihuman IgM (23 ng/mL in conjugate
//   diluent + 0.05% Pluronic L64) delivered to particle
//   pellet. Mixed for 2 min. HRP-anti-IgM binds selectively
//   to RV IgM captured on particles. Magnetic lens
//   re-activates; unbound conjugate removed to waste.
//   Note: IgM-specific conjugate distinguishes this step
//   from Protocol 1 STEP 1d (antihuman IgG conjugate).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igMConjugate     = dispense 2 units of HRPConjugateIgM
igMConjugateStep = mix igMParticles with igMConjugate for 2m
dispose igMConjugateStep

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2f: Second wash cycle x4 (4 x 1.8 uL wash buffer).
//   Identical to STEP 2d. Removes excess HRP-anti-IgM
//   conjugate before substrate addition.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igMWash5         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igMWashStep5     = mix igMParticles with igMWash5 for 30s
dispose igMWashStep5

igMWash6         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igMWashStep6     = mix igMParticles with igMWash6 for 30s
dispose igMWashStep6

igMWash7         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igMWashStep7     = mix igMParticles with igMWash7 for 30s
dispose igMWashStep7

igMWash8         = dispense 2 units of WashBuffer
igMWashStep8     = mix igMParticles with igMWash8 for 30s
dispose igMWashStep8

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2g: Chemiluminescent substrate addition and detection.
//   Identical substrate system to Protocol 1 STEP 1f:
//   900 nL H2O2 + 900 nL luminol-enhancer merged with particle
//   pellet and mixed for 2 min. Motorised PMT collects
//   photons over 10 s; signal reported as RLUs.
//   Result interpreted against RV IgM cutoff calibrator
//   (Cal 1, adapted from Abbott Architect rubella IgM kit):
//     s/co >= 1.0    -> positive (active rubella infection)
//     s/co 0.75-0.99 -> equivocal (gray zone; retest required)
//     s/co < 0.75    -> negative
//   Cal 1 defines the s/co = 1 cutoff; its RF IgM content
//   (27.0 IU/mL) makes on-chip pretreatment essential for
//   accurate cutoff calibration (Fig. 3C of paper).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

igMH2O2          = dispense 1 units of H2O2
igMLuminol       = dispense 1 units of LuminolEnhancer

igMSubstrateMix  = mix igMH2O2 with igMLuminol for 5s
igMReaction      = mix igMParticles with igMSubstrateMix for 2m

igMSignal        = detect rvIgMDetection on igMReaction for 10s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: RV IgM diagnostic gate
// Pass (BB5): igMSignal > 0 -> IgM detected; s/co >= 1 implies
//             active rubella infection; patient (especially
//             if pregnant) at risk of CRS.
//             Dispose reaction droplet (particles + substrate).
// Fail (BB6): igMSignal <= 0 -> IgM not detected;
//             no evidence of acute rubella infection.
//             Drain reaction droplet.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
//
// NOTE ON EQUIVOCAL RESULTS: In clinical practice, equivocal
// results (s/co 0.75-0.99) are interpreted as positive for
// diagnostic performance calculations (WHO guidance) and
// referred for retest. Two of 25 panel samples produced
// positive-equivocal discrepancies between DMF and Abbott EIA
// at s/co near the cutoff; attributed to assay variability
// (CV ~15%) and sample age (~20 years). These did not affect
// the final 100% sensitivity and 100% specificity calculations.
// ============================================================

if (igMSignal > 0) {
    dispose igMReaction
} else {
    drain igMReaction
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose both assay QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose igMSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module chemiluminescenceDetection
module phosphoProteinDetection

manifest MagneticParticles_TSH
manifest MagneticParticles_E2
manifest SampleAnalyte_TSH
manifest SampleAnalyte_E2
manifest AntiTSH_HRP_Conjugate
manifest E2_HRP_Conjugate
manifest WashBuffer
manifest H2O2_Peroxide
manifest Luminol_Enhancer
manifest Cells_NIH3T3
manifest CultureMedia
manifest StarvingMedia
manifest SerumFreeMedia
manifest PDGF_BB_Ligand
manifest FixingSolution
manifest WashBuffer1
manifest WashBuffer2
manifest BlockingSolution
manifest PermeabilizationSolution
manifest PrimaryAntibody_Akt
manifest SecondaryAntibody

// ============================================================
// Development of Digital Microfluidic Heterogeneous
// Immunoassays
// Alphonsus H.C. Ng - PhD Thesis, University of Toronto, 2015
//
// Two protocols are encoded in sequence:
//
// PROTOCOL 1: DMF Magnetic Particle-Based Immunoassay
//   (Chapter 2 -- Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone [TSH] and
//   17beta-Estradiol [E2] immunoassays using supernatant
//   separation washing on a two-plate DMF device operated
//   in air with Pluronic L64 additive.)
//
//   Eight-step protocol:
//     Step 1: Dispense and separate magnetic particles
//             from diluent (supernatant separation method).
//     Step 2: Merge 3 droplets of sample with particles;
//             incubate 6 min (TSH: sandwich/non-competitive;
//             E2: competitive).
//     Step 3: Wash particles 4x in wash buffer;
//             separate supernatant each time.
//     Step 4: Merge 1 droplet of conjugate (Anti-TSH-HRP
//             or E2-HRP); incubate 2 min.
//     Step 5: Wash particles 4x in wash buffer;
//             wash 2x in H2O2; separate supernatant.
//     Step 6: Merge 1 droplet H2O2; incubate 2 min.
//     Step 7: Merge 1 droplet luminol/enhancer; mix.
//     Step 8: Incubate 1 min; detect chemiluminescence.
//   Actuation: ~150 Vrms, 10 kHz, air-surrounded droplets.
//   Unit droplet volume: ~800 nL.
//   Particle density: ~3.0e8 particles/mL.
//
// PROTOCOL 2: DISC -- Digital Microfluidic
//   Immunocytochemistry in Single Cells (Chapter 5)
//   NIH 3T3 cells stimulated with PDGF-BB (50 ng/mL for
//   2 min), then fixed and probed for phospho-Akt (S473)
//   and total Akt via two-antibody ICC on-chip.
//
//   Eleven-step protocol (DISC standard):
//     Step 1: Seed cells (3 unit droplets, ~400 nL VM).
//             Incubate 24 h at 37 C.
//     Step 2: Starve cells in starving media (24 h, 37 C).
//     Step 3: Rinse with serum-free DMEM.
//     Step 4: Stimulate with PDGF-BB (50 ng/mL, 2 min).
//     Step 5: Fix with 4% paraformaldehyde (10 min).
//     Step 6: Rinse with WB1.
//     Step 7: Permeabilize with 0.2% Tween-20 (10 min).
//     Step 8: Rinse with WB2.
//     Step 9: Block with 1% NFDM in DPBS (1 h).
//     Step 10: Stain with primary antibodies
//              (pan-Akt + p-Akt S473; overnight, 4 C).
//     Step 11: Rinse unbound primary antibodies (WB2).
//     Step 12: Stain with secondary antibodies (2 h).
//     Step 13: Detect phosphoprotein signal.
//   Virtual microwell (VM) volume: ~400 nL.
//   Actuation: 80-100 Vrms, 10 kHz.
//
// NOTE ON SUPERNATANT SEPARATION (Protocol 1):
//   `mix` steps in the wash loop abstract the DMF magnet
//   actuation sequence: (a) magnet immobilises pellet,
//   (b) supernatant droplet is actuated to waste, (c)
//   magnet removed, (d) fresh droplet passed over pellet,
//   (e) droplet shuttled circularly to resuspend particles.
//   This outperforms the serial-dilution method, achieving
//   >98% unbound reagent removal in 2 washes (paper Fig 2.4C).
//
// NOTE ON PASSIVE DISPENSING (Protocol 2):
//   DISC relies on passive dispensing over lift-off spots
//   (hydrophilic ITO pads) to form virtual microwells.
//   `mix` steps throughout Protocol 2 abstract each passive
//   dispense event -- the source droplet is translated across
//   the vacant lift-off spot and a sub-droplet is captured
//   by hydrophilic-hydrophobic contrast. One exchange
//   removes ~93% of the prior solution; two exchanges >99%.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main                 -- Protocol 1 entry: particle
//                               dispense through sample incubation
//   BB2  washLoop1            -- 4x wash after sample (pass)
//   BB3  washFail1            -- wash signal <= 0, drain
//   BB4  washJoin1            -- join: conjugate incubation
//   BB5  washLoop2            -- 4x wash after conjugate + 2x H2O2
//   BB6  washFail2            -- wash signal <= 0, drain
//   BB7  washJoin2            -- join: substrate activation
//   BB8  detectionPass        -- chemiluminescence detected
//   BB9  detectionFail        -- no signal, drain
//   BB10 discMain             -- Protocol 2 entry: cell seeding
//                               through permeabilization
//   BB11 stainPass            -- phospho signal > 0, dispose
//   BB12 stainFail            -- no signal, drain
//   BB13 finalCleanup         -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 1: DMF MAGNETIC PARTICLE IMMUNOASSAY (Chapter 2)
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Dispense magnetic particle suspension (~800 nL) from
//   reservoir. Magnet positioned beneath device immobilises
//   the pellet (~470 uN magnetic force, Fig 2.2C). The
//   ARCHITECT particle diluent (supernatant) is separated by
//   actuating it to waste, leaving the pellet behind.
//   Particles were pre-processed off-chip: washed 2x DI water,
//   blocked with NFDM (TSH only), resuspended at 3.0e8/mL in
//   DMF-compatible particle diluent (pH 10, Tris/NaCl/BSA/L64).
//   NOTE: `mix` here abstracts the supernatant separation step
//   (magnet on -> actuate supernatant -> magnet off -> rinse).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

particleDrop     = dispense 1 units of MagneticParticles_TSH
diluent          = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer

// Supernatant separation: magnet pellets particles;
// diluent is actuated away to waste; particle pellet remains.
particlePellet   = mix particleDrop with diluent for 5s
dispose diluent

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Merge 3 droplets of sample analyte with particle
//   pellet (one at a time, as in paper Fig 2.5A).
//   Incubate 6 min during circular shuttling mixing (4 electrodes).
//   TSH non-competitive: analyte forms sandwich with capture
//   Ab on particle and Anti-TSH-HRP conjugate.
//   E2 competitive: E2-HRP competes with analyte for Anti-E2
//   capture Ab sites; higher analyte -> lower signal.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sampleDrop1      = dispense 1 units of SampleAnalyte_TSH
sampleDrop2      = dispense 1 units of SampleAnalyte_TSH
sampleDrop3      = dispense 1 units of SampleAnalyte_TSH

sampleMix1       = mix particlePellet with sampleDrop1 for 2m
sampleMix2       = mix sampleMix1 with sampleDrop2 for 2m
sampleIncubated  = mix sampleMix2 with sampleDrop3 for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Wash particles 4x in wash buffer using supernatant
//   separation method.
//   Wash buffer (pH 7.7): Tris-base/Tris-HCl/NaCl/L64 0.05%.
//   Each wash: magnet on -> actuate supernatant -> magnet off
//   -> dispense wash buffer -> circularly mix to resuspend.
//   >98% unbound reagent removed by wash 2 (Fig 2.4C).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

wash1Drop        = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
wash2Drop        = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
wash3Drop        = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
wash4Drop        = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer

washed1          = mix sampleIncubated with wash1Drop for 5s
dispose wash1Drop
washed2          = mix washed1 with wash2Drop for 5s
dispose wash2Drop
washed3          = mix washed2 with wash3Drop for 5s
dispose wash3Drop
washed4          = mix washed3 with wash4Drop for 5s

// QC gate: check wash efficiency proxy signal.
washSignal1      = detect chemiluminescenceDetection on washed4 for 5s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Wash QC Gate #1
// Pass (BB2): washSignal1 > 0 -> particles washed, continue.
// Fail (BB3): washSignal1 <= 0 -> separation failure, drain.
// Both converge at BB4 (washJoin1).
// ============================================================

if (washSignal1 > 0) {
    dispose washed4
} else {
    drain washed4
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: washJoin1
// Dispose wash QC signal; proceed to conjugate incubation.
// ============================================================

dispose washSignal1

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Merge 1 droplet of conjugate solution with particle
//   pellet. Incubate 2 min.
//   TSH: Anti-TSH-HRP (2 ug/mL in conjugate diluent).
//   E2: E2-HRP (1 ug/mL in conjugate diluent).
//   Conjugate diluent (pH 7.4): Tris/NaCl/BSA/gelatin/L64.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pelletAfterWash1 = dispense 1 units of MagneticParticles_TSH
conjugateDrop    = dispense 1 units of AntiTSH_HRP_Conjugate
conjugateMix     = mix pelletAfterWash1 with conjugateDrop for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Wash 4x in wash buffer then 2x in H2O2.
//   Two H2O2 wash steps flush residual wash buffer and prevent
//   cross-contamination with luminol/enhancer.
//   (Paper protocol: replaces pre-mixing H2O2/luminol off-chip.)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cwash1Drop       = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
cwash2Drop       = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
cwash3Drop       = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
cwash4Drop       = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer
h2o2wash1        = dispense 1 units of H2O2_Peroxide
h2o2wash2        = dispense 1 units of H2O2_Peroxide

cwashed1         = mix conjugateMix with cwash1Drop for 5s
dispose cwash1Drop
cwashed2         = mix cwashed1 with cwash2Drop for 5s
dispose cwash2Drop
cwashed3         = mix cwashed2 with cwash3Drop for 5s
dispose cwash3Drop
cwashed4         = mix cwashed3 with cwash4Drop for 5s
dispose cwash4Drop
h2oWashed1       = mix cwashed4 with h2o2wash1 for 5s
dispose h2o2wash1
h2oWashed2       = mix h2oWashed1 with h2o2wash2 for 5s

// QC gate: check background after conjugate wash.
washSignal2      = detect chemiluminescenceDetection on h2oWashed2 for 5s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Wash QC Gate #2
// Pass (BB5): washSignal2 > 0 -> conjugate removed, continue.
// Fail (BB6): washSignal2 <= 0 -> wash failure, drain.
// Both converge at BB7 (washJoin2).
// ============================================================

if (washSignal2 > 0) {
    dispose h2oWashed2
} else {
    drain h2oWashed2
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: washJoin2
// Dispose wash QC signal #2; proceed to substrate activation.
// ============================================================

dispose washSignal2

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Reconstitute particle pellet in 1 droplet of H2O2.
//   Incubate 2 min. HRP on conjugate catalyzes H2O2 reduction;
//   particles moved to detection region (96-well plate format).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pelletAfterWash2 = dispense 1 units of MagneticParticles_TSH
h2o2Substrate    = dispense 1 units of H2O2_Peroxide
h2o2Incubated    = mix pelletAfterWash2 with h2o2Substrate for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Merge 1 droplet luminol/enhancer solution.
//   On-chip mixing (circular shuttling, 4 electrodes) activates
//   enzymatic chemiluminescence immediately before detection.
//   (Alternative to off-chip premixing, which had high CV.)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

luminolDrop      = dispense 1 units of Luminol_Enhancer
reactionMix      = mix h2o2Incubated with luminolDrop for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Incubate 1 min; detect chemiluminescence.
//   Device affixed to empty 96-well Stripwell plate; droplet
//   aligned to well. PHERAstar reader: LUM module,
//   focal height 8.6 mm, gain 3600.
//   Signal saturates at 1 min -> measurement endpoint.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat reactionMix at 25c for 60s

assaySignal = detect chemiluminescenceDetection on reactionMix for 60s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Immunoassay Detection Gate
// Pass (BB8): assaySignal > 0 -> valid chemiluminescence;
//             dispose, proceed to Protocol 2 (DISC).
// Fail (BB9): assaySignal <= 0 -> no signal; drain.
// Both converge at BB10 (discMain).
// ============================================================

if (assaySignal > 0) {
    dispose reactionMix
} else {
    drain reactionMix
}

// ============================================================
// PROTOCOL 2: DISC -- DIGITAL MICROFLUIDIC
// IMMUNOCYTOCHEMISTRY IN SINGLE CELLS (Chapter 5)
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB10: discMain
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1 (DISC): Seed NIH 3T3 cells onto DMF device top plate.
//   3 unit droplets (2.7 uL total) passively dispensed over
//   each lift-off spot, forming a ~400 nL virtual microwell.
//   Cell density: 500,000 cells/mL -> ~150-300 cells/VM.
//   Device inverted; incubate 24 h at 37 C in humidified
//   chamber to allow cell adhesion to hydrophilic ITO sites.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts passive dispensing over the lift-off
//   spot; not electrode-driven mixing in the DMF sense.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cellDrop1        = dispense 1 units of Cells_NIH3T3
cellDrop2        = dispense 1 units of Cells_NIH3T3
cellDrop3        = dispense 1 units of Cells_NIH3T3
mediaDrop1       = dispense 1 units of CultureMedia

cellSeed1        = mix cellDrop1 with mediaDrop1 for 5s
cellSeed2        = mix cellSeed1 with cellDrop2 for 5s
virtualMicrowell = mix cellSeed2 with cellDrop3 for 5s

// 24 h adhesion incubation at 37 C (= 1440 min).
heat virtualMicrowell at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2 (DISC): Starve cells to remove serum growth factors.
//   Starving media: 0.5% serum + 0.05% Pluronic F68 in DMEM.
//   5.4 uL (3 unit droplets passively dispensed) per VM.
//   Incubate 24 h at 37 C. Serum starvation synchronizes
//   basal PDGFR/Akt activity prior to PDGF-BB stimulation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

starveDrop1      = dispense 1 units of StarvingMedia
starveDrop2      = dispense 1 units of StarvingMedia
starveDrop3      = dispense 1 units of StarvingMedia

starve1          = mix virtualMicrowell with starveDrop1 for 5s
starve2          = mix starve1 with starveDrop2 for 5s
starvedCells     = mix starve2 with starveDrop3 for 5s

// 24 h starvation at 37 C (= 1440 min).
heat starvedCells at 37c for 1440m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3 (DISC): Rinse starving media with serum-free DMEM.
//   5.4 uL (3 unit droplets) per VM. One passive dispense
//   exchange removes ~93% of prior solution (Fig 5.2B);
//   three exchanges effectively clear starving media.
//   Serum-free DMEM: 0.05% F68, no serum, no growth factors.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sfDrop1          = dispense 1 units of SerumFreeMedia
sfDrop2          = dispense 1 units of SerumFreeMedia
sfDrop3          = dispense 1 units of SerumFreeMedia

sfRinse1         = mix starvedCells with sfDrop1 for 5s
sfRinse2         = mix sfRinse1 with sfDrop2 for 5s
rinsedCells      = mix sfRinse2 with sfDrop3 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4 (DISC): Stimulate cells with PDGF-BB (50 ng/mL).
//   2 unit droplets (1.8 uL) passively dispensed per VM.
//   Incubation: 2 min for PDGFR phosphorylation; 5 min for
//   Akt phosphorylation (this protocol targets Akt).
//   Dose-response: half-maximal at ~12.5 ng/mL (Hill n~1.73).
//   At 50 ng/mL: maximal PDGFR and Akt phosphorylation.
//   NOTE: DISC achieves ~4 s between stimulation and fixing
//   (Fig 5.2A), enabling sub-minute temporal resolution.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pdgfDrop1        = dispense 1 units of PDGF_BB_Ligand
pdgfDrop2        = dispense 1 units of PDGF_BB_Ligand

stimulated1      = mix rinsedCells with pdgfDrop1 for 5s
stimulatedCells  = mix stimulated1 with pdgfDrop2 for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5 (DISC): Fix cells immediately after stimulation.
//   2.7 uL of fixing solution (FS): 4% paraformaldehyde +
//   0.05% Pluronic F68 in DPBS. Incubate 10 min at room temp.
//   Rapid fixation (<4 s switch) preserves transient
//   phosphorylation state at the stimulation endpoint.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fixDrop1         = dispense 1 units of FixingSolution
fixDrop2         = dispense 1 units of FixingSolution
fixDrop3         = dispense 1 units of FixingSolution

fix1             = mix stimulatedCells with fixDrop1 for 5s
fix2             = mix fix1 with fixDrop2 for 5s
fixedCells       = mix fix2 with fixDrop3 for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6 (DISC): Rinse fixing solution with WB1.
//   5.4 uL (3 unit droplets) of WB1 (0.05% Pluronic F68 in
//   DPBS). Removes residual paraformaldehyde before
//   permeabilization.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

wb1Drop1         = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer1
wb1Drop2         = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer1
wb1Drop3         = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer1

wb1Rinse1        = mix fixedCells with wb1Drop1 for 5s
wb1Rinse2        = mix wb1Rinse1 with wb1Drop2 for 5s
rinsedFixed      = mix wb1Rinse2 with wb1Drop3 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7 (DISC): Permeabilize cells.
//   2.7 uL permeabilization solution (PS): 0.2% Tween-20 in
//   DPBS. Incubate 10 min at room temperature.
//   Tween-20 disrupts lipid membranes, enabling intracellular
//   antibody access to phospho-Akt (S473) and total Akt.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

permDrop1        = dispense 1 units of PermeabilizationSolution
permDrop2        = dispense 1 units of PermeabilizationSolution
permDrop3        = dispense 1 units of PermeabilizationSolution

perm1            = mix rinsedFixed with permDrop1 for 5s
perm2            = mix perm1 with permDrop2 for 5s
permCells        = mix perm2 with permDrop3 for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8 (DISC): Rinse with WB2.
//   8.1 uL (4.5 unit droplets, rounded to 4 dispensed here)
//   of WB2 (0.05% Brij 35 in DPBS). Brij 35 is used post-
//   permeabilization to maintain membrane permeability
//   without over-solubilizing intracellular structures.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

wb2Drop1         = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer2
wb2Drop2         = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer2
wb2Drop3         = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer2
wb2Drop4         = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer2

wb2Rinse1        = mix permCells with wb2Drop1 for 5s
wb2Rinse2        = mix wb2Rinse1 with wb2Drop2 for 5s
wb2Rinse3        = mix wb2Rinse2 with wb2Drop3 for 5s
rinsedPerm       = mix wb2Rinse3 with wb2Drop4 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9 (DISC): Block cells.
//   2.7 uL blocking solution (BS): 1% bovine non-fat dried
//   milk (NFDM) + 0.05% Brij 35 in DPBS.
//   Incubate 1 h at room temperature. Blocks non-specific
//   antibody binding sites on fixed, permeabilized cells.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

blockDrop1       = dispense 1 units of BlockingSolution
blockDrop2       = dispense 1 units of BlockingSolution
blockDrop3       = dispense 1 units of BlockingSolution

block1           = mix rinsedPerm with blockDrop1 for 5s
block2           = mix block1 with blockDrop2 for 5s
blockedCells     = mix block2 with blockDrop3 for 60m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10 (DISC): Primary antibody staining.
//   2.7 uL of primary antibody solution in BS:
//     - Pan Akt (Mouse mAb, 1:50, CST): detects total Akt.
//     - p-Akt S473 (Rabbit mAb, 1:200, CST): detects
//       phosphorylated (active) Akt.
//   Incubate overnight at 4 C (= 720 min modelled at 4 C).
//   Dual-antibody approach enables per-cell ratio of
//   phospho-Akt to total Akt (normalizes expression level).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

primAbDrop1      = dispense 1 units of PrimaryAntibody_Akt
primAbDrop2      = dispense 1 units of PrimaryAntibody_Akt
primAbDrop3      = dispense 1 units of PrimaryAntibody_Akt

primAb1          = mix blockedCells with primAbDrop1 for 5s
primAb2          = mix primAb1 with primAbDrop2 for 5s
primAbStained    = mix primAb2 with primAbDrop3 for 5s

// Overnight incubation at 4 C (720 min).
heat primAbStained at 4c for 720m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11 (DISC): Rinse unbound primary antibodies.
//   5.4 uL (3 unit droplets) WB2 (0.05% Brij 35 in DPBS).
//   Removes unbound primary antibodies; three passive
//   dispense exchanges -> >99% removal of prior solution.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pbwash1          = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer2
pbwash2          = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer2
pbwash3          = dispense 1 units of WashBuffer2

pbRinse1         = mix primAbStained with pbwash1 for 5s
pbRinse2         = mix pbRinse1 with pbwash2 for 5s
rinsedPrimAb     = mix pbRinse2 with pbwash3 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 12 (DISC): Secondary antibody staining.
//   2.7 uL secondary antibody solution in BS:
//     - Anti-Mouse IgG Alexa Fluor 647 (Fab2, 1:500, CST):
//       detects pan-Akt primary antibody.
//     - Anti-Rabbit IgG Alexa Fluor 555 (Fab2, 1:500, CST):
//       detects p-Akt S473 primary antibody.
//   Incubate 2 h at room temperature.
//   Readout: Axon GenePix 4000B scanner (532 nm / 635 nm),
//   CellProfiler quantifies single-cell p-Akt/total-Akt ratio.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

secAbDrop1       = dispense 1 units of SecondaryAntibody
secAbDrop2       = dispense 1 units of SecondaryAntibody
secAbDrop3       = dispense 1 units of SecondaryAntibody

secAb1           = mix rinsedPrimAb with secAbDrop1 for 5s
secAb2           = mix secAb1 with secAbDrop2 for 5s
secAbStained     = mix secAb2 with secAbDrop3 for 120m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 13 (DISC): Detect phospho-Akt signal.
//   phosphoProteinDetection module abstracts the off-chip
//   laser scanning cytometry step:
//     - Top plate removed from device.
//     - Rinsed in 10x-diluted DPBS/0.5% Tween-20 (30 s).
//     - Rinsed in DI water (30 s). Air dried in dark.
//     - Imaged: GenePix 4000B, 5 um resolution, 0 um focus,
//       532 nm + 635 nm lasers (100% power), PMT 500 V.
//   QC gate uses the Akt phosphorylation signal at 50 ng/mL
//   PDGF-BB stimulation (expected: ~3-5x fold change vs.
//   serum-free negative control, based on Fig 5.3C).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

phosphoSignal    = detect phosphoProteinDetection on secAbStained for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB11 / BB12: DISC Phospho-Protein Detection Gate
// Pass (BB11): phosphoSignal > 0 -> valid phospho-Akt signal;
//              dispose stained cell droplet.
// Fail (BB12): phosphoSignal <= 0 -> no signal (staining or
//              stimulation failure); drain.
// Both converge at BB13 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (phosphoSignal > 0) {
    dispose secAbStained
} else {
    drain secAbStained
}

// ============================================================
// BB13: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose both QC detection signals.
// Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose assaySignal
dispose phosphoSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module nimsLaserDetection
module nimsFluorescenceDetection
module nimsIonDetection

manifest BoronSilicon
manifest HydrofluoricAcid_Ethanol
manifest PerfluorinatedSiloxane_Initiator
manifest Nitrogen_Gas
manifest BSA_TrypticDigest
manifest Verapamil
manifest Bradykinin_1_7
manifest Bradykinin_2_9
manifest Neurotensin
manifest MCF7_Cells_MDA_MB231
manifest FDA_PI_Stain
manifest MouseEmbryoTissueSection
manifest FreshBlood
manifest FreshUrine
manifest AcetonitrileWater_01TFA

// ============================================================
// Clathrate Nanostructures for Mass Spectrometry
// Northen, Yanes, Northen, Marrinucci, Uritboonthai,
// Apon, Golledge, Nordstrom, Siuzdak -- Nature 2007, 449,
// 1033-1036. DOI: 10.1038/nature06195
//
// Protocol: Nanostructure-Initiator Mass Spectrometry (NIMS).
// The NIMS surface (nanostructured porous silicon, ~10-nm pores)
// traps perfluorinated siloxane initiator molecules within
// clathrate-like pores. Laser or ion irradiation vaporises
// the initiator, triggering desorption and ionisation of
// surface-adsorbed analytes without a conventional MALDI matrix.
//
// This BioScript file encodes the four primary application
// demonstrations from the paper in sequence:
//   (A) BSA tryptic digest and small molecule detection
//       (verapamil, 1-palmitoyllysophosphatidylcholine) -- Fig 1d
//   (B) Single cancer cell metabolite profiling (MDA-MB-231) -- Fig 2
//   (C) Peptide microarray characterisation (ion-NIMS, Bi3+) -- Fig 3
//   (D) Tissue imaging (mouse embryo, laser-NIMS) -- Fig 4
//   (E) Direct biofluid analysis (blood and urine) -- Fig 5
//
// NOTE ON NIMS SURFACE FABRICATION:
//   The NIMS chip itself is fabricated off-chip by anodic etching
//   of low-resistivity P-type <100> silicon (0.01-0.02 Ohm.cm) at
//   48 mA.cm^-2 in 25% HF/ethanol for 30 min, followed by initiator
//   loading and nitrogen-jet removal of excess. The `mix` steps in
//   SECTION I abstract this surface preparation sequence as a DMF
//   analogue. All subsequent analyte deposition steps represent
//   direct spotting (0.5 uL drops) or robotic contact printing
//   (1.4 nL drops) onto the prepared NIMS surface.
//
// NOTE ON DETECTOR MODULES:
//   nimsLaserDetection  -- abstracts VoyagerDE STR MALDI-TOF
//                          (337 nm N2 laser, reflector mode,
//                          +20 kV, 100-shot average)
//   nimsFluorescenceDetection -- abstracts Nikon TE2000
//                          epi-fluorescence with DAPI/FITC/TRITC
//                          filter sets (FDA/PI viability readout)
//   nimsIonDetection    -- abstracts ION-TOF IV TOF-SIMS with
//                          Bi3+ primary ion source (25 kV, 45deg
//                          angle, 50 ns pulse, <static dose limit)
//
// NOTE ON DURATION ENCODING:
//   BioScript duration tokens are `s` and `m` only.
//   30 min anodic etch = 30m; 30-60 min initiator loading = 45m
//   (midpoint); 100-shot laser average modelled as 30s detect.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main               -- entry: NIMS surface prep
//   BB2  surfacePrepPass    -- surfaceQC > 0: surface ready,
//                             proceed to analyte studies
//   BB3  surfacePrepFail    -- surfaceQC <= 0: etch failed, drain
//   BB4  surfacePrepJoin    -- join: analyte deposition begins
//   BB5  peptideAssayPass   -- peptideSignal > 0: BSA digest
//                             detected (>55% sequence coverage),
//                             dispose
//   BB6  peptideAssayFail   -- peptideSignal <= 0: no signal, drain
//   BB7  peptideAssayJoin   -- join: single-cell branch begins
//   BB8  cellAssayPass      -- cellSignal > 0: phospholipid
//                             ions from single cell detected,
//                             dispose
//   BB9  cellAssayFail      -- cellSignal <= 0: no cell signal,
//                             drain
//   BB10 cellAssayJoin      -- join: microarray branch begins
//   BB11 arrayAssayPass     -- arraySignal > 0: peptide array
//                             ions detected at 1 fmol, dispose
//   BB12 arrayAssayFail     -- arraySignal <= 0: no array signal,
//                             drain
//   BB13 arrayAssayJoin     -- join: tissue imaging branch begins
//   BB14 tissueImagingPass  -- tissueSignal > 0: lipid ion
//                             images acquired, dispose
//   BB15 tissueImagingFail  -- tissueSignal <= 0: no tissue
//                             signal, drain
//   BB16 tissueImagingJoin  -- join: biofluid branch begins
//   BB17 biofluidPass       -- biofluidSignal > 0: blood/urine
//                             metabolites detected, dispose
//   BB18 biofluidFail       -- biofluidSignal <= 0: no biofluid
//                             signal, drain
//   BB19 finalCleanup       -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// SECTION I: NIMS SURFACE FABRICATION
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Anodic etching of silicon to form nanostructured
//         porous silicon surface.
//         Material: P-type <100> boron-doped silicon wafer,
//         resistivity 0.01-0.02 Ohm.cm, 525 +/- 25 um thick,
//         100 mm diameter (Silicon Valley Microelectronics).
//         Etchant: 25% hydrofluoric acid in ethanol.
//         Current density: 48 mA.cm^-2, duration: 30 min.
//         Result: ~10-nm pore nanostructured silicon surface
//         (confirmed by SEM, Fig 1c). CAUTION: HF is acutely
//         hazardous; all handling under certified fume hood
//         with appropriate PPE and neutralising agent on hand.
//         `heat` at 25c models room-temperature anodic etch.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

siliconWafer  = dispense 1 units of BoronSilicon
etchSolution  = dispense 1 units of HydrofluoricAcid_Ethanol

// Anodic etch: 48 mA.cm^-2 for 30 min at ambient temperature.
// `mix` abstracts the electrochemical etching of the wafer
// surface by the HF/ethanol solution under applied current.
etchedSurface = mix siliconWafer with etchSolution for 30m
heat etchedSurface at 25c for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Initiator loading.
//         Undiluted bis(tridecafluoro-1,1,2,2-tetrahydrooctyl)
//         tetramethyldisiloxane applied to etched surface at
//         25degC for 30-60 min (45 min modelled here).
//         Initiator molecules migrate into the pores under
//         capillary forces and adopt a highly ordered clathrate-
//         like arrangement (confirmed by Supplementary Movie 1:
//         reversible migration on slight heating/cooling).
//         Excess initiator removed with nitrogen jet.
//         This step is the same for silanized and unsilanized
//         surfaces (Supplementary Information).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

initiator    = dispense 1 units of PerfluorinatedSiloxane_Initiator
nitrogenJet  = dispense 1 units of Nitrogen_Gas

// Load initiator: 45 min incubation at 25degC.
loadedSurface = mix etchedSurface with initiator for 45m
heat loadedSurface at 25c for 45m

// Nitrogen-jet removal of excess surface initiator.
nimsSurface   = mix loadedSurface with nitrogenJet for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: NIMS surface QC.
//         nimsLaserDetection module abstracts a test laser shot
//         to verify that the surface produces initiator-derived
//         background ions under 337 nm irradiation, confirming
//         successful pore filling before analyte deposition.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

surfaceQC = detect nimsLaserDetection on nimsSurface for 10s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Surface preparation QC gate
// Pass (BB2): surfaceQC > 0 -> initiator signal confirmed;
//             proceed to analyte characterisation sections.
// Fail (BB3): surfaceQC <= 0 -> etching or loading failed;
//             drain and repeat surface preparation.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (surfacePrepJoin).
// ============================================================

if (surfaceQC > 0) {
    dispose nimsSurface
} else {
    drain nimsSurface
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: surfacePrepJoin
// Dispose surface QC signal. Begin analyte deposition studies.
// ============================================================

dispose surfaceQC

// ============================================================
// SECTION II: LASER-NIMS -- PEPTIDE AND SMALL MOLECULE DETECTION
// Corresponds to Figure 1d (paper).
// Laser: 337 nm pulsed N2 (Voyager DE STR, Applied Biosystems).
// Initiator: bis(tridecafluoro-1,1,2,2-tetrahydrooctyl)
//            tetramethyldisiloxane, undiluted.
// Drop volume: 0.5 uL per spot (contact spotting).
// Acquisition: reflector mode, +20 kV, 100-shot average,
//              first grid 60%, guide wire 0.15%, delay 100 ns.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: BSA tryptic digest detection.
//         BSA reduced (DTT), alkylated (iodoacetamide), then
//         digested with mass-spec-grade porcine trypsin (Promega).
//         500 amol of BSA tryptic digest spotted in 0.5 uL
//         onto NIMS surface; solvent evaporated before analysis.
//         Expected result: 55% sequence coverage (Fig 1d,
//         upper right panel). Analyte in AcN/water/0.1% TFA.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bsaDigest     = dispense 1 units of BSA_TrypticDigest
bsaSolvent    = dispense 1 units of AcetonitrileWater_01TFA

// Prepare BSA tryptic digest spot: mix analyte in solvent.
// `mix` abstracts robotic or manual 0.5 uL drop deposition.
bsaSpot       = mix bsaDigest with bsaSolvent for 10s

// Evaporate solvent at room temperature (25degC, 5 min) before
// laser irradiation. Modelled as a 25degC heat step.
heat bsaSpot at 25c for 5m

// Laser-NIMS analysis: 337 nm, 100 shots averaged.
bsaSpot_nims  = mix bsaSpot with nimsSurface for 5s
peptideSignal = detect nimsLaserDetection on bsaSpot_nims for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Small molecule detection -- verapamil (700 ymol).
//         Calcium antagonist drug; molecular weight 454.6 Da.
//         700 ymol = 7e-22 mol. Lowest amount demonstrated
//         in Fig 1d (lower left panel). Spotted in 0.5 uL.
//         NOTE: 1 ymol (yoctomole) = 1e-24 mol; the paper
//         reports 700 ymol = 7e-22 mol, which equates to
//         approximately 420 molecules on the surface. This
//         represents the extreme sensitivity limit of NIMS
//         versus MALDI (typically 1 fmol range).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

verapamilDrop = dispense 1 units of Verapamil
verapamilSpot = mix verapamilDrop with nimsSurface for 5s
heat verapamilSpot at 25c for 5m

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Peptide/small molecule assay QC gate
// Pass (BB5): peptideSignal > 0 -> BSA digest detected at
//             500 amol (>55% coverage); dispose spots.
// Fail (BB6): peptideSignal <= 0 -> no signal; drain spots.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (peptideAssayJoin).
// ============================================================

if (peptideSignal > 0) {
    dispose bsaSpot_nims
    dispose verapamilSpot
} else {
    drain bsaSpot_nims
    drain verapamilSpot
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: peptideAssayJoin
// Dispose peptide QC signal. Begin single-cell branch.
// ============================================================

dispose peptideSignal

// ============================================================
// SECTION III: SINGLE-CELL METABOLITE PROFILING BY LASER-NIMS
// Corresponds to Figure 2 (paper).
// Cell line: MDA-MB-231 (highly metastatic breast cancer,
//            ATCC HTB-26). Grown in Leibovitz's L-15 medium
//            + 2 mM L-glutamine + 10% calf bovine serum at
//            37degC. Harvested by trypsin-EDTA treatment.
// Deposition: micromanipulator (MINJ-PD; Tritech Research)
//             used to deposit single cells directly onto NIMS.
// Initiator: bis(tridecafluoro-1,1,2,2-tetrahydrooctyl)
//            tetramethyldisiloxane calibrated with Na+, K+,
//            phosphocholine, 1-palmitoyllysophosphatidylcholine.
// Detection: phospholipids (endogenous), m/z 400-900 range.
//            Superior spectral complexity vs hundreds of cells
//            measured by nanoESI, MALDI, or ion-NIMS (Fig 2c).
// Post-MS: cells fixed (2% paraformaldehyde), permeabilised
//          (cold methanol), immunofluorescently stained with
//          DAPI + Alexa555-anti-Pan-cytokeratin.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Deposit single MDA-MB-231 cell onto NIMS surface.
//         Micromanipulator precisely counts and deposits a
//         single cell; cell dried before MS analysis.
//         `mix` abstracts cell deposition and drying step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

singleCell      = dispense 1 units of MCF7_Cells_MDA_MB231
cellOnSurface   = mix singleCell with nimsSurface for 2s
heat cellOnSurface at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Laser-NIMS acquisition on single cell.
//         337 nm irradiation identifies the cell location
//         (green fluorescent box, same laser at high intensity
//         -- Fig 2a). Lower-energy pulses then profile the
//         cell's phospholipid content. 100-shot average.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cellSignal = detect nimsLaserDetection on cellOnSurface for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Post-MS viability / immunofluorescence staining.
//         FDA/PI stain for live/dead assessment; DAPI nuclear
//         counterstain; Alexa555-cytokeratin for cell identity.
//         nimsFluo module abstracts Nikon TE2000 acquisition
//         with 20x Plan Fluor (NA 0.45) and 60x oil Plan Apo
//         (NA 1.40) objectives, Retiga EXi camera.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fdaPiStain      = dispense 1 units of FDA_PI_Stain
stainedCell     = mix cellOnSurface with fdaPiStain for 10m

fluoroSignal = detect nimsFluorescenceDetection on stainedCell for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB8 / BB9: Single-cell MS QC gate
// Pass (BB8): cellSignal > 0 -> endogenous phospholipids
//             detected from single cell; dispose.
// Fail (BB9): cellSignal <= 0 -> no cell signal; drain.
// Both branches converge at BB10 (cellAssayJoin).
// ============================================================

if (cellSignal > 0) {
    dispose stainedCell
    dispose fluoroSignal
} else {
    drain stainedCell
    drain fluoroSignal
}

// ============================================================
// BB10: cellAssayJoin
// Dispose cell QC signal. Begin peptide microarray branch.
// ============================================================

dispose cellSignal

// ============================================================
// SECTION IV: ION-NIMS PEPTIDE MICROARRAY CHARACTERISATION
// Corresponds to Figure 3 (paper).
// Ion source: ION-TOF IV TOF-SIMS with Bi3+ primary ions.
//             25 kV beam energy, 45deg angle, 50 ns pulse width,
//             primary ion dose below static limit.
//             Secondary ions directed to TOF tube at +/-2.5 kV
//             (sample at +2.5 kV, extraction lens at -4.5 kV).
// Lateral resolution: ~150 nm ion beam (Fig 3c inset).
// Peptides printed: bradykinin 2-9 (904 Da, red),
//                  neurotensin (1673 Da, blue),
//                  bradykinin 1-7 (757 Da, yellow) at 1 fmol.
// Printing: custom arrayer, ArrayIt contact printing head,
//           1.4 nL drops, 1:1 AcN/water + 0.1% TFA solution.
// Sensitivity: 1 fmol per peptide -- 1000x improvement over
//              conventional TOF-SIMS strategies (Ref 16, 21).
// Ion-NIMS surface: 14-kDa poly(3,3,3-trifluoropropylmethyl-
//   siloxanes) on silanized/vacuum-baked silicon, baked 100degC
//   overnight; excess removed with N2 jet + tBME rinse.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Print peptide array spots onto ion-NIMS surface.
//         Three peptide species deposited by robotic contact
//         printing (1.4 nL drops) from 1:1 AcN/water + 0.1%
//         TFA. `mix` abstracts robotic array printing.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bk27_solution   = dispense 1 units of Bradykinin_2_9
bk17_solution   = dispense 1 units of Bradykinin_1_7
nt_solution     = dispense 1 units of Neurotensin
arraySolvent    = dispense 1 units of AcetonitrileWater_01TFA

// Mix each peptide in solvent (1:1 AcN/water, 0.1% TFA).
bk27_spot       = mix bk27_solution with arraySolvent for 5s
bk17_spot       = mix bk17_solution with arraySolvent for 5s
nt_spot         = mix nt_solution with arraySolvent for 5s

// Print array onto ion-NIMS surface via robotic contact printing.
peptideArray    = mix bk27_spot with nimsSurface for 5s
peptideArray    = mix bk17_spot with peptideArray for 5s
peptideArray    = mix nt_spot with peptideArray for 5s

// Dry printed array at 25degC before Bi3+ ion analysis.
heat peptideArray at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Ion-NIMS Bi3+ imaging.
//          nimsIonDetection abstracts TOF-SIMS acquisition:
//          selective ion images for m/z 904 (BK 2-9, red),
//          m/z 1673 (neurotensin, blue), m/z 757 (BK 1-7,
//          yellow). High-resolution image with 150-nm beam
//          shows localisation at centre of printed spot
//          (Fig 3c). BK 1-7 on silicon control shows
//          significant fragmentation vs NIMS (Fig 3d).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

arraySignal = detect nimsIonDetection on peptideArray for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB11 / BB12: Peptide microarray QC gate
// Pass (BB11): arraySignal > 0 -> all three peptides detected
//              at 1 fmol with spatial localisation; dispose.
// Fail (BB12): arraySignal <= 0 -> ion imaging failed; drain.
// Both branches converge at BB13 (arrayAssayJoin).
// ============================================================

if (arraySignal > 0) {
    dispose peptideArray
} else {
    drain peptideArray
}

// ============================================================
// BB13: arrayAssayJoin
// Dispose array QC signal. Begin tissue imaging branch.
// ============================================================

dispose arraySignal

// ============================================================
// SECTION V: TISSUE IMAGING BY LASER-NIMS
// Corresponds to Figure 4 (paper).
// Sample: Mouse embryo, C57Bl/6J, embryonic day 16.5.
//         Embryos frozen in OCT compound on dry ice, equilibrated
//         to -20degC, cryosectioned at 12 um thickness
//         (Leica CM3050 S), thaw-mounted onto NIMS chip.
// Initiator: bis(heptadecafluoro-1,1,2,2-tetrahydrodecyl)
//            tetramethyldisiloxane.
// Imaging: 20-um step size, ~15-um diameter laser beam.
//          Two-step protocol:
//   (i)  Laser etching: 0.4 J.cm^-2 per pulse to ablate tissue
//        and expose underlying NIMS surface.
//   (ii) NIMS imaging: 0.01 J.cm^-2 per pulse to desorb/ionise
//        metabolites at the tissue/NIMS interface.
// Target lipids: glycerophosphatidylcholine (m/z 782, arrow,
//   Fig 4b) localised around developing vertebra. Also
//   phosphatidylethanolamine (via post-source decay fragment ions).
// Result: 300-um-wide false-colour mass intensity image shows
//   higher lipid signal (red/yellow/cyan) surrounding the
//   developing vertebra (dark blue). Scale bar: 1 mm.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: Tissue section preparation and mounting.
//          12-um cryosections thaw-mounted directly onto
//          NIMS chip. `mix` abstracts the thaw-mount step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tissueSection   = dispense 1 units of MouseEmbryoTissueSection
mountedTissue   = mix tissueSection with nimsSurface for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 12: Laser etching (ablation) phase.
//          High-energy laser pulses (0.4 J.cm^-2, 337 nm)
//          ablate the 12-um tissue slice to expose the
//          underlying NIMS surface at the tissue interface.
//          `heat` at 25degC abstracts the photothermal ablation
//          step (pulsed laser; no bulk heating intended).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat mountedTissue at 25c for 2m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 13: NIMS metabolite imaging (low-energy irradiation).
//          Low-energy pulses (0.01 J.cm^-2, 337 nm) interrogate
//          the tissue/NIMS interface; initiator vaporisation
//          desorbs metabolites from the etched surface.
//          100-shot average per raster point; 20-um steps.
//          nimsLaserDetection abstracts the spatially resolved
//          mass spectral acquisition across the tissue section.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tissueSignal = detect nimsLaserDetection on mountedTissue for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB14 / BB15: Tissue imaging QC gate
// Pass (BB14): tissueSignal > 0 -> lipid ion images (m/z 782,
//              GPC) acquired with anatomical contrast; dispose.
// Fail (BB15): tissueSignal <= 0 -> no tissue MS signal; drain.
// Both branches converge at BB16 (tissueImagingJoin).
// ============================================================

if (tissueSignal > 0) {
    dispose mountedTissue
} else {
    drain mountedTissue
}

// ============================================================
// BB16: tissueImagingJoin
// Dispose tissue QC signal. Begin direct biofluid branch.
// ============================================================

dispose tissueSignal

// ============================================================
// SECTION VI: DIRECT BIOFLUID ANALYSIS BY LASER-NIMS
// Corresponds to Figure 5 (paper).
// Samples: 1 uL fresh human blood and 1 uL fresh urine spotted
//          directly onto NIMS without any sample preparation.
//          After 30 s, droplets blown off with nitrogen stream.
// Blood:   Laser-etched first (0.4 J.cm^-2, ~0.5 s) to ablate
//          cellular material, then NIMS analysis of surface layer.
//          Initiator: bis(heptadecafluoro-1,1,2,2-tetrahydrodecyl)
//          tetramethyldisiloxane (perfluorinated, lipid-selective).
//          Dominant ions: phospholipids (e.g. PC lipids).
// Urine:   Spotted directly, no etch step. Two initiators tested:
//          (a) bis(heptadecafluoro...) -- lipid-selective, blue spectrum
//          (b) 1,3-bis(hydroxybutyl)tetramethyldisiloxane
//              (polar hydroxylated) -- small metabolites, green spectrum.
//          ~86% of ions from (b) unique to polar initiator surface.
// Quantitation: Codeine spiked into urine at multiple concentrations.
//          Linear NIMS response up to 15 ng.mL^-1 (Fig 5 inset).
//          Limit of detection <15 ng.mL^-1 vs 75-250 ng.mL^-1
//          for conventional bioanalytical approaches (Ref 28).
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 14: Blood analysis -- direct spot + laser etch.
//          1 uL fresh blood spotted, held 30 s, then excess
//          blown off with nitrogen. Laser-etch step ablates
//          red blood cell matrix before NIMS interrogation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bloodSample     = dispense 1 units of FreshBlood
nitrogenBlowOff = dispense 1 units of Nitrogen_Gas

bloodOnSurface  = mix bloodSample with nimsSurface for 30s
bloodExcess     = mix bloodOnSurface with nitrogenBlowOff for 5s

// Laser etch at high energy to ablate cellular blood components.
heat bloodExcess at 25c for 1m

// NIMS analysis of the laser-etched blood surface interface.
bloodNIMS       = mix bloodExcess with nimsSurface for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 15: Urine analysis -- direct spot, no etch.
//          1 uL fresh urine spotted directly onto NIMS; excess
//          blown off with N2. No laser-etch step for urine.
//          Urine spectra dominated by small metabolites <500 Da
//          (e.g. putative gangliosides GM2/GM1 also detected).
//          Spectra converted to ASCII, baseline-corrected,
//          normalised (TIC), and realigned in SpecAlign.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

urineSample     = dispense 1 units of FreshUrine
nitrogenBlowOff2 = dispense 1 units of Nitrogen_Gas

urineOnSurface  = mix urineSample with nimsSurface for 30s
urineExcess     = mix urineOnSurface with nitrogenBlowOff2 for 5s

urineNIMS       = mix urineExcess with nimsSurface for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 16: Laser-NIMS acquisition on blood and urine.
//          biofluidSignal keyed on blood (red spectrum, Fig 5).
//          Urine detection run in parallel.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

biofluidSignal  = detect nimsLaserDetection on bloodNIMS for 30s
urineSignal     = detect nimsLaserDetection on urineNIMS for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB17 / BB18: Biofluid analysis QC gate
// Pass (BB17): biofluidSignal > 0 -> blood phospholipids and
//              urine metabolites detected without background;
//              dispose all biofluid droplets.
// Fail (BB18): biofluidSignal <= 0 -> no biofluid signal
//              (initiator loading failure or spotting error);
//              drain all.
// Both branches converge at BB19 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (biofluidSignal > 0) {
    dispose bloodNIMS
    dispose urineNIMS
    dispose urineSignal
} else {
    drain bloodNIMS
    drain urineNIMS
    drain urineSignal
}

// ============================================================
// BB19: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all remaining QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose biofluidSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module transfectionEfficiencyDetection
module knockoutDetection
module knockInDetection
module exhaustionPhenotypeDetection
module cytokineDetection

manifest DMFectionBuffer
manifest SurfactantF_Buffer
manifest CD4_T_Cells
manifest SpCas9_Nuclease
manifest TRAC_sgRNA
manifest NTC_sgRNA
manifest ExhaustionTarget_sgRNA
manifest EGFP_mRNA
manifest ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator
manifest IL2_200IU
manifest IL2_10IU
manifest BasalTCellMedia
manifest GFP_cssDNA_Template
manifest AntiHER2_CAR_dsDNA_Template
manifest ProteinL_Stain
manifest PE_LAG3_Stain
manifest FC_Oil

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Miniaturized Scalable Arrayed CRISPR Screening in Primary
// Cells Enables Discovery at the Single Donor Resolution
// Patel, Boribong et al., Scientific Reports (2025) 15:29350
// DOI: 10.1038/s41598-025-13532-z
//
// Platform: DropGenie DMF Transfection System (48-site array,
// SBS-format cartridge, Echo 655 acoustic liquid handler for
// guide deposition, Assist Plus 8-channel liquid handler for
// cell/payload loading and offloading).
//
// This file encodes three sequential protocol arms in the
// order presented in the paper:
//
// ARM 1 (BB1-BB4):  TRAC knockout in primary human CD4+ /
//                   CD8+ T cells. Validates high-efficiency
//                   RNP delivery at 10,000 cells/edit.
//                   (Fig. 2B; ~90% TCR alpha/beta KO,
//                   >95% viability.)
//
// ARM 2 (BB5-BB8):  GFP knock-in at the TRAC locus using
//                   a circular single-stranded DNA (cssDNA)
//                   template (4 kb). Validates HDR delivery
//                   at 10,000 cells/edit.
//                   (Fig. 2D; ~52% GFP+ at 500V/2ms/2pulses.)
//
// ARM 3 (BB9-BB14): Arrayed CRISPR-Cas9 exhaustion screen
//                   in chronically stimulated primary human
//                   CD4+ T cells. 45 targets screened
//                   (192 edits total), 10,000 cells/condition.
//                   Readouts: LAG-3 surface expression,
//                   IFNgamma, TNFalpha secretion (ELISA).
//                   Top hits: SIN3A, PAF1, PRDM1, SETBP1,
//                   STAT1. (Fig. 3.)
//
// DROPLET / CELL LOADING ABSTRACTION:
//   All `mix` steps at the loading stage abstract the
//   Assist Plus 8-channel pipette loading operation into
//   the DropGenie cartridge (100 uL per well), not a
//   bulk-reagent mixing event. The DropGenie cartridge
//   uses Tri-Drop Electroporation: two conductive buffer
//   (DMFection Buffer) droplets flank a central cell/cargo
//   droplet; electrode actuation forms a transient low-
//   current electroporation zone.
//
// ELECTROPORATION PARAMETERS (all arms):
//   T cell KO (Pan CD3+):   500 V, 3 ms, 2 pulses, 5 min incubation
//   T cell KO (CD4+/Treg):  550 V, 2 ms, 2 pulses, 5 min incubation
//   T cell knock-in:         500 V, 2 ms, 2 pulses
//   Exhaustion screen:       550 V, 2 ms, 2 pulses
//   BioScript `heat` steps model the post-load incubation
//   period at 37 C before the electrode pulse sequence fires.
//   Pulse delivery itself is instantaneous and is abstracted
//   into the `mix` (loading/merge) step.
//
// RNP AMOUNTS PER EDIT (T cells, ARM 1 and ARM 3):
//   SpCas9:  0.42 pmol  (ARM 1 KO) / 1 pmol (ARM 3 screen)
//   sgRNA:   2.1  pmol  (ARM 1 KO) / 2 pmol (ARM 3 screen)
//   Cells:   10,000 per edit
//   Volume:  100 uL loaded per cartridge well
//   vs. conventional: 500,000 cells, 50 pmol sgRNA, 100 pmol Cas9
//   Reduction: 50x cells, ~25x sgRNA, ~100x Cas9 (Fig. 3G)
//
// GUIDE PRE-DEPOSITION (all arms):
//   sgRNAs are deposited dry onto the DropGenie substrate in
//   40 nL spots by the Echo 655 acoustic liquid handler,
//   mixed with Poly-L-glutamic acid (PGA) as a carrier, then
//   desiccated before cartridge assembly. The `dispense`
//   statements for sgRNA manifest this pre-deposition as a
//   unit-volume dispense event; BioScript has no native
//   acoustic dispense primitive.
//
// CELL ACTIVATION (pre-protocol, off-chip):
//   CD4+ T cells are thawed and cultured at 1.25e6 cells/mL
//   in Basal T Cell Media + 400 IU/mL IL-2 + 25 ng/mL IL-7
//   + 50 ng/mL IL-15 for 3 days prior to transfection.
//   This activation step is encoded as the first `heat`
//   step (37 C, 4320 min = 3 days) before cartridge loading.
//
// EXHAUSTION INDUCTION (ARM 3 only):
//   Post-KO, CD4+ T cells are re-stimulated every 3 days
//   with ImmunoCult CD3/CD28 + 10 IU/mL IL-2 for 28 days
//   to model chronic T cell exhaustion in vitro.
//   Each 3-day re-stimulation cycle encoded as:
//     heat at 37c for 4320m (3 days = 4320 min).
//   Nine cycles cover the full 28-day exhaustion window
//   (9 x 3 days = 27 days + terminal Day 28 assay readout).
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- T cell activation; ARM 1 TRAC KO
//                            cartridge loading; electroporation;
//                            post-KO culture
//   BB2  knockoutPass      -- knockoutSignal > 0: >80% TCR KO;
//                            dispose; proceed to ARM 2
//   BB3  knockoutFail      -- knockoutSignal <= 0: low KO; drain
//   BB4  knockoutJoin      -- join: ARM 2 (knock-in) begins
//   BB5  knockInPass       -- knockInSignal > 0: GFP+ detected;
//                            dispose; proceed to ARM 3
//   BB6  knockInFail       -- knockInSignal <= 0: no GFP+; drain
//   BB7  knockInJoin       -- join: ARM 3 (screen) begins
//   BB8  screenSetup       -- screen RNP assembly and loading
//                            (inline -- no branch; part of main
//                            before exhaustion induction)
//   BB9  exhaustionLoop    -- 9x chronic re-stimulation cycles
//                            (unrolled; BioScript has no loop
//                            primitive; see NOTE below)
//   BB10 assayReadout      -- LAG-3 and cytokine detection
//   BB11 screenPass        -- screenSignal > 0: escape hits
//                            detected; dispose
//   BB12 screenFail        -- screenSignal <= 0: no escape; drain
//   BB13 finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
//
// NOTE ON LOOP UNROLLING:
//   BioScript (lilott8/BioScript) does not support `repeat`
//   or `while` loop constructs. The 9 chronic re-stimulation
//   cycles are therefore unrolled as 9 sequential `mix` +
//   `heat` pairs. Each pair models one 3-day stimulation
//   cycle (ImmunoCult + IL-2 addition, 37 C for 4320 min).
//   This is the same unrolling strategy used in reference
//   BioScript files for multi-step incubation protocols.
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRE-PROTOCOL: T cell activation (off-chip, 3 days).
//   CD4+ T cells thawed into Basal T Cell Media + cytokines.
//   Cultured at 37 C / 5% CO2 for 72 h before transfection.
//   `heat` abstracts the 3-day activation incubation
//   (3 days = 4320 min).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

basalMedia      = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2Stock        = dispense 1 units of IL2_200IU
activator       = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator
cd4Cells        = dispense 1 units of CD4_T_Cells

activationMedia = mix basalMedia with il2Stock for 5s
activationMix   = mix activationMedia with activator for 5s
activatedCells  = mix cd4Cells with activationMix for 5s

// 3-day activation at 37 C / 5% CO2 (3 days = 4320 min).
heat activatedCells at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 1 - STEP 1: Prepare Transfection Buffer (cell wash).
//   Cells washed in DMFection Buffer then resuspended in
//   Transfection Buffer (0.01% Surfactant F in DMFection
//   Buffer) at 1.0e7 cells/mL before cartridge loading.
//   `mix` abstracts the wash and resuspension step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dmfBuffer1      = dispense 1 units of DMFectionBuffer
surfactantBuf1  = dispense 1 units of SurfactantF_Buffer

transfectionBuf1 = mix dmfBuffer1 with surfactantBuf1 for 5s
cellSuspension1  = mix activatedCells with transfectionBuf1 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 1 - STEP 2: Pre-deposit TRAC sgRNA dry onto substrate
//   via Echo 655 (40 nL spots, sgRNA + PGA carrier).
//   SpCas9 loaded into cartridge wells with cells.
//   `mix` abstracts cartridge well loading via Assist Plus.
//   Each edit: 10,000 cells, 2.1 pmol TRAC sgRNA (pre-dry),
//   0.42 pmol SpCas9, 100 uL total volume.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tracSgRNA1      = dispense 1 units of TRAC_sgRNA
cas9Load1       = dispense 1 units of SpCas9_Nuclease

rnpMix1         = mix tracSgRNA1 with cas9Load1 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 1 - STEP 3: Load cells + RNP onto DropGenie cartridge.
//   Incubate 5 min at room temperature (pre-pulse incubation).
//   `heat` at 25 C for 5 min models the 5 min incubation
//   before electrode pulse delivery.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cartridgeLoad1  = mix cellSuspension1 with rnpMix1 for 5s

// 5-min pre-pulse incubation at room temperature (25 C).
heat cartridgeLoad1 at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 1 - STEP 4: Electroporation (550 V, 2 ms, 2 pulses).
//   FC_Oil filler droplets abstract the Tri-Drop flanking
//   buffer droplets that complete the electroporation circuit.
//   `mix` with FC_Oil models droplet merge / circuit formation
//   on the electrode array. The actual pulse is abstracted
//   into this merge event.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

oilBuffer1      = dispense 1 units of FC_Oil

electroporatedCells1 = mix cartridgeLoad1 with oilBuffer1 for 2s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 1 - STEP 5: Offload and post-KO culture (3 days).
//   Cells offloaded into Basal T Cell Media + 200 IU/mL IL-2
//   in a 96-well round-bottom plate via Assist Plus.
//   Cultured 72 h at 37 C / 5% CO2 before flow cytometry.
//   (3 days = 4320 min).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

recoveryMedia1  = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2Recovery1    = dispense 1 units of IL2_200IU

recoveryMix1    = mix recoveryMedia1 with il2Recovery1 for 5s
kulturedCells1  = mix electroporatedCells1 with recoveryMix1 for 5s

// 72 h post-KO culture at 37 C / 5% CO2.
heat kulturedCells1 at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 1 - STEP 6: KO detection via flow cytometry.
//   knockoutDetection module abstracts TCR alpha/beta surface
//   staining and flow cytometry analysis (Attune NxT).
//   Readout: % TCR alpha/beta-negative cells in CD4+ and
//   CD8+ subsets. Target: >80% TCR KO (paper: 90.41% CD4+,
//   91.87% CD8+; viability >97%).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

knockoutSignal  = detect knockoutDetection on kulturedCells1 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: TRAC KO QC gate
// Pass (BB2): knockoutSignal > 0 -> >80% TCR alpha/beta KO
//             confirmed; dispose cells; proceed to ARM 2.
// Fail (BB3): knockoutSignal <= 0 -> insufficient KO
//             (e.g. guide failure, electroporation fault);
//             drain and do not proceed.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (knockoutJoin).
// ============================================================

if (knockoutSignal > 0) {
    dispose kulturedCells1
} else {
    drain kulturedCells1
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: knockoutJoin
// Dispose KO QC signal. Control falls into ARM 2 (knock-in).
// ============================================================

dispose knockoutSignal

// ============================================================
// ARM 2: GFP Knock-In at TRAC Locus via cssDNA HDR Template
// (Fig. 2D; 10,000 Pan CD3+ T cells/edit, 4 kb GFP cssDNA,
//  0.03-0.06 ug template/edit, 500 V, 2 ms, 2 pulses,
//  10-day expansion, ~52% GFP+ at 0.03 ug/500V/2ms/2pulses)
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// BB5 - ARM 2, STEP 1: T cell activation (separate donor,
//   Dynabead CD3/CD28 protocol for knock-in experiments).
//   Pan CD3+ T cells activated 3 days at 37 C.
//   Dynabeads removed before transfection (off-chip).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

basalMedia2     = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2KI           = dispense 1 units of IL2_200IU
cd3Cells2       = dispense 1 units of CD4_T_Cells

activationMix2  = mix basalMedia2 with il2KI for 5s
activatedCells2 = mix cd3Cells2 with activationMix2 for 5s

// 3-day Dynabead activation at 37 C (3 days = 4320 min).
heat activatedCells2 at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 2 - STEP 2: Prepare Transfection Buffer for KI run.
//   Cells resuspended at 1.0e7 cells/mL in Transfection Buffer.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dmfBuffer2      = dispense 1 units of DMFectionBuffer
surfactantBuf2  = dispense 1 units of SurfactantF_Buffer

transfectionBuf2 = mix dmfBuffer2 with surfactantBuf2 for 5s
cellSuspension2  = mix activatedCells2 with transfectionBuf2 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 2 - STEP 3: Assemble TRAC RNP for KI experiment.
//   110 pmol TRAC sgRNA + 33 pmol SpCas9, complexed on ice
//   for 20 min. Per edit: 1.1 pmol sgRNA, 0.33 pmol Cas9.
//   `heat` at 4 C for 20 min models on-ice RNP assembly.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tracSgRNA2      = dispense 1 units of TRAC_sgRNA
cas9Load2       = dispense 1 units of SpCas9_Nuclease

rnpAssembly2    = mix tracSgRNA2 with cas9Load2 for 5s

// 20 min on-ice RNP complexing (4 C, 20 min).
heat rnpAssembly2 at 4c for 20m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 2 - STEP 4: Add GFP cssDNA template to cartridge load.
//   0.03 ug GFP cssDNA (circular single-stranded DNA) per edit.
//   cssDNA from Full Circles Therapeutics; provides improved
//   intracellular stability and targeted genomic integration
//   efficiency vs dsDNA.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

gfpTemplate     = dispense 1 units of GFP_cssDNA_Template

rnpTemplateMix2 = mix rnpAssembly2 with gfpTemplate for 5s
cartridgeLoad2  = mix cellSuspension2 with rnpTemplateMix2 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 2 - STEP 5: Electroporation (500 V, 2 ms, 2 pulses).
//   Tri-Drop circuit formation abstracted as mix with FC_Oil.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

oilBuffer2      = dispense 1 units of FC_Oil

electroporatedCells2 = mix cartridgeLoad2 with oilBuffer2 for 2s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 2 - STEP 6: Offload and 10-day expansion.
//   Cells offloaded into Basal T Cell Media + 300 IU/mL IL-2.
//   10-day culture at 37 C / 5% CO2 before flow cytometry
//   and fluorescence microscopy readout.
//   (10 days = 14400 min).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

recoveryMedia2  = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2Recovery2    = dispense 1 units of IL2_200IU

recoveryMix2    = mix recoveryMedia2 with il2Recovery2 for 5s
kulturedCells2  = mix electroporatedCells2 with recoveryMix2 for 5s

// 10-day expansion at 37 C (10 days = 14400 min).
heat kulturedCells2 at 37c for 14400m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 2 - STEP 7: GFP knock-in detection via flow cytometry.
//   knockInDetection module abstracts GFP positivity gating
//   (Attune NxT; TRAC KO co-stain to confirm on-target HDR).
//   Expected: ~52% GFP+ cells at 0.03 ug / 500V / 2ms / 2p.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

knockInSignal   = detect knockInDetection on kulturedCells2 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: KI QC gate
// Pass (BB5): knockInSignal > 0 -> GFP+ cells confirmed
//             (HDR successful); dispose; proceed to ARM 3.
// Fail (BB6): knockInSignal <= 0 -> no GFP integration;
//             drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (knockInJoin).
// ============================================================

if (knockInSignal > 0) {
    dispose kulturedCells2
} else {
    drain kulturedCells2
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: knockInJoin
// Dispose KI QC signal. Control falls into ARM 3 (screen).
// ============================================================

dispose knockInSignal

// ============================================================
// ARM 3: Arrayed CRISPR-Cas9 Exhaustion Screen
// 45 targets x 4 replicates = 192 edits on DropGenie array.
// 10,000 CD4+ T cells / edit, 2 pmol sgRNA, 1 pmol Cas9.
// 28-day chronic stimulation model, LAG-3 + cytokine readout.
// (Fig. 3; top hits: SIN3A, PAF1, PRDM1, SETBP1, STAT1)
//
// This arm encodes one representative screen condition
// (ExhaustionTarget_sgRNA) in full. The same cartridge
// loading, electroporation, and exhaustion induction workflow
// is applied in parallel across all 48 addressable sites of
// the DropGenie cartridge per run (4 runs for 192 edits).
// Parallelism is abstracted here as sequential BioScript
// statements, consistent with the reference .bs files.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// BB8 - ARM 3, STEP 1: CD4+ T cell activation (screen donor).
//   CD4+ Helper T cells (AllCells) thawed and activated:
//   1.25e6 cells/mL, Basal T Cell Media + 400 IU/mL IL-2 +
//   25 ng/mL IL-7 + 50 ng/mL IL-15, 3 days at 37 C.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

basalMedia3     = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2Screen       = dispense 1 units of IL2_200IU
cd4Cells3       = dispense 1 units of CD4_T_Cells

activationMix3  = mix basalMedia3 with il2Screen for 5s
activatedCells3 = mix cd4Cells3 with activationMix3 for 5s

// 3-day activation at 37 C (3 days = 4320 min).
heat activatedCells3 at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 3 - STEP 2: Prepare Transfection Buffer (screen).
//   Cells washed and resuspended at 1.0e7 cells/mL in
//   Transfection Buffer. Echo 655 pre-deposits ExhaustionTarget
//   sgRNA + PGA (40 nL / site) onto substrate before assembly.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dmfBuffer3      = dispense 1 units of DMFectionBuffer
surfactantBuf3  = dispense 1 units of SurfactantF_Buffer

transfectionBuf3 = mix dmfBuffer3 with surfactantBuf3 for 5s
cellSuspension3  = mix activatedCells3 with transfectionBuf3 for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 3 - STEP 3: Assemble screen RNP.
//   ExhaustionTarget_sgRNA pre-deposited dry on substrate.
//   SpCas9 loaded into cartridge wells with cells.
//   Per edit: 10,000 cells, 2 pmol sgRNA, 1 pmol Cas9,
//   100 uL total volume.
//   RNP complexing occurs in situ on the cartridge during
//   the 5-min pre-pulse incubation (modelled as heat 25 C, 5m).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

screenSgRNA     = dispense 1 units of ExhaustionTarget_sgRNA
cas9Screen      = dispense 1 units of SpCas9_Nuclease

screenRnpMix    = mix screenSgRNA with cas9Screen for 5s
cartridgeLoad3  = mix cellSuspension3 with screenRnpMix for 5s

// 5-min pre-pulse incubation at room temperature (25 C).
heat cartridgeLoad3 at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 3 - STEP 4: Electroporation (550 V, 2 ms, 2 pulses).
//   Tri-Drop circuit formation abstracted as mix with FC_Oil.
//   DropGenie fires each of the 48 sites independently under
//   user-defined parameters set via the instrument software.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

oilBuffer3      = dispense 1 units of FC_Oil

electroporatedCells3 = mix cartridgeLoad3 with oilBuffer3 for 2s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 3 - STEP 5: Offload and 3-day initial recovery.
//   Cells offloaded into Basal T Cell Media + 200 IU/mL IL-2
//   (96-well round-bottom plate via Assist Plus).
//   3-day recovery culture before exhaustion induction begins.
//   (3 days = 4320 min).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

recoveryMedia3  = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2Recovery3    = dispense 1 units of IL2_200IU

recoveryMix3    = mix recoveryMedia3 with il2Recovery3 for 5s
recoveredCells3 = mix electroporatedCells3 with recoveryMix3 for 5s

// 3-day initial recovery at 37 C.
heat recoveredCells3 at 37c for 4320m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 3 - STEPS 6-14: Chronic re-stimulation for exhaustion
//   induction (9 cycles x 3 days = 27 days, Days 3-30).
//
//   Protocol: ImmunoCult CD3/CD28 (25 uL/mL) + 10 IU/mL IL-2
//   in 50 uL Basal T Cell Media, media change every 3 days.
//   This recapitulates progressive T cell dysfunction in vivo
//   (chronic antigen exposure model).
//
//   BioScript has no loop primitive; 9 cycles unrolled below.
//   Each cycle: dispense fresh stimulation media, mix with
//   cells, heat 37 C for 4320 min (3 days).
//
//   NOTE: ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator manifest is reused
//   across cycles (same reagent, fresh aliquot each media
//   change). IL2_10IU manifest = 10 IU/mL IL-2 stock (lower
//   concentration than activation arm; paper M&M specifies
//   10 IU/mL for exhaustion induction vs 200 IU/mL activation).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 1 (Days 3-6) --
stimMedia_c1    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c1          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c1    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c1      = mix stimMedia_c1 with il2_c1 for 5s
stimMix_c1b     = mix stimMix_c1 with activator_c1 for 5s
stimCells_c1    = mix recoveredCells3 with stimMix_c1b for 5s
heat stimCells_c1 at 37c for 4320m

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 2 (Days 6-9) --
stimMedia_c2    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c2          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c2    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c2      = mix stimMedia_c2 with il2_c2 for 5s
stimMix_c2b     = mix stimMix_c2 with activator_c2 for 5s
stimCells_c2    = mix stimCells_c1 with stimMix_c2b for 5s
heat stimCells_c2 at 37c for 4320m

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 3 (Days 9-12) --
stimMedia_c3    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c3          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c3    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c3      = mix stimMedia_c3 with il2_c3 for 5s
stimMix_c3b     = mix stimMix_c3 with activator_c3 for 5s
stimCells_c3    = mix stimCells_c2 with stimMix_c3b for 5s
heat stimCells_c3 at 37c for 4320m

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 4 (Days 12-15) --
stimMedia_c4    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c4          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c4    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c4      = mix stimMedia_c4 with il2_c4 for 5s
stimMix_c4b     = mix stimMix_c4 with activator_c4 for 5s
stimCells_c4    = mix stimCells_c3 with stimMix_c4b for 5s
heat stimCells_c4 at 37c for 4320m

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 5 (Days 15-18) --
stimMedia_c5    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c5          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c5    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c5      = mix stimMedia_c5 with il2_c5 for 5s
stimMix_c5b     = mix stimMix_c5 with activator_c5 for 5s
stimCells_c5    = mix stimCells_c4 with stimMix_c5b for 5s
heat stimCells_c5 at 37c for 4320m

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 6 (Days 18-21) --
stimMedia_c6    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c6          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c6    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c6      = mix stimMedia_c6 with il2_c6 for 5s
stimMix_c6b     = mix stimMix_c6 with activator_c6 for 5s
stimCells_c6    = mix stimCells_c5 with stimMix_c6b for 5s
heat stimCells_c6 at 37c for 4320m

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 7 (Days 21-24) --
stimMedia_c7    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c7          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c7    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c7      = mix stimMedia_c7 with il2_c7 for 5s
stimMix_c7b     = mix stimMix_c7 with activator_c7 for 5s
stimCells_c7    = mix stimCells_c6 with stimMix_c7b for 5s
heat stimCells_c7 at 37c for 4320m

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 8 (Days 24-27) --
stimMedia_c8    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c8          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c8    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c8      = mix stimMedia_c8 with il2_c8 for 5s
stimMix_c8b     = mix stimMix_c8 with activator_c8 for 5s
stimCells_c8    = mix stimCells_c7 with stimMix_c8b for 5s
heat stimCells_c8 at 37c for 4320m

// -- Exhaustion Cycle 9 / Final Re-stimulation (Days 27-28) --
// Day 26 (paper): cells re-stimulated for 48 h before
// supernatant collection and final flow cytometry on Day 28.
// 48 h final stimulation = 2880 min.
stimMedia_c9    = dispense 1 units of BasalTCellMedia
il2_c9          = dispense 1 units of IL2_10IU
activator_c9    = dispense 1 units of ImmunoCult_CD3CD28_Activator

stimMix_c9      = mix stimMedia_c9 with il2_c9 for 5s
stimMix_c9b     = mix stimMix_c9 with activator_c9 for 5s
stimCells_c9    = mix stimCells_c8 with stimMix_c9b for 5s

// Final 48 h re-stimulation at 37 C before assay readout.
heat stimCells_c9 at 37c for 2880m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 3 - STEP 15: LAG-3 exhaustion phenotype detection.
//   exhaustionPhenotypeDetection module abstracts:
//     1. PE-LAG3 surface staining (BioLegend)
//     2. Ghost Dye Violet 510 viability staining
//     3. CD4 co-stain (lineage gate)
//     4. Attune NxT flow cytometry analysis
//   Readout: % LAG3-negative CD4+ T cells (exhaustion escape).
//   screenSignal > 0 if LAG3- frequency > NTC control mean.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lag3Stain       = dispense 1 units of PE_LAG3_Stain
stainedForFACS  = mix stimCells_c9 with lag3Stain for 20m

screenSignal    = detect exhaustionPhenotypeDetection on stainedForFACS for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB10 / BB11: Screen assay QC gate (LAG-3 readout)
// Pass (BB10): screenSignal > 0 -> LAG3- frequency above
//              NTC baseline; candidate escape hit confirmed;
//              proceed to cytokine validation branch.
// Fail (BB11): screenSignal <= 0 -> no exhaustion rescue;
//              drain.
// Both branches converge at BB12 before cytokine dispose.
// ============================================================

if (screenSignal > 0) {
    dispose stainedForFACS
} else {
    drain stainedForFACS
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ARM 3 - STEP 16: Cytokine secretion readout (ELISA).
//   Supernatants collected 48 h after final re-stimulation
//   (Day 28) and assayed for IFNgamma and TNFalpha by
//   sandwich ELISA (RayBiotech kits).
//   4PL regression used to interpolate concentrations.
//   Background subtracted using media-only (MO) control.
//   cytokineDetection module abstracts ELISA plate reader
//   readout and signal normalisation.
//   This step runs regardless of LAG-3 gate outcome to
//   capture composite exhaustion escape score data
//   (z-score of IFNy + TNFa + %LAG3- per Fig. 3F/S5C).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cytokineSignal  = detect cytokineDetection on screenSignal for 60s

// ============================================================
// BB13: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose both QC signal droplets.
// Experiment ends; data passed to composite escape scoring
// pipeline (off-chip; z-score normalization, radar plot).
// ============================================================

dispose cytokineSignal
dispose screenSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module nfkbActivationDetection
module immunofluorescenceDetection

manifest NIH3T3_p65dsRed_H2BGFP_Cells
manifest FibronectinCoating
manifest PBS
manifest FluoroBriteDMEM_TNFalpha
manifest ParaformaldehydeFixative
manifest BlockingPermeabilizationBuffer
manifest IkBa_PrimaryAntibody
manifest AlexaFluor647_SecondaryAntibody

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Computer Vision Reveals Hidden Variables Underlying
// NF-kB Activation in Single Cells
// Patel, Drayman, Liu, Bilgic, Tay - Sci. Adv. 2021; 7: eabg4135
// DOI: 10.1126/sciadv.abg4135
//
// Protocol: High-throughput microfluidic live-cell imaging of
// mouse NIH 3T3 fibroblasts expressing p65-dsRed and H2B-GFP
// reporters, TNF-alpha stimulation, single-cell NF-kB nuclear
// translocation tracking, and fixed-cell immunofluorescence
// validation of the IkB/NF-kB ratio as the primary determinant
// of heterogeneous single-cell activation.
//
// CELL LINE: Knockout p65(-/-) mouse NIH 3T3 fibroblasts
//   re-engineered with p65-dsRed under native p65 promotor and
//   ubiquitin-promotor-driven H2B-GFP (nuclear marker).
//   Minimum fluorescence clone selected for endogenous NF-kB
//   expression levels. (Materials and Methods, paper p. 8)
//
// MICROFLUIDIC DEVICE: PDMS chambers coated with fibronectin
//   (FC010-10MG) overnight. Cells seeded at ~20,000 cells/cm2
//   and cultured for 5 h before stimulation at 37 C / 5% CO2.
//   TNF-alpha vials pressurized at 5 psi, connected via PEEK
//   microbore tubing (VICI). Device mounted on Nikon Eclipse Ti2
//   microscope with Hamamatsu ORCA-Flash4.0 V3 Digital CMOS
//   camera (C13440), 20x magnification, 5 min imaging interval.
//
// STIMULATION: TNF-alpha (PMC3014_3671982503) diluted in
//   FluoroBrite DMEM (A1896701) with 2x GlutaMAX (35050061),
//   penicillin/streptomycin (15140-122), and FBS (16140071).
//   Final concentration: 0.1 ng/mL for main single-dose dataset
//   (n = 2113 cells). Multi-dose dataset: 0.005, 0.05, 0.5,
//   5 ng/mL (n = 3456 cells total). (Materials and Methods, p. 8)
//
// ACTIVATION LABELING: NF-kB activation quantified as mean
//   nuclear fluorescence intensity (background corrected).
//   Peak analysis: MATLAB smooth + z-score normalization,
//   then peak detection. Binary activation label: nuclear
//   p65_peak - nuclear p65_(t=0) > 500 threshold. (Fig. S4A)
//   Activated cells show complete cytoplasm-to-nucleus p65
//   translocation in digital (switch-like) fashion. (Fig. 1D)
//
// MACHINE LEARNING: Image-based SVM (cubic kernel, 73% accuracy
//   on full multi-dose dataset; 79.4% on single-dose independent
//   validation set, n = 2113). 236 hand-crafted features per
//   cell including nuclear p65-dsRed level, SD, phase texture
//   (entropy, pixel correlation, information measure),
//   Otsu dimension, SFTA. Top predictor: mean nuclear p65-dsRed
//   prestimulation level (r = 0.62 with activation probability).
//   (Results section, paper pp. 2-4; table S1; fig. S4)
//
// FIXED-CELL IMMUNOFLUORESCENCE: Validates IkBa/NF-kB ratio
//   as activation probability determinant. Primary: rabbit
//   polyclonal anti-IkB-alpha (ab7217). Secondary: goat
//   polyclonal anti-rabbit IgG H&L Alexa Fluor 647 (G378361).
//   Imaging at time point immediately preceding fixation (5 min
//   before fixation) for UMAP alignment. (Materials and Methods)
//
// DURATION TOKENS: BioScript supports s and m only.
//   No h token. 5 h culture = 300m. Overnight fibronectin
//   coating = 720m. 1.5 h prestimulation imaging = 90m.
//   6 h post-stimulation imaging = 360m.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main               -- device prep, cell seeding, culture,
//                              prestimulation imaging, TNF stimulation,
//                              post-stimulation imaging, QC detection
//   BB2  activationPass     -- activationSignal > 0: NF-kB activated
//                              (p65 peak threshold exceeded); proceed
//                              to fixed-cell immunofluorescence
//   BB3  activationFail     -- activationSignal <= 0: non-responding
//                              cell; drain unstained cell droplet
//   BB4  activationJoin     -- join: IkBa staining branch begins
//   BB5  stainingPass       -- ifStainingSignal > 0: IkB/NF-kB ratio
//                              quantified; dispose stained sample
//   BB6  stainingFail       -- ifStainingSignal <= 0: staining failed;
//                              drain stained sample
//   BB7  finalCleanup       -- terminal: dispose QC signals; end
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Coat PDMS microfluidic chamber with fibronectin
//         (FC010-10MG) overnight at room temperature to
//         promote cell adhesion. Overnight = 720m (12 h;
//         paper states "coated with fibronectin overnight").
//         This models the surface functionalization step
//         prior to cell loading.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fibronectinSolution = dispense 1 units of FibronectinCoating
coatedChamber       = mix fibronectinSolution with fibronectinSolution for 720m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Wash unbound fibronectin with PBS before cell
//         seeding. 1-min PBS wash to remove excess coating.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pbsWash1    = dispense 1 units of PBS
chamberwash = mix coatedChamber with pbsWash1 for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Seed NIH 3T3 p65-dsRed / H2B-GFP reporter cells
//         into the fibronectin-coated microfluidic chamber.
//         Density: ~20,000 cells/cm2. Cells taken at 100%
//         confluence via 0.25% trypsin-ETDA (25200-056) for
//         5 min before loading (off-chip; modelled as part
//         of the cell dispense step here).
//         Culture conditions: 5% CO2, 37 C, 5 h before
//         stimulation (= 300m).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cellSuspension  = dispense 1 units of NIH3T3_p65dsRed_H2BGFP_Cells
seededChamber   = mix chamberwash with cellSuspension for 5s

// 5-hour pre-stimulation culture at 37 C / 5% CO2.
// Cells allowed to adhere and equilibrate to endogenous
// p65-dsRed / H2B-GFP expression levels before imaging.
heat seededChamber at 37c for 300m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Prestimulation imaging (1.5 h = 90m).
//         Phase and fluorescence images captured every 5 min
//         at 20x magnification. p65-dsRed (red) and H2B-GFP
//         (green) channels acquired simultaneously.
//         This unperturbed imaging window provides the input
//         feature set for the SVM classifier (236 features
//         per cell, including nuclear p65 level, texture,
//         H2B morphology). Images fed to machine learning
//         pipeline for prestimulation activation prediction.
//         (Fig. 1C-E; Results section p. 2)
//
//         NOTE: mix models the imaging acquisition loop
//         (repeated field-of-view capture over 90 min).
//         No reagent mixing occurs at this step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pbsImaging      = dispense 1 units of PBS
prestimImaging  = mix seededChamber with pbsImaging for 90m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: TNF-alpha stimulation at 0.1 ng/mL.
//         TNF-alpha diluted in FluoroBrite DMEM + 2x GlutaMAX
//         + penicillin/streptomycin + FBS. Vial pressurized
//         at 5 psi; stimulus delivered via PEEK microbore
//         tubing. Stimulation marks t = 0 for all trace
//         analyses. (Materials and Methods, paper p. 8)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfStimulus     = dispense 1 units of FluoroBriteDMEM_TNFalpha
stimulatedCells = mix prestimImaging with tnfStimulus for 2s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Post-stimulation live-cell imaging (6 h = 360m).
//         Cells imaged every 5 min. Nuclear NF-kB (p65-dsRed)
//         translocation tracked in real time per cell.
//         Activated cells: rapid cytoplasm-to-nucleus p65
//         translocation (digital / switch-like, within
//         ~40 min of stimulation). Non-responding cells:
//         no translocation, no target gene expression.
//         Custom MATLAB scripts: background correction,
//         smooth + z-score normalization, peak detection.
//         Activation label: binary threshold on p65_peak
//         - p65_(t=0) > 500. (Fig. 1C-D; Fig. S4A)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat stimulatedCells at 37c for 360m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: NF-kB activation QC detection.
//         nfkbActivationDetection module abstracts the MATLAB
//         image analysis pipeline: nuclear segmentation,
//         mean nuclear fluorescence extraction, peak height
//         computation, and binary label assignment per cell.
//         Detection window: 30 s (represents the final image
//         analysis classification step at end of experiment).
//         Signal > 0: activated cell (p65 peak exceeded
//         threshold of 500; nuclear translocation confirmed).
//         Signal <= 0: non-responding cell.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

activationSignal = detect nfkbActivationDetection on stimulatedCells for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Activation QC gate
// Pass (BB2): activationSignal > 0 -> NF-kB activated cell
//             identified; proceed to fixed-cell IF staining
//             to validate IkB/NF-kB ratio hypothesis.
// Fail (BB3): activationSignal <= 0 -> non-responding cell;
//             drain (no downstream staining required).
// Both branches converge at BB4 (activationJoin).
// ============================================================

if (activationSignal > 0) {
    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 8 (Pass path): Fix cells for immunofluorescence.
    //   PBS rinse (1 min), then 4% paraformaldehyde (PFA)
    //   fixation for 10 min at room temperature (25 C).
    //   Fixation captures the cellular state immediately
    //   before fixation (images taken at t = -5 min used
    //   for UMAP alignment with live imaging data).
    //   (Materials and Methods - Fixed-cell IF, paper p. 8)
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    pbsRinse        = dispense 1 units of PBS
    rinsedCells     = mix stimulatedCells with pbsRinse for 1m

    pfaSolution     = dispense 1 units of ParaformaldehydeFixative
    fixedCells      = mix rinsedCells with pfaSolution for 10m

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 9 (Pass path): Block and permeabilize.
    //   10% BSA + 0.5% Triton X-100 in PBS for 1 h at
    //   room temperature (25 C). Blocks non-specific antibody
    //   binding and permeabilizes cell membranes to allow
    //   access of antibodies to intracellular IkB-alpha.
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    blockingBuffer      = dispense 1 units of BlockingPermeabilizationBuffer
    blockedCells        = mix fixedCells with blockingBuffer for 60m

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 10 (Pass path): Primary antibody incubation.
    //   Anti-IkB-alpha rabbit polyclonal (ab7217) in staining
    //   solution (2% BSA + 0.1% Triton X-100 in PBS) for
    //   1 h at 37 C. IkB-alpha is the main inhibitor of
    //   NF-kB (p65), sequestering it in the cytoplasm of
    //   unstimulated cells. The IkB/NF-kB ratio is the
    //   primary molecular determinant of p65 leakiness and
    //   thus activation probability (Fig. 4; Fig. 5).
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    primaryAntibody     = dispense 1 units of IkBa_PrimaryAntibody
    primaryStained      = mix blockedCells with primaryAntibody for 60m
    heat primaryStained at 37c for 60m

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 11 (Pass path): PBS wash to remove unbound
    //   primary antibody before secondary incubation.
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    pbsWash2            = dispense 1 units of PBS
    washedCells         = mix primaryStained with pbsWash2 for 1m

    // --------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 12 (Pass path): Secondary antibody incubation.
    //   Goat polyclonal anti-rabbit IgG H&L Alexa Fluor 647
    //   (G378361) in staining solution for 1 h at 37 C.
    //   Alexa Fluor 647: excitation ~650 nm, emission ~668 nm.
    //   Enables quantification of IkB-alpha protein levels
    //   in nuclear and cytoplasmic compartments by
    //   immunofluorescence for IkB/NF-kB ratio analysis.
    //   (Materials and Methods; Fig. 5A-D, paper pp. 6-7)
    // --------------------------------------------------------

    secondaryAntibody   = dispense 1 units of AlexaFluor647_SecondaryAntibody
    stainedCells        = mix washedCells with secondaryAntibody for 60m
    heat stainedCells at 37c for 60m

    dispose stainedCells
} else {
    drain stimulatedCells
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: activationJoin
// Dispose activation QC signal. Control falls into the
// immunofluorescence staining QC branch (BB5/BB6).
// ============================================================

dispose activationSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 13: Immunofluorescence staining QC detection.
//   immunofluorescenceDetection module abstracts the image
//   analysis step for fixed cells: nuclear and cytoplasmic
//   segmentation of Alexa Fluor 647 signal (IkB-alpha),
//   p65-dsRed channel quantification, and computation of
//   the IkB/NF-kB ratio per cell.
//   Validation outcome: significant inverse correlation
//   between IkB/NF-kB ratio and leaky nuclear p65
//   (rho = -0.256, P = 2.7e-11; Fig. 5C). Activated
//   (high-scoring) fixed cells show significantly lower
//   IkB/NF-kB ratio, higher nuclear NF-kB, and larger
//   cellular area than inactive cells (Fig. 5D).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pbsFinalWash        = dispense 1 units of PBS
finalSample         = mix pbsFinalWash with pbsFinalWash for 1m

ifStainingSignal    = detect immunofluorescenceDetection on finalSample for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: Immunofluorescence staining QC gate
// Pass (BB5): ifStainingSignal > 0 -> IkB/NF-kB ratio
//             successfully quantified; dispose stained sample.
// Fail (BB6): ifStainingSignal <= 0 -> staining failure
//             (antibody issue or segmentation error); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (ifStainingSignal > 0) {
    dispose finalSample
} else {
    drain finalSample
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose immunofluorescence QC signal. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose ifStainingSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]

module barcodeIntegrityDetection
module wqaQCDetection

